window.TANDEM_SPEAK = [{"id":"welcome.s1","q":"\"Your concurrent code passed every test you ran. How do you convince yourself, and me, that it is actually correct?\"","points":["A passing run proves little: the failing interleaving can be rare and depend on timing, cores and load","Reason first: name the shared state and its invariant, and check every access is under the same lock or is one atomic step","Walk the risky interleavings on purpose: read-modify-write, check then act, and the order locks are taken in","Tools: ThreadSanitizer for C++ data races, where any report on ordinary code means it is broken","Stress tests with many threads and iterations, exact invariants checked at the end, and a timeout that fails with a thread dump instead of hanging"],"model":"Passing tests are the weakest evidence I have, because a concurrency bug lives in one unlucky interleaving that may show up once in a million runs, or only on a busier machine. So I start with reasoning. I name the shared state and the rule it must keep, say the balance always matches the history, and I check that every read and write of that state happens under the same lock, or is a single atomic step. Then I walk the risky interleavings on purpose: two threads doing read, modify, write, a check followed by an act, and the order in which locks are taken. Then tools. In C++ I run the tests under ThreadSanitizer, and any report on ordinary code means it is broken, even if the output looked right. Java has no race detector in the JDK, so there I lean on stress tests: many threads, many iterations, exact final counts, and a timeout that fails the test and prints a thread dump instead of hanging. Reasoning tells me why it is correct. The tests and the sanitizer catch what my reasoning missed.","follow":"Why can a stress test pass on your laptop and still fail in production?","seconds":90,"lesson":"welcome","title":"Welcome to Tandem","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"welcome.s2","q":"\"Two threads each print one line, and main joins both, then prints 'done'. Walk me through every output that is possible. What changes if you forget one of the joins?\"","points":["Starting a thread only makes it runnable; the scheduler picks the order, so the two lines can come either way","<code>join<\/code> waits for the thread to finish, and everything it did happens before <code>join<\/code> returns, so \"done\" is always last","Forget one join in Java: \"done\" can print before that thread's line, and the JVM still waits for the non daemon thread before it exits","Forget one join in C++: destroying a joinable <code>std::thread<\/code> calls <code>std::terminate<\/code>; <code>std::jthread<\/code> joins in its destructor","Whole lines are not free: Java's <code>println<\/code> locks the stream, but in C++ characters from two threads can interleave unless you lock"],"model":"There are exactly two possible outputs: the two thread lines in either order, then \"done\". Starting a thread only makes it runnable. The scheduler decides who runs first, so the thread I started second can print first. \"Done\" is always last because <code>join<\/code> waits until that thread has finished, and everything the thread did happens before <code>join<\/code> returns. Main prints only after both joins. If I forget one join, it depends on the language. In Java, \"done\" can now print before that thread's line, and the program still waits for the thread before it exits, because it is not a daemon. In C++ it is a crash: destroying a <code>std::thread<\/code> that was never joined calls <code>std::terminate<\/code>. That is one reason I like <code>std::jthread<\/code>, which joins in its destructor. One more detail: in C++, two threads writing to <code>std::cout<\/code> can interleave characters inside a line, so I print under a lock.","follow":"How would you make the two threads always print in a fixed order?","seconds":70,"lesson":"welcome","title":"Welcome to Tandem","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"welcome.s3","q":"\"A service hangs under load, and CPU is close to zero. What do you look at first, and what are you hoping to find?\"","points":["Near zero CPU plus a hang means threads are blocked, not spinning: a deadlock, a lost wakeup, or a wait for something that never arrives","First move is evidence: dump every thread's stack (<code>jstack<\/code> or <code>jcmd &lt;pid&gt; Thread.print<\/code> in Java, a debugger or <code>sample<\/code> for C++), twice, a few seconds apart","A cycle of threads, each holding one lock and waiting for another's, is a deadlock; the JVM prints the cycle for you","No cycle: threads waiting on a condition nobody will signal, pool threads all waiting on tasks queued behind them in the same pool, or calls to a slow dependency with no timeout","The fix follows the cause: one global lock order, signal under the lock and wait in a loop, a separate pool, or a timeout on every remote call"],"model":"Near zero CPU tells me the threads are not spinning, they are parked, so I am looking for what they are all waiting on. My first move is evidence, not guessing. I take a dump of every thread's stack: in Java that is <code>jstack<\/code>, or <code>jcmd<\/code> with <code>Thread.print<\/code>; in C++ I attach a debugger and print every thread's backtrace, or use <code>sample<\/code> on a Mac. I take two dumps a few seconds apart, so I know the threads are stuck and not just slow. Then I look for one of three shapes. A cycle, where thread A holds lock one and waits for lock two while B holds two and waits for one, is a deadlock, and the JVM prints that cycle for me. Threads waiting on a condition that nobody will ever signal is a lost wakeup. And every pool thread blocked on the result of a task still sitting in the same pool's queue is pool starvation; a slow dependency with no timeout looks similar. The fix follows the cause: one lock order, signaling under the lock with waits in a loop, a separate pool, or timeouts.","follow":"The dump shows no cycle, and every worker thread is in WAITING inside your queue's <code>take<\/code>. What next?","seconds":90,"lesson":"welcome","title":"Welcome to Tandem","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"how-companies-test.s1","q":"\"Here is the problem: build a job scheduler that many threads submit to. Before you write anything, what do you want to ask me, and why does each answer matter?\"","points":["Ask before designing, and say what each answer changes in the code","Callers: how many threads submit, and may a submit block or must it return at once","Toolbox: is the standard concurrency library allowed, or should the queue and pool be built from a lock and condition variables","Format: must the code compile and run here, or is detailed pseudocode enough","Rules and lifecycle: ordering or fairness, what shutdown does with queued jobs, and what happens when a job throws or hangs","Then the plan: a working single threaded core first, then the shared state, the invariant, the primitive and the interleaving that breaks a naive version"],"model":"Before I design anything, I want five answers, because each one changes the code. First, how many threads submit, and may a submit block, or must it return at once? That decides whether I need a queue in front of the workers and what happens when it fills. Second, may I use the standard library, or should I build the queue and the pool myself from a lock and condition variables? Some companies ban the library on purpose. Third, must this compile and run here, or is detailed pseudocode enough? Fourth, is there an ordering or fairness rule, say jobs start in submission order, or no submitter starves? And fifth, lifecycle: what does shutdown do with jobs still queued, and what should happen when a job throws or never returns? Then I would say my plan: a working single threaded core first, then I name the shared state and its invariant, pick the simplest primitive that protects it, and walk through the interleaving that would break a naive version.","follow":"No libraries allowed. What is the first class you write, and what does it guard?","seconds":80,"lesson":"how-companies-test","title":"How top companies test concurrency","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"how-companies-test.s2","q":"\"Your LRU cache works. Now many threads call <code>get<\/code> and <code>put<\/code> at the same time. Talk me through what changes, before you write any code.\"","points":["Name the shared state, the map and the recency list, and the invariants: they always agree, and the size never exceeds capacity","<code>get<\/code> is a write, because it moves the entry to the front, so a read-write lock buys almost nothing","First version: one mutex around every <code>get<\/code> and <code>put<\/code>, with short critical sections and no I/O inside","Name the races the lock prevents: two puts overfilling the cache, or a <code>get<\/code> moving a node that a concurrent <code>put<\/code> is evicting","To scale: shard by key hash into smaller caches with their own locks, and say that eviction becomes only approximately least recently used","Test it: many threads, check size and map list consistency, and run C++ under ThreadSanitizer"],"model":"First I name the shared state: the hash map and the recency list, and the rules that they always agree and the size never goes past capacity. The trap is that <code>get<\/code> is not a read. It moves the entry to the front of the list, so it changes shared state, and a read-write lock buys almost nothing. So my first version is one mutex around every <code>get<\/code> and <code>put<\/code>, with short critical sections and nothing slow inside. Each acquisition prevents a specific race: two puts both seeing room and both inserting past capacity, or a <code>get<\/code> moving a node that a concurrent <code>put<\/code> is evicting, which corrupts the list. If that one lock becomes the bottleneck, I would shard by key hash into, say, sixteen smaller caches, each with its own lock. That scales, but eviction becomes approximately least recently used rather than exact, and I would say that trade off out loud. To test it, I run many threads, check the size never exceeds capacity and the map and list stay consistent, and in C++ I run it under ThreadSanitizer.","follow":"Why not a <code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code> for the map and a separate lock for the list?","seconds":90,"lesson":"how-companies-test","title":"How top companies test concurrency","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"how-companies-test.s3","q":"\"Two users click 'book' on the last seat at the same moment. How do you make sure exactly one of them gets it?\"","points":["The check and the reserve must be one atomic step, or both users see the seat as free","In one process: a lock per seat or per show around the check and the reserve","Across many servers a process lock does not help: the database decides, with a conditional update where the seat is still free, and a check of rows changed","A hold with an expiry, confirmed after payment, with no row lock held through payment","An idempotency key, so a retried request does not book twice; for a hugely popular event, a queue or waiting room in front"],"model":"Exactly one wins only if checking the seat and reserving it are a single atomic step. Otherwise both requests read \"free\" and both book. Inside one process, I would keep a lock per show, or per seat, and do \"if free, mark it held by this user\" inside that lock, so the second caller sees it taken. But a real booking service runs on many servers, and a lock in one process means nothing to the others. So the database is the arbiter: one conditional update that makes this user the holder only where the seat is still free, with no holder or only an expired hold, and I check that exactly one row changed. The loser gets a clear \"seat taken\". The hold carries an expiry, so an abandoned checkout frees the seat, and after payment a second conditional update confirms it only if the hold still belongs to this user. I never keep a row lock open through payment, because payment can take seconds or fail. Each request also carries an idempotency key, so a retry after a timeout does not book twice. And for a hugely popular event, I would put a queue or a waiting room in front, so the database is not hit by everyone at once.","follow":"The hold expires while the payment is still in flight, and the payment then succeeds. What happens, and what should happen?","seconds":90,"lesson":"how-companies-test","title":"How top companies test concurrency","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"study-plan.s1","q":"\"Here is the problem: a bounded blocking queue, where <code>put<\/code> blocks when it is full and <code>take<\/code> blocks when it is empty. Before you write any code, talk me through how you will approach it.\"","points":["Restate the contract and ask the clarifying questions: capacity, block forever or time out, shutdown, which libraries are allowed","Name the shared state: the buffer and its count","State the invariant: size between zero and capacity, first in first out, nothing lost or duplicated","Pick the simplest primitive and say why: one lock with two conditions, not full and not empty","Every wait sits in a while loop, shown with the interleaving that breaks an if","Say how you will test it: several producers and consumers, every item out exactly once"],"model":"First I would restate it and pin down the contract. What is the capacity? Should <code>put<\/code> and <code>take<\/code> block forever or take a timeout? Do we need shutdown? And may I use the library queue, or should I build it from a lock and conditions? Then I name the shared state, which is just the buffer and its count, and the invariant: the size stays between zero and the capacity, items come out in the order they went in, and nothing is lost or duplicated. The simplest primitive that keeps that is one lock with two conditions, not full and not empty. <code>put<\/code> takes the lock, waits while the queue is full, adds the item and signals not empty. <code>take<\/code> mirrors it. Every wait sits in a while loop. To show why, I walk the naive version with an if: two consumers wait on an empty queue, one item arrives, both are woken by a notify all or a spurious wakeup, the first takes it, and the second takes from an empty buffer. Then I write it, and test it with several producers and consumers, checking that every item comes out exactly once.","follow":"How would you add a <code>shutdown<\/code> that releases every blocked thread?","seconds":90,"lesson":"study-plan","title":"Your study plan","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"study-plan.s2","q":"\"Tell me about a concurrency or performance problem you handled at work. What was shared, what broke, and how did you fix it?\"","points":["The context in two sentences: the system, its scale, and why the problem mattered","The shared state and the rule it had to keep","The symptom, and the evidence that found the cause: a thread dump, a profile or a metric","The fix, the trade off chosen, and the option rejected","The result in numbers, and what now stops it coming back"],"model":"The clearest one was in a metrics ingestion service that handled about twenty thousand requests a second on two hundred threads. Every request read a tenant's settings from an in memory cache, and the cache sat behind one lock. The symptom was that p99 latency jumped from about forty milliseconds to two seconds every few minutes, with no rise in traffic. I took a few thread dumps during a spike. Most request threads were blocked on that one monitor, and the thread holding it was refreshing a tenant's settings over the network while still holding the lock. So one slow call stalled the whole service. The fix was to keep the network call out of the lock. The refresher builds a new immutable settings map without the lock, then swaps one volatile reference, and readers take no lock at all. I considered a read write lock, but it only helps once the writer stops calling out while holding it, and after that change a single reference swap is simpler and lets readers skip the lock entirely. p99 went back to about forty five milliseconds. I also added a metric for lock hold time and a code review rule: no input or output while holding a lock.","follow":"What would you design differently if you built that service again?","seconds":90,"lesson":"study-plan","title":"Your study plan","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"study-plan.s3","q":"\"You are 35 minutes into a 60 minute round. Your queue hangs in your own test and you cannot see why. Talk me through what you do.\"","points":["Keep talking: say what you are checking so the interviewer can help","A hang means a thread waits for a signal that never comes, or threads wait on each other's locks","Get evidence: a thread dump with <code>jstack<\/code> in Java, every thread's stack in the debugger in C++","Check the usual causes: a signal on the wrong condition, a lock not released on an error path, two locks in different orders","After about five minutes, fall back to the simplest correct version and get it working"],"model":"First, I keep talking, because silence wastes the interviewer's chance to help me. A hang means some thread is waiting for something that will never happen, so I look for who is waiting and on what. In Java I take a thread dump with <code>jstack<\/code> and look for threads waiting on a condition, and threads blocked on a lock that another thread holds. In C++ I pause it in the debugger and print every thread's stack. Then I check the usual causes in order. A signal sent on the wrong condition, for example <code>put<\/code> signalling not full instead of not empty. A lock taken by hand and not released when something throws, which is why I use <code>try<\/code> and <code>finally<\/code>, or a guard. Two locks taken in different orders. If I have not found it in about five minutes, I fall back to the simplest version I trust: one lock, notify all after every change, and every wait in a while loop. A correct simple queue at minute 45 beats a clever one that hangs, and I tell the interviewer which optimization I would add back with more time.","follow":"Your fixed queue now uses notify all everywhere. What does that cost, and when is a single notify safe?","seconds":90,"lesson":"study-plan","title":"Your study plan","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"speaking-like-a-senior.s1","q":"\"Explain a mutex to a junior engineer in 45 seconds.\"","points":["Define a mutex as allowing one thread at a time to hold the same lock","Explain that every relevant access must follow the locking rule","Give a concrete example of two callers competing for one ticket","State the cost of waiting and keeping protected work short","Close with how you would use and release the lock safely"],"model":"A mutex lets one thread at a time hold a particular lock. Think of one key to a shared room. Every thread accessing the protected state must follow the same locking rule. Suppose two callers try to reserve the last ticket. I put the availability check and reservation under that lock, so the second caller cannot inspect the count halfway through the first reservation. Waiting for the lock costs time, so I keep that section short. I would use automatic release: a synchronized block in Java or a scoped lock guard in C++, including when an exception leaves the section.","follow":"What happens if one reader ignores the locking rule?","seconds":45,"lesson":"speaking-like-a-senior","title":"Speaking like a senior: how to say what you know","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"speaking-like-a-senior.s2","q":"\"A service hangs in production. Walk me through your first five minutes.\"","points":["Establish impact, scope and whether requests make progress before naming a cause","Check request rate, errors, CPU, queue growth and recent changes","Compare repeated thread stacks and dependency latency, using the learner's language tools","Describe a concrete wait cycle and distinguish it from slow I/O or worker exhaustion","Balance evidence collection against urgent mitigation","Close with evidence that would justify the next action and verify recovery"],"model":"I would first establish the impact and collect evidence about where progress stopped. A hang alone does not prove deadlock. In the first minute I would check whether all requests or one endpoint are affected, whether completions have stopped, and whether a deployment or traffic change preceded it. Then I would compare CPU, queue depth, errors and dependency latency with normal behaviour. I would collect repeated thread stacks: Java thread dumps or all thread backtraces from a C++ debugger. If thread A holds one lock and waits for a second held by B, while B waits for A's lock, that is a concrete deadlock hypothesis. Many workers waiting on a slow dependency suggest a different problem. I would inspect lock owners and wait conditions rather than guessing from low CPU alone. If impact is severe, I would use the incident procedure to reduce load or roll back a suspect change while preserving evidence where feasible. By minute five I want a supported hypothesis and a safe next action, then I would verify that completions and latency recover. Mitigation restores service; it does not establish the root cause.","follow":"All workers are waiting for queued tasks in the same pool. What would you check next?","seconds":90,"lesson":"speaking-like-a-senior","title":"Speaking like a senior: how to say what you know","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"speaking-like-a-senior.s3","q":"\"Before coding the last-ticket reservation, talk me through your rule, synchronization choice and test. Tell me what remains uncertain.\"","points":["Clarify that callers share one in-process object and state the no-overselling rule","Choose one mutex around availability check and decrement","Show the two-caller interleaving prevented by that scope","Name the serialization cost and avoid claiming fairness","Propose competing callers, exact success counts and repeated tests without treating tests as proof"],"model":"I would first confirm that all callers share one in-process ticket object. My rule is that at most one call can reserve the final ticket. I would use one mutex around both the availability check and the decrement, with every access following that rule. Otherwise two callers can both see one ticket before either reserves it. The cost is that reservations take turns, so I would keep the protected work short. I have not established a fairness guarantee; that is separate from preventing overselling. I would start two callers from a common gate, join them, and assert exactly one successful reservation. Then I would repeat with more contention. Tests add evidence, while the locking argument explains why the rule holds.","follow":"What changes if the callers run in separate service processes?","seconds":60,"lesson":"speaking-like-a-senior","title":"Speaking like a senior: how to say what you know","track":"core","module":0},{"id":"processes-and-threads.s1","q":"\"A junior engineer on your team says local variables are always thread-safe. Are they? How would you explain it to them?\"","points":["The variable itself is private: every call gets its own copy on the stack of the thread that made the call","A local is often a reference or pointer, and the object it points to lives on the shared heap","The object becomes shared the moment it escapes: stored in a field, handed to another thread, captured by a task","In C++ a <code>[&amp;]<\/code> capture or a pointer to a local shares the stack slot itself, and it dangles if a detached thread outlives the function","The real question is who else can reach the object; then keep it confined, make it immutable, or synchronize it"],"model":"Partly. The variable itself is always private. Every call gets its own copy on the stack of the thread that made the call, so an <code>int<\/code> counter inside a method can never race. But a local is often a reference, and the object it points to lives on the heap, which every thread can reach. A local list is safe only until it escapes. The moment I store it in a field, hand it to an executor, or capture it in a task that runs elsewhere, two threads can touch the same list. C++ has a sharper version. If I start a thread with a lambda that captures by reference, the stack slot itself is shared, and if that thread is detached and the function returns, it writes into a dead frame. So the question I teach is not whether it is local. It is who else can reach this object. If nobody can, it is confined and needs nothing. Otherwise it must be immutable, or guarded by a lock or an atomic.","follow":"Then is an object held in a <code>final<\/code> field safe to share between threads?","seconds":75,"lesson":"processes-and-threads","title":"Processes and threads: the memory picture","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"processes-and-threads.s2","q":"\"A third party native library you call to resize images segfaults about once a week, and every time it does, your whole service goes down with every request in flight. What would you change?\"","points":["A segfault in any thread kills the whole process, because all threads share one address space","Catching it inside the process does not work: it is a signal in C++, and a native crash takes the whole JVM down in Java","The fix is a process boundary: run the library in a pool of separate worker processes","The service talks to the workers over IPC, a pipe or a local socket, with shared memory for large images","A supervisor restarts dead workers, only the request on that worker fails and can be retried, and every job gets a timeout","The cost is memory per worker and a copy or mapping per request, paid for crash containment"],"model":"I would move the library out of the service's process. All threads share one address space, so a segfault in any thread, even deep inside a native call, kills the whole process and every request with it. I cannot catch my way out of it: in C++ it is a signal, not an exception, and in Java a crash in native code takes the whole JVM down. Only a process boundary contains it. So I would run a small pool of worker processes that do nothing but resize images. The service sends each job over a pipe or a local socket, and for large images it puts the bytes in a shared memory segment and sends only a handle. A supervisor restarts any worker that dies, and only the one request that was on it fails, so it can be retried. Every job also gets a timeout, because a native bug can hang as easily as it can crash. The price is extra memory per worker and a copy or a mapping per request, which is small next to resizing an image. It is the same reason browsers put sites in separate processes.","follow":"How many worker processes would you run, and what happens when all of them are busy?","seconds":90,"lesson":"processes-and-threads","title":"Processes and threads: the memory picture","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"processes-and-threads.s3","q":"\"Your multithreaded C++ server forks a child to run a helper tool. Once in a while the child hangs forever before the helper ever starts. What is going on, and how do you fix it?\"","points":["<code>fork<\/code> copies the whole address space but only the thread that called it","A lock that another thread held at that instant is copied in the locked state, with no owner left to release it","If the child calls code that takes that lock before <code>exec<\/code>, a logging call for example, it blocks forever; that is why it is intermittent","POSIX allows only async-signal-safe calls between <code>fork<\/code> and <code>exec<\/code> in a threaded program","The fix: prepare everything before forking and call <code>execve<\/code> straight away in the child, or use <code>posix_spawn<\/code>"],"model":"This is the classic fork in a threaded program bug. <code>fork<\/code> copies the whole address space, but only the thread that called it. Every other thread simply does not exist in the child. Now suppose a logging thread held the logger's mutex at the instant of the fork. The child gets a copy of that mutex in the locked state, and the thread that would unlock it is gone. If the child logs anything before it calls <code>exec<\/code>, it waits on that lock forever. It is intermittent because it only happens when the fork lands while some other thread holds a lock. That is why POSIX says the child of a multithreaded process may only call async-signal-safe functions until it execs. So the fix is to do all the work before forking. Build the argument array and the environment first, then in the child call <code>execve<\/code> at once, with nothing in between except closing or redirecting file descriptors. Better still, use <code>posix_spawn<\/code>, which does both steps in one call, with only safe code inside the C library in between.","follow":"Why does Java's <code>ProcessBuilder<\/code> not expose you to this bug?","seconds":90,"lesson":"processes-and-threads","title":"Processes and threads: the memory picture","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"concurrency-vs-parallelism.s1","q":"\"A teammate says: this service runs in a container with one CPU, so we can drop the locks. What do you tell them?\"","points":["Keep the locks: races need concurrency, not parallelism","Preemption can stop a thread between the read and the write of <code>count += 1<\/code>","A concrete interleaving: A reads 5 and is preempted, B writes 6, A writes 6, one update is lost","The compiler can still cache and reorder, and in C++ a data race is undefined behavior on any core count","Correctness must never depend on the core count, which is a deployment setting that will change"],"model":"I would keep the locks. A race needs two threads that interleave, not two cores. On one CPU the scheduler can still stop a thread between any two instructions. Say thread A reads a counter of 5 into a register, and its time slice ends. Thread B reads 5, adds one and writes 6. A resumes with its stale 5 and also writes 6. One update is gone, and no second core was involved. A single core does remove some hardware reordering, but the compiler can still keep values in registers and reorder code, and in C++ an unsynchronized conflicting access is a data race, which is undefined behavior whatever the hardware. There is also a practical point. The CPU limit is a deployment setting, and someone will raise it next quarter. Code that is only correct on one core breaks quietly when they do. So correctness has to come from synchronization, never from the core count.","follow":"Then what does a single core actually change for concurrent code?","seconds":70,"lesson":"concurrency-vs-parallelism","title":"Concurrency versus parallelism","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"concurrency-vs-parallelism.s2","q":"\"A batch job runs about 3 times faster on 32 cores than on 1. Walk me through why it is not 32 times, and what you would check.\"","points":["Amdahl's law: the serial fraction caps the speedup, 1 / ((1 - p) + p / n)","Put a number on it: 3 times on 32 cores means only about 70 percent of the run is parallel, so more cores give little more","Serial parts to look for: a single threaded read or merge, and every critical section under one shared lock","Limits Amdahl does not model: uneven chunks, logical versus physical cores, memory bandwidth, start and join overhead","Measure per phase and profile lock contention, then shrink the serial part instead of buying cores"],"model":"Because the part that cannot run in parallel sets a ceiling. Amdahl's law says the speedup is one over the serial fraction plus the parallel fraction divided by the cores. If I work backwards, 3 times on 32 cores means only about 70 percent of the run is parallel. The other 30 percent runs on one core no matter what, so even unlimited cores would give little more than 3. So my first question is where the serial time goes. The usual culprits are a single threaded phase, like reading the input or merging the results at the end, and any critical section under one shared lock, because only one thread can be inside it at a time. Then I would look at what Amdahl does not model. Uneven chunks, so a few threads finish late while the rest sit idle. Logical versus physical cores, since 32 logical CPUs may be 16 physical cores with hyper-threading. Memory bandwidth, if the threads mostly stream data. And the fixed cost of starting threads and joining results. I would measure before changing anything: time each phase, and profile for lock contention. The fix is almost always to shrink the serial part, by splitting the lock or merging in parallel, rather than to buy more cores, because halving the serial part doubles the ceiling.","follow":"If the dataset grows with the machine, does Amdahl's law still describe what you will see?","seconds":95,"lesson":"concurrency-vs-parallelism","title":"Concurrency versus parallelism","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"concurrency-vs-parallelism.s3","q":"\"A request handler calls three downstream services one after another, about 100 milliseconds each. Would a machine with more cores make it faster? What would?\"","points":["No: the handler is waiting, not computing, so extra cores sit idle","If the calls are independent, issue them concurrently, so latency is the slowest call, about 100 ms, not the sum","That is concurrency, not parallelism: it works even on one core, because a waiting call needs no core","If a call needs the previous answer, the chain cannot overlap: cache it, batch it, or ask for a combined API","More cores matter only when CPU is the bottleneck: high CPU and flat throughput under load"],"model":"No. That request spends about 300 milliseconds waiting and almost nothing computing, so more cores would just sit idle. What helps is overlapping the waits. If the three calls do not depend on each other, I would start all three at once, with futures or virtual threads in Java, or <code>std::async<\/code> in C++, and wait for all of them. Then the latency is the slowest call, about 100 milliseconds, instead of the sum. That is concurrency, not parallelism, and it works even on a single core, because a waiting call does not need a core. If call two needs call one's answer, overlapping is impossible, so I would look at caching, or a batched or combined endpoint, to shorten the chain. I would also put a timeout on each call, so one slow service cannot hold the whole request. More cores only matter when the machine is CPU bound under load, which shows up as high CPU and flat throughput, not as slow single requests.","follow":"Now one of the three services gets slow. What happens to your handler, and how do you protect it?","seconds":75,"lesson":"concurrency-vs-parallelism","title":"Concurrency versus parallelism","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"cpu-vs-io-bound.s1","q":"\"Here is a symptom: p99 latency went from 50 milliseconds to 800, CPU is at 5 percent, and the team wants to double the thread pool. What do you check first?\"","points":["Low CPU with high latency means the threads are waiting, not computing, so cores are not the limit","Take a few thread dumps and see where threads wait: one dependency's socket read, a connection pool, or a lock","Check that dependency's latency and saturation; it is probably the real bottleneck","Doubling threads sends more work to a saturated dependency and grows its queue","A CPU profiler cannot see waiting; use wall clock or off CPU profiling","Fix: cap concurrency at what the dependency can take, with timeouts and backpressure"],"model":"I would not double the pool yet. CPU at 5 percent with latency up sixteen times tells me the threads are waiting, not computing, so the cores are not the limit. First I would take three or four thread dumps a few seconds apart and look at where the threads sit. If most of them are in a socket read to the same downstream, or waiting to borrow a database connection, or blocked on one lock, that is my bottleneck. Then I would check that dependency's own numbers: its latency, its errors, its connection count. If it is saturated, doubling our threads only sends it more concurrent work, so its queue grows and everyone gets slower. I would not trust a CPU profile here either. It only samples threads that are on a core, so it shows the small slice of parsing and misses most of the 800 milliseconds. The fix is usually the opposite of more threads: cap concurrency to that dependency at what it can serve, with a semaphore or the connection pool, put a timeout on every call, and shed the excess, so one slow dependency cannot hold every thread.","follow":"The dumps show threads waiting to borrow from our own connection pool, and the database looks idle. What now?","seconds":85,"lesson":"cpu-vs-io-bound","title":"CPU bound versus I/O bound work","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"cpu-vs-io-bound.s2","q":"\"You own two services: one makes image thumbnails, the other calls three downstream APIs for each request and merges the answers. How do you size the thread pools, and why differently?\"","points":["Classify first: thumbnails are CPU bound, the aggregator is I/O bound; confirm with CPU time against wall time","CPU pool: about the number of cores, perhaps cores plus one; more threads add switching, not throughput","I/O pool: about cores times one plus W over C, for example 8 cores with W/C of 20 is roughly 170","Cap the I/O side by what each dependency can take, with a connection pool or a semaphore per dependency","Verify by raising the limit step by step and watching throughput, CPU and dependency latency","Virtual threads make many waiting threads cheap, but the dependency limits stay"],"model":"I would size them differently because they spend their time differently. Thumbnails are CPU bound: decoding, resizing and encoding an image keeps a core busy the whole time. So that pool gets about one thread per core, maybe cores plus one so a thread is ready when another stalls. More threads than that add no throughput; they take turns on the same cores and evict each other's caches. The aggregator is I/O bound: each request computes for a few milliseconds and waits much longer on the three APIs. There I would start from Goetz's formula, cores times one plus the wait to compute ratio. With 8 cores and requests that wait twenty times longer than they compute, that is roughly 170 threads to keep the CPU busy. But that number only describes my CPU. Each downstream has its own limit, so I would put a semaphore or a connection pool per dependency, sized to what it can serve, so one slow API cannot hold every thread. Then I would verify: raise the limit step by step, watch throughput, CPU and the dependencies' latency, and stop where throughput flattens. On Java 21 I might run the aggregator on virtual threads, which makes thousands of waiting threads cheap, but the per dependency limits stay, because cheap threads do not make the downstream any faster.","follow":"One request type calls an API and then renders a large PDF. Where does each part of that work run?","seconds":95,"lesson":"cpu-vs-io-bound","title":"CPU bound versus I/O bound work","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"cpu-vs-io-bound.s3","q":"\"How would you tell whether a piece of work is CPU bound or I/O bound, without guessing?\"","points":["Compare CPU time with wall time for the same work","A ratio near 1 means computing; near 0 means waiting, and the ratio says how much","Tools: <code>time<\/code> (real against user plus sys), per thread CPU in <code>top<\/code>, <code>ThreadMXBean<\/code> or the thread CPU clock in C++","Thread dumps show what the waiting threads wait for: socket reads, pool borrows, locks","Two traps: waiting for a core on an overloaded machine looks like I/O, and spinning or GC looks like computing"],"model":"I measure two clocks and compare them. Wall time is how long the work took. CPU time is how long a core was actually executing it. If the two are close, the work is computing, so it is CPU bound. If CPU time is a small fraction of wall time, it spent most of its life waiting, so it is I/O bound, and the ratio tells me how much. For a whole program I run <code>time<\/code> and compare real with user plus sys. Per thread, I read <code>ThreadMXBean<\/code> in Java or the thread CPU clock in C++, or look at per thread CPU in <code>top<\/code>. A few thread dumps then tell me what the waiting threads wait for: a socket read, a connection pool, a lock. There are two traps. On an overloaded machine, a thread waiting for a free core also looks like waiting, so I check the load against the core count. And busy CPU is not always useful work: spinning, garbage collection and cache misses all look busy.","follow":"The work turns out to be mostly waiting. Would switching to async I/O make it faster than threads?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpu-vs-io-bound","title":"CPU bound versus I/O bound work","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"scheduler-and-context-switch.s1","q":"\"People use system call, context switch and process switch as if they meant the same thing. Tell me how they differ, and why their costs differ.\"","points":["A system call is a mode switch: user mode to kernel mode and back, and the same thread keeps running","A context switch is the kernel saving one thread's registers and restoring another's, because the first blocked or was preempted","A process switch also changes the address space, so it disturbs the TLB and caches more","Orders of magnitude: around a hundred nanoseconds for a trivial system call, around a microsecond direct cost for a switch","The indirect cost, cold caches and TLB for the incoming thread, is usually larger than the direct cost"],"model":"They are three different things, and the cost grows from one to the next. A system call is a mode switch. The thread asks the kernel for something, the CPU moves into kernel mode and back, and the same thread keeps running. A trivial one costs around a hundred nanoseconds, as an order of magnitude. A context switch is the kernel taking the core away from one thread and giving it to another. It saves the old thread's registers, picks the next thread from the run queue and restores its registers. That happens when a thread blocks, or when its time slice runs out while others are waiting. The direct cost is around a microsecond, but the bigger cost is indirect: the incoming thread finds the caches and the TLB full of someone else's data. A process switch is a context switch that also changes the address space, so it disturbs the TLB and caches even more. So a system call that returns at once is cheap. The expensive moment is when a thread has to wait.","follow":"Why is an uncontended mutex so much cheaper than a contended one?","seconds":75,"lesson":"scheduler-and-context-switch","title":"The scheduler, time slices and context switches","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"scheduler-and-context-switch.s2","q":"\"A CPU bound service runs 400 worker threads on a 16 core machine. Throughput is flat, system CPU time is high and tail latency is bad. What is going on, and what do you check?\"","points":["Oversubscription: about 25 runnable threads per core, so threads are preempted while they still have work","The tax: the direct switch cost, cold caches and TLB, and lock holders preempted while waiters queue behind them","Evidence: high involuntary switch counts per thread (<code>pidstat -wt<\/code>), the <code>cs<\/code> column in <code>vmstat<\/code>, high system time","Fix: size the CPU pool near the core count, and move blocking I/O to its own pool or to asynchronous calls","Verify by measuring throughput, tail latency and switch counts again after the change"],"model":"It is almost certainly oversubscription. With 400 CPU bound threads on 16 cores, about 25 threads are runnable per core, so the scheduler keeps preempting threads that still have work to do. Each switch costs around a microsecond directly, and more through cold caches and TLB misses, and that shows up as high system time with no gain in throughput. Latency suffers too, because a request can sit in a run queue for many time slices, and if a thread is preempted while it holds a lock, everyone waiting for that lock waits with it. To confirm, I would look at involuntary context switches per thread with <code>pidstat -wt<\/code>, the <code>cs<\/code> column in <code>vmstat<\/code>, and the split between user and system time. If involuntary switches dominate, that is the switching tax. The fix is to size the pool for CPU work near the core count, so about 16, and if some of the work blocks on I/O, to move that part to its own pool or make it asynchronous. Then I measure again: throughput should rise, tail latency should fall, and involuntary switches should drop sharply.","follow":"What would it mean if the switches were mostly voluntary instead?","seconds":80,"lesson":"scheduler-and-context-switch","title":"The scheduler, time slices and context switches","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"scheduler-and-context-switch.s3","q":"\"Compare first come first served, shortest job first and round robin. Which would you choose for an interactive system, and what does each one cost you?\"","points":["The pick for interactive work: round robin or a multilevel feedback queue, because response time is what users feel","FCFS: simple with few switches, but the convoy effect makes short jobs wait behind long ones","SJF, and its preemptive form SRTF: lowest average waiting time, but burst lengths must be estimated and long jobs can starve without aging","Round robin: fast first response and fairness, worse average turnaround, and the quantum trades switching overhead against FCFS behaviour","MLFQ approximates SJF without knowing burst lengths; the same trade offs appear in locks (FIFO versus barging)"],"model":"For an interactive system I would pick round robin, or better a multilevel feedback queue, because what users feel is response time. First come first served is the simplest and has the fewest switches, but it suffers from the convoy effect: one long job makes every short job behind it wait. Shortest job first fixes that and gives the lowest average waiting time, and its preemptive version, shortest remaining time first, does even better. The catch is that you have to estimate burst lengths, and long jobs can starve unless you add aging, which raises a job's priority the longer it waits. Round robin gives every job one quantum in turn, so everyone starts quickly, but average turnaround is usually worse than shortest job first. The quantum is the knob: too small and switching eats the CPU, too large and it turns back into first come first served. A multilevel feedback queue gets close to shortest job first without knowing bursts, by demoting jobs that use their whole quantum. The same trade off shows up in my own code: a FIFO lock is first come first served, and a lock that lets newcomers barge buys throughput at the risk of starvation.","follow":"Three jobs arrive together with bursts of 8, 4 and 2 milliseconds. What is the average waiting time under each of the three policies?","seconds":90,"lesson":"scheduler-and-context-switch","title":"The scheduler, time slices and context switches","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"thread-states.s1","q":"\"In a Java thread dump, what is the difference between BLOCKED, WAITING and RUNNABLE, and how can each one fool you?\"","points":["BLOCKED means waiting to enter, or re-enter after a notify, a <code>synchronized<\/code> monitor, and nothing else","WAITING and TIMED_WAITING mean parked: <code>wait<\/code>, <code>join<\/code>, <code>LockSupport.park<\/code> and everything in <code>java.util.concurrent<\/code>","Trap one: a contended <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> shows WAITING, so searching the dump for BLOCKED misses it","Trap two: RUNNABLE includes threads waiting for a core and platform threads asleep in native I/O such as a socket read","So read each state together with the top frames, and ask what each group is waiting for"],"model":"BLOCKED is the narrowest: the thread is waiting to enter a <code>synchronized<\/code> monitor, or to take it back after a <code>wait<\/code> was notified. WAITING and TIMED_WAITING mean the thread is parked: in <code>Object.wait<\/code>, in <code>join<\/code>, in <code>LockSupport.park<\/code>, and in everything in <code>java.util.concurrent<\/code> that is built on parking. RUNNABLE means the JVM thinks the thread could run. Each one can fool you. A thread stuck on a contended <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> is parked, so it shows WAITING, not BLOCKED, and if I grep the dump for BLOCKED to find contention, I miss every explicit lock. RUNNABLE does not mean busy either. It includes threads waiting in a run queue for a core, and platform threads asleep inside a native socket read, which the JVM cannot see into. A dump full of RUNNABLE threads in socket reads, with low CPU, is a service waiting on the network. So I always read the state together with the top frames, and ask what each group of threads is waiting for.","follow":"What state does a virtual thread show while it waits in a socket read?","seconds":70,"lesson":"thread-states","title":"Thread states and what blocking really does","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"thread-states.s2","q":"\"Our order service has stopped responding. CPU is at 2 percent and there are no errors in the logs. Walk me through how you find the cause.\"","points":["Low CPU with no progress means the threads are waiting, not computing: a deadlock, a starved resource or a slow dependency","Take two or three thread dumps a few seconds apart (<code>jstack<\/code> or <code>jcmd PID Thread.print<\/code>; gdb's <code>thread apply all bt<\/code> for C++)","Check for the JVM's deadlock report, then group threads by state and by the top frame in your own code","Follow the chain: threads parked in a pool lead to the threads holding its resources, and to what those are waiting on","Fix the cause and limit the blast radius: short timeouts, a separate pool for the slow path, no slow calls while holding a connection or lock"],"model":"Low CPU with no progress tells me the threads are waiting, not computing, so the question is what they are waiting for. I take a thread dump with <code>jstack<\/code> or <code>jcmd<\/code>, and a second one a few seconds later, so I can tell stuck threads from slow ones. First I look for the deadlock report at the end: if the JVM found a cycle, it names the threads and the locks, and the fix is a consistent lock order. If there is no cycle, I group the threads by state and by the top frame in our own code. Usually one big group is parked in the same place, say 190 threads in the connection pool's <code>borrow<\/code>. Then I look at who holds the scarce thing. If the ten threads holding connections are RUNNABLE in socket reads to the database, the real cause is a slow query, and everything else is queued behind it. A restart only buys time. The fix is the query itself, plus short timeouts on borrowing and on queries so requests fail fast, and a separate pool for the expensive endpoint, so that one slow dependency cannot take the whole service down. In C++ the method is the same, with <code>thread apply all bt<\/code> in gdb.","follow":"The deadlock report is empty and both dumps look the same. How do you tell a deadlock from a dependency that is just very slow?","seconds":90,"lesson":"thread-states","title":"Thread states and what blocking really does","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"thread-states.s3","q":"\"A thread is inside <code>wait()<\/code> and another thread calls <code>notify()<\/code>. Walk me through the states the waiting thread passes through until its code runs again, and what that means for how you write the wait.\"","points":["While inside <code>wait()<\/code> it is WAITING: parked, using no CPU, and it has released the monitor","After <code>notify()<\/code> it becomes BLOCKED, because it must take the monitor back and the notifier still holds it","It competes for the monitor once the notifier leaves, so another thread can get in first and change the condition","Then it is RUNNABLE, which in OS terms means ready: it still has to be dispatched onto a core","So the condition is rechecked in a <code>while<\/code> loop; C++ <code>condition_variable::wait<\/code> behaves the same way"],"model":"It goes through three states, and the middle one is the one people forget. While it sits in <code>wait()<\/code>, it is WAITING: parked, using no CPU, and it has released the monitor so that others can change the state. When another thread calls <code>notify()<\/code>, the waiter does not run. It becomes BLOCKED, because <code>wait()<\/code> cannot return until it owns the monitor again, and the notifier is still inside its <code>synchronized<\/code> block. When the notifier leaves, the waiter competes for the monitor with any other thread that wants it, and a newcomer can get in first and take whatever the waiter was waiting for. Once it wins the monitor it is RUNNABLE, which in operating system terms means ready, not running: it still has to be dispatched onto a core. So by the time my code after <code>wait()<\/code> runs, the condition may be false again. That is why I always wait in a <code>while<\/code> loop that rechecks the condition. C++ is the same: <code>condition_variable::wait<\/code> returns only after the mutex is taken back, and I use the overload with a predicate.","follow":"What is the difference between <code>sleep<\/code> and <code>wait<\/code>?","seconds":80,"lesson":"thread-states","title":"Thread states and what blocking really does","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"cost-of-threads.s1","q":"\"We raised a worker pool from 32 to 256 threads and throughput fell. Which of the reasons more threads can be slower is it, and how would you tell them apart?\"","points":["Each reason leaves a different fingerprint, so measure instead of guessing","Cores pegged and involuntary context switches up: oversubscription and cache pollution","Cores not full, voluntary switches up, dumps parked at one lock or queue: contention","A dependency's latency rising with your concurrency: downstream amplification","Fix: go back to the measured peak, bound the queue, and fix the real bottleneck"],"model":"I would not guess, because each reason leaves a different fingerprint. First I look at CPU and context switches. If the cores are pegged and involuntary switches jumped, the work is CPU bound and I am oversubscribed: 256 runnable threads take turns on the same cores, and every switch evicts the last thread's data from the caches. If the cores are not full but voluntary switches jumped, threads are blocking. A few thread dumps show whether most of them are parked at the same lock or the same queue. That is contention, and the critical section is serial no matter how many threads wait for it. Then I check the dependencies. If the database's latency rose in step with our concurrency, we pushed it past capacity, and our extra threads just wait longer. Whichever it is, the fix has the same shape. I measure throughput and p99 at a few pool sizes, go back to the size where throughput peaked, put a bounded queue in front, and fix the real bottleneck: the hot lock, the algorithm, or the limit on the dependency. More threads only help when cores sit idle and the dependency has headroom.","follow":"Throughput stayed flat from 32 to 64 threads, but p99 latency doubled. Why?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cost-of-threads","title":"What a thread costs, and why more threads can be slower","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"cost-of-threads.s2","q":"\"What does a thread actually cost? Give me the numbers you would put on a whiteboard.\"","points":["A stack reservation: 1 MB for Java on x86-64 Linux, often 8 MB for pthreads, 512 KB on macOS; address space, not RAM","Always resident: a kernel stack of about 16 KB plus scheduler state","Creating, starting and joining one: tens of microseconds","Running costs: about a microsecond per switch plus cold caches, and in Java GC scans every stack","Limits in the thousands to tens of thousands per process, then <code>OutOfMemoryError<\/code> or <code>std::system_error<\/code>"],"model":"A thread is cheap to write and expensive in large numbers. Memory first. Each thread reserves a stack: 1 MB by default for Java on x86-64 Linux, often 8 MB for pthreads on Linux, 512 KB for secondary threads on macOS. That is address space, not RAM, because only the pages a thread touches become resident. What is always resident is the kernel's side: about 16 KB of kernel stack plus scheduler structures. Then time. Creating, starting and joining a thread costs tens of microseconds, because it takes system calls and a fresh stack. Running costs come on top: a context switch is about a microsecond of kernel work plus cold caches afterwards, and in Java every garbage collection scans every thread's stack. Finally, limits. A process runs out somewhere in the thousands to tens of thousands, and then Java throws an out of memory error about native threads and C++ throws <code>std::system_error<\/code>. So a few hundred threads are free in practice, and a hundred thousand are not.","follow":"So do 10,000 Java threads use 10 GB of RAM?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cost-of-threads","title":"What a thread costs, and why more threads can be slower","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"cost-of-threads.s3","q":"\"Why not just start a new thread for every request? It is simpler than a pool.\"","points":["Creation costs tens of microseconds, which can be more than the work in a small request","No bound: a burst or a slow dependency piles up thousands of threads","The pile up costs memory, switching and GC, then creation fails with <code>OutOfMemoryError<\/code> or <code>std::system_error<\/code>","A pool pays creation once, and its size plus a bounded queue is the concurrency limit and the overload policy","Java 21 virtual threads remove the creation cost, not the need for a limit"],"model":"Because simple stops being cheap at scale, and nothing bounds it. Creating a thread costs tens of microseconds, so if the request itself is a few microseconds of work, most of the time goes on the thread, not the request. Worse, a thread per request has no limit. Under a burst, or when a dependency slows down and requests pile up, the service creates thousands of threads. Memory, switching and garbage collection costs grow with them, and eventually creation fails: Java throws an out of memory error saying it could not create a native thread, and C++ throws <code>std::system_error<\/code>. A pool fixes both problems. It pays for creation once and hands each task to a waiting thread for the cost of a wake-up. And its size is a concurrency limit: with a bounded queue in front, I decide what happens under overload instead of the operating system deciding for me. On Java 21, virtual threads remove the creation cost, so a thread per request is fine there, but I still put an explicit limit, such as a semaphore, around anything scarce.","follow":"What goes wrong if you use <code>Executors.newCachedThreadPool()<\/code> for this?","seconds":80,"lesson":"cost-of-threads","title":"What a thread costs, and why more threads can be slower","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"kernel-and-user-threads.s1","q":"\"What is the difference between a kernel thread, a green thread and a virtual thread? Who schedules each one, and what happens when it blocks?\"","points":["The real difference is who schedules the thread, which decides what blocking costs","Kernel threads (1:1): the OS schedules them, they run in parallel, only the blocker waits, but each costs a stack, kernel memory and tens of microseconds","Green threads (N:1): a runtime schedules them on one kernel thread, no parallelism, one blocking call stops them all","Virtual threads (M:N): the JVM runs them on carriers, one per core, and unmounts them when they block","Only blocking the runtime controls is cheap: on Java 21, <code>synchronized<\/code> and native code pin the carrier","Cheap threads make waiting cheap, not computing faster"],"model":"The difference is who schedules the thread, and that decides what blocking costs. A kernel thread is scheduled by the operating system, one to one. Threads run in parallel, and when one blocks, only that one waits. But each costs a stack reservation, kernel memory and tens of microseconds to create, so a process sensibly runs thousands, not millions. A green thread, in the old N to 1 model, is scheduled by a runtime on top of a single kernel thread. Switching is cheap, but nothing runs in parallel, and one blocking system call stops every green thread in the process. A virtual thread is M to N. The JVM runs many virtual threads on a few carrier threads, one per core, so they do run in parallel. When a virtual thread blocks on a socket, a sleep or a lock, the JVM saves its stack to the heap and gives the carrier to another one. That is why a million can wait at once. The catch is that this works only for blocking the runtime controls: on Java 21, a blocking call inside <code>synchronized<\/code> or native code pins the carrier. And cheap threads make waiting cheap, not computing faster.","follow":"Should you put virtual threads in a pool?","seconds":90,"lesson":"kernel-and-user-threads","title":"Kernel threads, green threads and M:N scheduling","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"kernel-and-user-threads.s2","q":"\"We moved a Java 21 service to virtual threads. Under load, it never has more requests in progress than the machine has cores, CPU is low and the database is idle. What do you suspect?\"","points":["In-flight work capped near the core count with low CPU means the carriers are held without doing work","On Java 21, blocking inside <code>synchronized<\/code> or native code pins the virtual thread to its carrier","The scheduler does not add carriers for pinning, so every carrier ends up stuck","Confirm with <code>-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full<\/code> or the JFR <code>jdk.VirtualThreadPinned<\/code> event, which shows the frame holding the monitor","Fix: <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> or move the blocking call out of the lock, upgrade the library, or move to Java 24 or later"],"model":"A cap at about the core count, with low CPU, tells me something is holding the carriers without using them, and on Java 21 the usual cause is pinning. The scheduler has one carrier per core. A virtual thread that blocks normally unmounts and frees its carrier. But if it blocks inside a <code>synchronized<\/code> block or method, or inside native code, it is pinned, and the carrier blocks with it. The scheduler does not add carriers to make up for pinning, so once every carrier holds a pinned sleeper, nothing else runs, and the other virtual threads just queue. To confirm, I would run with <code>-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full<\/code>, which prints a stack trace when a pinned thread blocks, or look for the JFR event <code>jdk.VirtualThreadPinned<\/code>. The stack shows where the monitor is held. Often it is not our code but a library, such as a database driver that waits on the network inside <code>synchronized<\/code>. The fixes are to replace <code>synchronized<\/code> with <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> around the blocking call, move the call outside the lock, upgrade the library, or move to Java 24 or later, where <code>synchronized<\/code> no longer pins.","follow":"Pinning is fixed, and now the database is overloaded. What limit is missing?","seconds":80,"lesson":"kernel-and-user-threads","title":"Kernel threads, green threads and M:N scheduling","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"kernel-and-user-threads.s3","q":"\"C++20 has coroutines. Why are they not the same thing as goroutines or Java virtual threads?\"","points":["C++20 coroutines are stackless: they suspend only at a <code>co_await<\/code> in their own body","A plain blocking call inside one blocks the executor thread and every coroutine scheduled on it","So async spreads: every function on the path to a wait must be a coroutine that is awaited","Goroutines and virtual threads are stackful, so ordinary blocking code works at any depth","The standard ships no scheduler and no async I/O; you bring a library such as Boost.Asio","Trade off: stackless frames are tiny and every suspension point is visible; stackful threads keep existing code unchanged"],"model":"Because C++20 coroutines are stackless, and the standard gives you no runtime. A stackless coroutine keeps its locals in a small heap frame, and it can only suspend at a <code>co_await<\/code> in its own body. If it calls an ordinary function that blocks, that function cannot suspend on its behalf, so it blocks the thread underneath, and every other coroutine scheduled on that thread waits. To wait deep in a call chain, every function on the path has to be a coroutine that is awaited, so async spreads through the code. Goroutines and virtual threads are stackful: each has its own stack, so ordinary blocking code works at any depth, and the runtime parks it. The second difference is the runtime. Go and the JVM ship a scheduler and non-blocking I/O. C++20 ships only <code>co_await<\/code> and its hooks, so I bring Boost.Asio or a similar library for the event loop. The upside of stackless is cost: frames are tiny, millions are cheap, and every suspension point is visible in the code. If I want stackful threads in C++, Boost.Fiber offers them.","follow":"How would you keep a CPU heavy step from stalling a coroutine event loop?","seconds":80,"lesson":"kernel-and-user-threads","title":"Kernel threads, green threads and M:N scheduling","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"virtual-memory-and-paging.s1","q":"\"A Java service on a 16 GB box is fine with 200 requests in flight. Someone raises the limit to 800, and p99 latency jumps from 30 ms to several seconds while CPU use drops. What do you suspect, and what do you check?\"","points":["Name it: the in-flight working set no longer fits in RAM, so the box is thrashing, a cliff rather than a slope","Why CPU drops: threads sit blocked on major faults, waiting for the disk instead of running","Evidence: major fault rate and swap traffic (the <code>si<\/code> and <code>so<\/code> columns in <code>vmstat<\/code>), resident size against RAM, I/O wait","The JVM twist: a full garbage collection walks every live object, faulting swapped pages back in, so pauses explode","Fix: bound concurrency to what fits, shrink per request memory or the heap, keep swap away from a latency sensitive box, then measure again"],"model":"My first suspect is thrashing. Every request in flight brings its own working set: buffers, objects, a stack. At 200 it all fit in RAM. At 800 it does not, so the kernel keeps evicting pages that someone needs again a moment later. Memory reads turn into major faults, and each one blocks the thread on the disk for tens of microseconds to milliseconds. That is why CPU use falls while latency climbs: the threads are waiting, not running. It is a cliff, not a slope. To confirm, I would check the major fault rate and swap traffic, for example the <code>si<\/code> and <code>so<\/code> columns in <code>vmstat<\/code>, the resident size of the process against physical memory, and I/O wait. A JVM makes it worse, because a full garbage collection walks every live object and faults swapped pages back in one by one, so pauses go from milliseconds to seconds. The fix is to bound concurrency to what fits in memory, or make each request use less, size the heap so it stays resident, and keep swap off a latency sensitive box. Then I measure again.","follow":"How would you pick the concurrency limit instead of guessing it?","seconds":90,"lesson":"virtual-memory-and-paging","title":"Virtual memory and paging","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"virtual-memory-and-paging.s2","q":"\"A teammate wants to serve lookups from a 50 GB read-only index by memory mapping the file instead of calling read. What do you tell them?\"","points":["What mmap gives: the file becomes an array, pages arrive on demand through the page cache, no copy into user buffers, and the cache is shared across processes","The catch: touching a page that is not resident blocks the thread on disk inside an ordinary memory access, with no timeout and no system call to see","Who suffers: a cold start can stall every worker at once, and in Java a fault blocks a virtual thread's carrier","Mitigations: warm or pre-fault the hot set, watch the major fault rate; a truncated file gives a bus error","Verdict tied to the hot set: mmap when it fits in RAM, explicit reads with an own cache and timeouts when it does not"],"model":"It can be a good choice, with one big catch. Mapping the file turns it into an array. Lookups become plain memory reads, pages arrive on demand through the page cache, nothing is copied into my own buffers, and every process that maps the file shares the same cached pages. Search engines and databases do exactly this. The catch is that a page fault is invisible blocking. If a lookup touches a page that is not in memory, the thread stops for a disk read inside what looks like an array access. There is no timeout, nothing shows up as a system call, and in Java it blocks a virtual thread's carrier as well. After a restart, with a cold cache, every worker can stall on faults at once. So my question is whether the hot part of the index fits in RAM. If it does, mmap is great: I would warm the hot pages at startup and watch the major fault rate. If it does not, I would use explicit reads with my own cache and timeouts, so the slow path is visible and bounded.","follow":"Now the index is written through the mapping too. When is a write durable?","seconds":90,"lesson":"virtual-memory-and-paging","title":"Virtual memory and paging","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"virtual-memory-and-paging.s3","q":"\"How can <code>fork<\/code> return almost instantly for a process with a 20 GB heap? And when does that stop being free?\"","points":["fork copies the page tables, not the memory: parent and child point at the same frames","Private writable pages become read-only in both; the first write to one faults and the kernel copies only that page","A child that calls exec right away copies almost nothing","It stops being free when the parent keeps writing during the child's life: memory grows with every page written, as in a Redis snapshot","Huge pages make each copy 2 MB instead of 4 KB, and copying the page tables of a huge process still takes real time"],"model":"Because <code>fork<\/code> does not copy the memory. It copies the page tables, so the child points at the same physical frames as the parent. The kernel marks every private writable page read-only in both processes. The first time either one writes to such a page, the write faults, the kernel copies that one page, and the writer gets its own copy. That is copy-on-write, and a child that calls <code>exec<\/code> straight away copies almost nothing. It stops being free when the parent keeps writing while the child lives. Redis is the classic case: it forks to write a snapshot, and every page the parent changes meanwhile gets copied, so memory can grow toward double under heavy writes. Huge pages make it worse, because each copy is 2 megabytes instead of 4 kilobytes, which is why Redis says to turn transparent huge pages off. And copying the page tables of a huge process still costs real time.","follow":"If the child only wants to run another program, what would you use instead of a plain <code>fork<\/code>?","seconds":75,"lesson":"virtual-memory-and-paging","title":"Virtual memory and paging","track":"core","module":1},{"id":"creating-threads.s1","q":"\"I gave each download its own thread, but eight downloads still run one after another. Walk me through what you would check.\"","points":["Check whether each worker is joined inside the creation loop, which serializes the downloads","In Java, calling <code>run()<\/code> directly also executes on the caller; <code>start()<\/code> schedules a new thread","In C++, construction starts the thread; there is no separate <code>start()<\/code> call","Start all workers before joining any, retain their handles, and collect results safely","Concurrency permits overlap but does not guarantee an eightfold speedup or a particular run order"],"model":"I would first check whether the creation loop immediately joins each worker. That makes download one finish before download two even starts. In Java I would also check for a direct call to <code>run()<\/code>: that is an ordinary call on the current thread. <code>start()<\/code> schedules a new thread. In C++, constructing the thread starts it, so there is no separate start call to forget. I would keep the handles, start all eight downloads, then join them in a second loop. Each worker would write its own result slot, which I would read after its join. That avoids turning the scheduling fix into a shared collection bug. This allows overlap, but does not promise eight times the speed: bandwidth, server limits and scheduling still matter. Starting the first worker first does not guarantee that its download starts or finishes first.","follow":"If every worker appends to the same list, what new bug does moving the joins expose?","seconds":60,"lesson":"creating-threads","title":"Creating, starting and joining threads","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"creating-threads.s2","q":"\"Our C++ program terminates on an early return even though the worker already finished. Why, and how would you make thread ownership safe in both languages?\"","points":["A finished <code>std::thread<\/code> remains joinable until joined, detached or moved from","Destroying a joinable <code>std::thread<\/code> calls <code>std::terminate<\/code>, including on an early return or exception","Use C++20 <code>std::jthread<\/code>, or a C++17 joining guard; automatic joining may still block","Keep borrowed data alive until the join; detaching does not solve ownership","Java does not terminate on losing a handle, but cleanup must account for interrupted joins"],"model":"The worker finishing does not clear ownership of the thread. In C++, a finished thread is still joinable until somebody joins it, detaches it, or moves ownership elsewhere. Destroying that joinable handle calls <code>std::terminate<\/code>, even if the work finished long ago. I would use a C++20 <code>std::jthread<\/code>, whose destructor requests a stop and joins, or a joining guard in C++17. For example, if processing throws after launch, the guard still waits during unwinding. The data the worker borrows must outlive that wait, so I declare it before the thread owner. Detaching would remove the join obligation but could leave the worker using destroyed data. Automatic joining can still block forever if the task never finishes or responds to cancellation. Java has no terminating destructor, but losing the handle can still abandon work. I would arrange cleanup on every exit path and handle interruption of the cleanup join explicitly. If ownership requires waiting until completion, I finish joining and restore the interrupt status afterwards. The key is that somebody remains responsible for every worker until it ends.","follow":"What if creating worker three fails after workers one and two have already started?","seconds":75,"lesson":"creating-threads","title":"Creating, starting and joining threads","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"creating-threads.s3","q":"\"This request can wait only two seconds for a worker. How do you enforce the timeout, and who owns the worker afterwards?\"","points":["A timeout bounds the caller's wait; it does not stop the worker","Java offers timed joins; C++ needs a timed future or condition variable wait","After timeout, request cooperative cancellation and retain ownership for later joining","Keep the worker's input and output storage alive until it finishes","Moving cleanup to another owner may be needed because a local <code>std::jthread<\/code> destructor still joins"],"model":"I would bound the wait and separately define what happens to work that is still running. A timeout does not kill the worker. In Java I can use a timed join and check whether it terminated. Standard C++ has no timed thread join, so I would wait on a completion future or condition variable with a deadline. If the deadline expires, I request cooperative cancellation and keep the handle so somebody can join later. The task must support cancellation, including its blocking operations, and its input and result storage must stay alive meanwhile. For example, I cannot return while a C++ worker still writes into a local result variable. There is also a subtle ownership trap: putting the worker in a local <code>std::jthread<\/code> does not bound the whole function, because its destructor joins before return. If the response must return promptly, a longer lived owner, such as a service executor, must own the task and its data through cleanup. I would report timeout to the caller without pretending that the underlying work has already stopped.","follow":"Does a completion future becoming ready prove that the worker has stopped accessing all of its captured data?","seconds":75,"lesson":"creating-threads","title":"Creating, starting and joining threads","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"passing-data-to-threads.s1","q":"\"Every worker is supposed to process a different chunk, but some process the same one. How would you investigate the captured input?\"","points":["A C++ reference capture of the loop counter shares a variable that the creator keeps modifying","The accesses race, and the captured reference also dangles once the loop counter's lifetime ends","Capture the index by value; borrow the chunks only while their lifetime and access discipline are guaranteed","Java rejects a changing local capture, but an effectively final reference can still point to mutable shared data","Choose a private snapshot, ownership transfer or synchronized sharing deliberately"],"model":"I would check whether all workers captured the same changing input instead of a value for their own iteration. In C++, capturing the loop counter by reference lets workers read it while the creator increments it. That is a data race, so the behaviour is undefined, not just a predictable final index. Once the loop ends, that reference also dangles. I would capture the index by value, while borrowing the chunk collection only if it stays alive and nobody changes it incompatibly until all workers finish. Java rejects a lambda that captures a changing loop counter, so I create a fresh local index for each iteration. But effectively final does not mean the object is immutable. If every lambda captures one reusable string builder, they still share its changing contents. I would create a separate immutable string before launching each task. Copies cost memory and copying time, so large inputs may justify borrowing or sharing. That choice needs an explicit lifetime and synchronization rule; merely copying a pointer or Java reference does not create a private snapshot.","follow":"Would replacing the C++ reference with a <code>std::shared_ptr<\/code> fix both the lifetime problem and the race?","seconds":75,"lesson":"passing-data-to-threads","title":"Passing data in and getting results out","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"passing-data-to-threads.s2","q":"\"Fetch ten pages concurrently and return them in URL order. Would you use a shared list, result slots, or futures? Talk me through the choice.\"","points":["Choose one future per URL and launch all tasks before collecting any result","Collect futures in input order; completion order does not dictate result order","Plain concurrent appends are unsafe, while locked appends still need explicit ordering","Separate pre-sized slots plus joins are safe but need a separate failure channel","Futures transport failures; Java wraps task failures and C++ rethrows stored exceptions","Use an overall deadline and define cancellation and cleanup for remaining tasks"],"model":"I would keep one future per URL, launch all ten tasks, and collect the futures in URL order. The downloads can finish in any order; the position of each future determines the output position. Waiting for the first page does not prevent the other nine from running, provided I launched them first. Plain concurrent appends to one list or vector are unsafe. Adding a lock makes appends safe, but records completion order unless I also store an index. Separate slots in a pre-sized array are another correct design: each worker owns one slot, and I read it after joining that worker. That is simple for computations that cannot fail, but a default value is not a useful failure report. Futures carry either a result or a failure. Java exposes a task failure through an execution exception with the original cause; C++ rethrows the stored exception. For a request, I would use one overall deadline rather than spending a fresh timeout on every future. On failure or timeout, I would request cancellation of unnecessary work and arrange cleanup. Input order can delay noticing a later failure, so if immediate failure detection is required, I would add a separate completion or failure signal while preserving the output indices.","follow":"Why can throwing away each temporary <code>std::async<\/code> future accidentally serialize the downloads?","seconds":90,"lesson":"passing-data-to-threads","title":"Passing data in and getting results out","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"passing-data-to-threads.s3","q":"\"Your worker writes a plain result field. Why do you trust the read after a join, but not after sleeping for a second?\"","points":["A successful join of the terminated worker establishes ordering and visibility for its writes","A sole worker writes the slot; the caller reads only after joining, so no atomic is needed","Sleep changes timing but creates no happens-before edge","An unsynchronized early read can see stale data in Java and is undefined behaviour in C++","A future publishes work before its completion, but may become ready before the thread itself ends"],"model":"I trust the join because it establishes synchronization, not because enough time has passed. If one worker writes the result and I read it only after successfully joining that worker, its write happens before my read. A plain field is sufficient for that ownership pattern. Sleeping for a second creates no such edge. The worker might still be running, and elapsed time does not publish its writes. Java can return stale data; the conflicting unsynchronized accesses in C++ are a data race and give undefined behaviour. A future is another way to publish the result: getting its value makes the writes covered by that completion visible. But a promise can be fulfilled before the worker finishes other cleanup. So receiving the value does not always mean the thread has ended. I still need a lifetime plan for anything the worker uses afterwards.","follow":"If two workers increment the same plain field and I join both, does joining repair their lost updates?","seconds":60,"lesson":"passing-data-to-threads","title":"Passing data in and getting results out","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"interruption-and-cancellation.s1","q":"\"I interrupted a Java worker. It logged an exception and went straight back to waiting on its empty queue. What happened?\"","points":["The queue wait threw <code>InterruptedException<\/code> and cleared the interrupt status","A broad catch inside the loop treated cancellation as a task failure and resumed waiting","Propagate interruption from helpers, or restore the flag and return when propagation is impossible","The thread owner may consume the interrupt only as part of its explicit cancellation policy","Separate task failures from cancellation and make a pool's stop state survive a task swallowing an interrupt"],"model":"The worker probably swallowed its cancellation request. An interruptible queue wait throws an interrupted exception and clears the thread's interrupt status. If a broad catch logs that exception and continues the loop, the next wait sees neither an item nor an interrupt and can block again. I would separate cancellation from ordinary task failure. A helper that can throw should propagate the interrupted exception. A runnable that cannot propagate it should restore the current thread's interrupt status and return promptly. Restoring and then blindly retrying the same interruptible wait can create a busy loop, so restoration alone is not a policy. The code that owns the worker may consume the interrupt if it deliberately exits or otherwise handles the request. For a pool, I would also keep a separate stop state that each worker checks between tasks. That way a task which accidentally clears the flag cannot send the worker back to idle waiting after the task returns. This still cannot stop a task that never returns. Cancellation requires cooperation inside the task as well as in the worker loop.","follow":"What changes if the worker is waiting to enter a <code>synchronized<\/code> block instead of waiting in <code>take()<\/code>?","seconds":75,"lesson":"interruption-and-cancellation","title":"Stopping a thread: interruption and cooperative cancellation","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"interruption-and-cancellation.s2","q":"\"My C++ jthread destructor hangs even though it requests a stop. The worker is waiting on an empty queue. What would you change?\"","points":["The destructor requests cancellation and joins; it does not forcibly terminate the worker","A plain condition variable wait does not observe the jthread's stop token","Use a C++20 <code>condition_variable_any<\/code> wait with that token and a queue predicate","Define whether stop wins over queued work, since the wait can return true when work is available despite cancellation","For C++17, protect the stop predicate with the wait mutex and notify all; check running tasks too"],"model":"I would make the queue wait observe the stop request. A jthread destructor requests cancellation and then joins; it does not kill the worker. If the worker is sleeping on a plain condition variable whose predicate only checks for a job, an empty queue can keep that join waiting forever. In C++20 I would use a condition variable any wait with the worker's stop token and the queue predicate. That lets an idle worker wake and return when cancellation is requested. I would also define what happens when cancellation and queued work are both present. The wait returns the predicate's value, so true does not mean there was no stop request. For immediate shutdown, I check the stop state before claiming another job. In C++17 I would put a stopping flag in the predicate, set it under the same mutex, and notify all waiters. That prevents a request from falling between checking the predicate and going to sleep. Finally, a worker already running a job needs cancellation checks inside that job; fixing the queue wait only fixes idle shutdown.","follow":"Can you hold the queue mutex while requesting a stop if a registered stop callback also takes that mutex?","seconds":75,"lesson":"interruption-and-cancellation","title":"Stopping a thread: interruption and cooperative cancellation","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"interruption-and-cancellation.s3","q":"\"A request times out while its background task is transferring money. Design cancellation so the caller returns promptly without corrupting the accounts.\"","points":["A caller timeout and task cancellation are separate events; neither is proof of worker termination","Java cancellation can request interruption; C++ needs an explicit token, and neither stops an uncooperative CPU loop","Check cancellation at invariant-preserving boundaries; finish or roll back a partly applied transfer","Make blocking operations cancellation-aware and bound work between checks","Retain ownership through cleanup and joining, even if the response returns earlier","Define the operation's reported outcome because cancellation does not prove its side effects were undone"],"model":"I would treat the timeout as the caller's deadline and cancellation as a separate request to the task. Neither proves the task has stopped. In Java, cancelling an executor future with interruption enabled can ask the running task to stop. In C++, I would pass a stop token explicitly. A tight loop that never checks either signal will continue running. The transfer must observe cancellation only at a safe boundary. If it has debited one account, it must complete the credit or undo the debit before releasing the lock and exiting. I would release resources through structured cleanup and make blocking operations interruptible, stop-aware, or bounded by deadlines. Cancellation latency then depends on the longest unit of work and wait between checks, so I would keep those bounded. If the caller must return before cleanup finishes, a longer lived owner must retain the task, its data and responsibility for joining it. Finally, I would define what the timeout means to the caller. A timeout or cancelled future is not proof that the money never moved. The operation needs a recorded outcome, and retries may need an idempotency key. Promptly returning an error and safely finishing the underlying operation are two different responsibilities, and both need an owner.","follow":"What does Java <code>Future.get()<\/code> report after successful cancellation if the task ignores interruption and later performs its side effect?","seconds":90,"lesson":"interruption-and-cancellation","title":"Stopping a thread: interruption and cooperative cancellation","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"exceptions-in-threads.s1","q":"\"Some background jobs fail with nothing in the logs. I put a catch around join, but it never catches them. Where should I look?\"","points":["Worker exceptions do not propagate through a thread join to the creator","Raw Java threads invoke an uncaught exception handler; an exception escaping a C++ thread calls <code>std::terminate<\/code>","Futures can store failures silently: Java <code>submit<\/code>, C++ <code>async<\/code> and <code>packaged_task<\/code>","Observe every task outcome; Java <code>get()<\/code> wraps task failure, while C++ rethrows the stored exception","For ordinary tasks on a Java <code>ThreadPoolExecutor<\/code>, distinguish <code>execute<\/code> from <code>submit<\/code> and inspect futures in hooks","Assign failure reporting to an owner; waiting or checking completion alone does not report success"],"model":"I would look at how the task was launched and who observes its result. A thread join does not transport the worker's exception, so putting a catch around it cannot catch that failure. For a raw Java thread, an uncaught exception reaches its handler and the thread ends. In C++, an exception escaping the thread function calls terminate, so silence while the process continues points me toward a stored or swallowed failure instead. Futures are the common hiding place. Java submit and C++ async or packaged tasks store exceptions in their result state. Waiting until a task is done does not mean it succeeded. Somebody must retrieve or otherwise inspect the outcome. Java get throws an execution exception with the task's failure as its cause; C++ get rethrows the stored exception. On a Java thread pool executor, an ordinary runnable passed to execute can fail through the worker's uncaught exception handler. Submit wraps it in a future, so a logging hook must inspect that future rather than assume a missing throwable means success. I would give every task a failure owner, record its identity and failure, and ensure request waiters receive a failed outcome. Logging alone is insufficient if the caller is still waiting for a success signal that will never arrive.","follow":"Why can a scheduled task disappear after one exception even though the scheduler's worker thread is still alive?","seconds":90,"lesson":"exceptions-in-threads","title":"Exceptions and failures inside threads","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"exceptions-in-threads.s2","q":"\"Four parallel tasks belong to one request. If one fails, cancel the others and return the first failure. Walk me through the races and cleanup.\"","points":["Define first failure as the first successfully recorded failure, not an assumed wall-clock ordering","Record it once using Java compare-and-set or a mutex-protected C++ exception pointer","Publish group cancellation and ensure tasks starting after the failure also observe it","Request cancellation outside locks that stop callbacks could acquire; make tasks and waits cooperate","Join all started workers before releasing borrowed state, including on launch or caller-interruption paths","Rethrow after cleanup without replacing the original failure with cancellation noise; latency is cooperative"],"model":"I would record one failure, request cancellation of the group, wait for every started worker, and then rethrow the recorded failure on the caller's thread. I define first as the failure that wins the recording operation, because two threads can fail concurrently without an observable global order. In Java I would use compare and set on an initially empty failure reference. In C++ I would protect an exception pointer with a mutex. The winner publishes a group stop request. Tasks must check that shared state even if they start after the failure; interrupting a thread before it starts is not a portable substitute for that check. I would request cancellation outside any mutex that a stop callback might acquire. Each running task must cooperate at safe points and in blocking waits. Cleanup joins every thread before borrowed data disappears, including when launching a later thread fails or the caller is interrupted while joining. Cancellation exceptions from sibling tasks must not overwrite the original failure. Only after cleanup do I rethrow it. This gives prompt cancellation signalling, not a hard bound on return time: an uncooperative task can delay joining. If the response has a strict deadline, a longer lived supervisor must own the remaining cleanup and report the stuck task.","follow":"If you collect futures in input order, how do you notice task four failing while task one is still blocked?","seconds":90,"lesson":"exceptions-in-threads","title":"Exceptions and failures inside threads","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"exceptions-in-threads.s3","q":"\"The lock guard released the mutex after an exception, but our balances are wrong. Why did exception-safe locking not protect the transfer?\"","points":["Automatic unlocking protects lock lifetime, not the transaction's invariant","A debit followed by a throwing operation can leave the credit unapplied and expose partial state","Validate and prepare potentially throwing work before modifying shared state","Commit with operations known not to throw, or use a rollback path that restores the invariant reliably","The strong exception guarantee is all or nothing for the protected state; external effects need their own protocol"],"model":"Releasing the mutex only lets other threads proceed; it does not roll back changes. If I debit one account, call an audit function that throws, and never credit the other account, the next thread acquires the lock and sees money missing. A Java synchronized block or C++ lock guard handles lock ownership correctly, but cannot repair that invariant. I would validate the transfer and prepare anything that can fail before making the first balance change. Then I would commit using operations whose failure behaviour I have actually checked. For example, allocating an audit entry belongs in preparation; simply moving allocation under a lock does not make it safe. If a later step can still throw, I need a rollback path that reliably restores the earlier state, or a different transaction design. This is the strong exception guarantee for the protected state: either the entire change happens or the original state remains. It does not automatically undo an external payment or durable log write. Those effects need an explicit commit and recovery protocol as well.","follow":"Would moving validation outside the lock be safe if validation reads the current account balance?","seconds":75,"lesson":"exceptions-in-threads","title":"Exceptions and failures inside threads","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"thread-local-daemon-sleep.s1","q":"\"An anonymous request occasionally runs as the previous user on a pool thread. We use thread-local context. What would you check and change?\"","points":["The context belongs to the worker thread and survives task completion unless cleared","A logged-in request leaves its user behind; an anonymous request on that worker reads it","Initialize context at request entry and clear on every exit with Java finally or C++ RAII","Capture context at task submission; thread-local values do not automatically follow work into a pool","Thread-local references do not make their referenced objects private","Test sequential requests on one worker, including an exception before cleanup"],"model":"I would first check for context left behind on a reused worker. Thread-local means one slot per thread, not one slot per request. Alice's request sets the user and returns without clearing it. An anonymous request then runs on that same worker, sets nothing, and reads Alice from the old slot. There need not be two requests running at once for this bug to happen. I would put context setup and cleanup at the request boundary. At entry, explicitly install the current user or the anonymous state. At exit, remove the Java thread-local in a finally block, or use a C++ scope guard that clears it even when handling throws. If work moves into another task, capture the context at submission and install it within that task's scope. An inheritable Java thread-local copies at thread creation, so it does not solve propagation into an existing pool. I would also check that the context does not hold a shared mutable object: a private slot does not make its referent private. My regression test would use one worker, run Alice's request and then an anonymous request, and repeat with Alice's handler throwing. The anonymous request must see no previous user, and the cleanup must still run.","follow":"How would the cleanup change if one context scope could be nested inside another?","seconds":90,"lesson":"thread-local-daemon-sleep","title":"Thread-local storage, daemon threads, sleep, yield and priority","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"thread-local-daemon-sleep.s2","q":"\"The worker needs this lock to set <code>ready<\/code>. My waiting thread holds it and sleeps in a loop. Why does nothing progress, and would yielding help?\"","points":["Sleeping retains the lock, so the worker cannot acquire it to change the predicate","Yield and priority do not release the lock or establish ordering","Wait on a condition associated with the lock, releasing it while blocked","Reacquire the lock and recheck the predicate in a loop after every wakeup","Use Java interruption or a C++ stop-aware wait for cancellation, and join for completion"],"model":"The waiting thread is preventing the state change it is waiting for. Sleep keeps the lock, so the worker cannot acquire that lock and set ready. Yielding would not release it either, and changing priorities would not repair the protocol. I would use a condition wait tied to the same lock that protects ready. In Java, that can be the lock object's wait method inside synchronized; in C++, a condition variable with a unique lock. Waiting releases that lock while blocked and reacquires it before checking the predicate again. The check belongs in a loop because wakeups can be spurious, and another thread can change the state before the waiter resumes. The worker sets ready under that lock and notifies the waiter. I would also define cancellation: Java waits can throw on interruption, while a C++ stop-aware wait needs explicit support. If all I need is to wait for the worker to finish, joining is simpler. The choice is based on the event I need, not a guessed number of milliseconds.","follow":"What if the waiting thread also holds a second lock that the worker needs?","seconds":75,"lesson":"thread-local-daemon-sleep","title":"Thread-local storage, daemon threads, sleep, yield and priority","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"thread-local-daemon-sleep.s3","q":"\"Our background log flusher delays shutdown. Can I make it a Java daemon or detach it in C++?\"","points":["Required log writes need an explicit completion protocol, not abandonment at process exit","Java daemon threads do not keep the JVM alive; C++ detach gives up joining","Neither mechanism guarantees cleanup or completion when the process exits","Stop admission, drain accepted work, flush, signal completion and join","Define timeout failure and keep C++ resources alive until the worker stops"],"model":"I would first decide whether losing accepted log records is allowed. If those records must be preserved, making the thread disposable is the wrong shutdown policy. A Java daemon does not keep the JVM alive after the last non-daemon thread finishes. C++ detach gives up the ability to join through that thread object; it does not make the worker keep the process alive. At process exit, neither mechanism guarantees that the worker finishes its flush or runs its cleanup. I would stop accepting new records, tell the worker to drain what was accepted, flush according to the logger's durability contract, and join after it reports completion. If shutdown has a deadline, exceeding it must produce an explicit failure outcome rather than a false claim that everything was saved. In C++, the queue, stream and other resources must remain alive until the worker has stopped using them. A daemon can be appropriate for disposable metrics, but that is a deliberate loss policy. The real fix is a shutdown protocol that matches the value of the work.","follow":"What does a successful flush need to mean if the caller requires durability after a machine crash?","seconds":75,"lesson":"thread-local-daemon-sleep","title":"Thread-local storage, daemon threads, sleep, yield and priority","track":"core","module":2},{"id":"race-vs-data-race.s1","q":"\"Every account method takes the same lock, but two withdrawals still overdraw the account. Is that a data race or a race condition? Walk me through it.\"","points":["It is a race condition without a data race if every balance access uses the same lock","Data races concern unordered conflicting accesses; C++ also requires a non-atomic access","Two callers each read 100 before either withdraws 100, leaving a balance of minus 100","Expose <code>tryWithdraw<\/code> with check and subtraction inside one critical section","ThreadSanitizer cannot diagnose this logical violation; tests must assert the invariant"],"model":"It is a race condition without a data race, assuming every access to the balance really uses the same lock. A data race concerns conflicting accesses to the same memory location that are not ordered by happens-before. C++ also requires at least one of those accesses to be non-atomic. The lock orders the accesses here, so that problem is absent. The contract can still fail because the decision spans two separate calls. Start with a balance of one hundred. Caller A locks, reads one hundred and unlocks. Caller B does the same. Both have decided they can withdraw one hundred. A now locks and subtracts, leaving zero. B then locks and subtracts, leaving minus one hundred. Every memory access was protected, but the decision became stale. I would expose one operation, tryWithdraw, that checks the amount and balance and performs the subtraction while holding the same lock throughout. Readers would follow the same locking policy. ThreadSanitizer should not report a data race in the original example because there is none to report. I would test the actual invariant: with one hundred available and two competing withdrawals of one hundred, at most one succeeds and the balance never becomes negative. The lock must cover the operation the caller needs.","follow":"Could a compare-and-set loop implement the same operation, and when would you prefer the lock?","seconds":90,"lesson":"race-vs-data-race","title":"Race conditions versus data races","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"race-vs-data-race.s2","q":"\"Our counter uses atomic loads and stores, but totals are low and ThreadSanitizer is quiet. What would you look for?\"","points":["Look for a load followed by a separate store of the incremented value","Two threads can both load 5 and store 6, losing one increment without a data race","Use one atomic read-modify-write operation, not stronger ordering on separate accesses","Use a lock if the count must move consistently with other state","Assert exact totals after joining; a clean detector run does not prove logical correctness"],"model":"I would look for an increment written as a load followed by a separate store. Atomic access prevents a data race on the counter, but it does not combine those two operations into one. If two threads both load five, both calculate six, and both store six, the final count is six when it should be seven. That is a lost update and a race condition, even with the strongest default ordering. ThreadSanitizer can stay quiet because each access is valid. I would replace the sequence with one atomic read-modify-write: an atomic increment in Java, or fetch-add in C++. If recording the hit also updates another field that must agree with the count, I would protect the complete operation and its readers with one lock instead. For validation, I would start several workers, have each add a known number of hits, join them all, and check the exact total. I would retain the sanitizer run for other data races. Passing that run alone does not establish that the counter meets its contract.","follow":"If the update is a more complicated function than addition, how would you make it atomic?","seconds":75,"lesson":"race-vs-data-race","title":"Race conditions versus data races","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"race-vs-data-race.s3","q":"\"A worker writes 42 into a plain field. Main sleeps, reads 42, then joins. It has passed a thousand times. Would you approve it?\"","points":["Reject it: sleeping and repeated success do not order the worker's write before the read","The later join does not retroactively protect the earlier read","C++ has undefined behavior; Java may read 0 or 42 for a zero-initialized int in this example","Move the read after successful join to order completion and visibility","Atomic access can remove the data race without guaranteeing the worker has finished"],"model":"No. The read happens before the join, so the code has no synchronization that orders the worker's write before that read. Sleeping changes timing, and a thousand successful runs do not add a happens-before relationship. Joining afterwards does not retroactively make the earlier access safe. In C++, the conflicting plain accesses form a data race, so I cannot give a bounded list of valid outputs: the behavior is undefined. In Java, for this zero-initialized int with only the worker writing forty-two, main can read zero or forty-two; seeing forty-two repeatedly does not establish visibility. The simplest fix is to join successfully and then read. Worker completion is then ordered before main continues, so the result is available without making this field atomic. I would choose a future if I wanted to express result delivery and failure together. Merely making the field atomic removes the data race on that field, but an early load can still read the initial value. I need both safe access and the completion guarantee required by the operation.","follow":"What must you check before reading if you use a Java join with a timeout?","seconds":75,"lesson":"race-vs-data-race","title":"Race conditions versus data races","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"lost-update.s1","q":"\"A teammate says <code>count++<\/code> is fine because it is one line of code. Walk me through why it can lose updates, and what you would use instead.\"","points":["<code>count++<\/code> is three steps: read the value, add one, write it back","A concrete interleaving: both threads read 5, both write 6, one increment is gone","The cost: the count drifts low under load, and how low it can go is surprising","In C++ it is a data race, so undefined behaviour; in Java it is defined but still loses updates","The fix: an atomic increment for one variable, a lock when an invariant spans several fields","Under heavy contention: LongAdder in Java, sharded or per thread counters in C++"],"model":"It is not fine, because one line is not one step. <code>count++<\/code> reads the value, adds one, and writes it back. If two threads both read 5, both compute 6 and both write 6, one increment has vanished, and under real load the count drifts low. In C++ it is worse than wrong numbers: two unsynchronized writes are a data race, which is undefined behaviour. For a single counter I would make the increment atomic: <code>AtomicLong.incrementAndGet<\/code> in Java, <code>fetch_add<\/code> on a <code>std::atomic<\/code> in C++. If the counter has to stay consistent with another field, I would put both under one lock instead. And if many threads hammer it, I would use <code>LongAdder<\/code> or per thread counters that are summed on read, because one atomic becomes a contention hot spot.","follow":"Would making the field <code>volatile<\/code> fix it?","seconds":90,"lesson":"lost-update","title":"The lost update: read, modify, write","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"lost-update.s2","q":"\"Atomic or lock: how do you decide?\"","points":["Atomic: one variable, one indivisible operation (increment, swap, compare and set)","Lock: an invariant across two or more variables, or a compound check then act","Atomics do not compose: two atomic operations in a row are not atomic together","Contention: both slow down when many threads fight; striping or sharding helps either","Say which you would pick for the example in front of you, and why"],"model":"I ask what has to stay true. If it is a single variable and the operation is one step, like an increment or a compare and set, an atomic is the simplest correct choice and avoids blocking. The moment the rule spans two fields, say a balance and a history list, or a check followed by an act, I need a lock, because two atomic operations in a row are not atomic together. So for a hit counter I would use an atomic, and for a transfer between two accounts I would use locks taken in a fixed order.","follow":"When would a compare and set loop be better than a lock?","seconds":60,"lesson":"lost-update","title":"The lost update: read, modify, write","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"check-then-act.s1","q":"\"We use a concurrent map, but two requests load the same missing key and overwrite each other. How would you fix that, especially if loading is slow?\"","points":["Two contains-then-put sequences can both observe absence before either inserts","Use <code>ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent<\/code> in Java or one mutex around lookup and insertion in C++","Successful non-null installation is shared; null, exceptions or removal can permit recomputation","<code>putIfAbsent<\/code> protects installation but does not prevent eager construction by both callers","For slow loads, atomically install one future; its owner loads outside the map operation and completes it for waiters"],"model":"I would make the decision to create an entry atomic with claiming that entry. Right now, request A checks and sees no value. Request B checks before A inserts and also sees no value. Both load, and the later put can replace the earlier result. The map has protected each call, but not their sequence. For a short computation in Java, I would use <code>computeIfAbsent<\/code> on a <code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code>. For a C++ map, I would initially protect lookup, creation and insertion with the same mutex used by other accesses. I would state the Java guarantee carefully: callers share a successful non-null installation while it remains present. A null result, exception or removal can allow another computation. <code>putIfAbsent<\/code> alone prevents replacement, but if both callers build the argument first, both still do the expensive work. For a slow database call, I would instead atomically install a future representing the in-progress load. Only the caller that installed it performs the load, outside the map operation. Other callers wait on that same future. The owner completes it with a value or failure, and the cache has an explicit retry policy for failures. This keeps slow work out of the map's critical section while coordinating callers for the same key.","follow":"If a failed future should be removed for retry, how do you avoid removing a newer entry for the same key?","seconds":90,"lesson":"check-then-act","title":"Check then act: two safe calls, one unsafe sequence","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"check-then-act.s2","q":"\"My limit is 100. The atomic counter is 99, and each request checks it before incrementing. Show me the failure and fix it without a lock.\"","points":["Both requests can read 99 before either increments, admitting two and reaching 101","Atomic accesses or stronger memory ordering do not combine the check and update","Read the count, reject at the limit, then CAS the observed count to one more","On CAS failure, recheck using a fresh value; only successful CAS admits the request","The reasoning assumes one fixed window; coordinating a reset adds state to protect"],"model":"Both requests can pass the check before either increments. A reads ninety-nine, B reads ninety-nine, and both decide to admit. Their atomic increments then take the counter to one hundred and one hundred and one. There is no data race on the counter, but the admission rule has failed. I would use a compare-and-set loop. Read the current count and reject if it is already at the limit. Otherwise, attempt to replace that exact observed value with one more. Only a successful replacement grants admission. If another request changed the count first, retry from the updated value and check the limit again. In Java I reload after a failed compare-and-set. In C++, compare-exchange updates its expected argument on failure, and the weak form belongs in a retry loop. Stronger memory ordering alone would not close the original gap. This answer assumes one fixed window with no independent reset. If the window timestamp and counter must change together, I would coordinate them as one state, often under a lock, rather than reset the counter separately.","follow":"What can go wrong if you increment first, reject above the limit, and allow unlimited rejected attempts?","seconds":75,"lesson":"check-then-act","title":"Check then act: two safe calls, one unsafe sequence","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"check-then-act.s3","q":"\"Two app servers both check that a seat is free and then book it. Where would you put the synchronization?\"","points":["A process-local lock cannot coordinate the independent app servers","Put the check and update in one operation at the shared database","Use a conditional update and treat the affected row count as the decision","Report success only after commit; a unique booking constraint protects the shared rule","Separate contention from retries after an uncertain response, using an idempotency key"],"model":"I would put the decision in the database that owns the seat state. A mutex in either server protects only that process, so both servers could still decide that the seat is free. Instead of reading and then writing, I would issue one conditional update that assigns the holder only if that show's seat is still unassigned. In a database with atomic conditional row updates, the affected row count is the decision: one means this transaction claimed it; zero means it did not. I would report success only after the transaction commits. A unique constraint on show and seat in the bookings table can also enforce that there is only one confirmed booking. The losing request receives an ordinary unavailable result rather than overwriting the winner. I would separately handle retries after a lost response: the client may not know that its first transaction committed, so a request identifier should let it recover the same outcome. That retry policy complements the atomic claim. It does not replace the database operation that prevents two different users winning.","follow":"If you add expiring holds, what must confirmation check atomically before it succeeds?","seconds":75,"lesson":"check-then-act","title":"Check then act: two safe calls, one unsafe sequence","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"invariants-and-critical-sections.s1","q":"\"Here is an inventory service with a stock map, a total and an audit list. Many threads restock, sell and report. How do you decide what to lock?\"","points":["Classify shared mutable state separately from confined and safely published immutable state","State the invariants: nonnegative stock, total equals stock sum, one audit entry per successful change","Guard the related fields and the entire stock check and update with one lock","Read a consistent report snapshot under that same lock and format outside it","Keep mutable internals private and preserve invariants on exceptional exits"],"model":"I would start with the rules the service must preserve, then choose the lock boundary. The stock map, total and audit list are shared mutable state. A warehouse name that never changes can be read without locking once the service is safely published, and a request's local parsing variables need no shared lock. The main invariants are that stock never goes negative, the total equals the sum of stock, and each successful change has exactly one audit entry. Those rules connect the three mutable structures, so I would initially guard them with one private lock. Selling checks availability and updates the map, total and audit list in one critical section. Restocking updates the same state under that lock. Reporting must acquire it too, copy a consistent snapshot, then release it before formatting or sending the response. Three separately safe containers would still allow a report to observe a partial update. I would avoid returning the mutable map or list, because that lets callers bypass the policy. I would also check exception paths: releasing a lock does not undo half a sale. Potentially failing preparation belongs before mutation where possible, with rollback if necessary. I would split locks only after identifying independent invariants and measuring contention.","follow":"Can the dashboard read the latest restock timestamp atomically without taking this lock?","seconds":90,"lesson":"invariants-and-critical-sections","title":"Invariants, critical sections and finding shared state","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"invariants-and-critical-sections.s2","q":"\"Both transfer writes take the same lock, and the auditor takes it too. Yet the total sometimes drops. Explain the missing critical section.\"","points":["Separate locked debit and credit leave the conservation invariant broken while the lock is free","From balances 500 and 500, an audit between a debit of 10 and its credit sees 990","The whole transfer and each consistent audit must hold the common lock throughout","Two atomic fields or separately locked getters do not provide a consistent snapshot","Validate totals during transfers; a correct final total alone misses the bug"],"model":"The missing critical section is the whole transfer. Locking the debit and credit separately protects each access, but releases the lock while the conservation rule is false. Suppose both accounts start at five hundred. The mover locks, subtracts ten from the first account, and unlocks. Before it credits the second account, the auditor acquires the same lock and reads a total of nine hundred and ninety. No data race occurred; the auditor observed a state the locking policy allowed it to see. I would hold the common lock from the balance check through both updates. The auditor must also hold it across both reads, so it sees either the state before the transfer or the state after it. Two atomic balances would not make their updates one transaction, and two separately locked getters would not make a snapshot. I would copy the balances under the lock and format the report afterwards. To test the fix, I would assert the total while transfers are running. Checking only after joining the workers can miss this bug completely.","follow":"If each account has its own lock, how must transfers and audits acquire locks to preserve consistency without deadlock?","seconds":75,"lesson":"invariants-and-critical-sections","title":"Invariants, critical sections and finding shared state","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"invariants-and-critical-sections.s3","q":"\"A worker occasionally sees missing configuration just after startup. The constructor starts it, then initializes the fields. How would you review the object's lifecycle?\"","points":["The constructor exposes this before the worker's required state is ready","Java can expose default field values, and final does not fix premature access","Finish Java construction before start; thread start publishes preceding initialization","In C++, construct dependent members before the thread and join before destroying them","Declaring <code>std::jthread<\/code> last helps member ordering but does not make later constructor work or shutdown automatically safe"],"model":"I would treat this as premature publication. The constructor has exposed the object to a worker before the worker's required state is ready. In Java, the worker can read a default null value before the constructor assigns the configuration, even if that field is final. I would finish construction first, then start the worker from a factory or a separate lifecycle method. Thread start publishes the initialization performed before it. In C++, I would check member declaration order as well as the constructor body. Members are constructed in declaration order, so a thread member must not start using a mutex or queue declared after it. Declaring the thread last helps: a <code>std::jthread<\/code> is then stopped and joined before earlier members are destroyed. But that is sufficient only if the worker uses already initialized state, later constructor code does not race with it, and the worker responds to stopping. With an ordinary thread I must arrange the join explicitly. I would review startup and teardown together, ensuring the worker never runs outside the lifetime of its dependencies.","follow":"What if the worker is blocked on a condition variable when shutdown requests a stop?","seconds":75,"lesson":"invariants-and-critical-sections","title":"Invariants, critical sections and finding shared state","track":"core","module":3},{"id":"mutex-basics.s1","q":"\"A teammate locks every write but leaves the getters unlocked. Walk me through what the lock guarantees and why that design is still wrong.\"","points":["A mutex provides mutual exclusion and visibility only through a shared locking protocol","An unlock orders earlier writes before accesses after a later successful acquisition of the same lock","An unlocked reader can observe an inconsistent pair of fields; in C++ a conflicting ordinary access is a data race and undefined behavior","All accesses to guarded state use the same lock, including copying a snapshot","Locking does not promise fairness or make a sequence of separately locked calls atomic"],"model":"The getters need the same lock as the writers. A mutex gives mutual exclusion and a visibility guarantee, but only when threads follow the same locking protocol. One thread's unlock orders its earlier writes before another thread's accesses after acquiring that same mutex. Taking an unrelated lock does not create that connection. Suppose a writer changes a lower bound and an upper bound together. An unlocked getter can read one old field and one new field, even though the writer holds a lock throughout. In C++, those conflicting ordinary accesses are a data race, so the program has undefined behavior. I would lock the getters too, and copy both fields while holding the lock when callers need a consistent snapshot. An independently atomic field is a different design, and I would document it explicitly. I would also avoid promising more than the mutex provides. Ordinary locking does not guarantee which waiter goes next, and two separately locked calls still leave a gap. If a caller must check and update together, the class needs one operation covering both.","follow":"If a getter takes the lock but returns a reference to the guarded collection, is the caller protected?","seconds":75,"lesson":"mutex-basics","title":"Mutexes and RAII guards","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"mutex-basics.s2","q":"\"How would you choose the locking API for a simple cache, a blocking queue, and a transfer between two accounts?\"","points":["A simple cache uses a private lock with scope-based release in C++, or <code>synchronized<\/code> in Java","Condition waiting needs <code>unique_lock<\/code> with <code>std::condition_variable<\/code>; Java can use a monitor or <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> with conditions","Java <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> needs unlock in <code>finally<\/code> after successful acquisition","Transfers handle the same-account case first and acquire distinct locks in a global order or with C++17 <code>scoped_lock<\/code>","Releasing locks automatically does not by itself prevent deadlock or roll back partial updates"],"model":"I would choose the simplest API that supports the operation, with release guaranteed on ordinary returns and exceptions. For a cache with short critical sections, that means a private monitor with <code>synchronized<\/code> in Java, or a named guard around a mutex in C++. A blocking queue adds a wait for a state change. Java can wait on the same monitor, or use a <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> with separate conditions. In C++, a standard condition variable needs a unique lock because waiting releases the mutex and reacquires it before returning. With an explicit Java lock, I acquire before the try block and unlock in finally. For a transfer, I first handle the case where both accounts are the same. For distinct accounts, Java takes both locks in one global order, such as unique immutable account identifiers. In C++17 and later, scoped lock can acquire both mutexes with deadlock avoidance. These choices manage lock ownership, but they do not make the business operation exception safe automatically. I still validate before committing changes and preserve the invariant if something fails.","follow":"Could recursive locks replace the global ordering rule for a transfer?","seconds":75,"lesson":"mutex-basics","title":"Mutexes and RAII guards","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"mutex-basics.s3","q":"\"An inventory service hangs after an out-of-stock request. The reservation method calls lock, checks stock, and unlocks at the bottom. What do you inspect?\"","points":["Inspect the early return and exception paths for an acquired lock that is never released","An out-of-stock return before unlock leaves other threads blocked on that mutex","Use a named C++ guard or Java <code>synchronized<\/code>; explicit Java locking releases in finally","An unnamed C++ guard is destroyed at its statement and does not protect later accesses","Exercise the failing reservation path, then another reservation, with a bounded completion check"],"model":"I would inspect the out-of-stock return first. If the method acquired the lock and returned before reaching unlock, it leaked ownership. Other threads then wait for a release that never happens. That matches a service that works until one particular request arrives. Exceptions between acquisition and release create the same failure. In C++, I would replace manual locking with a named guard whose scope covers the check and the update. The name matters: an unnamed temporary guard is destroyed at the end of its own statement. In Java, I would use <code>synchronized<\/code>, or put unlock in finally after successful explicit acquisition. Then I would test an out-of-stock reservation followed by a valid one from another thread, with a deadline on completion. I would also exercise an exception path. A successful reservation alone never reaches the branch that caused this hang.","follow":"The lock now releases on exceptions, but an exception occurs halfway through a multi-field update. Is the state necessarily valid?","seconds":60,"lesson":"mutex-basics","title":"Mutexes and RAII guards","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"lock-rules.s1","q":"\"You are reviewing a session store with a map, a separate active count, and an unrelated last-login timestamp. Where do you put the locks, and why?\"","points":["Start with the invariant that the active count equals the map size, preferably eliminating the redundant counter","Guard the map and every related access with one private lock; give the independent timestamp its own synchronization","Prepare inputs outside the lock, but keep duplicate checks and insertion together","Return snapshots or answers computed under the lock instead of exposing guarded state","Keep I/O and callbacks outside, and document the fields and invariant protected by the lock"],"model":"I would start with the invariant: the active count must equal the number of sessions. My first choice is to remove that counter and read the map's size under its lock. That deletes a consistency obligation. If the counter must stay, its updates and the map updates belong in the same critical section. Every getter and logout operation must follow that same private lock. The unrelated timestamp can have separate synchronization, such as an atomic field, rather than waiting behind session changes. I would prepare and validate a new session before locking. But the duplicate check and insertion stay together, because another login could otherwise slip between them. A snapshot getter copies the map while locked and returns the copy; for mutable session objects, I must also define how their state is protected. Finally, database calls and listener notifications happen outside the critical section, using the state copied for that event. I would document the guarded fields and the invariant next to them. For each lock, I should be able to name the conflicting operations it prevents.","follow":"If two logins use the same session identifier, what can go wrong with an unconditional increment of the separate counter?","seconds":75,"lesson":"lock-rules","title":"The rules of locking","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"lock-rules.s2","q":"\"Withdrawals hang while a reconciliation job is running. Withdraw calls a listener under the account lock; the listener takes the audit lock. Walk me through the failure and your fix.\"","points":["Establish the cycle: withdrawal holds account and wants audit; reconciliation holds audit and wants account","Reentrancy does not resolve a cycle involving two threads and two locks","Commit the account update and capture notification data while locked, then invoke listeners after unlocking","Keep the notification snapshot valid independently of later account changes","Open calls can reorder notifications; preserve commit order by enqueueing under the commit lock for one consumer, without blocking for queue space","Check thread stacks and use controlled scheduling to exercise the cycle"],"model":"I would look for a lock order cycle. The withdrawal thread holds the account lock and calls the listener, which waits for the audit lock. If reconciliation already holds the audit lock and asks for the account balance, it waits for the account lock. Neither thread can release its first lock because neither call returns. A reentrant lock would not solve this: each thread needs a lock owned by the other thread. My fix is an open call. Under the account lock, I complete the update and capture the new balance and a safe listener snapshot. Then I release the lock before invoking any listener. The event must own its data, so a later update cannot change the notification underneath the callback. The tradeoff is that notifications can arrive out of commit order. If order matters, I would enqueue each event in the same critical section that commits its update, and use one consumer for delivery. That enqueue must not block waiting for space while holding the account lock; a full queue needs an explicit policy. To confirm the original diagnosis, I would inspect both thread stacks, then use a controlled test that makes both threads hold their first lock before requesting the second.","follow":"If the event queue is full, how would you reject or defer the update without losing a committed notification?","seconds":90,"lesson":"lock-rules","title":"The rules of locking","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"lock-rules.s3","q":"\"Each request holds one global lock for 40 microseconds, and arrivals reach 30,000 requests a second. Can adding worker threads fix the latency?\"","points":["The lock permits at most 25,000 critical sections per second, ignoring overhead","Arrival rate times hold time is 1.2, so offered load exceeds capacity","More workers cannot parallelize the critical section and can increase the backlog","Move independent work outside while preserving the entire invariant inside","Partition independent keys only when cross-key invariants allow it; measure contention and bound overload"],"model":"No. The lock is already the capacity limit. Forty microseconds per request means at most twenty-five thousand critical sections per second, even before acquisition overhead. Thirty thousand arrivals per second times forty microseconds gives a utilization demand of one point two. The offered work exceeds what one lock can serve. More workers cannot execute that critical section in parallel; they mostly add waiters and consume resources. With an unbounded backlog, latency keeps growing rather than reaching a stable average. I would first measure lock wait time and hold time, then move parsing, formatting, I/O and callbacks out of the critical section where possible. I would keep the shared-state check and update together so the performance fix does not introduce a race. If hold time cannot shrink enough, independent keys may use different locks, but a cross-key invariant needs its own coordination. Finally, I would bound admission or queueing so overload has a defined outcome. After the change, I would measure tail latency and contention again, aiming for headroom rather than running continuously at the theoretical ceiling.","follow":"If the protected operation touches two keys, what must change before you introduce lock striping?","seconds":75,"lesson":"lock-rules","title":"The rules of locking","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"trylock-timeouts-fairness.s1","q":"\"Walk me through what happens at the moment a contended lock is released, first on a barging lock and then on a fair one. Why can the fair one be orders of magnitude slower?\"","points":["Barging: the lock just becomes free, and a running thread, often the one that released it, takes it while the woken waiter is still getting onto a CPU","Fair: ownership goes to the longest waiter, which is asleep, so the lock sits owned but idle for the whole wake up","The cost: microseconds to wake a thread against nanoseconds of work, paid on every hand off, which forms a lock convoy","What fairness buys: no waiter is overtaken forever, so the wait at the lock is bounded","The choice: unfair by default, fair only when a bounded wait is a requirement, and shrink or split the hot lock first"],"model":"The difference is who gets the lock in the instant after it is released. On a barging lock, the lock simply becomes free. The waiter at the front is woken, but while it is still getting back onto a CPU, a thread that is already running, often the one that just released it, grabs the lock and does more work. The lock is never idle. On a fair lock, the release hands ownership to the longest waiter, and that thread is asleep. Waking it costs microseconds, while the critical section may take tens of nanoseconds, and for that whole wake up the lock is owned but unused. That happens on every hand off, so threads march through the lock one context switch at a time: a lock convoy. In a quick benchmark with a tiny critical section, the gap is one to two orders of magnitude. What fairness buys is that no waiter is overtaken forever. So I keep locks unfair by default, and choose fairness only when a bounded wait is a real requirement, after shrinking or splitting the hot lock.","follow":"On a fair <code>ReentrantLock<\/code>, does a plain <code>tryLock()<\/code> respect the queue?","seconds":80,"lesson":"trylock-timeouts-fairness","title":"tryLock, timeouts, interruptible locking and fairness","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"trylock-timeouts-fairness.s2","q":"\"Two threads move money in opposite directions. Each takes its first account lock, tries the second, and on failure releases and sleeps exactly one millisecond before retrying. Walk me through how this can run forever without ever deadlocking, and how you fix it.\"","points":["It cannot deadlock: no thread ever waits while holding a lock","The interleaving: A locks 1, B locks 2, both tries fail, both release, both sleep exactly 1 ms, both wake and collide again","Name it: livelock, the threads are busy and not blocked, but no transfer completes","Fix: a random sleep (jitter) whose upper bound doubles after each failure, up to a cap, so the threads fall out of step","Add an overall deadline that returns an error, or use a fixed lock order when the accounts have one"],"model":"It cannot deadlock, because no thread ever waits while holding a lock. But it can livelock. Thread A locks account 1 and thread B locks account 2 at the same moment. A tries account 2 and fails, B tries account 1 and fails. Both release, both sleep exactly one millisecond, both wake together, and both grab their first lock again. The same collision repeats for as long as their timing stays in step. Nobody is blocked and the CPUs are busy, but no money moves. The fix is to break the symmetry. I make the sleep random, so one thread usually wakes first and takes both locks before the other comes back. I double the upper bound after each failure, up to a cap, so heavy contention backs off harder. And I give the whole transfer a deadline, so the caller gets an error instead of retrying forever. If the accounts have a natural order, like an id, the simpler fix is to always lock the lower id first, with no retry loop at all.","follow":"When would you choose lock ordering over try and back off, and when the other way round?","seconds":75,"lesson":"trylock-timeouts-fairness","title":"tryLock, timeouts, interruptible locking and fairness","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"trylock-timeouts-fairness.s3","q":"\"During a traffic spike, every request thread in your service ends up queued on one hot lock and latency explodes. You have two minutes: how would you use timeouts here, and what happens when one fires?\"","points":["Replace the unbounded wait with a timed attempt, and take the limit from the request's remaining deadline","Touch the shared state and unlock only if the attempt succeeded","A real plan when it fires: fail fast with a busy error, serve a stale value, or back off with jitter; never spin on the lock","Make the wait cancellable too, so a cancelled request or a shutdown can pull the thread out","Say the limit: a timeout bounds the waiting but does not cool the lock; shrink or split the critical section, then measure"],"model":"First, I would stop threads from waiting without a limit. Instead of <code>lock()<\/code>, each request makes a timed attempt, <code>tryLock<\/code> with a timeout in Java or <code>try_lock_for<\/code> on a timed mutex in C++, and the timeout comes from the time the request has left, not from a number I like. The code touches the shared state and unlocks only if the attempt succeeded. Then I need a real plan for when it fails. Usually that is failing fast with a busy error that the caller can retry elsewhere. If the lock guards a cache refresh, the loser serves the slightly stale value instead. If it is a background job, it backs off with jitter. I would also make the wait cancellable, so a cancelled request or a shutdown can pull the thread out, which in Java means <code>lockInterruptibly<\/code> or the timed form. But I would be clear that a timeout only bounds the waiting. It does not make the lock any less hot. The real fix is to find out why the lock is held so long, move slow work such as I/O out of the critical section or split the lock, and then measure again.","follow":"C++ has no <code>lockInterruptibly<\/code>. How would you let a thread that is waiting for a lock be cancelled?","seconds":90,"lesson":"trylock-timeouts-fairness","title":"tryLock, timeouts, interruptible locking and fairness","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"reentrancy.s1","q":"\"What does it mean for a lock to be reentrant? Tell me how you would build one on top of a plain mutex.\"","points":["The owner thread can acquire it again without blocking; every other thread still waits","It needs two fields a plain lock does not: the owner thread and a hold count","lock: if the caller is the owner, add one; otherwise wait until the count is zero, then become owner with count one","unlock: only the owner may unlock; subtract one, and at zero clear the owner and wake a waiter","Java's <code>synchronized<\/code> and <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> are reentrant; C++ <code>std::mutex<\/code> is not, <code>std::recursive_mutex<\/code> is","Real implementations make the uncontended path a single compare and swap"],"model":"A reentrant lock lets the thread that already holds it take it again without blocking, while every other thread still has to wait. To do that it keeps two things a plain lock does not need: the owner, and a hold count. I would build it from a mutex and a condition variable. In lock, I take the internal mutex. If the caller is already the owner, I add one to the count and return. Otherwise I wait on the condition until the count is zero, then record myself as the owner with a count of one. In unlock, I check that the caller is the owner and throw if not, because unlocking someone else's lock is a bug. I subtract one, and only when the count reaches zero do I clear the owner and wake one waiter. Java's <code>synchronized<\/code> and <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> work this way. C++ made the opposite default: <code>std::mutex<\/code> is not reentrant, and <code>std::recursive_mutex<\/code> is the opt in. A production lock keeps the same two fields, but makes the uncontended path one compare and swap and only parks threads under contention.","follow":"How would you add a <code>tryLock<\/code> with a timeout, and does that change whether <code>notify<\/code> is enough in unlock?","seconds":90,"lesson":"reentrancy","title":"Reentrant and recursive locks","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"reentrancy.s2","q":"\"A teammate fixed a production hang by swapping a plain mutex for a reentrant one, and the tests pass now. You are reviewing the change. Do you approve it?\"","points":["Not as it stands: the hang was a self-deadlock, a locked method reaching another method that takes the same lock","A reentrant lock removes the symptom, not the cause","The inner method now runs while the outer one may have the invariant half updated, so holding the lock no longer proves the state is consistent","The fix: lock once at the public boundary, private helpers that assume the lock is held (asserted in Java, a guard parameter in C++)","If the path goes through a callback or listener, make it an open call: copy under the lock, call after releasing"],"model":"I would not approve it as it stands. The hang was a self-deadlock: a method that holds the lock reached another method that takes the same lock, maybe through a callback. Making the lock reentrant removes the hang but not the cause. Now the inner method runs in the middle of the outer one, possibly while the invariant is half updated, and it cannot tell. Holding the lock used to mean the object is consistent. After this change it only means no other thread is inside. So I would ask for the re-entry path from the stack dump and restructure it. Public methods take the lock exactly once. The shared logic moves into private helpers that assume the lock is held, checked with an assertion in Java or a guard parameter in C++. If the path runs through a listener, that call moves outside the lock: copy what it needs, release, then call. If the fix is urgent, I could accept the reentrant lock as a stop gap, but with a ticket and a test that reproduces the original path.","follow":"The hang happens about once a day in production. How do you find the path that re-enters?","seconds":90,"lesson":"reentrancy","title":"Reentrant and recursive locks","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"reentrancy.s3","q":"\"Your event bus holds its lock while it calls the listeners. Walk me through what happens when a listener subscribes a new listener during a publish, first with a reentrant lock and then with a plain one.\"","points":["With a reentrant lock, subscribe gets straight in, because the same thread is asking again","It changes the list that publish is iterating: <code>ConcurrentModificationException<\/code> in Java, invalidated iterators and undefined behavior in C++","With a plain lock, subscribe waits for a lock its own thread holds: a self-deadlock","A listener that takes another lock adds a lock order nobody chose, so a two thread deadlock is waiting","The fix is an open call: copy the listeners under the lock, release, then call them"],"model":"The publish loop holds the bus lock and calls a listener, and that listener calls subscribe on the same thread. With a reentrant lock, subscribe is let straight in, because the owner is asking again. It adds to the very list that publish is iterating, so when control comes back, the loop is walking a list that changed under it. In Java the next step throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException<\/code>. In C++ the loop's iterators are invalid, which is undefined behavior. With a plain lock the failure is different: subscribe waits for the lock, the only thread that could release it is the one waiting, and the bus hangs forever. And if a listener takes some other lock, the bus lock is now held while acquiring it, an order nobody chose, so a deadlock with another thread is waiting to happen. The fix for all three is an open call. Copy the listener list under the lock, release it, then call the listeners. A listener added during a publish simply starts with the next event.","follow":"Would a <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList<\/code> solve this, and what does it cost?","seconds":80,"lesson":"reentrancy","title":"Reentrant and recursive locks","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"read-write-locks.s1","q":"\"Most of your traffic is reads. A read-write lock, a plain mutex, or something else: how do you choose?\"","points":["It depends on how long a read takes, not only on the read ratio","Every read acquire and release is still an atomic write to the lock's shared state, so for short reads that bookkeeping dominates and a plain mutex can win","A read-write lock wins for long reads and rare writes, where overlapping readers buys real parallelism","Short reads with regular writes: a concurrent map; read-mostly data: an immutable snapshot swapped atomically, so readers take no lock","If you pick a read-write lock, check its policy (writer starvation) and never upgrade in place","Measure under real contention before and after"],"model":"It depends on how long a read takes, not just on the read ratio. A read-write lock lets readers overlap, but every read acquisition and release is still an atomic write to the lock's shared state. With many cores, that cache line bounces between them on every read. So if the read is a fifty nanosecond map lookup, the bookkeeping costs more than the work, and a plain mutex can actually be faster. The read-write lock wins when reads are long, say a scan of a big in-memory index, and writes are rare, because then letting readers run together really buys parallelism. For short reads with regular writes, I would use a concurrent map. For read-mostly data like configuration, I would use an immutable snapshot: writers build a new copy and swap one reference, and readers take no lock at all. If I do pick a read-write lock, I check its policy, because steady readers can starve writers on some implementations, and I never upgrade in place. Either way, I measure under real contention before and after.","follow":"When would <code>StampedLock<\/code>'s optimistic read beat all of these, and what does it cost you?","seconds":90,"lesson":"read-write-locks","title":"Read-write locks and StampedLock","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"read-write-locks.s2","q":"\"Why can't you upgrade a read lock to a write lock? Walk me through what goes wrong, and show me the safe way.\"","points":["One thread: the write lock waits for every reader to leave, including the thread itself, so it waits forever","Two threads: both hold the read lock, both ask for the write lock, and each waits for the other's read lock","A lock that offers conversion must refuse at least one: <code>tryConvertToWriteLock<\/code> returns 0 unless the caller is the only reader","Safe pattern: release the read lock, take the write lock, and re-check, because another thread may have acted in the gap","Downgrading is fine in Java; C++ <code>std::shared_mutex<\/code> has no downgrade"],"model":"Because the write lock waits until no thread holds the read lock, and that includes you. Even alone, a thread that holds the read lock and asks for the write lock waits for itself forever. With two threads it is a textbook deadlock. A and B both hold the read lock. A asks for the write lock and waits for B's read lock to go. B asks for the write lock and waits for A's. Each holds exactly what the other needs. So any lock that offers conversion has to refuse someone: Java's <code>StampedLock<\/code> converts only when you are the sole reader, and otherwise returns zero. The safe pattern is to release the read lock, take the write lock, and check the condition again, because another thread may have done the same work in the gap. In a cache, that is the second lookup under the write lock before loading. Going the other way is fine in Java: take the read lock while holding the write lock, then release the write lock.","follow":"The loader in that cache is a slow network call. What changes?","seconds":80,"lesson":"read-write-locks","title":"Read-write locks and StampedLock","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"read-write-locks.s3","q":"\"Here is a symptom: on a busy server, config updates take minutes to show up. The config sits behind a read-write lock, and a thread dump shows the updater blocked on the write lock while request threads hold the read lock. What do you check, and what do you change?\"","points":["Name it: writer starvation; the writer needs a moment with zero readers, and overlapping readers never give it one","Check the policy: glibc's default under libstdc++'s <code>std::shared_mutex<\/code> prefers readers; Java's default makes new readers wait once a writer is first in the queue","Check how long readers hold the lock: a read lock held across slow work, such as a remote call, makes the writer wait for all of it","Quick fixes: a writer-preferring or fair lock, and slow work moved out of the read section","Better design for config: an immutable snapshot swapped atomically, so readers never lock and the writer never waits for them"],"model":"This looks like writer starvation. The writer needs a moment with zero readers, and under steady traffic the readers keep overlapping, so that moment never comes. First I would check the lock's policy, because it differs by library. On Linux, libstdc++'s <code>std::shared_mutex<\/code> sits on glibc's read-write lock, which prefers readers by default, so a stream of readers can hold off a writer indefinitely. Java's default lock makes new readers wait once a writer is first in the queue, so there I would look at the second cause: how long each reader holds the lock. If request threads hold the read lock across slow work, like a remote call, the writer waits for every one of them to finish. The quick fixes are a writer-preferring or fair lock, and moving slow work out of the read section. But for config, the better design is an immutable snapshot. The updater builds a new copy and swaps one reference, readers grab the current reference without any lock, and the next request sees the update at once.","follow":"With a writer-preferring lock, what can go wrong for the readers instead?","seconds":90,"lesson":"read-write-locks","title":"Read-write locks and StampedLock","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"lock-granularity-and-striping.s1","q":"\"Coarse or fine-grained locking: how do you decide how much one lock should guard?\"","points":["A lock can be no finer than the invariant it protects: state that must agree shares one lock","Start coarse, because one lock is simple and correct, then measure contention before splitting","One hot lock caps throughput at one over its hold time, so shrink the critical section first","Then split truly independent state, or stripe one big collection by a hash of the key","Name the costs of fine locking: multi-lock ordering, whole-structure operations like size, more code","Know when coarse wins: tiny critical sections, and a hot key that striping cannot split"],"model":"I start from the invariants. A lock can be no finer than the rule it protects, so if a balance and its history must agree, they share one lock, however tempting two would be. Within that limit I start coarse, because one lock is simple, easy to reason about, and cannot deadlock with itself. Then I measure. One lock caps throughput at one over the time it is held, whatever the core count, so if it is hot, the first fix is to shrink the critical section and move formatting and I/O out of it. If it is still hot, I split state that is truly independent, like the head and the tail of a queue, or I stripe one big collection: N locks, chosen by a hash of the key. Finer locking has a price. Anything that needs two parts must take the locks in a fixed order, operations like size become approximate or stop the world, and there is more code to get wrong. And sometimes coarse wins. With a twenty nanosecond critical section, one lock can beat stripes because the data stays in one cache, and no amount of striping helps a single hot key.","follow":"How would you implement <code>size()<\/code> on your striped map, and is it exact?","seconds":90,"lesson":"lock-granularity-and-striping","title":"Lock granularity, contention and lock striping","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"lock-granularity-and-striping.s2","q":"\"Explain how Java's <code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code> gets its concurrency. Then tell me how you would build the same idea yourself.\"","points":["Up to Java 7: segments, 16 by default, each its own small hash table and its own <code>ReentrantLock<\/code>: classic striping","Since Java 8 the stripe is one bin: a CAS fills an empty bin, a non-empty bin is locked by synchronizing on its first node","Reads take no lock: they rely on volatile reads of the table and the nodes","Size lives in striped counter cells, so it is an estimate while writers run; <code>computeIfAbsent<\/code> and <code>merge<\/code> run your function under the bin lock","To build it: a power-of-two array of stripes, each a lock plus a small map, picked by a mixed hash masked with N minus one","Operations on two stripes lock them in index order; a hot key still serializes on one stripe"],"model":"It is lock striping, refined over two generations. Up to Java 7 the map was an array of segments, sixteen by default. Each segment was its own small hash table and was itself a <code>ReentrantLock<\/code>, so a write locked one segment, and writes to different segments ran in parallel. Java 8 removed the segments and made the stripe a single bin. If the bin is empty, a put installs its node with one compare and swap and takes no lock at all. If the bin already has nodes, the put synchronizes on the first node, so only writers to that one bin contend. Reads take no lock; they rely on volatile reads of the table and the nodes. The size is kept in striped counter cells, so <code>size<\/code> is only an estimate while writers are active. Two consequences matter in practice. <code>computeIfAbsent<\/code> and <code>merge<\/code> run your function while holding the bin lock, so keep it short. And nulls are banned, because a null from <code>get<\/code> must mean absent. To build the same idea myself, in either language, I would make an array of stripes, a power of two in size, each one a mutex plus a small hash map. A key goes to the stripe given by its hash, with the high bits mixed in, masked with N minus one, so every per-key operation takes exactly one lock. Anything that needs two stripes locks them in index order. And <code>size<\/code> either sums stripe by stripe and says it is approximate, or locks every stripe in order for an exact answer.","follow":"Why is a plain <code>HashMap<\/code> unsafe with two writers, even if it never resizes?","seconds":120,"lesson":"lock-granularity-and-striping","title":"Lock granularity, contention and lock striping","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"lock-granularity-and-striping.s3","q":"\"We moved this service from 8 cores to 32 and throughput did not move. The CPUs sit mostly idle. What do you suspect, and how do you confirm it?\"","points":["Flat throughput with idle cores means threads are waiting, not working: suspect a serial bottleneck, usually one hot lock","The arithmetic: a lock held for t caps the process at about 1/t operations a second on any number of cores","Confirm with several thread dumps under load: many threads blocked or parked on the same lock, one owner","Look at what runs inside that critical section, and move out anything slow such as I/O, logging or serialization","Then split or stripe, and check for a hot key, which striping cannot fix","If no lock shows up, check other single points: a small connection pool, one writer thread, a downstream limit"],"model":"Flat throughput with idle cores means the threads are waiting, not working, so I suspect a serial bottleneck, and the usual one is a single hot lock. If every request holds one lock for two microseconds, the whole process tops out near five hundred thousand requests a second, on eight cores or on thirty-two. To confirm it, I take a few thread dumps under load. In Java, dozens of threads blocked on the same monitor, or parked on the same <code>ReentrantLock<\/code>, with one owner, is the signature. In C++ I would look at all the thread stacks for threads waiting in the same mutex. Then I look at what runs inside that critical section. Often it is something that never needed the lock, like logging, serialization or a network call, so the first fix is to move that out. If the lock is still hot, I split independent state or stripe the structure by key, and I check whether one key gets most of the traffic, because striping cannot help a hot key. If no lock shows up at all, I look for the other single points: a small connection pool, one writer thread, or a downstream limit.","follow":"The hot lock guards a map, and one customer sends ninety percent of the traffic. What now?","seconds":90,"lesson":"lock-granularity-and-striping","title":"Lock granularity, contention and lock striping","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"multiple-locks.s1","q":"\"Here is a <code>transfer(from, to)<\/code> that locks <code>from<\/code>, then <code>to<\/code>. Walk me through how it can hang, and how you would fix it.\"","points":["The interleaving: thread 1 runs transfer(a, b) and locks a, thread 2 runs transfer(b, a) and locks b, each then waits for the other","Name it: circular wait, and the order comes from the arguments, so no single line looks wrong and tests usually pass","The fix: lock in one global order by a unique, immutable id, whatever the argument order","Handle the same account before locking: locking one <code>std::mutex<\/code> twice is undefined behavior in C++","Ties or no natural key: a tie-breaking lock in Java, address order with <code>std::less<\/code> in C++","The alternative: <code>std::scoped_lock<\/code> in C++, a <code>tryLock<\/code> loop with randomized backoff in Java"],"model":"It hangs when two transfers run in opposite directions. Thread one calls transfer from a to b and locks a. At the same moment thread two calls transfer from b to a and locks b. Now thread one wants b, which thread two holds, and thread two wants a, which thread one holds. Neither lets go, so both wait forever. That is circular wait, and the nasty part is that the order comes from the arguments, so no single line looks wrong and the tests usually pass. The fix is to stop letting the caller choose the order. Every account has a unique id that never changes, and I always lock the smaller id first. Then a thread holding a lower lock only ever waits for a higher one, so the circle cannot close. Two details. If from and to are the same account, I return early, because locking one <code>std::mutex<\/code> twice is undefined behavior in C++. And if the key could ever tie, I add a tie-breaking lock, or in C++ order by address. In C++17 I could also hand both mutexes to <code>std::scoped_lock<\/code>, which avoids the deadlock for me.","follow":"Now one transfer touches N accounts. What changes?","seconds":90,"lesson":"multiple-locks","title":"Taking several locks safely","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"multiple-locks.s2","q":"\"Sometimes you only learn which second lock you need after you hold the first. How do you take both without risking a deadlock?\"","points":["You cannot sort the locks up front, so plain ordering does not apply directly","Never block on the second lock while holding the first out of order","Try the second without waiting; on failure release the first and take both in the global order","Re-check after reacquiring: what you read under the first lock may have changed while you held nothing","Alternatives: all or nothing with randomized backoff, or one coarser lock if the path is rare"],"model":"The one thing I must not do is block on the second lock while holding the first, because another thread may be taking the same two the other way round. So I try the second lock without waiting, with <code>tryLock<\/code> in Java or <code>try_lock<\/code> in C++. If I get it, I am done, because I never waited while holding anything. If I do not, I release the first lock, and since I now know both, I take them in the global order, say by id, where blocking is safe. The step people forget is the re-check. I learned which second lock I needed while holding the first, and then I let go for a moment, so the node may have moved to another parent, or the account may have closed. After reacquiring I validate again, and if it changed, I release and start over. If there is no usable order at all, all or nothing with randomized backoff works too, at the cost of retries. And if this path is rare, one coarser lock over both may simply be better.","follow":"Could that retry loop run forever? What would you do about it?","seconds":80,"lesson":"multiple-locks","title":"Taking several locks safely","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"multiple-locks.s3","q":"\"A service hangs a few times a month and only a restart fixes it. How do you find out whether it is a lock order deadlock, and how do you stop it coming back?\"","points":["Capture every thread's stack while it is hung, before the restart: <code>jstack<\/code> or <code>jcmd Thread.print<\/code>, or gdb or lldb in C++","A deadlock shows threads blocked on locks, each holding what the next wants; a Java dump names the cycle","Threads in <code>wait<\/code>, on a condition or on a queue with no cycle of owners point to a lost wakeup or a starved pool instead","Find the two code paths that take the same locks in opposite orders, often a call into another object under a lock","Fix with one global order or open calls; guard with checked lock levels in tests, ThreadSanitizer on Linux and a deadlock watchdog"],"model":"First I get evidence while it is hung, before anyone restarts it. In Java I take a thread dump with <code>jstack<\/code> or <code>jcmd Thread.print<\/code>. If it is a lock order deadlock, the JVM says so: it reports a Java-level deadlock and names which thread holds which lock. In C++ I attach gdb or lldb and dump every thread's stack, looking for threads stuck in a mutex lock, each holding what the next one wants. If instead the threads are sitting in <code>wait<\/code>, on a condition or on a queue, and nobody owns what another needs, it is not a deadlock. It is a lost wakeup or a starved pool, and that is a different hunt. Once I see the two locks, I find the two code paths that take them in opposite orders. Often one of them calls into another object while holding its own lock, so the fix is an open call, or one documented global order. To stop it coming back, I check lock levels in test builds, run the tests under ThreadSanitizer on Linux, which reports lock order inversions even when nothing hangs, and in Java I keep a watchdog calling <code>findDeadlockedThreads<\/code>.","follow":"The dump shows no cycle at all, just every pool thread waiting on a future. What is going on?","seconds":90,"lesson":"multiple-locks","title":"Taking several locks safely","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"how-locks-work.s1","q":"\"Walk me through what actually happens inside <code>lock()<\/code> and <code>unlock()<\/code> of a mutex on Linux, from the atomic instruction down to the kernel.\"","points":["The lock is one integer in user memory; locking is a CAS from free to held, which is also an acquire","Uncontended, lock and unlock are one atomic each and no system call","On failure: maybe a short spin, then mark the word \"locked with waiters\" (2) and futex wait","The futex wait sleeps only if the word still holds the expected value, so a release just before the sleep is not missed","Unlock is a release decrement, and it calls futex wake only when waiters were recorded","Costs: tens of nanoseconds uncontended, microseconds per contended hand off to a sleeping thread"],"model":"A mutex is one integer in ordinary memory plus a way to sleep. Locking starts with a compare and swap from 0, free, to 1, locked. If it succeeds, I own the lock and no system call happened. That CAS is also an acquire, so I see everything the previous owner wrote. If the CAS fails, some implementations spin for a moment, because the holder may be about to leave. Then the waiter swaps in 2, which means locked and someone may be asleep, and calls futex wait on the word, passing 2 as the value it expects. The kernel checks that the word still holds 2 and puts the thread to sleep in one step. That is the key detail: if the holder released the lock a nanosecond earlier, the word is no longer 2 and the wait returns at once, so the wakeup cannot be lost. Unlocking is an atomic decrement with release ordering. If the old value was 1, nobody is waiting and we are done, still with no system call. If it was 2, the unlocker stores 0 and calls futex wake for one thread, which wakes up, swaps in 2 again and retries. So the uncontended path is a couple of atomic instructions, tens of nanoseconds, and the kernel only sees contention, where each hand off to a sleeping thread costs microseconds. That is why I worry about contended locks, not about locks.","follow":"How does Java's <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> do the same job without a futex in sight?","seconds":110,"lesson":"how-locks-work","title":"How locks are built: CAS, spinning, parking and futexes","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"how-locks-work.s2","q":"\"Walk me through the lost wakeup a naive sleeping lock would have, and how a futex, or Java's park and unpark, closes the gap.\"","points":["The interleaving: the waiter sees \"held\" and decides to sleep, but is preempted before it sleeps","The holder unlocks, finds nobody asleep and wakes nobody; the waiter then sleeps on a free lock and may never wake","The waiter announces itself first (writes 2), so the unlocker knows a wake is needed","Futex wait checks the expected value and sleeps as one step, so a changed word makes it return at once","Java: the <code>LockSupport<\/code> permit makes an <code>unpark<\/code> that arrives before <code>park<\/code> count; AQS marks the node as waiting before parking","C++20 <code>atomic::wait(old)<\/code> has the same contract as the futex"],"model":"The danger is the gap between deciding to sleep and actually sleeping. Say thread B tries the lock and sees it held. B decides to sleep, but before it gets into the kernel it is preempted. Now A unlocks, looks for sleepers, finds none because B is not asleep yet, and wakes nobody. Then B finally goes to sleep, on a lock that is free, and it may never be woken. That is a lost wakeup. The futex closes the gap by making the check part of the sleep. B first writes 2 into the word to announce that a waiter exists, then calls futex wait saying \"sleep only if the word is still 2\". The kernel does that comparison and the sleep as one step with respect to wakes, so if A already released, the word is 0 and the call returns at once. Java gets the same guarantee differently. <code>LockSupport<\/code> gives each thread a permit, so an <code>unpark<\/code> that arrives before the <code>park<\/code> makes the <code>park<\/code> return immediately, and AQS marks the node as waiting before it parks, so the releaser knows whom to unpark. C++20's <code>atomic::wait<\/code> with an old value has exactly the futex contract.","follow":"Why does the three-state lock bother with state 2 at all? Why not call wake on every unlock?","seconds":90,"lesson":"how-locks-work","title":"How locks are built: CAS, spinning, parking and futexes","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"how-locks-work.s3","q":"\"A teammate replaced the mutex on a hot path with a hand-written spinlock to save system calls. CPU went up and p99 latency got worse. What happened, and what would you do?\"","points":["An uncontended mutex makes no system call anyway, so there was nothing to save on the fast path","Under contention every spinner burns a full core, and with more threads than cores spinners steal CPU from the holder","A descheduled holder makes each spinner waste its whole time slice: the millisecond tail in p99","Spinning on the exchange itself bounces the cache line and slows even the release","Fix: revert to the mutex and cut contention (shorter or split critical section); keep a spinlock only for tiny sections, pinned threads and holders that never block"],"model":"The spinlock solved a problem that did not exist and created new ones. An uncontended mutex never enters the kernel anyway: it is one CAS to lock and one atomic to unlock, about what the spinlock costs. The only time the mutex makes system calls is under contention, and that is exactly when spinning hurts. Every waiter now burns a full core, so CPU goes up. If there are more threads than cores, the spinners steal CPU from the thread holding the lock, and when the holder's time slice ends, every spinner wastes its own slice waiting for a thread that is not running. That is the millisecond tail in p99. And if the loop spins on the exchange itself rather than on a read, the cache line bounces between cores and even the release gets slower. I would revert to the mutex, and treat the system calls as a symptom: the lock is contended, so I would shrink the critical section, move work out of it, or split the lock. I would keep a spinlock only for a few dozen instructions, with threads pinned to cores and a holder that never blocks.","follow":"How would you confirm in production that this lock is contended before changing anything?","seconds":90,"lesson":"how-locks-work","title":"How locks are built: CAS, spinning, parking and futexes","track":"core","module":4},{"id":"busy-waiting.s1","q":"\"One thread has to wait until another finishes some work. Compare spinning, sleeping in a loop and blocking. Which do you use, and when would you pick another?\"","points":["Blocking is the default: parked at no CPU cost, woken directly by the signaller within microseconds","Spinning notices in nanoseconds but burns a whole core and can delay the thread it waits for","Sleep polling saves CPU but adds up to one interval of latency, and no interval gives both","Names the primitive: a latch or future for a one-time event, a condition variable for a condition, a blocking queue for hand-offs","Spin only for waits shorter than a park and wake, with the other thread running on another core and spare cores; briefly, with a pause hint, then block","Poll only when nobody can signal you, with backoff, jitter and a deadline"],"model":"I block by default, because it is the only option that is both prompt and cheap. Spinning notices the event within nanoseconds, but it burns a whole core for the entire wait, and if cores are scarce it competes with the very thread that would end the wait. Sleeping in a loop fixes the CPU but adds up to one sleep interval of latency, and there is no good interval: shorten it and you burn CPU on empty checks, lengthen it and you react late. Blocking parks the waiter, which costs no CPU, and the thread that finishes the work wakes it directly within microseconds. So for a one-time event I use a latch or a future, for a condition on shared state a condition variable, and for handing items over a blocking queue. I would spin only when the wait is shorter than a park and wake, the other thread is running on another core right now, and there are spare cores, which in practice means inside lock implementations. Even then I spin briefly with a pause hint and fall back to blocking. And I poll only when nobody can signal me, like a remote job, with backoff, jitter and a deadline.","follow":"What changes if the machine has only one core?","seconds":90,"lesson":"busy-waiting","title":"Busy waiting, and when spinning is right","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"busy-waiting.s2","q":"\"After a deploy, CPU on a service is pinned near 100 percent, yet throughput fell and latency rose. A thread dump shows dozens of threads RUNNABLE inside a loop that checks a flag. What is happening, and how do you fix it?\"","points":["Diagnosis: busy waiting; spinning threads stay RUNNABLE and burn CPU while doing no work","Feedback loop: spinners take cores from the threads that would set the flags, so waits get longer and more threads spin","Check how the flag is declared: a plain field can spin forever in Java and is a data race in C++","Fix: the thread that finishes the work signals the waiter (latch, future, condition variable, blocking queue, <code>atomic::wait<\/code>)","Confirm: CPU drops to the real work, waiters show as WAITING or parked in the next dump, latency recovers"],"model":"That is busy waiting, and it is feeding on itself. A thread that spins on a flag never leaves the CPU, so the dump shows it RUNNABLE even though it is doing no useful work. Dozens of them occupy most of the cores. The threads that would actually do the work and set those flags now queue for CPU, so each wait lasts longer, more requests pile up spinning, and throughput falls while CPU stays pinned. Before anything else I would check how the flag is declared. If it is a plain field in Java, some of those loops may never see the write at all, and in C++ it is a data race. The fix is to stop spinning: the thread that completes the work should signal the waiter, through a latch or a future for a one-time result, a condition variable for a condition, a blocking queue for hand-offs, or <code>atomic::wait<\/code> in C++20. After the change I would expect CPU to drop to the real work, the waiters to show as WAITING or parked in the next dump, and latency to recover.","follow":"Suppose the wait really is only a few microseconds. Would you still block?","seconds":85,"lesson":"busy-waiting","title":"Busy waiting, and when spinning is right","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"busy-waiting.s3","q":"\"You are building a pipeline stage that must react within a few microseconds of new data arriving. Would you ever spin on purpose? Defend the design.\"","points":["Yes, as a deliberate trade of CPU for latency: a park and wake costs microseconds, which is the whole budget","Dedicated cores: one pinned spinner per core, never more spinners than cores, other work kept off them","The loop reads an atomic or <code>volatile<\/code> value with a pause hint, and never locks or blocks","The price: those cores run at 100 percent and burn power even when idle, and it does not scale to many waiters","Spin only on that hot path, measure tail latency to prove it pays, block everywhere else"],"model":"Yes, but only as a deliberate design, not a habit. If the budget is a few microseconds end to end, the microseconds it takes to wake a parked thread are the whole budget, so the consumer stage spins. I would give each spinning thread its own core, pin it there, and keep other work off those cores, so there are never more spinners than cores and nothing has to preempt them. The loop reads an atomic or volatile sequence number with a pause hint, and never takes a lock or makes a blocking call. The price is explicit: those cores show 100 percent busy and burn power even when no data arrives, and the design does not scale to hundreds of waiters. So I would spin only on that hot path, measure tail latency to prove it pays, and use ordinary blocking everywhere else, including on this same path if the budget were milliseconds.","follow":"What does the pause hint do in that loop, and how is it different from <code>yield<\/code>?","seconds":75,"lesson":"busy-waiting","title":"Busy waiting, and when spinning is right","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"condition-variables.s1","q":"\"How would you explain to a junior engineer why a condition variable is useless without a lock and a piece of state next to it?\"","points":["A notification is not stored: a notify with nobody waiting does nothing","So a thread waits for a condition on shared state, and a notification only means \"check again\"","The waiter checks the state while holding the lock","<code>wait<\/code> releases the lock and sleeps as one step, and re-acquires the lock before returning","The signaller changes the state under the same lock, then notifies","Without the state an early notify is lost; without the lock a change slips between the check and the sleep"],"model":"Because a condition variable has no memory. If a thread notifies and nobody is waiting yet, the notification simply disappears. So a thread never waits for a notification. It waits for something to be true about shared state, like the queue being non-empty, and a notification only means go and look again. That state needs a lock, and the lock is what makes the check and the sleep safe together. The waiter checks the state while holding the lock, and <code>wait<\/code> releases that lock and goes to sleep as one step, then takes the lock back before it returns. The signaller changes the state under the same lock, then notifies. So no change can land between my check and my sleep. Take away the state, and an early notify is lost for good. Take away the lock, and the change can slip into that gap.","follow":"Then what does a semaphore give you that a condition variable does not?","seconds":65,"lesson":"condition-variables","title":"Condition variables and monitors","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"condition-variables.s2","q":"\"You are building a bounded buffer. One condition variable shared by producers and consumers, or two? Talk me through the trade off.\"","points":["Two predicates: producers wait for \"not full\", consumers wait for \"not empty\"","With one shared condition, a single notify can wake the wrong kind of thread, so you are pushed to notify all","Notify all is correct with a loop, but wakes every waiter to let one through","Two conditions on the same lock let each change signal only the kind that can proceed, so a single signal is enough","Java: a monitor has one wait set, so two conditions means <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> with two <code>Condition<\/code> objects; C++: two <code>std::condition_variable<\/code> members on one mutex","One condition is fine when every waiter waits for the same thing, such as a gate or a latch"],"model":"Two, for a bounded buffer. There are two different things to wait for: producers wait until the buffer is not full, and consumers wait until it is not empty. If both kinds sleep on one condition variable, a notification can land on the wrong kind. Say a consumer takes an item and notifies one thread. It can wake another consumer instead of the producer that could now add, and the producer stays asleep. So with one condition I am forced to notify all, which is correct as long as every wait is a loop, but it wakes everybody to let one through. With two conditions on the same lock, a put signals not empty and a take signals not full, so each signal reaches a thread that can actually proceed, and a single signal is enough. In Java that means a <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> with two <code>Condition<\/code> objects, because a plain monitor has only one wait set. In C++ it is two <code>std::condition_variable<\/code> members sharing one mutex. When every waiter waits for the same thing, like threads at a gate, one condition with notify all is simpler and fine.","follow":"With two conditions and single signals, what changes if a waiting consumer can time out or be interrupted?","seconds":80,"lesson":"condition-variables","title":"Condition variables and monitors","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"condition-variables.s3","q":"\"Here is a symptom: a worker pool stops making progress. The thread dump shows every worker asleep in a condition wait, and the deadlock detector reports nothing. What do you check?\"","points":["Asleep in a condition wait is not blocked on a lock, so there is no lock cycle for the detector to find: a signal was never sent or never reached them","Name the predicate each worker waits for, and find every code path that can make it true","Check that each path changes the state under the same lock, then signals the right condition object","Check whether a single signal can land on a thread that cannot use it","Check that every wait is a loop that also watches the shutdown flag","Look for a second lock held while waiting, with a signaller blocked on it: nested monitor lockout"],"model":"First, what the dump says. Workers asleep in a condition wait are not stuck trying to take a lock, so there is no cycle for the deadlock detector to find. Somebody was supposed to make their condition true and wake them, and did not. So I name the predicate each worker waits for, say the task queue being non-empty, and I find every code path that can make it true. For each one I check that it changes the state under the same lock the workers use, and that it then signals the right condition object, not a different one. If it uses a single signal, I ask whether that signal can land on a thread that cannot use it. Then I check the waiters: is every wait a loop, and does the loop also watch the shutdown flag? Last, I look for a second lock. If a worker holds another lock while it waits, and the producer is blocked on that lock, that is nested monitor lockout. Before fixing anything, I would reproduce it in a stress test.","follow":"How would you write a test that reproduces this hang reliably, without waiting forever when it happens?","seconds":80,"lesson":"condition-variables","title":"Condition variables and monitors","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"wait-in-a-loop.s1","q":"\"A colleague says spurious wakeups are so rare that the <code>while<\/code> loop around <code>wait<\/code> is just paranoia. How do you respond?\"","points":["Spurious wakeups are allowed by Java, C++ and POSIX: rare, but correct code cannot rely on their absence","The bigger reason is stolen wakeups: under Mesa semantics a notify makes the waiter runnable but does not hand it the lock","A thread that never waited can barge in and take the item before the woken thread re-acquires the lock","<code>notifyAll<\/code>, or one wait set shared by different kinds of waiters, wakes threads whose condition is still false","So <code>if<\/code> is wrong even with zero spurious wakeups, and the loop costs only one extra check","In C++ the predicate overload of <code>wait<\/code> is that loop"],"model":"It is not paranoia, and spurious wakeups are the smaller half of the reason. Yes, Java, C++ and POSIX all allow a wait to return with no signal, and they are rare, but correct code cannot depend on rare. The bigger reason is stolen wakeups. These libraries use Mesa semantics: a notify only makes the waiting thread runnable, it does not hand it the lock. Before the woken consumer gets the lock back, another consumer, maybe one that never waited at all, can take the lock and the item. Then the woken one finds the queue empty. And <code>notifyAll<\/code>, or one wait set shared by producers and consumers, wakes threads whose condition is still false by design. So even on a platform with zero spurious wakeups, <code>if<\/code> is wrong. A quick test with four consumers and notify all shows hundreds of wakeups that find the queue empty. The loop costs one extra check when the condition is already true, so there is nothing to save. In C++ I just use the predicate overload of <code>wait<\/code>, which is that loop.","follow":"Under Hoare semantics, would <code>if<\/code> be enough?","seconds":80,"lesson":"wait-in-a-loop","title":"Spurious and stolen wakeups: always wait in a loop","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"wait-in-a-loop.s2","q":"\"Here is a symptom: under load, consumers occasionally fail on an empty queue, a <code>NoSuchElementException<\/code> from <code>take()<\/code> in Java or a crash in <code>pop_front<\/code> in C++. The unit test with one consumer always passes. What is your first hypothesis, and how do you confirm it?\"","points":["Failing on an empty removal means a consumer went past its wait while the condition was false","First suspect: <code>if<\/code> around the wait instead of <code>while<\/code>","A single consumer test cannot show it, because nobody else can take the item","Under load a barging consumer steals the item, or <code>notifyAll<\/code> wakes several consumers for one item","Confirm with a stress test of several consumers and bursty producers; fix with <code>while<\/code> or the predicate overload, and keep the test"],"model":"My first guess is an <code>if<\/code> around the wait where a <code>while<\/code> belongs. The failure says a consumer removed from an empty queue, so it went past the wait while the condition was false. The unit test has one consumer, so nobody can steal the item and the bug never shows. Under load there are many consumers. A producer adds one item and notifies, but before the woken consumer gets the lock back, another consumer barges in and takes it. Or <code>notifyAll<\/code> wakes four consumers for one item. The first takes it, and the other three walk straight into an empty queue. To confirm, I would read the wait code, then write a stress test with several consumers and short bursts, which reproduces it in seconds. The fix is <code>while<\/code> instead of <code>if<\/code>, or the predicate overload in C++, and the stress test stays as a regression test.","follow":"Would switching <code>notifyAll<\/code> to a single <code>notify<\/code> make the <code>if<\/code> safe?","seconds":65,"lesson":"wait-in-a-loop","title":"Spurious and stolen wakeups: always wait in a loop","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"wait-in-a-loop.s3","q":"\"Walk me through what goes wrong if a consumer's wait loop checks only that the queue is empty, and the <code>closed<\/code> flag is checked after the loop.\"","points":["The outcome: a consumer can sleep forever, and shutdown hangs in <code>join<\/code>","The interleaving: the consumer waits on an empty queue; <code>close<\/code> sets the flag under the lock and notifies all; the consumer wakes, sees the queue still empty, and waits again","Nobody will ever notify it again, because the producers have finished","The fix: the loop condition covers every reason to stop, so wait while the queue is empty and not closed","After the loop, tell the cases apart: take an item if there is one, otherwise report that the queue is closed"],"model":"The consumer can sleep forever, and shutdown hangs. Say the loop is: while the queue is empty, wait, and <code>closed<\/code> is only checked after it. A consumer finds the queue empty and waits. Now shutdown runs: it sets <code>closed<\/code> under the lock and calls notify all. The consumer wakes and re-acquires the lock, but its loop condition only asks whether the queue is empty. It is, so it waits again. Nobody will ever notify it, because the producers are gone, and the thread that called shutdown hangs in <code>join<\/code>. The loop condition has to be every reason to stop waiting: wait while the queue is empty and not closed. After the loop I decide which one ended the wait. If there is an item, I take it, even during shutdown, so nothing is dropped. If the queue is empty, it must be closed, so I return a sentinel, like null or an empty optional.","follow":"How would you also let a consumer give up after a timeout, without early wakeups stretching the wait?","seconds":65,"lesson":"wait-in-a-loop","title":"Spurious and stolen wakeups: always wait in a loop","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"lost-wakeups-and-notify-all.s1","q":"\"Walk me through how a thread can sleep forever on a condition variable when the flag it is waiting for is already true. Would making the flag atomic fix it?\"","points":["A condition variable has no memory: a notify with no thread waiting is simply lost","The interleaving: the waiter checks the flag and sees false, the setter sets it and notifies, and only then does the waiter go to sleep","An atomic or <code>volatile<\/code> flag fixes visibility, not the gap between the check and the sleep","The fix: check the flag in a loop and wait under the mutex, and change the flag under the same mutex","Why that works: <code>wait<\/code> releases the mutex and starts sleeping as one step, so the setter cannot land in the gap","In C++ the notify call may move after the unlock, but the store to the flag may not"],"model":"Because a condition variable has no memory. A notify with nobody waiting is simply dropped. Picture the waiter checking <code>ready<\/code> and seeing false. Before it actually calls <code>wait<\/code>, the setter sets <code>ready<\/code> to true and notifies. Nobody is asleep yet, so that notify does nothing. Then the waiter goes to sleep, waiting for a change that has already happened, and nothing will ever wake it. Making the flag atomic does not help. That fixes visibility, but the bug is the gap between the check and the sleep, and the setter can still land in it. The fix is one rule. The waiter checks the flag in a loop while holding the mutex, and the setter changes the flag while holding the same mutex. Now the setter cannot run in the gap, because <code>wait<\/code> releases the mutex and goes to sleep as one step. And if the setter got there first, the waiter just sees true and never waits. In C++ the notify call itself can sit after the unlock, but the store must be under the mutex, even for a <code>std::atomic<\/code>.","follow":"Is it safe in C++ to call <code>notify_one<\/code> after releasing the mutex?","seconds":90,"lesson":"lost-wakeups-and-notify-all","title":"Lost wakeups, notify versus notifyAll, Mesa versus Hoare","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"lost-wakeups-and-notify-all.s2","q":"\"Here is a symptom: a service stops making progress. The thread dump shows every worker in <code>WAITING<\/code>, the CPU is idle, and the deadlock detector reports nothing. What do you suspect, and how do you track it down?\"","points":["No lock cycle: the threads are waiting on a condition, not blocked acquiring a lock, so suspect a missed or misdirected notification","Check every wait: the condition is checked in a loop, under the lock, before waiting","Check every state change: it is made under the same lock the waiters use, atomic or not","Check every single notify: do different kinds of waiters, such as producers and consumers, share one condition","The fix: wait for state, never for a notification, and use notifyAll or one condition per predicate","Confirm it with a stress test that joins with a timeout, and a dump showing which condition each thread waits on"],"model":"I would suspect a missed or misdirected notification, not a deadlock. A deadlock detector looks for threads blocked acquiring locks in a cycle. Here nobody is acquiring anything. Every thread is sitting in <code>wait<\/code>, so the bug is in the signaling, not the locking. I would go through three checks. First, every wait. Is the condition checked in a <code>while<\/code> loop, under the lock, before waiting? A check outside the lock lets the notify arrive early and be lost. Second, every state change. Is it made under the same lock the waiters use? In C++ that applies even to an atomic flag. Third, every single <code>notify<\/code>. Do different kinds of waiters share one condition? The classic case is producers and consumers on one monitor. A consumer's notify wakes another consumer, which re-checks and waits again, and soon everyone is waiting with nobody left to notify. The fix is to wait for state, never for a notification, and to use <code>notifyAll<\/code> or one condition per predicate. To confirm it, I would reproduce it under a stress test with join timeouts and read which condition each thread is waiting on.","follow":"Why did it run fine for weeks before it hung?","seconds":90,"lesson":"lost-wakeups-and-notify-all","title":"Lost wakeups, notify versus notifyAll, Mesa versus Hoare","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"lost-wakeups-and-notify-all.s3","q":"\"<code>notify<\/code> or <code>notifyAll<\/code>: which do you reach for, and when is a single notify actually safe?\"","points":["<code>notifyAll<\/code> is always correct when every wait is a loop; its cost is extra wakeups","A single notify needs uniform waiters: one predicate, and the same action on waking","It also needs one in, one out: each change lets at most one waiter proceed","Producers and consumers on one monitor break the first rule, and a single notify can stall everyone","Best of both: one condition per predicate, so a single <code>signal<\/code> is safe and cheap","Events that concern every waiter, such as shutdown, always get <code>notifyAll<\/code>"],"model":"My default is <code>notifyAll<\/code>, because with waits in loops it is always correct. Its only cost is waking threads that go straight back to sleep. A single notify is safe only when two things hold. Every waiter on that condition waits for the same thing and does the same thing when it wakes. And each change lets at most one of them proceed. Producers and consumers sharing one monitor break the first rule. A consumer's notify can land on another consumer, which re-checks and sleeps again, and the producer that could have moved never hears it. So when I want the cheap single wakeup, I split the waiting into two conditions, not full for producers and not empty for consumers. Each condition then has one kind of waiter, and <code>signal<\/code> is safe and cheap. That is how <code>ArrayBlockingQueue<\/code> is built. The one exception is an event that concerns everyone, like shutdown, which always gets <code>signalAll<\/code>.","follow":"With fifty waiters on one condition, what does <code>notifyAll<\/code> actually cost you?","seconds":75,"lesson":"lost-wakeups-and-notify-all","title":"Lost wakeups, notify versus notifyAll, Mesa versus Hoare","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"producer-consumer.s1","q":"\"You have two minutes: design a bounded blocking queue for many producers and many consumers. Then tell me what a producer should do when it is full.\"","points":["The state: one mutex, a deque or circular array, a capacity; size stays between zero and capacity, items leave in FIFO order","Two conditions, not full and not empty, and every wait is a loop on its own predicate","Put signals not empty and take signals not full; a single signal is safe because each condition has one kind of waiter","Items are processed after <code>take<\/code> returns, never while holding the lock","When full, it depends on the producer: block for a background stage, a timed offer and then an error or a drop for request threads","Count every drop and alert on it; an unbounded queue is not an answer"],"model":"I would build it as a monitor. The state is a deque, a capacity, one mutex and two condition variables: not full, where producers wait, and not empty, where consumers wait. The invariant is that the size stays between zero and the capacity, and items leave in arrival order. <code>put<\/code> takes the lock, waits in a loop while the queue is full, adds the item and signals not empty. <code>take<\/code> is the mirror image. The loops matter because wakeups can be spurious, and another consumer may get the item first. A single signal is safe because each condition only ever has one kind of waiter, and each put or take frees exactly one of them. With one shared condition I would need <code>notifyAll<\/code>. Consumers process an item after <code>take<\/code> returns, never under the lock, or the queue stops being parallel. Now, when it is full. The capacity is really an overload policy, and the answer depends on who the producer is. A background stage, like a file reader feeding a parser, should just block, because running at the consumer's speed is exactly what we want. A request thread should not hang on a side task, so I would use an offer with a short timeout, and on failure return an error or drop the item and bump a counter we alert on. What I would not do is make the queue unbounded. That only turns a slow consumer into memory growth and latency that nobody sees until the process dies.","follow":"How would you add <code>close()<\/code> so that consumers finish the queued items and then exit?","seconds":120,"lesson":"producer-consumer","title":"Producer and consumer with a bounded buffer","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"producer-consumer.s2","q":"\"Here is a symptom: after we added graceful shutdown, the last few hundred items in the queue are sometimes never processed, and nothing crashes. What do you look for?\"","points":["Lost items with no crash mean consumers left while work was still queued, so check the shutdown ordering","The consumer's exit test: returning as soon as <code>closed<\/code> is set drops the queue; finish only when closed and empty","When close happens: only after every producer has finished, never when one producer alone is done","With poison pills: exactly one per consumer, put after the last real item","Interrupting consumers is a stop now tool that abandons queued work, wrong for a graceful stop","Prove the fix with a test that tags every item and checks each is processed exactly once"],"model":"Lost items with no crash usually mean consumers left while work was still queued, so I would check the shutdown ordering. First, the consumer's exit test. If <code>take<\/code> reports closed as soon as the flag is set, every item still in the buffer is dropped. The rule is drain first: a consumer reports finished only when the queue is closed and empty. Second, when close happens. It must come after every producer has finished, which in practice means after joining them. A common bug is a producer that closes the queue, or puts a poison pill, when it alone is done. A consumer then exits while other producers are still adding, and their last items are stranded or rejected. With pills, it is exactly one per consumer, after the last real item. Third, I would check whether the stop path interrupts the consumers. Interruption abandons whatever is queued. That is right for a stop now, and wrong for a graceful stop. To prove the fix, I would write a test that tags every item, runs several producers and consumers, and checks that each item is processed exactly once.","follow":"And if the consumers never exit at all after shutdown, what do you check?","seconds":90,"lesson":"producer-consumer","title":"Producer and consumer with a bounded buffer","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"producer-consumer.s3","q":"\"Walk me through exactly what each thread does, and where the context switches are, when a consumer calls <code>take<\/code> on an empty queue and a producer then calls <code>put<\/code>.\"","points":["The consumer takes the lock, sees the queue empty, and calls <code>await<\/code>, which releases the lock and sleeps as one step","The producer takes the free lock, adds the item and signals not empty; the signal makes the consumer eligible but does not hand it the lock","The consumer must reacquire the lock inside <code>await<\/code> before it returns, so it may wake only to block until the producer unlocks","Back in its loop, the consumer re-checks the condition, because another consumer may have taken the item or the wakeup may be spurious","It removes the item, signals not full, releases the lock, and processes the item outside the lock","Context switches: one when the consumer blocks, one when it is woken, and possibly one more if it wakes into a held lock"],"model":"The consumer goes first. It acquires the lock, sees the queue is empty and calls <code>await<\/code> on not empty. That call releases the lock and puts the consumer to sleep as one step. That is the first context switch, off the CPU. Now the producer calls <code>put<\/code>. The lock is free, so it gets it at once, sees there is room, adds the item and signals not empty. The signal does not hand over the lock. It only makes the consumer eligible to run, and the producer carries on until it unlocks. The consumer is scheduled again, the second switch, and it must reacquire the lock inside <code>await<\/code> before it returns. If the producer still holds it, the consumer may wake only to block again, depending on the implementation. Once it has the lock, the consumer re-checks the condition in its loop, because another consumer could have taken the item first, or the wakeup could be spurious. The queue is not empty, so it removes the item, signals not full in case a producer is waiting, releases the lock, and only then processes the item.","follow":"What changes if both kinds of thread share one condition and you use <code>notify<\/code>?","seconds":90,"lesson":"producer-consumer","title":"Producer and consumer with a bounded buffer","track":"core","module":5},{"id":"visibility.s1","q":"\"Here is a symptom: a service hangs on shutdown in production but never in unit tests. A thread dump shows a worker still inside its <code>while (running)<\/code> loop, and the log says <code>running<\/code> was set to false a minute ago. What is going on, and what do you check?\"","points":["A visibility bug: <code>running<\/code> is likely a plain field, so there is no happens-before edge from the write to the loop's reads","The JIT compiled the hot loop and hoisted the read into a register, so the loop never reads memory again","Why tests miss it: short runs stay in the interpreter, which rereads the field, and prints or sleeps in the loop hide it","Not a stale cache: caches are coherent, and the bug happens on one core too","Check every read and write of the flag; fix with <code>volatile<\/code> (Java) or <code>std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;<\/code> (C++), or use interruption or a stop token"],"model":"This looks like a visibility bug on that flag. If <code>running<\/code> is a plain field, nothing creates a happens-before edge between the thread that clears it and the worker that reads it, so the language promises nothing about when, or whether, the worker sees the change. In practice the JIT compiles the hot loop, sees that nothing inside it writes <code>running<\/code> and nothing synchronizes, and reads the field once into a register. From then on the loop never looks at memory again. That is also why the tests never catch it. A short test runs mostly in the interpreter, which rereads the field every time, and a print or a sleep in the loop tends to hide it. It is not a stale CPU cache, because caches are coherent, and it would happen on one core too. So I would check how the flag is declared and whether every read and write is synchronized. The fix is <code>volatile<\/code> in Java or <code>std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;<\/code> in C++, or better, the platform's own stop signal: interrupt the thread, or use a <code>std::jthread<\/code> with a stop token.","follow":"Why does adding a log line inside the loop make the hang go away?","seconds":80,"lesson":"visibility","title":"Visibility: why a thread may never see your write","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"visibility.s2","q":"\"You need a flag that tells worker threads to stop. Would you use a volatile or atomic, a lock, or the platform's interrupt or stop token? How do you choose?\"","points":["All three are correct because each creates a happens-before edge; a plain flag creates none","A volatile or atomic is the lightest choice for a lone flag that guards no other state","A lock when the flag belongs to a larger invariant already guarded by that lock, taken on both the read and the write","Interruption or a stop token when the worker can block, because a flag is not checked while the thread sleeps or waits","None of them makes check then act atomic: \"only the first caller\" needs compare and set or a lock"],"model":"All three are correct, because each one creates a happens-before edge from the write to the read, which a plain flag does not. So I choose by what else the flag has to do. If it is a lone flag that guards no other state, a <code>volatile boolean<\/code> in Java or a <code>std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;<\/code> in C++ is the lightest correct answer: every read is a real read, and nothing blocks. If the flag is part of a bigger invariant that a lock already protects, say a queue and its closed state, I keep it under that lock and take the same lock on both the read and the write. And if the worker can block, in a sleep, a wait or a queue take, a flag is not enough, because the worker will not look at it until it wakes up. Then I use interruption in Java, which wakes those calls, or a <code>std::jthread<\/code> stop token in C++, which can also wake a <code>condition_variable_any<\/code> wait. One thing none of them gives is an atomic check then act. For \"only the first caller runs shutdown\" I use compare and set.","follow":"Could the C++ flag use <code>memory_order_relaxed<\/code>?","seconds":80,"lesson":"visibility","title":"Visibility: why a thread may never see your write","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"visibility.s3","q":"\"A junior engineer says the stop flag bug happens because each core caches its own copy of the variable. How would you correct them?\"","points":["Caches on mainstream CPUs are coherent: a write invalidates the other cores' copies","The real culprit is the compiler or JIT keeping the value in a register after hoisting the read","Store buffers delay and reorder writes, but only briefly, never forever","The proof: it happens on a single core too, where a context switch just saves and restores the hoisted register","We design against the language memory model, not the hardware: no happens-before edge, no guarantee"],"model":"I would say the instinct is reasonable but the mechanism is wrong. On mainstream CPUs caches are coherent: when one core writes a line, the other copies are invalidated and the next read fetches the new value. The thing that can hold an old value forever is a register. The compiler or JIT sees a loop that never writes the flag, reads it once, and tests the register from then on. Store buffers do exist, but they delay a write only briefly and can reorder it; they never hide it forever. The easy proof is that the bug happens on a single core, where there is only one cache: the hoisted value is simply saved and restored with the thread's registers. And the bigger point is that we do not code against hardware at all. We code against the memory model, and the rule is simple: no happens-before edge, no guarantee.","follow":"Then what does <code>volatile<\/code> actually change on x86, where a volatile read is an ordinary load?","seconds":65,"lesson":"visibility","title":"Visibility: why a thread may never see your write","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"reordering.s1","q":"\"Two shared variables, <code>x<\/code> and <code>y<\/code>, start at zero. Thread A writes <code>x = 1<\/code> and then reads <code>y<\/code>. Thread B writes <code>y = 1<\/code> and then reads <code>x<\/code>. Can both reads return zero? Walk me through it.\"","points":["Yes, both can read 0, even on x86","Why it looks impossible: in every interleaving some write comes before the other thread's read","The mechanism: each store waits in its core's store buffer while that core's next load reads the other variable from the cache","The compiler or JIT may also swap the independent store and load","The fix: both volatile in Java or seq_cst on all four operations in C++, or a lock; release and acquire are not enough"],"model":"Yes, both can read zero, and it surprises people because every interleaving seems to rule it out: whichever thread writes first, the other should see it. The catch is that a store is not visible the instant it executes. Each core puts its store into a private store buffer and keeps going, and its next load reads the other variable from the cache, where the other core's store has not arrived yet. So A's write of x sits in its buffer, B's write of y sits in its buffer, A reads y as zero, B reads x as zero, and only then do the buffers drain. This is the one reordering x86 allows, a store followed by a load of a different address, so you see it on Intel too, and ARM allows even more. The compiler or JIT can also simply swap the store and the load, since they touch different variables. To forbid it, both variables must be volatile in Java, or all four operations seq_cst in C++. Then they fall into one total order, and one read must come after the other thread's write. Release and acquire do not fix it, because a release store followed by an acquire load can still be reordered.","follow":"If I asked you to implement Peterson's lock, what would you have to be careful about?","seconds":90,"lesson":"reordering","title":"Reordering by the compiler and the CPU","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"reordering.s2","q":"\"A config reload sets every field and then sets <code>loaded = true<\/code> last. Very rarely a request sees <code>loaded<\/code> as true but the region as null. The author says the order in the code makes that impossible. What do you tell them?\"","points":["Program order is a promise to the writing thread only; other threads may see the writes in another order","Three culprits: the compiler or JIT swaps the stores, the CPU publishes them out of order (ARM, POWER), or the reader's loads are reordered","<code>loaded<\/code> is plain, so no happens-before edge connects the field writes to the reader","Minimal fix: a volatile flag in Java, or a release store and acquire load in C++","Better fix: build an immutable config and publish it through one volatile or atomic reference","Rare and hardware dependent, so a passing test proves nothing"],"model":"I would tell them the order in the source is a promise to their own thread, not to anyone else. Without synchronization the JIT can swap the two stores, an ARM core can make them visible out of order, and the reader's two loads can be reordered too. Any one of those lets a request see the flag before the data. Because <code>loaded<\/code> is a plain field, no happens-before edge connects the field writes to the reader, so the language allows exactly what they are seeing. The minimal fix is to make <code>loaded<\/code> volatile in Java, or a release store with an acquire load in C++. Then everything written before the flag is visible to a reader that sees it true. The better fix is to build a new, immutable config object and publish it through one volatile or atomic reference, so a reader always gets a complete snapshot and a second reload is never half seen. And \"it is rare\" is the point: it depends on timing and hardware, so no test can prove it is gone.","follow":"In C++, how do you free the old config while a request thread might still be reading it?","seconds":80,"lesson":"reordering","title":"Reordering by the compiler and the CPU","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"reordering.s3","q":"\"Why do compilers and CPUs reorder memory operations at all, and what does it cost to forbid it?\"","points":["Memory is far slower than the core; executing every access strictly in order would leave the core idle","CPU side: store buffers, out of order execution and early, speculative loads","Compiler side: values kept in registers, instructions rescheduled, loads and stores merged or hoisted","The deal: the as-if rule hides all of it from the thread itself, and synchronization marks where other threads need order","The cost: fences and seq_cst stores make the core wait for its store buffer, so put ordering only at hand-off points"],"model":"Because memory is slow and cores are fast. A load that misses every cache costs on the order of a hundred nanoseconds, which is hundreds of cycles, so a core that waited for every access in turn would sit idle most of the time. So the CPU parks stores in a store buffer, executes out of order and starts loads early, and the compiler keeps values in registers and schedules instructions freely. None of it is visible to the thread itself; that is the as-if rule. The deal both languages make is that plain accesses get every optimization, and synchronization marks the few places where another thread needs to see things in order. Forbidding reordering everywhere would throw much of that speed away, because a full fence or a seq_cst store makes the core wait for its store buffer to drain. So I put ordering only at the hand-off points: a lock, a volatile flag, or a release and acquire pair.","follow":"What does a <code>volatile<\/code> write actually compile to on x86 and on ARM?","seconds":70,"lesson":"reordering","title":"Reordering by the compiler and the CPU","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"happens-before.s1","q":"\"Thread A writes a config object and then releases a lock. Thread B takes the same lock and then sets a volatile flag. Thread C never touches the lock: it waits until it sees the flag set, then reads the config. Is C guaranteed to see A's config? Walk me through it.\"","points":["Yes, as long as B took the lock after A released it","In A, the config write comes before the unlock in program order","A's unlock synchronizes with B's later lock of the same lock, and B's lock comes before its flag write","B's flag write synchronizes with C's read that sees it, and C reads the config after that","Transitivity joins the links into one chain, so C needs no lock of its own","What breaks the chain: B locking first, a plain flag, a relaxed flag in C++, or A changing the config after its unlock"],"model":"Yes, as long as B took the lock after A released it, and I can show it by naming each link. In thread A, the config write comes before the unlock in program order. A's unlock synchronizes with B's later lock of the same lock. In thread B, the lock comes before the flag write. The flag is volatile in Java, or an atomic with a release or sequentially consistent store in C++, so that write synchronizes with C's read when C sees the flag set. And in thread C, the config read comes after the flag read. Happens-before is transitive, so A's config write happens before C's read, and C sees it, even though C never took the lock. The chain is fragile in specific ways. If B took the lock before A, there is no edge from A at all. If the flag is a plain field, or a relaxed atomic in C++, the middle link is gone and it is a data race. And writes A makes after its unlock are not covered. So I say each link out loud, and if I cannot name one, I add synchronization instead of arguing about timing.","follow":"What changes if C reads the flag with <code>memory_order_relaxed<\/code> in C++?","seconds":90,"lesson":"happens-before","title":"Happens-before and synchronizes-with","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"happens-before.s2","q":"\"A teammate reads a result from a background thread after sleeping for 500 milliseconds. The test has passed a thousand times in a row, and they say half a second is plenty. What do you tell them?\"","points":["Time order is not happens-before: a sleep has no synchronization semantics, so there is no edge from the write to the read","So it is a data race: Java may return a stale value (or a torn <code>long<\/code>), and in C++ it is undefined behavior","A thousand passes only shows the race is rare; ThreadSanitizer reports it on every run, even when the output is right","Fix: replace the sleep with a real edge, such as a join, a future, a queue or a latch","Best design: return the result through a future, so the hand-off carries the value and the edge together"],"model":"I would tell them the sleep is not what makes it work. Happens-before is not about time. A sleep has no synchronization semantics, and the Java specification says so explicitly, so there is no edge from the worker's write to the reader's read. That makes it a data race. In Java the reader may see a stale value, or even half of a 64 bit <code>long<\/code>. In C++ it is undefined behavior, so the compiler may assume no other thread changes the variable. A thousand passes only tells me the race is rare on this machine today. ThreadSanitizer would report it on every run, even when the printed value is right, because it checks for the edge, not the outcome. The fix is to replace the sleep with a real edge. If the reader needs the worker to be finished, join it. Better still, have the worker return its result through a future, so <code>get<\/code> hands over the value and the happens-before edge together, and nobody has to guess how long is long enough.","follow":"Why would adding a print statement also seem to fix it?","seconds":80,"lesson":"happens-before","title":"Happens-before and synchronizes-with","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"happens-before.s3","q":"\"In Java a program with a data race still has defined behavior. In C++ it is undefined. Why did the two languages choose differently, and does it change how you write code?\"","points":["Java bounds the damage: a racy read returns some value written to that variable (or a torn <code>long<\/code> or <code>double<\/code>), never a value out of thin air","The reason: Java promises memory safety and type safety even for buggy or untrusted code","C++ makes a race undefined so the optimizer can treat plain variables as if no other thread could see them","Both give the same reward for race-free code: it behaves sequentially consistently (in C++, with locks and seq_cst atomics)","In practice the discipline is the same, no data races; in C++ a race can break code far from the variable, so run ThreadSanitizer"],"model":"The discipline is the same in both: write race-free code. The difference is how much a mistake can hurt. Java promises that even a racy program has defined behavior. A racy read returns some value that was written to that variable, maybe a stale one, or for a non volatile <code>long<\/code> or <code>double<\/code> a torn mix of two writes, but never a value out of thin air. Java needs that because it promises memory safety and type safety even to buggy or untrusted code; a thread must never conjure up a reference to an object nobody gave it. C++ chose freedom for the optimizer. A data race is undefined behavior, so the compiler may treat plain variables as if no other thread can see them, keep them in registers, and merge or reorder accesses. Both languages then give the same reward for race-free code: it behaves sequentially consistently, as long as the C++ code sticks to locks and sequentially consistent atomics. So in Java a race is a bug with bounded damage. In C++ a race can break code far away from the variable, which is why I run the tests under ThreadSanitizer.","follow":"Is there such a thing as a benign data race in C++?","seconds":90,"lesson":"happens-before","title":"Happens-before and synchronizes-with","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"volatile-and-atomic.s1","q":"\"How does <code>incrementAndGet<\/code> on an <code>AtomicInteger<\/code>, or <code>fetch_add<\/code> on a <code>std::atomic&lt;int&gt;<\/code>, actually work, down to the CPU? And what happens when 32 threads hit the same one?\"","points":["It is one atomic read-modify-write: no other core can act between the read and the write","On x86 it is a single <code>lock xadd<\/code>: the core owns the cache line for the whole instruction, which also acts as a full fence","On ARM it is <code>ldadd<\/code> on ARMv8.1 and later, or a load-exclusive and store-exclusive retry loop on older cores","The general form is a compare-and-set loop: read, compute, swap only if unchanged, retry if another thread won","Under contention the cache line bounces between cores, so each increment costs a cross-core transfer, an order of magnitude or two slower","For a hot counter: <code>LongAdder<\/code> in Java, sharded or per thread counters in C++, summed on read"],"model":"It is a single atomic read-modify-write, which means no other core can get between the read and the write. On x86 the JIT or the C++ compiler emits one instruction, <code>lock xadd<\/code>. The lock prefix makes the core hold the cache line exclusively for the whole instruction, and it also acts as a full fence, which is why it gives volatile or sequentially consistent ordering for free. On ARMv8.1 and later, including Apple's chips, it is one <code>ldadd<\/code> instruction; older ARM cores use a load-exclusive and store-exclusive pair in a retry loop. Conceptually all of these are a compare-and-set loop: read the value, compute the new one, swap only if nobody changed it, otherwise retry. That loop is also how <code>updateAndGet<\/code> handles any function. Under contention it gets expensive. With 32 threads on one counter, the cache line ping-pongs between cores, and each increment waits for a cross-core transfer, an order of magnitude or two slower than the uncontended few nanoseconds. It stays correct, but throughput collapses. So for a hot counter I would use <code>LongAdder<\/code> in Java, or sharded per thread counters in C++, and sum them on read.","follow":"What is the ABA problem, and when does a compare-and-set loop suffer from it?","seconds":90,"lesson":"volatile-and-atomic","title":"volatile in Java, std::atomic in C++","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"volatile-and-atomic.s2","q":"\"Here is a symptom: a C++ service hands work between threads with a <code>volatile bool ready<\/code> flag. The producer fills a buffer, then sets <code>ready<\/code>. It ran fine on x86 for years. On new ARM servers, consumers occasionally read garbage from the buffer. What is going on, and how do you fix it?\"","points":["C++ <code>volatile<\/code> is not synchronization: no atomicity, no fences, no happens-before, so the program has a data race and undefined behavior","It only stops the compiler removing or merging the flag accesses; the plain buffer writes may still move past the flag store","x86 hid the bug: it keeps stores in order and loads in order, and Microsoft's compiler even gives <code>volatile<\/code> acquire and release meaning there by default","ARM reorders more: the flag can become visible before the buffer, or the consumer can read the buffer early","Fix: <code>std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;<\/code>, a release store after filling and an acquire load before reading (or the seq_cst default)","Confirm with ThreadSanitizer, which flags the old code even on x86"],"model":"The flag was never correct; x86 was hiding the bug. In C++, <code>volatile<\/code> is not a threading tool. It stops the compiler from removing or merging accesses to the flag itself, but it emits no fences and creates no happens-before edge, so the program has a data race, which is undefined behavior. Worse, the buffer writes are ordinary writes, so even the compiler may move them after the flag store. On x86 it mostly worked because the hardware keeps stores in order and loads in order, and Microsoft's compiler even gives <code>volatile<\/code> acquire and release meaning on x86 by default. ARM has a weaker memory model. The flag can become visible to another core before the buffer writes, or the consumer can read the buffer before it reads the flag. That is the garbage. The fix is to make the flag a <code>std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;<\/code>, store it with release after filling the buffer, and load it with acquire before reading, or keep the default sequentially consistent ordering. The release and acquire pair is a happens-before edge, so the buffer is covered on every platform. I would also run the tests under ThreadSanitizer, which flags the old code even on x86.","follow":"So what is <code>volatile<\/code> actually for in C++?","seconds":90,"lesson":"volatile-and-atomic","title":"volatile in Java, std::atomic in C++","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"volatile-and-atomic.s3","q":"\"A junior engineer asks you for a rule of thumb: when is a <code>volatile<\/code> field enough, when do you need an atomic, and when do you need a lock? What do you tell them?\"","points":["Ask what the write depends on: if each write is independent of the old value (a flag, a latest reading, a reference to a new immutable object), <code>volatile<\/code> in Java or an atomic load and store in C++ is enough","If the new value depends on the old one (a counter, a running maximum), use one atomic read-modify-write or a compare-and-set loop","If the rule spans several variables (a min and a max that must agree), use a lock, or one atomic reference to an immutable object","Never <code>volatile<\/code> for <code>count++<\/code>: visibility is not atomicity; and in C++ never <code>volatile<\/code> for threads at all","A useful hybrid: volatile reads with writes inside a lock, for read-heavy state with compound updates"],"model":"I would tell them to ask what the write depends on. If every write is independent of the current value, like a stop flag, the latest temperature reading, or a reference to a freshly built immutable config, a <code>volatile<\/code> field in Java is enough, and in C++ a <code>std::atomic<\/code> with plain loads and stores. If the new value depends on the old one, like a counter or a running maximum, visibility is not enough, because two threads can read the same value. Then they need one atomic read-modify-write, <code>incrementAndGet<\/code> or <code>fetch_add<\/code>, or a compare-and-set loop. And if the rule spans two or more variables, like a min and a max that must agree, no single atomic helps, because a reader can combine values from different moments. Then use a lock, or put the fields in one immutable object and swap it through an <code>AtomicReference<\/code>. One hybrid is worth knowing: volatile reads with writes inside a lock, for read-heavy state. And in C++ the answer is never <code>volatile<\/code>; that is for hardware registers.","follow":"Under heavy writes, what does the <code>AtomicReference<\/code> approach cost compared with a lock?","seconds":80,"lesson":"volatile-and-atomic","title":"volatile in Java, std::atomic in C++","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"memory-orders.s1","q":"\"Walk me through the C++ memory orders you actually use. What does each one buy you, and how do you decide which one to put on an atomic?\"","points":["seq_cst is the default: acquire and release, plus one total order of seq_cst operations that every thread agrees on","A release store and an acquire load that reads its value form a happens-before edge: the tool for publishing data","Relaxed keeps the operation atomic and keeps one order of writes to that variable, but orders nothing else: counters, ids, a thread's own index","acq_rel is for read-modify-writes that both receive and publish, such as the last reference count decrement","Acquire and release do not stop a store from passing a later load, so \"set my flag, then check yours\" needs seq_cst","Weaken the default only on a measured hot path, naming the exact edge you need"],"model":"I use four of the six, and I start from the default. seq_cst gives acquire and release, plus one total order of all seq_cst operations that every thread agrees on, so I can reason about the code as a simple interleaving. When one thread hands data to another, a pair is enough: the writer fills the data and stores a flag with release, and the reader loads the flag with acquire. If the load sees the stored value, everything before the store happens before everything after the load. Relaxed keeps the operation atomic and keeps one order of writes to that one variable, but it orders nothing else, so it fits a counter read after a join, a unique id, or a thread reading its own index. acq_rel is for a read-modify-write that both receives and publishes, like the final reference count decrement. The trap is that acquire and release do not stop a store from passing a later load, so anything shaped like \"I set my flag, then I check yours\" needs seq_cst. And I weaken the default only on a measured hot path, where I can name the edge.","follow":"Which order would you put on each index load and store in a single producer, single consumer ring buffer?","seconds":90,"lesson":"memory-orders","title":"C++ memory orders: seq_cst, acquire, release and relaxed","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"memory-orders.s2","q":"\"Here is a symptom: a lock-free queue passes every test on our x86 laptops, but on the ARM build servers a consumer very occasionally reads an old value from a slot. The producer writes the slot and then stores the tail index with <code>memory_order_relaxed<\/code>. What is going on, and how do you fix it?\"","points":["The relaxed tail store publishes nothing: there is no edge from the slot write to the consumer's slot read","ARM may reorder accesses to different addresses, and so may the compiler; x86 keeps stores in order and loads in order, which hid the bug","In C++ it is a data race on the slot, so the code was broken on every platform, tests or not","Fix: release on the producer's tail store, acquire on the consumer's tail load","The head index needs the same pair in the other direction, so a slot is not reused while it is still being read","Prove it with ThreadSanitizer and a stress run on ARM, not a green x86 run"],"model":"The tail index is the flag that publishes the slot, and a relaxed store publishes nothing. Relaxed keeps the index itself atomic, but it does not keep the slot write before it, so on ARM the consumer can see the new tail and still read the old slot. ARM may reorder accesses to different addresses when nothing orders them, and the compiler may too. On x86 the hardware keeps stores in order with each other and loads in order with each other, so the bug hid there, but it was always a bug: in C++ it is a data race on the slot, which is undefined behaviour. The fix is a matched pair. The producer stores tail with release after filling the slot, and the consumer loads tail with acquire before reading it. The same reasoning runs the other way: the consumer stores head with release after reading the slot, and the producer loads head with acquire before reusing it. Then I would prove it with ThreadSanitizer, which sees the missing edge on any machine, and a long stress run on the ARM servers.","follow":"Why not just make every operation seq_cst and stop worrying?","seconds":85,"lesson":"memory-orders","title":"C++ memory orders: seq_cst, acquire, release and relaxed","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"memory-orders.s3","q":"\"Why can a reference count increment be relaxed, while the decrement needs acquire and release?\"","points":["Gaining a reference publishes nothing: the caller already holds one, so only exactness matters, and relaxed keeps the count exact","Each owner may have written to the object; the release half of its decrement keeps those writes before the decrement","The owner that takes the count to zero needs the acquire half to see every other owner's writes before it destroys the object","The decrements are read-modify-writes on one variable, so they form a chain that carries each release to the final acquire","Libraries often use a release decrement plus an acquire fence only on the path that reaches zero"],"model":"Because the increment publishes nothing and the decrement publishes everything. To increment, I must already hold a reference, so the object is already safely visible to me. All that matters is that the count stays exact, and relaxed atomicity gives me that. The decrement is different. Every owner may have written to the object before letting go, and the last owner is about to destroy it. The release half of each decrement keeps that owner's writes before its decrement. The acquire half of the final decrement, the one that sees the count reach zero, makes all of those writes visible before the destructor runs. It works across many owners because the decrements are read-modify-writes on one variable, so they form a chain that carries each release forward to the final acquire. Libraries often save a little by using a release decrement and an acquire fence only on the path that hits zero. Without the acquire, the destructor could run before another owner's last writes are visible to it.","follow":"How does <code>std::shared_ptr<\/code> use this, and is one <code>shared_ptr<\/code> object safe to read and reassign from two threads?","seconds":75,"lesson":"memory-orders","title":"C++ memory orders: seq_cst, acquire, release and relaxed","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"safe-publication.s1","q":"\"You have two minutes: a gateway routes every request using a table of backends, and a control thread rebuilds that table every few seconds. Design how the table is shared so requests never see a half-built table and never wait for a rebuild.\"","points":["Each table is immutable: built completely, inputs copied, never modified after it is published","Publish through one reference: volatile or <code>AtomicReference<\/code> in Java; a <code>shared_ptr<\/code> to const behind a small mutex, or an atomic <code>shared_ptr<\/code>, in C++","The swap is a happens-before edge, so a request that sees the new reference sees the whole table","Each request reads the reference once into a local and uses only that snapshot","Old tables are freed by the garbage collector in Java and by the last <code>shared_ptr<\/code> copy in C++","Racing writers are serialized, or use compare-and-set with a version check; the cost is a full copy per rebuild, fine for read-mostly data"],"model":"I would make each table immutable and publish it through one reference. The control thread builds a complete new table, copies anything it was given, and never touches it again once it is published. Then it swaps a single reference: a volatile field or an <code>AtomicReference<\/code> in Java, and in C++ a <code>shared_ptr<\/code> to a const table, behind a small mutex or in an atomic <code>shared_ptr<\/code>. That swap is a happens-before edge, so any request that sees the new reference sees the whole table. Each request reads the reference once into a local and uses only that snapshot, so it can never mix two tables halfway through. In Java readers take no lock at all; in C++ they hold the mutex only long enough to copy a pointer. Old tables free themselves: the garbage collector in Java, the last <code>shared_ptr<\/code> copy in C++, so a slow request simply keeps its version alive. If two rebuilds can race, I serialize them, or compare and set with a version check so an old table never overwrites a newer one. The cost is a full copy per rebuild, which is fine for data read constantly and changed every few seconds.","follow":"Now the table is 2 GB and changes many times a second. What do you change?","seconds":90,"lesson":"safe-publication","title":"Safe publication, immutability and final fields","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"safe-publication.s2","q":"\"Here is a symptom: once in a while a request thread gets a non-null <code>Config<\/code> whose timeout is zero. The reload thread runs <code>current = new Config(30, region)<\/code>, and <code>current<\/code> is a plain field. Walk me through how that can happen, and how you would fix it.\"","points":["Building and publishing is several steps: allocate, write the fields, store the reference","With no happens-before edge, the reference can become visible before the field writes, or the reader can read the fields early","One concrete interleaving: the reference lands, the reader sees non-null and reads timeout as 0, then the field writes arrive","In C++ the same code is a data race, so undefined behaviour","Fix: publish over an edge: volatile or <code>AtomicReference<\/code>, a shared lock, or release and acquire on an atomic pointer in C++","Final fields in Java protect the contents even through a race, but not the visibility of the new reference"],"model":"It is unsafe publication. <code>new Config<\/code> is several steps: allocate the object, write the fields, then store the reference into <code>current<\/code>. Because <code>current<\/code> is a plain field, there is no happens-before edge between the reload thread and the reader, so the compiler and the CPU may make the reference visible before the field writes, and the reader may even read the fields before it reads the reference. One interleaving: the reference lands, the request reads <code>current<\/code>, sees non-null, reads the timeout and gets the default zero, and only then do the field writes arrive. In C++ the same code is a data race, which is undefined behaviour. The fix is to publish over an edge. In Java I make <code>current<\/code> volatile or an <code>AtomicReference<\/code>; in C++ I store the pointer into an atomic with release and load it with acquire, or guard it with a mutex that readers take too. Making the fields final in Java also guarantees the contents through a race, but not that readers ever see the new reference, so I would still use volatile.","follow":"Would it be enough to make only the reload method <code>synchronized<\/code>?","seconds":80,"lesson":"safe-publication","title":"Safe publication, immutability and final fields","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"safe-publication.s3","q":"\"People call immutable objects the easiest kind of thread safety. Why is that, and what traps do you watch for when you make a class immutable?\"","points":["Nothing changes after construction, so readers cannot race with writers and need no locks","The hand-off of the reference still needs a safe channel; Java final fields add a guarantee for the contents even through a race","Trap: a final or const field that points to a mutable list freezes which list, not its contents; copy inputs, never hand out the live list","Trap: letting <code>this<\/code> escape the constructor, which breaks even final fields","Cost: every change builds a new object, so it suits read-mostly data"],"model":"Because once the constructor finishes, nothing changes, so there is nothing for two threads to race on, and readers need no locks, ever. The one moment that still needs care is the hand-off of the reference, which must go over a safe channel such as a volatile or atomic reference or a lock. Java adds an extra promise: final fields are visible to any thread that sees the object, even through a race. The traps are things that look immutable but are not. A final field that points to a mutable list only freezes which list, not what is in it, so I copy inputs with <code>List.copyOf<\/code> and never hand out the live list. In C++, a const pointer to a non-const vector has the same hole. The other trap is letting <code>this<\/code> escape from the constructor, by starting a thread or registering a listener, which breaks even the final field promise. And the price is that every change builds a new object, so immutability fits read-mostly data.","follow":"How do you \"change\" an immutable object, and what if it is large and changes often?","seconds":75,"lesson":"safe-publication","title":"Safe publication, immutability and final fields","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"double-checked-locking.s1","q":"\"Here is a symptom: a lazily created config object occasionally shows a port of zero, only on the first few requests after a deploy, and it never reproduces locally. The getter uses double-checked locking. What do you suspect, and how do you fix it?\"","points":["Suspect unsafe publication: the field is not <code>volatile<\/code> in Java, or not an atomic with acquire and release in C++","Mechanism: the reference can become visible before the constructor's writes, so the unlocked first check sees a non-null object whose port is still the default zero","The lock does not help that thread, because the fast path never takes it, so no happens-before edge reaches it","Why only after a deploy: only threads racing the very first call can hit it, and local runs rarely race that call","The fix: make the field volatile (or acquire load, release store) and publish only a fully built object; for a singleton, prefer the holder class or a function-local static"],"model":"I suspect unsafe publication in the double-checked lock. A port of zero is the default value, so some thread saw the object before its constructor's writes. That happens when the field is a plain field in Java, or a plain pointer in C++. The creating thread allocates, constructs and stores the reference, but nothing stops the reference from becoming visible first. A thread doing the unlocked first check then sees a non-null reference, skips the lock, and reads a port that is still zero. The lock cannot save it, because a lock only orders threads that actually take it, and the fast path never does. It shows up only after a deploy because only threads racing the very first call can hit it, and a local run rarely races that call. The fix in Java is one word: make the field <code>volatile<\/code>, and build the object fully in a local before assigning it. In C++, use a <code>std::atomic<\/code>, load with acquire and store with release. For a singleton I would delete the hand-written code and use the holder class or a function-local static.","follow":"If every field of the config is <code>final<\/code>, do you still need <code>volatile<\/code>?","seconds":80,"lesson":"double-checked-locking","title":"Double-checked locking and lazy initialization","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"double-checked-locking.s2","q":"\"You need a lazily created, thread-safe singleton. Which way would you write it in your language, and why not hand-written double-checked locking?\"","points":["Use the runtime's once-only initialization: the holder class or an enum in Java, a function-local static in C++","Why it is correct: the JVM initializes a class once, under its own lock, on first use; C++11 makes concurrent first callers wait for one initializer","Why it is lazy and cheap: built on first use, and afterwards no lock and no volatile read (in C++, one acquire load of a guard)","When hand-written double-checked locking or <code>call_once<\/code> is still right: a lazy field per object, or initialization that can fail and must be retried","Question the singleton: eager creation and passing the instance in is simpler and easier to test"],"model":"I would let the runtime do it. In Java that is the holder class idiom: a private nested class with a static final instance. The JVM initializes that class the first time it is touched, exactly once, under its own lock, and every later caller gets the finished object with no lock and no volatile read. An enum with one constant is just as good, and it also survives serialization and reflection. In C++ it is a function-local static. Since C++11, if several threads reach it at once, one initializes it and the others wait, and the compiler does that with a guard flag, which is double-checked locking written once and tested everywhere. I would still write double-checked locking by hand, on a volatile field, in two cases: a lazy field that belongs to each object, and initialization that can fail and must be retried, because a Java class whose initializer throws is broken for good. In C++ I would use <code>call_once<\/code> for both. And first I would ask whether it needs to be a lazy singleton at all, because creating it at startup and passing it in is simpler and easier to test.","follow":"What happens to the holder class if the constructor throws?","seconds":85,"lesson":"double-checked-locking","title":"Double-checked locking and lazy initialization","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"double-checked-locking.s3","q":"\"What exactly does <code>volatile<\/code> buy you in double-checked locking, and what does it cost on the fast path?\"","points":["A volatile write is a release: the constructor's writes become visible no later than the reference","A volatile read is an acquire: a thread that sees the non-null reference also sees those writes, the edge the unlocked check was missing","The second check needs no extra ordering because the lock orders it, which is why the C++ version can load it relaxed","Cost: an ordinary load on x86, a load-acquire on ARM; read the field once into a local so each call pays one volatile read","What it cannot fix: publishing before setup finishes, so build in a local and publish last"],"model":"It buys a happens-before edge on the path that never takes the lock. A volatile write acts as a release, so everything the constructor wrote is visible before the reference is. A volatile read acts as an acquire, so a thread that sees the non-null reference also sees those writes. That is exactly the edge the unlocked first check was missing. The second check, inside the lock, does not need it, because the lock already orders it against the write made under that same lock. That is why the C++ version can load it relaxed. The cost is small. On x86 an acquire load is an ordinary load, and on ARM it is a load-acquire instruction, so after initialization the fast path is nearly free. I also read the field once into a local, so each call pays for one volatile read, not two. What volatile cannot fix is publishing too early. If you assign the field and then call a configure method, other threads can use the half-configured object, so build everything in a local and publish last.","follow":"Would it still be correct if you removed the second check, the one inside the lock?","seconds":75,"lesson":"double-checked-locking","title":"Double-checked locking and lazy initialization","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"caches-and-false-sharing.s1","q":"\"Here is a symptom: a single producer, single consumer ring buffer is correct, but much slower than expected. The head and tail indices are declared next to each other. What would you look at, and how would you fix it?\"","points":["Suspect false sharing: the consumer writes the head and the producer writes the tail, and they share one cache line","Mechanism: a core can only write a line it holds as the only valid copy, so the line bounces between the two cores on almost every operation, tens of nanoseconds each","It is not a correctness bug: no data race and no wrong answers, so no test or race detector flags it","Fix the layout: head and tail on separate lines (align to 128 in C++; stride or pad in Java, since the JVM picks field order)","Go further: each side caches the other's index and rereads it only when the buffer looks full or empty; measure before and after, with <code>perf c2c<\/code> on Linux"],"model":"My first suspect is false sharing between the two indices. The consumer writes the head and the producer writes the tail, and if they sit next to each other they share one cache line. A core can only write a line when it holds the only valid copy, so every update by one side invalidates the other side's copy, and the line bounces between the two cores on almost every operation. Each bounce costs tens of nanoseconds, against about a nanosecond for a write to a line the core already owns. Nothing is wrong logically. There is no data race and no wrong answer, which is why no test or race detector flags it. The fix is layout. I would put the head and the tail on separate lines, aligned to 128 bytes in C++, and in Java spaced apart or padded, because the JVM decides field order. A further step is for each side to keep a cached copy of the other side's index and reread the shared one only when the buffer looks full or empty, so the lines move far less often. Then I would measure before and after, and on Linux <code>perf c2c<\/code> shows the contended line directly.","follow":"Would padding help if the producer and the consumer both updated one shared <code>count<\/code> field?","seconds":90,"lesson":"caches-and-false-sharing","title":"CPU caches, coherence and false sharing","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"caches-and-false-sharing.s2","q":"\"The hardware keeps caches coherent. So why do we still need <code>volatile<\/code> in Java or <code>std::atomic<\/code> in C++?\"","points":["Coherence keeps each single line consistent: a writer invalidates the other copies, so no core reads a stale copy of a line forever","The compiler or JIT may keep a value in a register and never read memory again, and coherence never sees registers","Store buffers and invalidation queues delay writes, so writes to different locations can be seen in a different order","Coherence is per location; ordering across locations is the memory model's job","<code>volatile<\/code>, atomics and locks restrain the compiler and emit the fences; coherence only decides how much that costs"],"model":"Because coherence solves a smaller problem than the one we have. Coherence means every core agrees on each single cache line: before a core writes a line it invalidates every other copy, so no core keeps reading a stale copy of that line forever. But three things sit outside it. First, the compiler or the JIT may keep a variable in a register and never read memory again, and coherence never sees a register. Second, each core has a store buffer, so its writes reach the cache late, and another core can see two writes to different locations in a different order than they were made. Third, the compiler and the CPU reorder independent operations for speed. Coherence is per location; ordering across locations is the memory model's job. So <code>volatile<\/code>, atomics and locks do what the hardware alone does not: they stop the compiler from caching or reordering across them, and they emit the fences that order the store buffer. Coherence then makes those guarantees cheap to deliver. Correctness comes from the language rules, and the caches decide the cost.","follow":"On a single core machine, can a thread still miss a plain flag that another thread set?","seconds":80,"lesson":"caches-and-false-sharing","title":"CPU caches, coherence and false sharing","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"caches-and-false-sharing.s3","q":"\"You have two minutes: design a request counter for a 64 core server. Every request increments it, and a metrics thread reads it once a second.\"","points":["One shared atomic is true sharing: every increment moves its line, so the whole machine is capped at tens of millions of increments a second, as an order of magnitude, however many cores you add","Stripe it: per-thread or per-cell counters, each on its own line, summed on read (<code>LongAdder<\/code> in Java; 128 byte aligned cells with relaxed <code>fetch_add<\/code> in C++)","Read side trade off: the sum is slower and not an atomic snapshot while writers run, which is fine for a metric read once a second","Cost: one cache line of memory per cell","If still hot: count in a thread-local and publish every N requests; confirm with a benchmark at 1, 8 and 64 threads"],"model":"I would not use one shared atomic. Every increment needs that counter's cache line in the incrementing core, so with 64 cores the line just moves from core to core, and the whole machine is capped at something like tens of millions of increments a second, as an order of magnitude, however many cores I add. Since the counter is written on every request and read once a second, I would stripe it. In Java that is <code>LongAdder<\/code>, which sends contending threads to different cells, padded onto separate lines, and adds them up in <code>sum()<\/code>. In C++ I would keep an array of counters, each aligned to 128 bytes so no two share a line, give each thread its own cell, increment it with a relaxed <code>fetch_add<\/code>, and sum the cells on read. The trade off is a slower read, and the sum is not an exact snapshot while writers are running, which is fine for a metric read once a second. It also costs a cache line of memory per cell. If that is still too hot, each thread can count in a local and add to its cell every thousand requests. Then I would benchmark at 1, 8 and 64 threads to confirm it scales.","follow":"Why align the cells to 128 bytes rather than 64?","seconds":90,"lesson":"caches-and-false-sharing","title":"CPU caches, coherence and false sharing","track":"core","module":6},{"id":"atomics-and-cas.s1","q":"\"Two threads on two different cores call <code>compareAndSet(0, 1)<\/code> on the same variable at the same instant. Walk me through what happens, all the way down to the hardware.\"","points":["Exactly one succeeds: one call returns true, the other returns false (in C++ the loser also gets the current value back in <code>expected<\/code>)","CAS is a read, a compare and a conditional write done as one indivisible step","x86: <code>lock cmpxchg<\/code> takes the cache line in exclusive state and keeps it through the read, compare and write, so the other core's request waits","ARM: one LSE instruction such as <code>casal<\/code>, or a load-exclusive, store-exclusive pair whose store fails if another core touched the line","Nobody blocks and the operating system is never involved; the loser learns at once and decides what to do","The real cost is moving the cache line between cores, which is why one hot atomic stops scaling"],"model":"Exactly one of them wins. Compare-and-swap is a read, a compare and a conditional write done as one indivisible step, so whichever core gets there first finds 0, writes 1 and returns true. The other finds 1, which no longer matches, and returns false. In C++ the loser also gets the current value written back into its expected variable. The hardware makes this indivisible through cache coherence: to write a cache line, a core must hold the only valid copy. On x86 the instruction is <code>lock cmpxchg<\/code>. The core takes the line in exclusive state and keeps it for the read, the compare and the write, so the second core's request just waits a few nanoseconds and then sees 1. On ARM it is either one LSE instruction, <code>casal<\/code>, or on older cores a load-exclusive, store-exclusive pair, where the store fails if anyone touched the line and the code goes round again. Nothing blocks and the operating system is never involved, so the loser knows at once that it lost and can retry or do something else. The real cost is moving the cache line between cores, tens of nanoseconds, which is why one heavily shared atomic stops scaling.","follow":"Can a compare-and-swap ever fail when the value did match?","seconds":90,"lesson":"atomics-and-cas","title":"Atomic variables and compare-and-swap","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"atomics-and-cas.s2","q":"\"This class keeps <code>lower<\/code> and <code>upper<\/code> in two separate atomic integers, and each setter checks the other bound before it writes. A colleague says it is thread-safe because every field is atomic. Do you agree?\"","points":["No: each operation is atomic, but check then set is two steps with a gap","The interleaving: from 0 to 10, <code>setLower(5)<\/code> and <code>setUpper(4)<\/code> both pass their checks, then both write, leaving 5 to 4","The invariant spans two variables, and an atomic only protects what lives inside one variable","Fix one: one lock around both fields, taken by readers too","Fix two: both bounds in one immutable object behind one atomic reference, replaced with a compare-and-swap loop"],"model":"No. Every single read and write is atomic, but each setter does a check and then a set, and those are two separate steps. Here is the interleaving. The range starts at 0 to 10. One thread calls <code>setLower(5)<\/code> and checks 5 against upper 10, fine. Another calls <code>setUpper(4)<\/code> and checks 4 against lower 0, also fine. Then both write, and the range is 5 to 4. Every atomic did its job and the invariant is still broken, because the rule, lower at most upper, lives across two variables, and an atomic only protects what lives inside one. I would fix it one of two ways. The simple way is one lock around both fields, taken by readers as well. The lock-free way is to put both bounds in one immutable object behind a single atomic reference. Each setter reads it, checks, builds a new pair and compare-and-swaps it in, retrying if it changed. A reader then gets a consistent pair in one read.","follow":"Your lock-free version allocates a new object on every update. Can you avoid that?","seconds":75,"lesson":"atomics-and-cas","title":"Atomic variables and compare-and-swap","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"atomics-and-cas.s3","q":"\"We replaced a locked request counter with a single atomic counter to make it faster. On a 32-core box, throughput got worse as we added threads. What is going on, and what would you do?\"","points":["Every increment needs exclusive ownership of the counter's cache line, so increments are served one at a time","Each hand over costs tens of nanoseconds, so the whole machine tops out around tens of millions of increments a second (an order of magnitude), however many cores","More threads only add queueing and coherence traffic, so total time grows","Fix: stripe it, <code>LongAdder<\/code> in Java, per thread or sharded counters on separate cache lines in C++, summed on read","Trade off: a read is a sum, not an instant snapshot, which is fine for metrics but not for an exact limit"],"model":"The atomic removed the lock but not the sharing. Every increment has to own the counter's cache line exclusively, so with 32 threads the line bounces from core to core and the increments are served one at a time. Each hand over costs tens of nanoseconds, so the whole machine tops out somewhere in the tens of millions of increments a second, as an order of magnitude, no matter how many cores I add. More threads only add queueing and coherence traffic, which is why it got slower. On a laptop, eight threads doing the same increments as one can take over ten times longer. The fix is to stop sharing one line. In Java I would use <code>LongAdder<\/code>, which spreads increments over padded cells and sums them on read. In C++ I would keep per thread or sharded counters, each on its own cache line, and add them up when someone reads. The trade off is that a read is a sum, not an instant snapshot, which is fine for metrics but not for enforcing an exact limit.","follow":"If the counter enforces a hard limit, say at most 100 requests in flight, what would you use instead?","seconds":75,"lesson":"atomics-and-cas","title":"Atomic variables and compare-and-swap","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"cas-loops.s1","q":"\"Without a lock, how would you implement <code>withdraw(amount)<\/code> so the balance can never go negative? Talk me through the loop and why it is correct.\"","points":["A plain atomic subtract cannot do it, because the decision depends on the current balance","The loop: read, refuse if too small, compute the new balance, compare-and-swap, and retry from the fresh value if the swap fails","Correctness: the swap succeeds only if the balance still equals the value the decision was made on, so a stale decision is never applied","A concrete race: two withdrawals both read 100, one swap wins, the other fails, re-reads and decides again","The body can run many times, so no side effects inside it","Progress: lock-free, so some thread always succeeds, but one unlucky thread can retry many times"],"model":"A plain atomic subtract will not do, because whether I may subtract depends on the current balance. So I write a compare-and-swap loop. I read the balance. If it is less than the amount, I return false. Otherwise I compute the new balance and compare-and-swap it in, expecting the value I read. If the swap fails, someone changed the balance after my read, so I go round again with the fresh value. In C++ the failed <code>compare_exchange_weak<\/code> has already handed me that value; in Java I call <code>get<\/code> again. Why is it correct? The swap only succeeds if the balance still holds exactly the value my decision was based on, so a decision is never applied to a different balance. Say the balance is 100 and two threads want 80 and 50. Both read 100 and both think there is enough. The 50 swaps first, and the balance is 50. The 80's swap fails, it re-reads 50, sees that is not enough, and returns false. No overdraft, nothing lost. Two rules close it out. The body can run many times, so no side effects inside it. And it is lock-free, not wait-free: some thread always succeeds, but an unlucky one can retry many times.","follow":"Why <code>compare_exchange_weak<\/code> in this loop, and where would the weak version be a bug?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cas-loops","title":"CAS loops and atomic state machines","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"cas-loops.s2","q":"\"A service's CPU is pinned at 100 percent but its throughput has dropped, and the profiler shows most of the time inside a compare-and-swap loop on one shared atomic. What is happening, and what do you do?\"","points":["Heavy contention: most attempts fail, and every attempt, failed or not, pulls the cache line across cores","Every failure reruns the body, and a long body (or one that allocates a new object) widens the window for losing","Lock-free is a progress guarantee, not a speed guarantee: nobody sleeps, so the losers burn CPU","First make the loop cheap: little work between read and swap, and exit without writing when nothing changes; use fetch-and-add if the update is a plain add","Then back off after a failure, or stripe the state so threads stop sharing one line","If the state cannot be split, a lock may be faster because waiters sleep; measure before and after"],"model":"That is contention on the CAS loop. With many threads on one variable, most attempts fail, and every attempt, failed or not, drags the cache line to its core, so the CPU is busy moving one line around instead of doing work. Each failure also reruns the loop body, and if that body is long or allocates a new object, the window for losing gets wider, so it gets worse exactly when load rises. Lock-free is a progress guarantee, not a speed guarantee. Nobody sleeps, so the losers burn CPU. I would first make the loop cheap: compute as little as possible between the read and the swap, and exit without writing when there is nothing to change. If the update is really just an add, I would use fetch-and-add, which does not fail and retry. Next, a short backoff after each failure, or splitting the state into stripes so threads stop fighting over one line. And if the state cannot be split, a plain lock is often faster here, because waiting threads sleep instead of spinning. Whatever I change, I measure before and after.","follow":"How would you add backoff to that loop, and what does backoff cost you?","seconds":80,"lesson":"cas-loops","title":"CAS loops and atomic state machines","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"cas-loops.s3","q":"\"When would you reach for a CAS loop instead of a lock, and when would you go back to the lock?\"","points":["CAS fits when all the state lives in one variable, or packs into one word or one immutable object","And the update is short and pure, so redoing it is cheap","What you gain: no blocking, no deadlock, and a stalled thread never holds others up","Lock when several variables or structures change together, the state is too big to copy, or the work has I/O or side effects that must happen once","Under very heavy contention a lock can win because waiters sleep; start simple and measure"],"model":"I reach for a CAS loop when everything that has to stay consistent lives in one variable, or I can pack it into one word or one immutable object, and the update is short and pure, so redoing it is cheap. Then I get no blocking, no deadlock, and a thread that stalls halfway never holds anyone else up. I go back to a lock when several variables or structures must change together, when the state is too big to copy on every update, or when the critical section does I/O or has side effects that must happen exactly once. Under very heavy contention a lock can even be faster, because the waiters sleep instead of spinning. So I start with the simplest correct design, often a lock, and move to CAS where measurement says it matters.","follow":"Your state is two fields that must change together. How do you make it fit in one compare-and-swap?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cas-loops","title":"CAS loops and atomic state machines","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"counters-at-scale.s1","q":"\"Our request counter is atomic, but throughput falls when we add workers. What would you investigate, and what would you change?\"","points":["Separate correct atomic updates from scalable throughput and check for cache-line contention","Confirm the required read semantics and measure representative writer and reader loads","Use LongAdder in Java or padded atomic stripes in C++, with possible stripe collisions","Explain scan cost, extra memory, non-snapshot live reads and exact totals after joining writers","Keep atomic reservation or locking for unique ids and hard limits; measure again after the change"],"model":"I would investigate contention on the counter's cache line, then choose a replacement based on what readers need. Atomic increments prevent lost updates, but they do not make one shared location scale across cores. Repeated transfers of write ownership can cost much more than adding one.\nFirst I would check the profile, the number of runnable writers, and how often readers scrape the value. I would compare representative loads rather than trust one timing on an idle laptop. If this is a metric read occasionally, I would try <code>LongAdder<\/code> in Java or padded atomic stripes in C++. Writers spread their updates across cells, which reduces competition for one location. Stripes can still collide, so their updates remain atomic.\nThe costs are extra memory and a scan on every read. A live sum is not a snapshot, while the final total after joining all writers is exact, assuming no overflow. If we only need a final total, local counting followed by one merge per worker is another option.\nFor unique ids or a hard admission limit, I would retain an atomic reservation or a lock. Finally I would measure throughput and tail latency again, including the actual scrape load, to confirm the change helps.","follow":"Would switching the C++ increment to relaxed ordering remove the bottleneck?","seconds":90,"lesson":"counters-at-scale","title":"Counters at scale: contention, LongAdder and striping","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"counters-at-scale.s2","q":"\"We need exact counts for each reporting minute. Can we just call <code>sumThenReset()<\/code>, or swap in a new striped counter?\"","points":["Neither separate cell resets nor a reference swap establishes an exact interval boundary by itself","Explain a writer retaining the old counter across the swap","Define whether the boundary follows event time or the recording operation, then coordinate writers","Use cumulative totals and reader-side deltas when approximate reporting periods are acceptable","Bound locally buffered events separately from visibility or scheduling delay"],"model":"Neither operation alone gives an exact reporting boundary. I would first define whether an event belongs to a minute by its timestamp or by when recording it takes effect. Those are different contracts, especially when a worker pauses across the boundary.\n<code>sumThenReset()<\/code> handles components separately, so it does not divide all concurrent updates at one instant. Swapping the current counter reference has another race. A worker can obtain the old counter, pause, and increment it after the reporting thread swaps and reads it. That update can disappear from reports even though the increment itself was thread-safe.\nFor an exact operation boundary, I would coordinate recording and rollover with one lock, or use a proven epoch handoff that waits for old writers to finish before reading the retired counter. Timestamp-based reporting also needs a policy for late events. The coordination cost is part of the requirement.\nFor ordinary metrics, I would prefer cumulative counters and subtract successive scrapes, accepting fuzzy intervals. If each of P workers buffers at most B events, at most P times B events are locally unpublished. That does not bound every reader's visibility lag. A timer alone also cannot guarantee a deadline when the scheduler can pause its worker.","follow":"How would key eviction race with a thread that has already looked up that key's adder?","seconds":90,"lesson":"counters-at-scale","title":"Counters at scale: contention, LongAdder and striping","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"spinlocks.s1","q":"\"Walk me through a correct spinlock, then explain why repeatedly exchanging the flag is expensive.\"","points":["An atomic exchange grants ownership to exactly one contender until release","Acquire on successful acquisition and release on unlock order protected data","TTAS polls with atomic reads and uses an RMW only when the flag looks free","CPU hints provide neither ownership nor synchronization; jitter reduces retry collisions","The algorithm remains unfair, non-reentrant and blocking in the progress-guarantee sense"],"model":"I would use an atomic exchange to acquire the flag, with acquire semantics, and a release operation to clear it on unlock. Only one contender can observe that the old flag was clear before the next release. That gives mutual exclusion. The release and the next successful acquire also order the protected data, so the counter inside the critical section can be plain.\nA simple test-and-set loop exchanges on every attempt, including while another thread owns the lock. Those read-modify-write operations compete for write ownership of the same cache line. With several waiters, the traffic can even delay the owner's unlock.\nI would instead use test-and-test-and-set. Waiters first poll with atomic reads, allowing shared cached copies. When a read sees clear, they attempt the exchange. Two threads can both see clear, so the read alone never grants ownership. In C++, that polling load can be relaxed because the successful exchange supplies acquire ordering.\nI would add a CPU spin hint and consider capped randomized backoff after collisions. Neither replaces synchronization or prevents preemption. The lock remains unfair and non-reentrant. It is also a blocking algorithm despite using atomic instructions: if the owner stops running, every waiter can stop making useful progress.","follow":"What exactly breaks if the C++ unlock store is relaxed?","seconds":90,"lesson":"spinlocks","title":"Spinlocks, backoff and when spinning beats blocking","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"spinlocks.s2","q":"\"A spinlock improved our microbenchmark, but production CPU and p99 latency got worse. How would you diagnose that, and what would you use?\"","points":["Keep a parking mutex as the baseline and treat preemption or oversubscription as hypotheses","Explain how a paused owner and runnable spinners can amplify one another's delay","Measure runnable load, hold and wait times, CPU stacks and tail latency under realistic contention","Explain why pinning, hints and backoff do not guarantee owner progress","Consider library adaptive waiting or reduced sharing; ticket fairness still depends on scheduling"],"model":"I would use the parking mutex as the baseline and investigate whether spinning is amplifying owner delays. The microbenchmark may have short critical sections and idle cores. Production can have more runnable workers than cores, page faults, interrupts or occasional slow work under the lock.\nIf the owner is paused, waiters keep consuming CPU without advancing the protected operation. They can occupy the capacity the owner needs to resume. That creates long waits for unlucky requests and can damage p99 much more visibly than the average. Repeated test-and-set operations can add cache traffic too, but I would confirm the cause rather than infer it from latency alone.\nI would inspect CPU stacks, runnable load, lock hold times and wait-time distributions, then reproduce the workload with realistic contention and forced scheduling pressure. Pinning restricts placement; it does not prevent preemption. Hints and jitter reduce some costs but cannot make a stopped owner run.\nMy first implementation choice would be a library mutex that can park, possibly with its own bounded spin phase. I would then shorten the critical section or shard the shared state. A ticket lock orders admission, but a descheduled next ticket can still stall everyone. Fairness is not a latency bound.","follow":"What changes if the waiters are Java virtual threads rather than platform threads?","seconds":90,"lesson":"spinlocks","title":"Spinlocks, backoff and when spinning beats blocking","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"lock-free-stack-and-aba.s1","q":"\"Walk me through an ABA failure on a stack. Would garbage collection or a version stamp fix it?\"","points":["CAS compares the current value, not its history or the validity of a saved successor","T1 saves head A and successor B; T2 pops A, pops B, and reinserts A above C","T1's stale CAS succeeds and incorrectly restores B, which T2 already owns","Fresh Java nodes and GC prevent harmful identity reuse; explicit live-node recycling can still fail","Compare pointer and incremented stamp together, with a wraparound bound","A stamp does not protect C++ node lifetime or make concurrent link mutation safe"],"model":"ABA is when the head compares equal again but the successor I saved is no longer valid. Start with A above B above C. My pop reads head A and saves B, then pauses. Another thread pops A, pops B, and puts A back above C. I resume and compare the head with A. That comparison succeeds, so I install B as the new head, even though the other thread already owns B. Every head access can be atomic and the stack is still wrong. For a Java stack that always allocates fresh nodes, garbage collection preserves object identity while I hold a reference, and popped objects are never reinserted. Explicitly recycling live nodes removes that protection. A stamped head is another approach: compare the reference and version together and increment the version on every head change. My old version then fails even when the reference matches. But a finite stamp can wrap, so I need a bound on updates while a stale observation is usable. And in C++, a stamp does not keep the node alive long enough to read its successor. I still need safe reclamation and safe access to any links that reinsertion changes.","follow":"Can the head return to A without causing a bug, and what makes that case harmless?","seconds":90,"lesson":"lock-free-stack-and-aba","title":"A lock-free stack and the ABA problem","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"lock-free-stack-and-aba.s2","q":"\"You have written the C++ CAS loop. When can you free a popped node, and where does the pop take effect?\"","points":["Successful head CAS is the nonempty pop's linearization point, not proof that all readers are gone","A stale reader may still dereference the unlinked node, so immediate freeing is unsafe","A bounded demo can retire nodes and reclaim only after all callers stop","Production reclamation protects readers through hazard publication and validation, or an epoch grace period","Separate link storage and non-throwing value moves are explicit contracts in this example","Allocation and reclamation must be included before claiming end-to-end lock-freedom"],"model":"The pop takes effect at its successful head CAS, but that is too early to free the node. Another thread may already have loaded the old head and be about to read its next pointer. Unlinking proves the node is no longer reachable from the stack, not that no reader holds it. For a bounded interview demo, I put removed nodes on a retired list and reclaim them only after every caller has stopped. That is simple, but memory grows with the total work. For a server I would use a proven reclamation protocol. Hazard pointers publish and validate protection before dereferencing; epochs wait for readers that could have seen the node to leave their protected intervals. I keep a separate retirement link because stale readers still access the stack link. This example also requires non-throwing value moves, so an exception cannot skip retirement after removal. Finally, calling the head protocol lock-free does not classify the whole method. The allocator, value transfer and reclamation path also need progress analysis.","follow":"What happens to memory usage if a reader is suspended indefinitely under hazards versus epochs?","seconds":75,"lesson":"lock-free-stack-and-aba","title":"A lock-free stack and the ABA problem","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"progress-guarantees.s1","q":"\"Explain blocking, obstruction-free, lock-free and wait-free. Where does a spinlock belong?\"","points":["A spinlock is blocking because a paused holder prevents acquisition even by a lone running waiter","Obstruction-free means an operation completes when it runs alone long enough","Lock-free guarantees system-wide completions under continued execution, allowing individual starvation","Wait-free bounds each active caller's own steps under the stated primitive assumptions","Wait-free implies lock-free, which implies obstruction-free","Step bounds do not guarantee scheduling or wall-clock deadlines"],"model":"A spinlock is blocking. If its owner pauses, another thread can spin alone forever without acquiring it. The use of CAS does not change that dependency. Obstruction-free means a caller finishes if it runs alone long enough; concurrent callers can still keep interfering without completing. Lock-free strengthens that to system-wide progress while operations keep executing. A Treiber stack can have many successful pops while one unlucky popper keeps losing its CAS. Wait-free goes further: every caller that continues taking steps finishes within its own step bound, regardless of interference. A fixed ring with one producer and one consumer can provide that for operations that return full or empty, provided the atomic primitives and value transfers are bounded. So wait-free implies lock-free, and lock-free implies obstruction-free. These promises count steps, not milliseconds. A paused caller does not get CPU time merely because its algorithm is wait-free, and a stop-the-world pause stops every application thread. I would state which operation I am classifying and the primitive and allocation assumptions behind that claim.","follow":"Does a fair mutex become wait-free if every waiting thread eventually acquires it?","seconds":75,"lesson":"progress-guarantees","title":"Blocking, lock-free and wait-free","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"progress-guarantees.s2","q":"\"Our lock-free queue is slower than the mutex queue. What would you investigate before replacing either one?\"","points":["Lock-freedom is a progress guarantee and does not imply higher throughput","Both designs can contend on shared cache lines; failed CAS attempts add work","Measure realistic throughput, tail latency and CPU consumption with matching semantics and load","Inspect allocation, GC or reclamation, callbacks and sleeping-consumer behaviour","Consider batching or sharding only with an explicit ordering contract"],"model":"That result is plausible: lock-freedom does not promise higher throughput. Both queues may contend on shared cache lines, while failed CAS attempts add traffic and wasted work. A mutex can let one thread proceed while other callers park. I would first confirm that the benchmark compares the same capacity, ordering and empty-queue behaviour under realistic producer and consumer counts. Then I would measure throughput, tail latency and CPU consumption, including oversubscribed runs where lock holders can be preempted. I would inspect allocation and garbage collection in Java, reclamation in C++, and any logging or callbacks that introduce blocking. Idle-consumer behaviour matters too: a spinning consumer and a sleeping consumer are different workloads. Batching or sharding may reduce contention more than changing synchronization, but sharding may change ordering. I would choose based on the required semantics and measured bottleneck, then state any progress assumptions separately.","follow":"How would an allocator lock inside an enqueue change your claim that the whole operation is lock-free?","seconds":60,"lesson":"progress-guarantees","title":"Blocking, lock-free and wait-free","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"progress-guarantees.s3","q":"\"The C++ atomic says <code>is_always_lock_free<\/code> is false. Does that prove it uses a lock?\"","points":["False means there is no unconditional type-wide lock-free guarantee","Query the atomic object's is_lock_free() for this execution","A true result classifies the primitive and does not prove the surrounding algorithm lock-free","Neither query establishes wait-freedom or a latency deadline"],"model":"No. False means this type is not guaranteed always lock-free. It covers implementations that are sometimes lock-free as well as implementations that never are. I would query the actual object's <code>is_lock_free()<\/code> for this execution. A false runtime result prevents me from relying on that primitive for a lock-free proof. A true result still does not classify my whole method: a spinlock built from that atomic is blocking, and allocation or callbacks may introduce other dependencies. Neither query establishes wait-freedom or a deadline. For those stronger claims, I need a bound on the complete operation's work and explicit assumptions about its primitives and execution environment.","follow":"Why can an atomic implemented with a retry loop be lock-free without being wait-free?","seconds":45,"lesson":"progress-guarantees","title":"Blocking, lock-free and wait-free","track":"core","module":7},{"id":"semaphores.s1","q":"\"Why would you choose a semaphore instead of a mutex in front of a database client?\"","points":["Choose a semaphore to bound concurrent calls, with one permit per active call","A mutex serializes access to one caller; a semaphore has no owner","Acquire with a timeout and release exactly once after successful acquisition, even on failure","The permit wait and the database operation need separate deadlines","A concurrency limit differs from a rate limit, and waiting requests also need a bound"],"model":"I would use a semaphore because the requirement is several concurrent calls with a fixed upper limit. A mutex around the query would allow only one caller at a time. With fifty permits, each caller takes one before starting the query and returns it after finishing. The semaphore has no owner, so my wrapper must enforce that accounting. I acquire before entering the cleanup scope, then release in finally or an RAII destructor. A timeout or interruption during acquisition must not create a permit. I would use a short admission timeout and a separate database deadline, since getting a permit does not bound the query duration. Fifty permits means fifty calls in flight, not fifty requests per second. Faster calls allow a higher request rate. Finally, I would bound the waiting requests or reject them immediately under overload. Otherwise a semaphore can protect the database while the service still runs out of request capacity. The limit also applies only to this instance unless we coordinate across replicas.","follow":"How do you retain the permit if the database client completes asynchronously on another thread?","seconds":75,"lesson":"semaphores","title":"Semaphores: permits as a resource limit","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"semaphores.s2","q":"\"Our three-permit limiter sometimes has four calls in flight. What would you inspect first?\"","points":["Suspect an unmatched release and audit every exit path","Acquiring inside a try with unconditional finally can release after interruption","A failed timed acquire must not release; successful work must always release","Java does not cap permits at the initial count; C++ max need not equal the template argument","Check that all callers share the limiter and hold a permit until the real work finishes"],"model":"I would first look for a release without a matching successful acquisition. A common Java bug puts acquire inside a try block and release in finally. If acquisition is interrupted, finally still runs and invents a permit. The same mistake happens when a timed acquire returns false. I would move acquisition before the cleanup scope and release exactly once on every path after success, including exceptions. Java does not enforce the initial count as a capacity. In C++, the template argument is only a minimum supported maximum, so it is not an application accounting check either. I would then check that all callers share the same limiter. For asynchronous calls, I would check that the permit survives until the operation actually completes. Returning it when the request is merely submitted would let real in-flight work exceed the intended limit.","follow":"What changes if cancellation is requested but the downstream operation keeps running?","seconds":60,"lesson":"semaphores","title":"Semaphores: permits as a resource limit","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"semaphores.s3","q":"\"A producer writes a result and releases a semaphore before the consumer waits. Is the signal lost, and is the result visible?\"","points":["A zero-initialized semaphore remembers the release as a permit","A successful matching acquire publishes writes before the release","An initial spare permit would let the consumer pass before publication","One permit releases one successful acquisition; it is not a broadcast","Repeated handoffs need a protocol preventing overwrite while the consumer still reads"],"model":"The signal is remembered, and the result is visible after the successful handoff, provided the semaphore started at zero. The producer writes the result and releases one permit. If the consumer has not started waiting, that permit stays available. When the consumer acquires it, the synchronization orders the earlier write before the later read, so the result itself can be an ordinary variable. Starting with a spare permit would break the argument because the consumer could pass before the producer writes. There are two other limits. One permit admits one acquisition, so this is not a broadcast gate for many consumers. Also, publication does not protect a later overwrite. If the producer reuses the same result slot, I need an acknowledgement or another permit before it writes again. For multiple results, a synchronized queue is usually clearer than one shared slot.","follow":"How would you use two semaphores for a single producer and consumer sharing one reusable slot?","seconds":60,"lesson":"semaphores","title":"Semaphores: permits as a resource limit","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"latches.s1","q":"\"Four workers write into an ordinary results array. Can I read it safely after waiting on a latch, or do I still need atomics?\"","points":["A successful completion wait publishes writes made before the counted events","The latch starts at four and each worker writes a distinct element before counting down once","A latch does not serialize workers or protect writes after the countdown","Timeout or interruption does not provide the successful completion guarantee","Record failures separately; completion alone does not mean all results are valid"],"model":"Yes, after a successful completion wait I can read those results without making each element atomic, provided the protocol is correct. I initialize the latch to four. Each worker writes its own array element, then counts down exactly once and stops changing that element. Those writes happen before the coordinator continues from the successful wait. The distinct slots matter because the latch does not serialize workers. If two workers update the same ordinary counter, counting down afterwards cannot repair that race. I also cannot read an unfinished slot safely just because a timed wait returned false, or because my wait was interrupted. I would wait for termination or use separate synchronization for partial results. Finally, completion does not mean success. A worker should record any failure before counting down, and the coordinator checks those failures before treating the array as a valid result.","follow":"What happens if a worker counts down first and only then writes its result?","seconds":60,"lesson":"latches","title":"Latches: wait until N things happen","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"latches.s2","q":"\"You say your start-gate test proves the class is thread-safe. Walk me through what it actually proves.\"","points":["A passing test is evidence for exercised schedules, not a proof for all schedules","Ready checks in each worker; go prevents operations until everyone has checked in","Opening go makes workers eligible but does not promise simultaneous execution","Check invariants, capture failures and bound readiness and completion waits","Avoid a pool smaller than the gate's participant count, which can deadlock before readiness"],"model":"A passing test is evidence, not a proof that the class is thread-safe under every schedule. I use a ready counter so each worker checks in, then a gate that prevents the tested operation from starting until the coordinator opens it. That removes the staggered launch as a reason for operations never meeting. It does not force simultaneous execution; the scheduler can still serialize them. After release I check the invariant, such as exactly one winner or no lost items, capture worker failures, and put readiness and completion under a deadline. I repeat with different inputs and use a race detector where available. I also use dedicated threads for this gate. If eight tasks must check in but only two pool workers exist, the first two can block at the gate while the other six never run. That would test a deadlocked harness instead of the class.","follow":"How would you force a particular check-then-act interleaving without relying on sleeps?","seconds":60,"lesson":"latches","title":"Latches: wait until N things happen","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"latches.s3","q":"\"Implement wait until all submitted tasks finish. Why isn't a latch enough, and what contract do you need me to clarify?\"","points":["A latch has a fixed initial count and opens once, so dynamic reusable submission needs another design","Clarify waiting for idle versus the tasks submitted at the start of the wait","For idle, increment pending before handoff and decrement exactly once on completion or rejected handoff","Guard pending and wait in a predicate loop, notifying all at zero","For a snapshot, track a submission boundary and out-of-order completions; a total count is insufficient"],"model":"A latch is useful for a fixed batch, but it does not directly model continuously arriving tasks and repeated waits. I would first clarify whether you want the executor to become idle or want only the tasks submitted before this call to finish. For idle, I keep a pending count under a mutex. Submission increments it before making the task visible to workers, and a failed handoff rolls that increment back. A worker records success or failure and decrements exactly once. Waiters check for zero in a condition-variable loop, and the transition to zero wakes everyone. That design is reusable, but later submissions can extend the wait forever if the executor never becomes idle. For a fixed snapshot, I capture a submission sequence boundary and track completion of every task through that boundary, or capture their futures. A total completion count is not enough: later short tasks could finish while an earlier slow task is still running. The contract determines which state I need to track.","follow":"What happens if a worker calls this wait while its own task is still counted as pending?","seconds":75,"lesson":"latches","title":"Latches: wait until N things happen","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"barriers-and-phasers.s1","q":"\"Walk me through why you would use a barrier for parallel BFS. What exactly does it protect?\"","points":["A reusable barrier separates frontier levels so no worker expands the next level early","CAS claims each unvisited node once; separate slots or a mutex protect concurrent appends","The phase action installs the next frontier before waiters resume","Arrival and successful waiting publish phase data but do not serialize work within a phase","Workers wait for the slowest participant and need enough execution slots to reach the barrier"],"model":"I would use a reusable barrier because each BFS level depends on finishing the previous frontier. Each worker expands its assigned nodes and claims an unvisited neighbour with compare and swap, so shared neighbours enter the next frontier only once. Workers collect results locally, then append under a short mutex. At the barrier, the phase action installs the next frontier and checks whether it is empty. The arrival and successful wait publish that data before workers read it, so the frontier itself does not need atomic elements. But the barrier does not make concurrent appends safe inside the phase; that is still the mutex's job. A latch would need replacing every round, and a semaphore alone only limits admission. The costs are waiting for the slowest worker and requiring enough execution slots for every participant to reach the barrier.","follow":"What happens if three pool threads run four tasks that all wait at the same barrier?","seconds":60,"lesson":"barriers-and-phasers","title":"Barriers and phasers","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"barriers-and-phasers.s2","q":"\"One simulation worker throws before reaching the barrier, and the other workers hang. How would you fix the failure protocol?\"","points":["An error outside a Java barrier wait does not break it; an uncaught C++ worker exception terminates the process","Record the original failure and cancel peers; Java wait interruption or timeout can break the generation","Reset is not cancellation because it immediately starts an unbroken generation","In C++, catch the error and publish failure before replacing the missing arrival with one drop","Use a common phase-boundary stop decision, join workers, and distinguish failure from unresponsive work"],"model":"I would make failure an explicit part of the protocol. A Java barrier does not notice an exception thrown during ordinary work, so the surviving workers can wait forever. In C++, an exception escaping the thread function terminates the process; a caught exception followed by return can strand the peers. I would record the original error first. In Java I would interrupt the published worker set and have workers exit on interruption or a broken barrier. A timed wait can also detect a missing arrival. I would not use reset as cancellation, because a late arrival could enter its fresh generation and hang again. In C++ I would catch the error, publish failure, and call arrive and drop instead of that phase's normal arrival. The completion publishes one stop decision for the survivors. I would never arrive and then drop again for the same phase. Finally, I would join every worker before destroying shared state. A hung worker is a separate problem: a timeout detects missing progress, but stopping its work still requires cooperation.","follow":"How does your cleanup change if the worker already called arrive and is doing unrelated work before waiting?","seconds":75,"lesson":"barriers-and-phasers","title":"Barriers and phasers","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"barriers-and-phasers.s3","q":"\"Our worker set grows and shrinks between rounds. Would you choose a Phaser or a barrier, and how would you cancel a wait?\"","points":["Java Phaser supports registration and deregistration; C++ std::barrier supports dropping but not adding parties","Register before task launch and arrive exactly once for each registered participation in a phase","Phaser arrival and ordinary waiting are not interruptible; use awaitAdvanceInterruptibly with the arrival phase","Timeout and interruption leave Phaser state intact, so cancellation needs an explicit policy","Whole-job cancellation can record failure, force termination, and make workers stop on termination"],"model":"In Java I would choose a Phaser because it supports both registration and deregistration. In C++, the standard barrier can shrink through arrive and drop, but it cannot add participants, so growth needs another protocol. I would register each task before launching it and make its arrivals match its registration. For cancellation, Phaser differs from CyclicBarrier: ordinary arrival and waiting are not interruptible. I can arrive, save the phase number, then use awaitAdvanceInterruptibly with that phase and optionally a timeout. Interruption or timeout does not break the phaser, and the arrival still counts. I must not blindly deregister again in the same phase. If one failure should abort the whole computation, I would record the cause, force termination, and have workers stop when they observe termination. Dynamic membership helps coordination, but it does not define the application's cancellation or task-submission boundary.","follow":"If task submission races with waitUntilComplete, which batch owns the new task?","seconds":60,"lesson":"barriers-and-phasers","title":"Barriers and phasers","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"build-semaphore-and-latch.s1","q":"\"Build me a counting semaphore from a mutex and a condition variable. Explain why each part is necessary.\"","points":["One mutex protects a nonnegative count equal to initial permits plus releases minus successful acquires","Acquire checks and waits in a loop, then decrements under the same lock","A concrete barging interleaving explains why notification does not reserve a permit","Release checks overflow, increments, and wakes one waiter for single-permit requests","Interruption before acquisition leaves the count unchanged; callers release only acquired permits","Multi-permit requests need broader notification or explicit waiter selection, with separate fairness policy"],"model":"I would use one mutex to protect the permit count and one condition for permit availability. The count stays nonnegative and equals the initial permits plus releases minus successful acquires. Acquire locks, waits while the count is zero, and decrements before unlocking. Waiting releases the mutex and reacquires it before checking again. That loop matters even without spurious wakeups. Suppose a release wakes Alice, but Bob acquires the mutex first and takes the permit. Alice must check again or she will subtract from zero. Release checks for integer overflow, adds one permit, and signals one waiter, because each acquire needs one permit. In Java I would propagate interruption before changing the count, and callers return permits in a finally block only after acquisition succeeded. C++ callers use a scope guard. The semaphore itself has no owner. If requests can take several permits, waking just one unsuitable waiter can strand usable permits. I would broadcast or explicitly select eligible waiters, then add a queue if fairness matters. Waking a thread alone does not reserve its permit.","follow":"How would a timeout remove a queued request without blocking every later ticket?","seconds":75,"lesson":"build-semaphore-and-latch","title":"Build it yourself: semaphore and latch","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"build-semaphore-and-latch.s2","q":"\"My 100 millisecond acquire sometimes takes several hundred milliseconds. What do you check before calling it a bug?\"","points":["Check whether each wakeup restarts the full duration instead of using one budget","Use monotonic elapsed time or a fixed steady-clock deadline","Check the predicate while holding the lock at expiry and leave state unchanged on failure","Account for initial lock acquisition, lock reacquisition, and scheduler delay","A timeout is a waiting policy, not a hard real-time return guarantee"],"model":"First I would check whether the implementation restarts a fresh hundred milliseconds after every wakeup. Three late wakeups can stretch that budget towards four hundred milliseconds. I would calculate one deadline on a monotonic clock, or subtract elapsed monotonic time from one original budget. Every wait uses only what remains. At expiry I check the predicate while holding the lock; a permit arriving near the deadline may make acquisition succeed, while failure leaves the count unchanged. Then I would inspect lock contention and scheduling. Our simple monitor can wait past its deadline while acquiring or reacquiring the mutex, and a ready thread may still be delayed by the scheduler. Timed initial locking can bound that part of the attempt, but it does not create a hard return deadline. So I would distinguish an incorrectly renewed budget from legitimate lock or scheduling delay.","follow":"What should a zero timeout do when a permit is already available?","seconds":60,"lesson":"build-semaphore-and-latch","title":"Build it yourself: semaphore and latch","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"build-semaphore-and-latch.s3","q":"\"Why does opening a latch wake everyone, and why can its waiters safely read the results?\"","points":["Zero is a permanent latch predicate that enables every waiter, so opening broadcasts","Countdowns and predicate checks share one mutex; notifications alone do not publish data","Writes before countdown reach successful waiters through the mutex synchronization chain","A failed timed wait does not promise completed results, and later writes need separate coordination","Keep C++ synchronization objects alive until every caller has finished"],"model":"A latch reaching zero enables every waiter permanently, so the transition must wake everyone. Waking one can leave the others asleep forever because there may be no later notification. Visibility comes from synchronization, not from the wakeup itself. In our monitor, each worker writes its result before counting down under the common mutex. Unlock and later lock operations form a chain through the countdowns to the waiter's final locked check of zero. That makes those earlier writes visible after a successful wait, so three workers writing ten, twenty and thirty give each waiter a sum of sixty. A timed wait returning false does not promise complete results, and writes after countdown need separate coordination. For C++ I would also keep the latch alive until all callers finish. Notifying under the mutex closes one destruction race, but it does not replace ownership and joining.","follow":"Could you reset the count to reuse this latch safely while old waiters are still waking?","seconds":60,"lesson":"build-semaphore-and-latch","title":"Build it yourself: semaphore and latch","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"build-rw-lock-and-barrier.s1","q":"\"Build me a read-write lock. How do you keep a stream of readers from delaying a writer forever, and what does your policy give up?\"","points":["State the invariant: multiple readers or one writer, never both","Guard reader count, active writer flag and waiting writer count with one mutex","Block new readers once a writer registers, and wait in predicate loops","Explain last-reader signalling and writer handoff or reader broadcast","Explain cancellation cleanup and publication through the internal mutex","Distinguish writer preference from FIFO fairness and forbid recursive reads and upgrades"],"model":"I would use a writer-preferring lock: many readers can hold it together, or one writer can hold it alone. One internal mutex protects the reader count, the active writer flag and the waiting writer count. A reader waits if a writer is active or waiting. A writer registers before waiting for no readers and no active writer. Every wait rechecks its predicate after reacquiring the mutex. For example, if one reader is inside and a writer queues, a second reader waits instead of extending the read phase. The last reader signals a writer. A writer leaving signals another writer if one waits, otherwise it wakes all readers. A cancelled writer must undo its waiting count and wake readers if it was the last writer holding them back. The internal mutex also publishes protected writes through the logical unlock and next acquisition. The trade-off is that readers can starve under continuous writes. Individual writers can also be overtaken because this is not a FIFO queue. For bounded overtaking I would queue arrivals explicitly. I would forbid recursive reads and upgrades: a reader waiting behind a writer can otherwise hold the very read lock that prevents that writer from entering.","follow":"How would a timed writer acquisition avoid leaving readers asleep after it gives up?","seconds":90,"lesson":"build-rw-lock-and-barrier","title":"Build it yourself: read-write lock and reusable barrier","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"build-rw-lock-and-barrier.s2","q":"\"Your barrier works once but hangs on the next iteration. Walk me through a reusable design, including what happens if a party fails.\"","points":["Use an arrival count and a generation protected by one mutex","Capture the generation on arrival and wait for it to change","Have the last arrival run the action, reset the count, advance the generation and broadcast","Explain a fast next-round arrival versus a slow previous-round waiter","Explain publication and why an action after returning is not a barrier action","Define interruption, action failure and missing-party behaviour"],"model":"I would identify each round with a generation, rather than wait for a count that the last arrival resets. Each party records the current generation and increments the arrival count under one mutex. Unless it is last, it waits until that generation changes. The last arrival runs the action, resets the count, advances the generation and wakes everyone, still under the mutex. Suppose a fast party reaches round two before a slow party has returned from round one. The fast party records the new generation and waits. The slow party sees that its old generation has changed and returns. They cannot confuse the two releases. The mutex also publishes all work before arrival to the action, and the action finishes before anyone resumes. Selecting index zero after returning does not give that last guarantee. For failure, my Java version rejects an interrupt already set on entry. An interrupted waiter or failing action breaks the current barrier and wakes peers with failure. It stays broken. My C++ version requires a non-throwing action and no abandoned parties. A failure before arrival is invisible to either count: the coordinator needs cancellation, timed waits, or an explicit dropout protocol. I would define that policy before shipping it.","follow":"Why might reading everyone else's results after the barrier require a second barrier?","seconds":90,"lesson":"build-rw-lock-and-barrier","title":"Build it yourself: read-write lock and reusable barrier","track":"core","module":8},{"id":"why-thread-pools.s1","q":"\"Why would you use a thread pool instead of starting a thread for every request? What changes if we use Java virtual threads?\"","points":["Lead with reuse and a fixed bound on simultaneously executing worker tasks","Explain platform-thread creation and stack costs without a universal timing claim","Separate worker bounds from queued memory and full-queue policy","Qualify downstream limits for synchronous tasks versus asynchronous fan-out","Explain virtual-thread-per-task with separate dependency admission control","Name an owner responsible for shutdown and completion or cancellation"],"model":"I would use a fixed platform-thread pool to reuse workers and cap how many tasks execute on them. Creating a native thread for every small request adds stack and scheduling costs, and a traffic burst can exhaust thread resources. Reuse amortizes those costs, while a queue lets a short burst wait for capacity. But the worker count does not bound queued memory. I would set a queue capacity and decide whether overload rejects, blocks submission or runs work on the caller. Caller execution changes the concurrency bound, so that choice matters. A pool also limits database calls only when tasks hold their worker until each call completes. If tasks launch asynchronous calls and return, I need a separate limit around the dependency. With Java virtual threads, I would normally use one virtual thread per task instead of pooling them for reuse. They make blocking tasks cheaper to represent, but they do not create database connections or CPU capacity. I would still limit admission and use a semaphore for a scarce downstream resource. Finally, the pool or executor needs an owner. That owner stops new submissions and waits for completion or requests cooperative cancellation; dropping the reference is not a shutdown policy.","follow":"What would happen if every worker submitted another task to the same pool and waited for its result?","seconds":90,"lesson":"why-thread-pools","title":"Why thread pools exist","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"why-thread-pools.s2","q":"\"We receive 400 tasks a second, and each occupies a worker for 50 milliseconds. Would you choose 20 workers? Explain what you would measure.\"","points":["Compute 400 times 0.05 as 20 workers of average service demand","Distinguish worker occupancy from total response time including queue wait","Explain why 20 workers gives no spare capacity under the model","Check CPU and downstream limits before adding workers","Bound the queue and choose overload behaviour","Monitor queue age, occupancy, completions, rejections and latency under sustained load"],"model":"Twenty workers is the average occupancy estimate, not yet a safe configuration. Four hundred tasks per second times fifty milliseconds is twenty worker-seconds of service per second. With twenty workers, the ideal capacity is exactly four hundred tasks per second, so there is no spare capacity to drain a burst or absorb a slow task. I would first confirm what the fifty milliseconds measures. For worker sizing it must be time occupying a worker, including blocking within the task, but excluding time waiting in the pool queue. Total response time includes both. Then I would check the bottleneck. If the tasks are CPU bound on eight available logical CPUs, adding twenty workers cannot supply twenty cores of computation. If they mostly wait on a database, more workers may help until database or connection limits dominate, and added concurrency may itself increase service time. I would test a size with headroom, bound the queue and define rejection or backpressure. During sustained load I would track busy workers, completed throughput, queue depth and oldest task age, rejections and downstream latency. Growing queue age with all workers busy means we are not keeping up; a short successful benchmark does not establish stability.","follow":"If each task now takes 100 milliseconds, what happens to capacity with the worker count unchanged?","seconds":90,"lesson":"why-thread-pools","title":"Why thread pools exist","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"executor-anatomy.s1","q":"\"Walk me through what happens when I submit work to a pool with core 2, maximum 4 and a queue of 2. Can later work overtake earlier work?\"","points":["Count all workers, including idle workers; below core a submission tries to add a worker","After core, offer to the queue before growing toward max","For held tasks, submissions 1 and 2 create workers, 3 and 4 queue, 5 and 6 create workers","Submission 7 invokes rejection; the default throws rather than waits","New workers receive their triggering task directly, so queued FIFO is not global FIFO"],"model":"Yes, later work can overtake earlier work, because a new worker receives the task that caused its creation. Assume the pool is running, worker creation succeeds, and none of these tasks finishes during the burst. The first two submissions each create a core worker. The next two go into the queue. The fifth submission finds the queue full and creates a third worker, and the sixth creates a fourth. The seventh finds both limits reached, so the default policy throws a rejection exception. It does not wait for a queue slot. Tasks five and six can therefore start while three and four are still queued. FIFO describes removal from that queue, not the order of all task starts or completions. One subtlety is that core counts workers, including idle ones. Below core, a submission still tries to create a worker even if another worker is idle. I would document this policy explicitly in a C++ pool, because C++20 does not provide a standard executor with these rules.","follow":"What changes if I replace the queue with a <code>SynchronousQueue<\/code>?","seconds":75,"lesson":"executor-anatomy","title":"Inside an executor: the queue-before-max trap","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"executor-anatomy.s2","q":"\"Our maximum is 200, but only eight workers exist and latency keeps rising. What do you check, and how do you fix it?\"","points":["Inspect queue type and capacity, core size, queue age and worker-creation failures","Explain queue-before-max: an accepting queue prevents ordinary growth beyond core","Choose worker limits from CPU and dependency capacity rather than the configured maximum","Bound waiting and choose explicit rejection; core equal to max plus positive keepalive and core timeouts grows before queueing","Validate admitted throughput, queue age, tail latency and rejection during bursts and slowdowns"],"model":"My first suspicion is a queue that keeps accepting work, so the maximum never comes into play. I would check the actual queue type and capacity, core size, queue age, and whether worker creation is failing. Java normally offers to the queue after reaching core and only grows beyond core when that offer fails. An effectively unbounded queue can therefore leave eight workers serving a growing backlog. I would then establish the CPU budget and the concurrency the dependency can tolerate. If eight workers is right, I would bound waiting and reject overload. If more workers are safe, I could increase core or reduce queue capacity to trigger growth earlier. Setting core equal to max, with a positive keepalive and core timeouts, gives growth before queueing and retirement when idle. Raising max alone does not help. Queue capacity also needs a latency and memory budget: accepting work that cannot finish before its deadline wastes resources. I would choose a rejection policy explicitly and make rejection visible to callers and monitoring. Finally, I would test bursts and dependency slowdowns, measuring admitted throughput, queue age, tail latency and rejection together. More workers are successful only if useful completions improve within the resource budget.","follow":"If shutdown happens immediately after a task is enqueued, when may the executor reject that task?","seconds":90,"lesson":"executor-anatomy","title":"Inside an executor: the queue-before-max trap","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"sizing-pools.s1","q":"\"How do you decide how many threads a blocking service needs? Derive your starting point rather than quoting a magic number.\"","points":["Start with this workload's available CPU budget and independent CPU work near one worker per processor","Derive the worker CPU fraction C / (C + W) and N × U × (1 + W / C)","Exclude executor queue delay and distinguish I/O waiting from throttling or lock contention","Use admitted throughput times worker occupancy time for mean occupied workers","Check dependency ceilings and load test bounded admission against tail latency"],"model":"I start with the CPU budget available to this workload and the resources each request holds. Independent CPU work starts near one worker per available processor. For blocking work, suppose a worker computes for C and waits for W. It uses CPU for the fraction C divided by C plus W. Filling one processor therefore takes about one plus W over C workers. I multiply by the available CPU budget and my target utilization. I measure those times while a task owns a worker, excluding the executor queue. I also check why it is off CPU: a contended lock or CPU throttling is not spare capacity that more workers can fix. Then I cross check with admitted throughput times mean worker occupancy time. That gives occupied workers, whereas end-to-end latency includes queued requests too. Finally I calculate dependency limits, bound waiting and test the candidate size. The result is a starting point, not a promise about tail latency, and I choose the smallest pool that meets the target with headroom.","follow":"What changes if the requests use virtual threads or asynchronous I/O?","seconds":75,"lesson":"sizing-pools","title":"Sizing a pool","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"sizing-pools.s2","q":"\"You have eight cores, four milliseconds of CPU per request, forty milliseconds in the database and five in a cache. We need fifteen hundred requests a second, but the database supports sixty concurrent queries. What would you propose?\"","points":["Compute the ideal CPU ceiling of 2,000 requests per second and 75 percent CPU demand at the target","Compute 73.5 mean occupied workers and a full-CPU starting point of 98 workers","Compute 60 database queries in flight at the target and identify zero dependency headroom","Propose a measured worker candidate, separate database limit, bounded waiting and deadlines","Improve capacity or provisionally reduce admitted rate; recalculate occupancy and load test"],"model":"I would not promise fifteen hundred requests a second safely with that database budget. The CPU ceiling is eight divided by four milliseconds, or two thousand requests a second. So the requested traffic uses seventy-five percent of the ideal CPU budget. Each request holds a worker for forty-nine milliseconds, giving seventy-three and a half occupied workers on average. The full-CPU formula suggests ninety-eight, so around ninety-six is a reasonable candidate to test. The limiting calculation is the database: fifteen hundred times forty milliseconds is sixty concurrent queries, exactly the healthy limit, with no room for variability. I would enforce that sixty-query budget separately, bound the executor queue and permit waiting, and carry a deadline through the request. More workers cannot create database headroom. We need fewer database calls or more database capacity, or a lower admitted rate. At twelve hundred requests a second, mean database occupancy is forty-eight and mean worker occupancy is fifty-eight point eight. That may allow a smaller worker pool, which I would retest. I would measure tail latency during bursts and slowdowns, including cache behaviour, and include other process CPU work before calling this a production size.","follow":"What happens to your database budget when five service instances share the same database?","seconds":90,"lesson":"sizing-pools","title":"Sizing a pool","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"rejection-and-pool-failures.s1","q":"\"Your pool is full. How would you choose between rejecting, running on the caller, and dropping work?\"","points":["Choose from the work's loss tolerance and the submitting thread's responsibilities","Reject request work explicitly and use bounded, safe retries","Caller-runs slows producers but can stall event loops and exceed the worker concurrency limit","Drop only optional work, count drops and complete or cancel any abandoned future","Account for shutdown and distinguish waiting for capacity from executing on the caller"],"model":"I choose from two things: whether the work may be lost, and whether the submitting thread can afford to do it. For a request pool, I usually reject explicitly so the caller can degrade or retry within a budget. Retries need backoff and must be safe for that operation. For an internal producer that can slow down, caller-runs provides feedback by making that producer execute the task. I would avoid it on an event loop or acceptor, because one slow task would stall everything that thread serves. It also means the pool's worker count no longer bounds total executing tasks. For optional telemetry, dropping may be acceptable, but I count every drop. If a future was created, I cancel or fail it so nobody waits forever. Finally, I check shutdown behavior: Java's caller-runs policy silently discards after shutdown. None of the built-in policies waits for queue capacity. If I need timed admission, I design a separate admission gate with permit cleanup on every exit path.","follow":"How would your permit accounting handle a task cancelled before it starts?","seconds":75,"lesson":"rejection-and-pool-failures","title":"Bounded queues, rejection and the ways pools fail","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"rejection-and-pool-failures.s2","q":"\"All eight workers are waiting in get, the queue is growing, and CPU is almost idle. Walk me through your diagnosis.\"","points":["Treat pool self-deadlock as a hypothesis and trace the awaited futures to their producers","Explain eight parent tasks occupying all workers while their children remain queued","Compose without waiting, run suitable children inline, or separate acyclic stages","Do not treat a larger pool or arbitrary work stealing as a general proof of progress","Recover with external cancellation when supported and monitor queue age plus worker stacks"],"model":"My first hypothesis is pool self-deadlock, but I would confirm where those futures come from. If all eight workers run parent tasks, and each parent waits for a child queued in that same pool, the children have no worker available. The workers are the exhausted resource, so no application mutex cycle is necessary. I would trace the queued tasks and awaited futures to establish that dependency, rather than infer it from low CPU alone. The preferred fix is to compose the next step without blocking a worker. A cheap child can run inline, or separate pools can serve stages whose dependencies remain acyclic. Simply adding workers postpones the same failure at higher load. Fork/join helping is useful for suitable acyclic fork/join tasks, not arbitrary blocking calls. For recovery, an external control thread may cancel the parents or interrupt their waits, then clean up abandoned children. I would add queue-age alerts and bounded admission, while remembering that rejection exposes overload but does not itself break the deadlock.","follow":"Could two separate pools still deadlock each other?","seconds":75,"lesson":"rejection-and-pool-failures","title":"Bounded queues, rejection and the ways pools fail","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"rejection-and-pool-failures.s3","q":"\"A task threw, but our logs are empty and the pool is still running. What would you inspect?\"","points":["Identify whether a task wrapper captured the exception","Java FutureTask stores failures; inspect get or a completed future in afterExecute","C++ packaged_task stores failures; wrap the callable before packaging to report automatically","Raw exceptions escaping a C++ thread terminate the process","Report once and cover caller-runs or rejected work outside worker hooks"],"model":"I would first check how the task was submitted. A future wrapper can capture an exception without printing anything, so a healthy worker does not prove the task succeeded. In Java, submit normally creates a FutureTask. Its get exposes the failure through ExecutionException, while afterExecute usually receives no throwable and must inspect the completed future. A raw throwing Runnable passed to execute follows the uncaught exception path instead. In C++, packaged_task also stores the callable's exception. Catching around its invocation will not reveal that stored failure. I would either consume every future or wrap the callable before packaging it, report there, then rethrow for the future. A raw exception escaping a C++ thread terminates the process. Finally, I would avoid counting the same failure twice and cover caller-runs tasks separately, since worker hooks do not see them.","follow":"What happens to the future of a task discarded by Java's rejection handler?","seconds":60,"lesson":"rejection-and-pool-failures","title":"Bounded queues, rejection and the ways pools fail","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"futures-and-promises.s1","q":"\"Explain a future and a promise to a teammate. What makes reading the result safe?\"","points":["A future consumes a result and a promise completes their shared state once","Completion delivers a value or failure and publishes preceding writes","Later mutations still require synchronization","Java permits repeated Future.get; C++ future.get consumes its state and shared_future enables repeated reads","An abandoned unset C++ promise produces broken_promise; an uncompleted Java CompletableFuture can wait forever"],"model":"A future is the read handle for a result, and a promise is the producer's way to complete that result. They refer to one shared state that moves from incomplete to a value or a failure. A waiting consumer can then retrieve the outcome. Completion also publishes the producer's preceding writes, so a consumer that successfully gets a constructed result does not need an extra lock just for that handoff. It does need synchronization for later shared mutations. In Java, CompletableFuture combines the producer and consumer roles, while an executor can return the narrower Future interface. Java callers may get the result repeatedly. In C++, promise and future are separate types, and future.get consumes its state, even when it rethrows a stored exception. For several readers I use shared_future copies and keep the state alive while using a returned reference. There is also a lifecycle difference: destroying an unset C++ promise reports broken_promise. Forgetting to complete a Java CompletableFuture can leave readers waiting forever, so every producer path needs an outcome.","follow":"Does valid being true mean a C++ future is ready?","seconds":75,"lesson":"futures-and-promises","title":"Futures and promises","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"futures-and-promises.s2","q":"\"A request times out after one second. Can you guarantee its background work has stopped?\"","points":["Timeout ends a wait and does not stop or roll back work","Successful FutureTask.cancel(true) requests interruption; CompletableFuture.cancel does not interrupt","C++ futures need a separate stop mechanism and tasks must cooperate","Use one deadline across admission and multiple waits","Joining or async state destruction can delay return; unfinished work needs a longer-lived owner"],"model":"No. A timeout tells me the caller stopped waiting; it does not prove the producer stopped or undo its side effects. With a Java FutureTask, successful cancel with interruption enabled requests an interrupt, but a CPU loop that ignores interruption can continue after its future reports cancelled. CompletableFuture cancellation does not even interrupt the producer. In C++, a future has no cancellation operation, so I pass a stop token and make the task check it or use an operation that supports cancellation. I would propagate one deadline through admission and every wait, instead of spending a fresh second at each step. Cleanup matters too. A jthread joins when destroyed, and releasing the last unfinished asynchronous state from std::async can wait for its thread. Either can delay the response beyond the deadline. If the caller must return promptly, a longer-lived executor must own unfinished work and its captured data. I would bound that outstanding work and monitor cleanup, rather than detach a thread that might reference a dead stack frame.","follow":"What would you do if the downstream call ignores both interruption and cancellation?","seconds":75,"lesson":"futures-and-promises","title":"Futures and promises","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"futures-and-promises.s3","q":"\"One replica fails immediately, another succeeds, and a third is slow. Which result should an any operation return?\"","points":["Clarify first completion versus first successful completion","invokeAny skips failures while success remains possible and cancels unfinished tasks","CompletableFuture.anyOf can complete exceptionally first and does not cancel losers","C++20 has no standard when_any; a helper needs synchronized winner and failure accounting","Request stop outside shared-state locks and distinguish selection latency from joining losers"],"model":"I would first clarify whether any means the first completion or the first success. For a replicated read, I usually want the first success. Java's invokeAny ignores an early task failure while another can succeed, returns a successful result, and cancels unfinished tasks. CompletableFuture.anyOf has a different contract: an early failure can complete the combined future exceptionally, and it does not automatically cancel the losers. In C++20 there is no standard when_any, so I need a completion queue or shared state recording the first winner and how many tasks failed. If all fail, I report a failure. I request stop outside the result mutex, because stop callbacks can execute synchronously. Finally, selecting a winner is different from returning it. The helper in this lesson joins its threads before returning, so an uncooperative loser can still delay the caller. That ownership decision must be explicit.","follow":"How would you return the winner promptly while keeping losing tasks and their data alive safely?","seconds":60,"lesson":"futures-and-promises","title":"Futures and promises","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"composing-async.s1","q":"\"A page needs a user, that user's orders, and an independent theme. Walk me through the async pipeline and tell me where each step runs.\"","points":["Start user and theme independently; orders start only after user succeeds","Use thenCompose for an asynchronous dependent step and thenCombine for independent results","Keep dependency waits out of bounded pool workers to avoid starvation deadlock","Plain callbacks can run inline; choose explicit executors for expensive or blocking work","Bound fan-out and give the request a deadline and an error policy","C++20 std::future has no then or direct co_await support; use a callback/task library or explicit continuation passing"],"model":"I would start the user and theme requests together, then start orders only after the user succeeds. In Java, I use <code>thenCompose<\/code> for orders because that operation already returns a future. A plain map would give me a future containing another future. Once I have orders and theme, I combine their results into the page. If either required result fails, I return an explicit error page or a documented fallback.\nI avoid calling a pending future's get inside a worker. If every worker waits for a child queued in the same pool, none of those children can start. Composition represents the dependency without that wait.\nI also choose where the work executes. A plain continuation can run inline on a completing or attaching thread, so I keep it cheap. Parsing belongs on a CPU executor; blocking service calls need an appropriate I/O executor. An Async suffix still follows the supplied executor's policy.\nFinally, I bound outstanding calls and use one request deadline. Composition alone provides neither backpressure nor cancellation. In C++20, standard futures have no continuation API and are not directly awaitable. I would use a callback or coroutine task library, or post each next step explicitly to the pool. A helper that blocks another thread is only a small-scale demonstration.","follow":"What changes if a service returns a future that is already complete, and your parsing callback takes 200 milliseconds?","seconds":90,"lesson":"composing-async","title":"Composing async work: CompletableFuture and continuations","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"composing-async.s2","q":"\"We added a timeout and the page now returns quickly, but our connections are still exhausted. What would you check?\"","points":["Separate completion of the caller's future from cancellation of the underlying operation","Java timeout methods mutate the same future; use copy for an independent consumer deadline","C++ wait_until reports readiness without completing or cancelling the future","Apply transport deadlines and cooperative cancellation, with bounded admission","Account for cleanup waits, reference lifetimes and inline timeout callbacks"],"model":"I would first check whether the timeout only changed the result the caller sees. That can make page latency look healthy while the remote operations keep their connections and workers for seconds. In Java, completing a future with a timeout or fallback does not stop its supplier. Cancelling a CompletableFuture does not interrupt that supplier either.\nThose timeout methods also mutate the receiver. If several consumers share it, I would put a private timeout on a copy when they need separate deadlines. In C++, a timed wait is different again: it reports readiness and leaves the future's eventual result intact.\nThe resource fix is a deadline on the actual HTTP or database operation, plus cancellation that the operation honours. I would bound admitted calls so timed-out work cannot accumulate faster than it finishes, and pass the remaining request budget through successive stages.\nI would also inspect cleanup. Destroying a future from asynchronous std::async can wait for unfinished work, and pool destruction can join workers. Captured references must remain alive. Finally, expensive inline callbacks after a timeout can occupy the timer thread, so I would move that work onto an appropriate executor.","follow":"How would you race three replicas for the first successful response without failing just because the fastest replica returned an error?","seconds":82,"lesson":"composing-async","title":"Composing async work: CompletableFuture and continuations","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"build-thread-pool.s1","q":"\"You have a bounded queue and raw threads. Design a pool with submit, shutdown and shutdownNow, and explain the race between submitting and stopping.\"","points":["One lock guards queue capacity, lifecycle state and live-worker count","Acceptance and lifecycle transitions share that lock, defining which racing operation wins","Workers wait on a predicate, claim a task under the lock and execute outside it","FutureTask or packaged_task publishes results and task exceptions","Graceful shutdown drains; immediate shutdown removes unclaimed tasks and requests cooperative stopping","Removed futures need a terminal outcome; logical retirement differs from joining threads"],"model":"I would use one lock for the queue, lifecycle state and worker count. Submission checks that the pool is running and the queue has room, then inserts under that lock. Shutdown changes state under the same lock. That gives the race a precise answer: a submit that wins admission is accepted; a submit that sees the stopped state is rejected.\nWorkers wait until either work exists or the lifecycle changes. They claim a task under the lock, then release it before running user code. Otherwise one slow task would serialize the pool. Submitted work is wrapped in a FutureTask in Java or a packaged task in C++, so its value or exception reaches its caller. Raw execution needs its own failure policy.\nGraceful shutdown rejects new work, wakes idle workers and drains accepted tasks. Immediate shutdown removes unclaimed tasks and requests interruption or cooperative stop for work already claimed. A claimed task may enter user code after that request; stopping is not a thread kill.\nI also settle the removed futures. Here the caller cancels returned Java future tasks or destroys returned C++ wrappers. Workers count themselves out when they retire. Waiting for that count proves no more tasks will run; joining establishes physical thread exit. The owner must keep the pool and task captures alive through cleanup.","follow":"What if every worker submits a child to the same full queue and submission waits for room?","seconds":94,"lesson":"build-thread-pool","title":"Build it yourself: a thread pool with futures and shutdown","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"build-thread-pool.s2","q":"\"Our shutdown sometimes hangs on an empty queue and sometimes loses queued work. The worker loop checks a running flag before taking a task. Talk me through the fix.\"","points":["A flag change does not wake a worker already blocked on an empty queue","Exiting solely because the flag is false drops accepted queued work","Guard queue and lifecycle together; wait for work or a lifecycle change","Graceful shutdown broadcasts and exits only after draining; STOP removes queued work explicitly","Use controlled gates and bounded waits to test both races; cancellation still requires task cooperation"],"model":"I would fix the worker's wait predicate and exit predicate together. A visible flag is not enough. A worker already blocked inside an empty queue never gets back to the loop condition unless something wakes it. Conversely, a worker finishing a task can see the flag change and exit while accepted tasks are still waiting. That explains both symptoms.\nI would protect queue and lifecycle state with the same lock. A worker waits while the pool is running and the queue is empty, rechecking after every wakeup. Graceful shutdown changes state and wakes all idle workers. They keep taking queued work and retire only when the queue is empty. Immediate shutdown is a separate state: it removes unclaimed work and requests cooperative stopping of claimed work.\nTo test this, I would first stop an idle pool and check bounded completion. Then I would hold workers behind a controlled gate, enqueue known tasks, close admission and release the gate. Every accepted task must finish exactly once. A separate immediate-stop test checks removed futures and a cooperative running task. If a task ignores cancellation, termination can still time out; that is a task limitation, not a lost queue wakeup.","follow":"Can you preserve a non-waiting shutdown API if you implement shutdown by inserting poison pills into a full bounded queue?","seconds":84,"lesson":"build-thread-pool","title":"Build it yourself: a thread pool with futures and shutdown","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"scheduled-executors.s1","q":"\"Design a delayed task scheduler. What makes an earlier task wake it, and what does cancellation promise?\"","points":["Use a min-heap of monotonic deadlines with a sequence number for ties, protected by one lock","Wait in a predicate loop and signal when insertion creates a new earliest deadline","Remove a due job under the lock, then execute the callback outside it","Define the claim as the point after which cancellation cannot prevent that run","Use completion callbacks to re-arm periodic work without overlap when adding a worker pool","Define shutdown, admission limits and measurements of lateness"],"model":"I would use a min-heap of monotonic deadlines, guarded by one lock, with a sequence number to break ties. One worker waits until the head is due. If another thread inserts an earlier job, it signals the condition so the worker recomputes its wait. Every wakeup goes through the predicate loop, including a timeout or a spurious wakeup.\nWhen a job is due, I remove it under the lock and run its callback outside the lock. That removal commits the run. Cancellation before the claim prevents execution; cancellation after it cannot undo the claim. For a periodic job it can still prevent future runs. I would document that boundary rather than promise cancellation always wins.\nFor example, if I am waiting ten seconds and a job arrives due in one second, inserting into the heap alone is insufficient. The signal changes the wait.\nIf callbacks are slow, I would hand them to a bounded worker pool. Completion re-arms periodic work, so one registration cannot overlap itself. Shutdown rejects new work, drops pending jobs under our chosen policy, wakes the worker and joins it from an owning thread. I would measure deadline lateness separately from worker-queue delay, and bound admission so overload cannot grow memory indefinitely.","follow":"How would cancellation change if a job has left the timer heap but is still queued in the worker pool?","seconds":90,"lesson":"scheduled-executors","title":"Scheduled executors and timers","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"scheduled-executors.s2","q":"\"Our cleanup job stopped running, and our heartbeat sometimes runs in bursts. How would you investigate both?\"","points":["Check the periodic future for failure because one uncaught exception suppresses later runs","Observe and report recoverable failures, rather than silently swallowing every failure","Explain fixed rate from the planned deadline and fixed delay from completion","Use a 150 ms task with a 100 ms period to explain overdue runs without overlap","Choose fixed delay or an explicit skip policy for heartbeats and inspect worker saturation"],"model":"I would first inspect the cleanup job's scheduled future. In Java, an uncaught failure ends that periodic registration, while other tasks keep running. The future becomes done, and getting it exposes the cause. I would report the failure and catch recoverable exceptions inside the callback only if continuing is the intended policy.\nThe heartbeat bursts suggest fixed-rate catch-up. Fixed rate advances the previous planned deadline by one period. If a run takes a hundred and fifty milliseconds but the period is a hundred, the next run is already overdue when the first finishes. It becomes eligible immediately, but the same registration never overlaps itself.\nFixed delay instead waits a full interval after completion. That gives breathing room, although the schedule drifts relative to the original timetable. For heartbeats I would usually choose fixed delay, or explicitly skip missed fixed-rate ticks. Replaying old heartbeats does not repair a missed observation. Finally, I would check callback duration, pool saturation and process pauses: neither scheduling mode guarantees that an eligible job starts on time.","follow":"Does cancelling the future with interruption enabled guarantee that the running callback has stopped?","seconds":75,"lesson":"scheduled-executors","title":"Scheduled executors and timers","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"fork-join-and-parallel.s1","q":"\"Walk me through fork/join and work stealing. Why does joining a child usually work here when waiting in a fixed pool can deadlock?\"","points":["Split into independent ranges with a sequential cutoff, fork one half and compute the other before joining","Describe default owner LIFO access and stealing older tasks from the opposite deque end","Explain reduced contention and why older tasks often represent larger recursive branches","Explain cooperative joining and contrast it with every fixed-pool worker blocking on queued children","Limit the progress claim to suitable acyclic dependencies without blocking locks or unmanaged I/O","Distinguish a bounded C++ async recursion from a reusable work-stealing pool"],"model":"Fork/join divides one computation into independent pieces and combines their results. I stop splitting below a cutoff. Above it, I fork one half, compute the other on the current thread, and then join. That exposes parallel work while keeping the current worker useful.\nIn the default work-stealing policy, each worker pushes and pops at its own end of a deque, newest first. An idle worker takes older work from the opposite end. For recursive splitting, older work often represents a larger branch, so a steal gives the worker plenty to do. Opposite ends reduce contention, although the last item still needs synchronization.\nA joining worker can help execute the child or related work, and the pool can attempt compensation when a worker must wait. Compare that with a fixed pool where every worker blocks on a child future and all the children remain queued. There is nobody left to execute them.\nThis is not a general deadlock guarantee. A cycle between tasks, or a parent holding a lock its child needs, can still deadlock. Blocking network calls also consume workers. In C++, our capped async example uses separate threads and ordinary future waits; it demonstrates divide and conquer, but it does not implement work stealing.","follow":"What would change if the tasks were mostly waiting for HTTP responses rather than computing?","seconds":90,"lesson":"fork-join-and-parallel","title":"Fork/join, work stealing and parallel algorithms","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"fork-join-and-parallel.s2","q":"\"A teammate replaced a loop with a parallel reduction. It is slower and sometimes returns a different result. What do you check?\"","points":["Separate correctness from performance and inspect shared mutable state","Check associativity, neutral identity, floating-point regrouping and integer overflow","Use private partial results and completion synchronization before combining","Measure input size, splitting cost, cutoff, ordering costs and memory bandwidth against a sequential baseline","Inspect common-pool contention and unmanaged blocking; choose explicit execution when isolation matters","Use measured sequential work and Amdahl's law to set expectations rather than promising linear speedup"],"model":"I would investigate correctness first, then performance. A parallel reduction needs independent partial results and a valid combining operation. If workers update one shared counter or append to one ordinary list, I would remove that shared mutation. I would also check the algebra: subtraction is not associative, and a nonzero identity for addition can be contributed once per partition.\nDifferent floating-point results do not necessarily mean a race. Regrouping changes rounding. Integer sums need range checks too: Java wraps on overflow, while signed C++ overflow is undefined behaviour. I would keep the input stable, compute private partial results, and join before combining them.\nFor performance, I would compare against the original loop with representative input and a warmed-up JVM. Small tasks, expensive splitting, ordered operations and memory bandwidth can erase the benefit. I would vary the cutoff and worker count instead of assuming more tasks help.\nIn Java, I would also inspect common-pool contention and blocking calls. Unrelated parallel streams and async stages can compete there; isolation may justify an explicit executor. Finally, I would measure the sequential fraction. With ten percent sequential work, eight ideal cores give only about four point seven times speedup, before overhead. That gives me a realistic target for the experiment.","follow":"Would putting a mutex around every update fix both the result and the slowdown?","seconds":90,"lesson":"fork-join-and-parallel","title":"Fork/join, work stealing and parallel algorithms","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"virtual-threads-and-coroutines.s1","q":"\"We replaced our request pool with virtual threads, but the database now collapses at peak load. What would you check and change?\"","points":["Virtual threads reduce the cost of waiting, not the database's capacity","Removing the pool may have removed a coarse concurrency limit","Bound database concurrency separately from request admission and rate limits","Apply permit and I/O deadlines, and release permits on every exit","Check Java 21 pinning and isolate CPU work rather than adding more carriers"],"model":"I would first restore explicit limits around the database and request admission. Virtual threads make waiting cheaper; they do not let the database execute more queries. The old worker pool may have accidentally limited concurrent calls. Removing it can turn a burst into thousands of outstanding queries and parked requests, each retaining memory. I would measure safe database concurrency, then enforce that with a semaphore or connection pool. Permit acquisition needs a deadline, queries need their own timeout, and permits must be released even after failure. Admission control should bound the total waiting population and reject excess work early. If the dependency also has a requests-per-second limit, I need a rate limiter as well; a semaphore alone does not provide it. Next I would check Java 21 pinning around blocking calls using JFR, and move substantial computation to a bounded CPU executor. I would keep one virtual thread per admitted request, then load-test throughput, tail latency and memory. Increasing carrier count alone cannot repair an overloaded database.","follow":"What happens if the query ignores interruption after the caller times out?","seconds":75,"lesson":"virtual-threads-and-coroutines","title":"Virtual threads and coroutines","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"virtual-threads-and-coroutines.s2","q":"\"Compare a Java virtual thread with a C++20 coroutine. What would you review before putting either into a server?\"","points":["Virtual threads preserve an ordinary call stack and can unmount on supported blocking calls","C++20 coroutines preserve a frame and suspend at their own suspension points","The C++ library must supply scheduling, I/O and ownership; awaits may complete inline","Blocking calls, pinning and long computation can occupy shared runtime threads","Review lifetime across suspension, lock ownership, cancellation and resource limits"],"model":"A virtual thread lets me keep ordinary blocking code; a C++ coroutine needs an asynchronous protocol supplied by a library. In Java, a supported socket read can suspend a virtual thread deep inside ordinary helper calls. Its stack is preserved and its carrier can run another task. Java 21 has exceptions, including monitor pinning and some operations that retain a carrier. A C++20 coroutine instead preserves its own frame. It suspends at its own explicit or implicit suspension points, not inside an arbitrary ordinary helper. Helpers can still return awaitables without themselves being coroutines. The standard supplies coroutine machinery, but no general task type, scheduler or asynchronous socket API. An await can also complete immediately, so its presence does not prove that execution yielded. In review, I would look for blocking calls on event loops, lengthy CPU work, and locks held across suspension. I would check who owns the frame and every referenced object, who may resume it, and how cancellation removes pending work before destruction. Passing a temporary through a reference parameter is a classic dangling-reference bug. Finally, both designs need bounded admission, dependency limits and synchronization for shared mutable state. Cheap suspended tasks do not make memory or downstream capacity unlimited.","follow":"Why is holding a <code>std::mutex<\/code> guard across <code>co_await<\/code> unsafe even when the coroutine body looks sequential?","seconds":90,"lesson":"virtual-threads-and-coroutines","title":"Virtual threads and coroutines","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"build-a-future.s1","q":"\"Design a future that I can complete and attach callbacks to. Prove that a callback cannot be lost or run twice when registration races completion.\"","points":["Use one lock for done, outcome and pending callbacks, with one winning completion","Completion publishes the outcome and detaches callbacks in the same critical section","Registration either appends while pending or takes responsibility for immediate dispatch","Prove the two possible lock orderings and the single owner in each","Wake all getters and run callbacks outside the lock with failures propagated downstream"],"model":"I would protect the completion flag, outcome and callback list with one lock. The first completion wins; later attempts return false without changing the outcome. Completion writes the value or error, marks the future done, and detaches the pending list in one critical section. It wakes all waiting getters and runs the detached callbacks after releasing the lock. Registration uses that same lock for one decision: append if pending, otherwise run the callback after unlocking. There are only two relevant orderings. If registration acquires the lock first, its callback enters the list and only the completer owns dispatch. If completion goes first, registration observes done and never appends, so only the registrar owns dispatch. The broken version checks done and appends in separate critical sections, allowing completion to drain between them. I would also make getters wait in a predicate loop and propagate callback exceptions to dependent futures. The guarantee assumes dispatched callbacks terminate; a blocking first callback can still delay later ones. Thread safety does not by itself guarantee progress through arbitrary user code.","follow":"Can a getter return while a callback is still running, and does that violate your contract?","seconds":75,"lesson":"build-a-future","title":"Build it yourself: a future you can complete and chain","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"build-a-future.s2","q":"\"Your async chaining method sometimes returns a future that never finishes. Walk me through the failure paths you would investigate.\"","points":["Trace source outcome, callback registration, executor acceptance and task execution","Forward source failure even when the transformation is skipped","Convert executor rejection and transformation exceptions into dependent failures","Check dropped work, executor lifetime and blocking dependencies that exhaust workers","Distinguish a timed get from completion timeout and cooperative cancellation"],"model":"I would trace which event is supposed to complete the returned future, then check every path that can bypass it. First, if the source fails, the dependent must fail too. Implementing async chaining by attaching only a success transformation can skip that propagation and leave the result pending forever. Second, executor submission can reject work. That exception must complete the dependent exceptionally, just like an exception from the transformation itself. Third, an executor that silently discards accepted tasks breaks progress even when my callback code is correct. I need observable rejection and a shutdown policy for queued work. In C++, I would also check that the executor and captured inputs outlive execution; a borrowed pointer from a completion callback must not escape into delayed work. Then I would inspect blocking dependencies: a worker waiting for another task queued to the same exhausted pool can deadlock. A timed getter only stops that caller waiting; it neither completes the shared future nor stops the producer. A completion timeout changes the outcome, while cancellation still needs cooperation from the underlying work. I would test failed sources, rejected submissions and throwing functions, both before and after source completion, and make each test use a bounded wait.","follow":"What should <code>allOf<\/code> do after one input fails while another is still running?","seconds":90,"lesson":"build-a-future","title":"Build it yourself: a future you can complete and chain","track":"core","module":9},{"id":"blocking-queues.s1","q":"\"Design a bounded blocking queue with a timeout and graceful shutdown. Talk me through the races you have to get right.\"","points":["One mutex protects contents, capacity checks and the closed flag","Separate not-empty and not-full conditions recheck predicates after every wake","Timed waits consume one budget rather than restarting it; return timing is not hard real time","Close rejects new insertions, wakes both sets of waiters and lets accepted entries drain","The shared mutex orders insertion against close and publishes payload writes","Stop admission and join users before destruction; dequeue is not processing or durability acknowledgement"],"model":"I would use one mutex, two conditions and an explicit closed flag. The invariant is that the number of entries stays between zero and capacity. Producers wait while full, consumers wait while empty, and both predicates also check whether the queue has closed. Every wake rechecks under the mutex because another thread may already have used the slot or item.\nFor a timeout, I preserve one deadline or a decreasing wait budget. I do not restart a full timeout after each notification. Lock acquisition and scheduling can still delay the return, so this is not a hard real-time promise.\nClose takes the same mutex, marks the queue closed and wakes every producer and consumer. New puts fail, while takes drain accepted items and then return an end marker. If a put races with close, whichever changes the state first under the mutex determines whether that item was accepted. The mutex also publishes the producer's earlier payload writes to the consumer.\nFinally, I stop admission and join every user before destroying the queue. Removing an item only transfers responsibility. It does not prove that processing succeeded or that a log record is durable; those require a separate acknowledgement protocol.","follow":"What happens if every consumer fails while producers are blocked on a full queue?","seconds":90,"lesson":"blocking-queues","title":"Blocking queues","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"blocking-queues.s2","q":"\"Our service gets slower during a burst, but adding queue capacity seems to help briefly. What would you check, and which queue policy would you choose?\"","points":["Compare sustained arrival and drain rates; extra capacity cannot fix a throughput deficit","Inspect depth, oldest-item age, service latency and rejection rate","Choose a finite bound from memory and latency budgets, with workload measurements","Use immediate rejection on event loops or timed admission on suitable request threads","Separate queue receipt from completion, including synchronous hand-off"],"model":"I would first check whether arrivals exceed the workers' drain rate. A larger queue can absorb a short burst, but it cannot fix a sustained throughput deficit. It stores more waiting work, which can hide overload until latency or memory becomes unacceptable.\nI would compare queue depth and oldest-item age with worker service times and rejection rate. A slow dependency or a failed consumer can look like too few workers, so adding threads is not automatically the fix.\nI would choose a finite capacity from the memory and queueing budgets, then validate it with realistic bursts and service-time variation. For example, at a steady four hundred completions a second, a hundred queued requests represent roughly a quarter-second of work, not a tail-latency guarantee.\nOn a network event loop I would reject when immediate admission fails. A dedicated request thread could wait within its remaining deadline. A zero-capacity hand-off is another choice when buffering is undesirable, but even that only confirms that a consumer received the job. The caller needs a separate completion signal if it must know the work succeeded.","follow":"When could a two-lock linked queue improve throughput, and what shared state would still contend?","seconds":75,"lesson":"blocking-queues","title":"Blocking queues","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"concurrent-hash-maps.s1","q":"\"We changed HashMap to ConcurrentHashMap, but our request counters still lose increments. Explain what is wrong and how you would fix it.\"","points":["Thread-safe individual key operations do not make get followed by put atomic","Give an interleaving where two reads of 41 both produce 42","Use Java merge or a C++ stripe lock around the complete read-modify-write","Distinguish mapping safety from mutable-value safety and counter lifetime","Explain LongAdder's contention benefit, approximate concurrent sum and removal race","Use a wider synchronization protocol for strict resets or multi-key invariants"],"model":"The map protects each key operation, but it does not combine a get and a later put into one increment. Two request threads can both read forty-one, both calculate forty-two and both store forty-two. One request has disappeared from the count even though the map itself is healthy.\nFor an exact per-user transition, I would use <code>merge<\/code> with an immutable numeric value in Java. In C++, I would hold that user's stripe lock across the lookup, addition and replacement. I would also define overflow behaviour. Different stripes can still progress independently.\nThe map only protects the mapping. If the value is a mutable list or counter, changes to that object need their own synchronization. For a very hot Java counter, a <code>LongAdder<\/code> can spread increment contention, but its concurrent sum is not an atomic snapshot. That is a useful monitoring tradeoff, not a strict quota mechanism.\nLifetime matters too. If a reset removes the adder after a request fetched it, that request may increment an object no longer in the map. For exact resets, I would order reset and increment through the same per-key protocol. If an invariant spans two users, I need a wider protocol that covers both keys and every reader of that invariant.","follow":"How would you get an exact total across every user while requests are still arriving?","seconds":90,"lesson":"concurrent-hash-maps","title":"Concurrent hash maps","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"concurrent-hash-maps.s2","q":"\"Build me a concurrent hash map in C++. Why is putting a lock around each key not enough?\"","points":["Use fixed stripes, each owning its mutex and independent unordered_map","Hash and equality must stay stable; protect full per-key operations including rehash","Several locks around one ordinary shared map do not isolate shared structural state","Return copied values or an explicit guarded handle; account for shared pointees","Define approximate size versus exact snapshots and ordered distinct-stripe locking","Measure skew, hot keys and callback time; keep I/O outside stripe locks"],"model":"I would start with a fixed array of stripes, each owning a mutex and its own unordered map. A stable hash selects the stripe. Get, put, remove and any compound update hold that stripe's lock for their complete operation, including any rehash of that local table.\nThe separate tables matter. Several key locks around one ordinary unordered map do not protect shared bucket management and size changes during insertion. Reserving capacity does not fix that. I also require stable key equality and hashing, with key functions that are safe to call concurrently.\nMy simple get returns a value copied under the lock. A returned iterator or reference would need an explicit lifetime and synchronization contract, and copying a shared pointer still leaves its pointee shared.\nFor size, summing stripes one at a time is only an estimate while writers run. An exact snapshot requires holding every stripe lock together. Two-key operations similarly lock the distinct affected stripes in a global order, taking a shared stripe only once. Readers needing the same invariant must follow that protocol too.\nFinally, I would measure key distribution and callback time. More stripes help independent keys, not one hot key. Slow database work belongs outside the locks, possibly coordinated through a stored future.","follow":"What would changing the number of stripes require while operations are running?","seconds":90,"lesson":"concurrent-hash-maps","title":"Concurrent hash maps","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"copy-on-write-and-snapshots.s1","q":"\"We reload routing rules while requests are running. How would you keep each request on one consistent version?\"","points":["Publish one fully constructed immutable routing snapshot","Capture the reference once per request and retain ownership through the read","Use Java volatile publication or correctly synchronized C++ shared ownership","Serialize the entire writer update or retry CAS without external side effects","State stale-version, copy-cost and C++ blocking trade-offs"],"model":"I would publish one immutable routing snapshot and have each request capture it once. The request uses that same object for every routing decision, so it cannot combine an old host with a new port. The writer builds the replacement privately, validates it, and publishes one reference. In Java I can use a volatile field. In C++ I need synchronized shared ownership, using atomic shared-pointer operations where supported or a short mutex around acquiring the pointer. I keep that owner alive for the entire read. Atomic shared ownership is not guaranteed to be lock-free. If two administrators update rules, I serialize the whole copy, modify and publish sequence, or use a compare-and-swap retry loop with no external side effects. Otherwise one administrator can overwrite the other's change. The trade-off is that a request may keep an older version until it finishes, and each update copies data. I would check that staleness is acceptable and measure allocation and retained memory before choosing this for a large table.","follow":"What changes if returning from reload must guarantee that no request still uses the old backend?","seconds":75,"lesson":"copy-on-write-and-snapshots","title":"Copy-on-write and immutable snapshots","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"copy-on-write-and-snapshots.s2","q":"\"Our snapshot store uses version numbers. Can we delete every entry older than the oldest open snapshot?\"","points":["Reject the rule because a snapshot can need a value written before its own version","Give a concrete predecessor-version example","Retain the newest entry at or before the oldest snapshot plus all newer entries","Coordinate snapshot registration and reclamation, including tombstones","Publish complete batches in commit order and bound retained history"],"model":"No. A snapshot can need a value that was written much earlier than its version number. Suppose a key changed at version three and then at version twelve. An open snapshot at version ten must still read the version-three value. Deleting everything below ten would silently lose it. For each key I retain the newest entry at or before the oldest open snapshot, plus every newer entry. Deletes need tombstones so older values do not reappear. Capturing and registering a snapshot must be coordinated with the cleaner; otherwise cleanup can remove its history before registration finishes. I also serialize commit publication so a later batch cannot expose an earlier unfinished batch. The simplest implementation uses a commit mutex for installation, registration and history access, with no lock held during the analyst's whole session. That is correct but does not promise nonblocking operations. Finally, I monitor snapshot age and retained bytes, because one forgotten snapshot can pin a large history. The choice is to budget for that retention or explicitly limit snapshot lifetimes.","follow":"How would you ensure a failed multi-key batch cannot become visible when a later version commits?","seconds":75,"lesson":"copy-on-write-and-snapshots","title":"Copy-on-write and immutable snapshots","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"concurrent-caches.s1","q":"\"Your LRU works with one thread. Make it thread-safe, then tell me what you would change if the lock became hot.\"","points":["A cache hit changes recency, so a shared read lock is insufficient","One mutex protects membership, recency and capacity together","Return an owned value and keep backend loading outside the lock","Shard only after measuring, accepting per-shard eviction and skew","Separate safe data lookup from optional policy updates before skipping recency","Measure latency and hit rate instead of assuming a shard-count speedup"],"model":"I would start with one mutex protecting the map, recency list and capacity together. A cache hit moves an entry, so it is a write to the list even though the method is called get. A shared read lock would let several threads rewrite those links at once. I return a copied value or immutable owned object, never a list reference that eviction could invalidate. The lock covers lookup and the policy update; backend loading and eviction listeners run outside it. Hashing, allocation and copying still have a cost inside that section. If measurements show contention, I would shard by a stable key hash and split the capacity budget. That gives per-shard LRU, so skew can waste capacity and a hot key remains a bottleneck. If policy updates still dominate, I would separate safe value access from approximate recency events. Skipping a policy update is acceptable; reading an ordinary map after failing its lock is not. I would compare latency, hit rate and backend load before accepting the change, without assuming a speedup equal to the shard count.","follow":"How would your capacity invariant change if entries had very different sizes?","seconds":75,"lesson":"concurrent-caches","title":"Thread-safe caches: LRU, TTL and single-flight","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"concurrent-caches.s2","q":"\"A popular key expires and hundreds of callers miss. Design the load path, including failures, timeouts and invalidation.\"","points":["Atomically install a per-key future before starting the load","Run the loader outside map locks and let followers await the same result","Publish cached success before flight removal and recheck after winning a separate flight","Complete failures and remove only the matching flight so later calls can retry","Keep caller deadlines separate from the shared future and bound backend work","Reject stale fills with a retained generation and address recursive loads"],"model":"I would atomically install a future for the key before starting the load. The winner loads outside the map lock, and followers await the same future, so unrelated keys can progress. If cached values and flights live in separate maps, the winner rechecks the value cache: another load may have finished between its original miss and winning this flight. It stores a successful value before completing and removing the flight. Alternatively, the cache can retain successful futures, with an explicit eviction policy. On failure I complete the future exceptionally and remove only my flight, releasing existing waiters and allowing a later retry. A caller deadline should stop that caller waiting, not fail the shared result for everyone. In Java I use timed get or a dependent future, not <code>orTimeout<\/code> on the shared future. The loader needs its own backend deadline because waiting less does not stop the work. For invalidation, I retain a generation and accept a fill only if that generation still matches; deleting the cached value alone loses that protection. I also reject recursive same-key loads, which would wait on their own result, and bound active distinct-key loads so many different misses cannot overwhelm the backend.","follow":"What happens if a loader pauses past a distributed refresh lease and then tries to publish?","seconds":90,"lesson":"concurrent-caches","title":"Thread-safe caches: LRU, TTL and single-flight","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"concurrent-caches.s3","q":"\"Users see expired values and memory keeps growing. What do you check in a TTL cache?\"","points":["Define expire-after-write versus expire-after-access and the freshness instant","Use a monotonic clock and check expiry under synchronization","Separate logical expiry from physical reclamation","Use expiry ordering or a complete incremental scan rather than assuming LRU order","Bound storage and make cleaner shutdown and deadline changes explicit"],"model":"I would first clarify whether TTL starts at write time or extends on every access, and whether freshness is checked at lookup or promised for longer. For the usual lookup contract, I use a monotonic clock and check the deadline while protecting the entry. An expired entry is a miss even if it is still physically stored. That distinction explains the memory symptom: lazy expiry only cleans keys that are requested again. I would add bounded capacity and scheduled or incremental cleanup. For expiry after write, the recency list is not sorted by deadline, because reads move entries without changing their expiry. A cleaner therefore needs a separate expiry order or a scan that eventually covers every entry. I would also check that new earlier deadlines wake the cleaner and that shutdown wakes and joins it. Tests would inject a controllable clock and cover the exact expiry boundary, replacements and unread expired entries.","follow":"Can a value become expired after get checks its deadline but before the caller receives it?","seconds":65,"lesson":"concurrent-caches","title":"Thread-safe caches: LRU, TTL and single-flight","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"concurrent-iteration.s1","q":"\"Why can a thread-safe map give me the wrong total when I iterate it, and what would you change?\"","points":["Thread-safe entry reads do not make a whole traversal an atomic snapshot","Read a source before a transfer and a destination after it to overcount","Choose weak consistency for approximate metrics and an exact snapshot for invariants","Capture under a lock used by every relevant writer, then process outside it","A copy of a concurrent map or a shallow copy of mutable values is not automatically an exact value snapshot"],"model":"The map can make each access safe without making the whole report describe one moment. Suppose Alice and Bob each have a hundred. I read Alice, then a writer moves thirty from Alice to Bob, then I read Bob. I report two hundred and thirty although the intended total is two hundred. Every individual read can be valid and the report still be wrong. For approximate monitoring, I would document that moving view and keep the concurrent iterator. For an exact report, I would capture the data under a lock that every relevant writer also uses, then release it before formatting or doing I/O. The transfer itself must follow that same protocol if both balances form one invariant. Merely constructing a new map from a concurrent map does not help: the copy can collect the same mixed observations. I would also check whether the values are immutable. A shallow copy of mutable accounts freezes their references, not their balances. The contract has to say exactly which state the snapshot freezes.","follow":"How would you avoid taking one global lock if the data is divided into stripes?","seconds":75,"lesson":"concurrent-iteration","title":"Iterating while others write","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"concurrent-iteration.s2","q":"\"Design listener notification while other threads register and unregister. What does unregister returning guarantee?\"","points":["Copy the registry under its lock and invoke listeners after releasing it","Unknown callbacks can block, re-enter or wait for a thread needing the registry lock","An in-flight snapshot can still invoke a removed listener after unregister returns","A stronger removal contract needs coordinated active-call accounting and a wait protocol","Keep listeners alive through the callback and synchronize their mutable state separately"],"model":"I would copy the listener registry under its lock and call listeners after releasing the lock. That keeps registry updates independent of slow callbacks. It also avoids a deadlock if a callback waits for another thread that needs the registry lock. The basic unregister contract is that future snapshots exclude that registration. It does not promise that no callback occurs after unregister returns, because an earlier notification may already hold a snapshot containing it. If the caller needs that stronger guarantee, I would track each registration's enabled state and active calls. Starting a callback and disabling the registration must coordinate; unregister can then wait for active calls to finish without holding the registry lock. Self-unregistration needs a special rule so a callback never waits for itself. In Java the snapshot retains references; in C++ I would retain shared ownership or another explicit lifetime guarantee through invocation. Neither choice makes the listener's own mutable fields thread-safe. I would document removal and callback concurrency separately before implementing the stronger contract.","follow":"What happens if one listener throws, or two notifications invoke the same listener concurrently?","seconds":75,"lesson":"concurrent-iteration","title":"Iterating while others write","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"linearizability.s1","q":"\"Two enqueues overlap, and the later caller's item comes out first. Is my queue broken? Walk me through the rule.\"","points":["Overlapping enqueues may linearize in either order; invocation order alone does not define FIFO","Each completed operation needs a point inside its call interval and the results must fit the sequential specification","A completed enqueue preceding another invocation must be ordered first","Sequential consistency preserves thread order but omits cross-thread real-time constraints","Linearizability is a safety property and does not guarantee fairness or completion"],"model":"That result can be correct because overlapping enqueues can take effect in either order. FIFO means dequeue returns the oldest item in the legal insertion order, not necessarily the item whose caller arrived first. I would draw each call from invocation to response and look for one point inside each interval. If enqueue one runs from time zero to four and enqueue two runs from one to five, I can place two first and one second. A dequeue starting at six can then return two. But if enqueue one had returned before enqueue two was even called, real time would force one first, so returning two as the first item would be wrong. The chosen order must explain every result in the history together. Sequential consistency also needs a legal order and preserves each thread's own order, but it drops that cross-thread real-time constraint. Finally, this says which results are allowed, not how quickly operations finish. A queue can be linearizable and still have poor fairness or block indefinitely.","follow":"Can a dequeue return an item whose enqueue has not returned yet?","seconds":75,"lesson":"linearizability","title":"Linearizability: what correct means","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"linearizability.s2","q":"\"Your queue passed a stress test and ThreadSanitizer. How would you convince me it is linearizable?\"","points":["Tests and absence of reported data races do not prove linearizability","State the sequential specification and representation invariant","Identify success, empty, timeout and cancellation outcomes with points inside their intervals","For one shared lock, exclusion and visibility make the chosen point order legal","Record short histories and search legal orders against an independent sequential model","Handle pending calls and test progress separately"],"model":"I would give a correctness argument as well as the test results. First I would state the contract: a bounded FIFO queue, with explicit rules for empty, full, cancellation and close. Then I would state the invariant: the deque contains exactly the inserted items not yet removed, in insertion order, with its size within capacity, and every access follows the same locking protocol. Successful put takes effect at insertion and take at removal. A timed-out offer takes effect at the final full check after its wait budget expires. A closed, empty take takes effect at its locked check. Cancellation linearizes at detection without changing queue state; it is a permitted outcome, not a discarded call. Each point lies inside its call interval. The common mutex excludes competing observations during the update and publishes the state to later lock holders, so these points produce a legal FIFO order. For testing, I would record short invocation and response histories and search for a legal ordering against an independent sequential queue. Pending calls need special handling because they may already have taken effect. ThreadSanitizer checks exercised memory races, not the FIFO specification. Finally I would test termination and cancellation separately, because linearizability alone promises neither fairness nor bounded waiting.","follow":"Why is the order in which methods return not necessarily their linearization order?","seconds":90,"lesson":"linearizability","title":"Linearizability: what correct means","track":"core","module":10},{"id":"deadlock.s1","q":"\"Two transfers lock the source account and then the destination. Why can this freeze, and how would you fix it?\"","points":["Show the cycle: one transfer holds A and wants B; the other holds B and wants A","Map mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait to those locks","Choose a global order using unique immutable ids and explain why a cycle cannot close","Handle same-account calls and equal ordering keys explicitly","Explain the scope of C++ scoped_lock and Java try, release and retry","Distinguish voluntary release from preemption, and deadlock freedom from starvation freedom"],"model":"I would put every transfer under one global lock order. The failure is that the first transfer holds account A and wants B, while the second holds B and wants A. Neither can reach the code that releases its first lock. The four conditions are all present: the locks are exclusive, each thread holds one while waiting for another, ownership cannot be forcibly taken away, and the waits form a circle. I would acquire the lower immutable account id first on every path. Every acquisition then moves upward in the same order, so a cycle cannot close. The ids must be unique; a hash needs a tie rule. I would return early for a same-account transfer, especially in C++, where taking the same ordinary mutex twice is undefined behavior. For two distinct C++ mutexes I could also use <code>std::scoped_lock<\/code>, provided I do not bring an outside lock into the operation. In Java, another option is to try the second lock and release the first on failure. That is voluntary release, not preemption. Random backoff reduces collisions, but does not guarantee fairness or bounded completion. I would keep both locks through balance validation and the update, and avoid callbacks or blocking work inside that critical section.","follow":"What if a callback inside the transfer acquires a third lock?","seconds":90,"lesson":"deadlock","title":"Deadlock: the four conditions and how to break each","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"deadlock.s2","q":"\"All eight workers are waiting in future get, and their queue contains the child jobs they need. No mutex cycle is reported. What do you check and change?\"","points":["Identify pool self-deadlock only after confirming the queued children complete those futures","Trace parent holds worker, parent waits for child, child needs a free worker","Explain why low-load tests and a negative monitor detector can miss it","Prefer asynchronous composition or an independent child execution resource","Limit fork/join to appropriate acyclic tasks and explain timeout cleanup"],"model":"This looks like a pool self-deadlock. I would first confirm that the queued children are the tasks that complete those eight futures, and that nothing outside the pool can complete them. Each parent occupies a worker while waiting for its child. Every child needs a worker, and all workers are occupied by waiting parents. That is the cycle, even though no mutex is involved. A monitor deadlock detector will not reconstruct it, and low-load tests may pass because spare workers run the children. I would have the parents return without blocking and assemble their results in continuations when the children complete. A separate child pool can also work if it has no dependency back on the blocked parent pool. For structured, acyclic CPU tasks, fork/join is another option; moving arbitrary blocking code to a work-stealing pool is not a general fix. I would add an overall deadline and explicitly cancel abandoned work where supported. A timed future wait does not cancel the child by itself, and adding more workers only moves the saturation point.","follow":"Would replacing every worker with a virtual thread eliminate all of these dependency cycles?","seconds":75,"lesson":"deadlock","title":"Deadlock: the four conditions and how to break each","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"deadlock-avoidance-and-detection.s1","q":"\"The resources are available, but your Banker rejects the request. Walk me through why that can be correct.\"","points":["Availability is an immediate capacity check; safety requires a completion order for all declared maxima","Check request against remaining need, then available resources","Tentatively update available, allocation and derived need under one allocator lock","Trace Work and explain why simulated completion adds Allocation, not Max","Grant only if every participant can finish; otherwise restore state and wait","Distinguish unsafe from already deadlocked and state the known-maxima limitation"],"model":"Availability is necessary, but it is not enough. Banker grants a request only if the resulting state still has an order in which every participant could obtain its declared maximum and finish. I first check that the request stays within the caller's remaining need, then within the currently available resources. I tentatively grant it and run the safety check under the allocator's lock. Work starts at the new available vector. Whenever an unfinished participant's remaining need fits, I pretend it finishes and add its current allocation back to Work. I add allocation, not maximum, because the extra resources it temporarily borrows are returned too. If everyone can finish, I commit the grant. If nobody else fits before all participants finish, I restore the tables and make the requester wait. For example, handing out the last A may leave every participant needing another A, even though the request itself fitted. That is unsafe, not necessarily already deadlocked: some participants might finish below their maximum. The tradeoff is that Banker needs credible maximum claims in advance and can delay requests conservatively.","follow":"How would your test change if you wanted to detect a current multi-instance deadlock rather than avoid a future one?","seconds":75,"lesson":"deadlock-avoidance-and-detection","title":"Deadlock avoidance and detection","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"deadlock-avoidance-and-detection.s2","q":"\"Production has stopped completing requests, but the Java deadlock detector returns null. Does that rule out deadlock? What is your next move?\"","points":["A null result excludes only supported detected cycles, not every liveness failure","State Java 21 platform-thread, monitor and ownable-synchronizer limits","Collect stacks plus lock owners, pool saturation, queue depth and future dependencies","Build a consistent dependency graph and distinguish a cycle from ordinary waiting","Explain C++ owner-instrumentation limits and the role of repeated observations","Use deadlines with cleanup; do not forcibly terminate an arbitrary lock holder"],"model":"No. A null result means that detector found no cycle it supports. In Java 21, <code>ThreadMXBean<\/code> detects platform-thread cycles involving monitors and ownable synchronizers. It does not detect cycles involving virtual threads, or infer which queued task will complete a future. I would capture platform-thread stacks with lock details, and use the virtual-thread dump facility when relevant. I would also collect active worker counts, queue depth and the tasks responsible for completing blocked requests. Then I would connect each wait to the owner or producer that can satisfy it. Eight parents waiting for children queued behind them can be a deadlock even with no mutex cycle. For a lock cycle, I need consistent ownership and wait edges; a thread parked in one stack frame alone proves little. Repeated captures help distinguish ordinary waits from persistent dependencies, but do not replace that graph. In C++, stack traces locate waits, while owner instrumentation may be needed because standard mutexes expose no owner query. For recovery I would use existing cooperative cancellation and cleanup paths if they are safe. A deadline only ends a wait; it does not roll back changes or release other locks automatically. Without a safe recovery path, I would capture evidence, restart and fix the dependency.","follow":"What could still block after a C++ future wait has timed out?","seconds":90,"lesson":"deadlock-avoidance-and-detection","title":"Deadlock avoidance and detection","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"deadlock-avoidance-and-detection.s3","q":"\"PostgreSQL aborts one of our money transfers as a deadlock victim. How would you stop that becoming a failed or duplicated payment?\"","points":["Describe opposite row-lock acquisition and PostgreSQL selecting a victim","Acquire account rows in a stable order and keep transactions short","Roll back and retry the entire transaction with fresh reads and a bounded deadline","Keep external side effects idempotent or behind an outbox","Distinguish a deadlock error from a generic timeout and avoid relying on victim choice"],"model":"I would combine consistent row-lock ordering with a bounded retry policy for the entire transaction. Two transfers can lock their source rows first, then each wait for the other's destination row. PostgreSQL detects the cycle and aborts a victim; I would not depend on which transaction loses. I would lock both account rows in ascending immutable id order before validating balances and applying the transfer. I would keep that transaction short and avoid remote calls while holding the locks. If a deadlock error still occurs, I would roll back and retry from fresh reads, with jitter, a retry limit and one overall deadline. Retrying only the failed statement is not enough because the transaction was aborted. Database rollback also does not undo an email or an external payment already sent. Those effects need an idempotency key or a transactional outbox so another attempt cannot duplicate them. Finally, I would classify errors carefully: a deadlock error establishes a detected cycle, whereas a generic timeout could mean ordinary overload. The retry budget must prevent recovery traffic from making that overload worse.","follow":"How does InnoDB's default handling of a lock wait timeout differ from its handling of a detected deadlock?","seconds":75,"lesson":"deadlock-avoidance-and-detection","title":"Deadlock avoidance and detection","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"livelock-and-starvation.s1","q":"\"The service is busy but barely finishing requests. How would you distinguish deadlock, livelock and starvation?\"","points":["Distinguish blocked dependencies, useless repeated reactions and one request losing while others finish","Use repeated stacks, ownership, retry counts and completion counts rather than CPU alone","Give a concrete symmetric retry sequence for livelock","Choose ordering or jitter with a retry budget, without promising success from randomness","Measure individual wait age and state fairness assumptions"],"model":"I would classify the failure by progress and dependencies, not CPU alone. Deadlock means participants cannot resolve their dependencies. Livelock means they keep reacting but complete no useful work. Starvation means other work finishes while one request keeps losing. I would compare several thread dumps with lock ownership, retry counters and completion counters. A blocking deadlock often uses little CPU, but a spinlock cycle can keep a core busy, and backoff can make livelock look quiet. For example, two threads each take one lock, fail to take the other, release and retry together. A consistent blocking lock order removes that retry cycle. Randomized backoff reduces repeated collisions, but I would release held locks first and cap retries with a deadline; randomness is not a success guarantee. If only one tenant is stuck, I would inspect admission order and oldest pending request age. FIFO or bounded batches can prevent overtaking, assuming holders finish and eligible threads run. I would verify individual completions as well as aggregate throughput before calling the issue fixed.","follow":"Could a lock-free algorithm still starve one request?","seconds":75,"lesson":"livelock-and-starvation","title":"Livelock, starvation and fairness","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"livelock-and-starvation.s2","q":"\"Your playground serves one team at a time. Prove that nobody starves, then tell me what fairness costs.\"","points":["Separate team fairness from fairness for each player","Use FIFO team turns, FIFO within teams and bounded admissions when another team waits","Explain the drain and finite-predecessor argument","Distinguish an admission bound from a time bound and state scheduling assumptions","Describe batching versus strict FIFO and the sleeping-next-waiter cost","Handle cancellation without leaving a blocking queue position"],"model":"I need fairness both between teams and within each team. Rotating teams alone does not stop new teammates from repeatedly passing one older player. I would keep FIFO queues of players, queue each waiting team once, and admit eligible queue heads whenever capacity remains. I would choose a positive batch limit K. When another team is waiting, the current team gets at most K further admissions, then admissions stop until everyone inside leaves. The next waiting team takes its turn. A team that still has players queues again behind other waiting teams. For a particular player, there are finitely many players ahead in their own queue and finitely many turns ahead of each service opportunity. Later arrivals cannot bypass those positions. Assuming every visit finishes and eligible threads get CPU time, that player eventually enters. This bounds overtaking, but a time bound also needs limits on visit duration and scheduling delay. Strict global FIFO is easier to reason about but can leave spare capacity unused when the head belongs to another team. Bounded batches can improve utilization, while a descheduled next player can still stall admission. I would remove cancelled waiters under the queue lock and advance the turn when necessary. The final check is per-player waiting age and completions under continuous competing arrivals, alongside throughput.","follow":"What changes if one player is allowed to stay inside forever?","seconds":90,"lesson":"livelock-and-starvation","title":"Livelock, starvation and fairness","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"priority-inversion-and-convoys.s1","q":"\"The highest priority thread is missing deadlines while a medium priority thread runs. Walk me through the inversion and choose a fix.\"","points":["H blocks on a mutex held by L, allowing runnable M to preempt L on one CPU","Explain why medium work extends blocking beyond the critical-section execution time","Donate priority to the owner and propagate through supported lock dependencies","Recompute donations on release and distinguish inheritance from immediate ceilings","State that deadlock, I/O and unbounded work survive a priority boost","State portable Java and C++ limits and evaluate remaining data dependencies"],"model":"I would check whether the urgent thread is blocked on a resource held by a lower priority thread. On one CPU with strict priorities, L takes the mutex, H arrives and blocks, then runnable M preempts L. M never needs the mutex, but it prevents L from releasing it. That extends H's wait beyond the time needed for L's protected work. With priority inheritance, H donates its priority to L while the dependency exists, allowing L to outrank M and finish. If L waits on another inheriting mutex, the donation follows that chain. When a dependency ends, the scheduler recomputes the holder's priority from its base and any remaining donations. Immediate ceiling protection instead raises the holder to a configured resource ceiling from acquisition, whether or not anyone is waiting. Both need platform support and appropriate scheduling assumptions. Neither fixes a lock cycle, a disk operation or an unbounded critical section. Java exposes no portable setting for these protocols, and standard C++ mutexes do not promise them. I would also consider separating ownership or publishing snapshots, but check whether urgent work still needs a result from the background task. The fix must address the dependency, not just change a priority number.","follow":"What happens if L owns two locks and releases only the one H was waiting for?","seconds":90,"lesson":"priority-inversion-and-convoys","title":"Priority inversion and lock convoys","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"priority-inversion-and-convoys.s2","q":"\"We made a hot lock fair. Throughput fell and CPU usage dropped. Would you revert the change?\"","points":["Treat handoff overhead as a hypothesis and establish why fairness was introduced","Explain the idle gap while a fair lock's next eligible waiter is unscheduled","Measure hold, wait and wake-to-run delays and correlate pauses","Reduce sharing and use bounded batches processed outside the lock","Compare throughput and individual waiting tails without claiming starvation freedom from shorter holds"],"model":"I would first establish why fairness was introduced and measure where time went. A fair lock can prevent a running caller from passing the next eligible waiter. If that waiter is parked or not scheduled, the lock may remain unused while useful work could otherwise run. Repeated handoffs can create a convoy, but falling CPU usage and changing owners alone do not prove it. I would measure critical-section hold times, acquisition waits and wake-to-run delays, then correlate those with GC and scheduler events. Long holds could point to I/O or callbacks inside the lock instead. I would move that work out where the invariant permits, stripe independent data, and try bounded batches that are processed after releasing the lock. Then I would compare fair and barging policies under representative load. If fairness protects a requirement, blindly reverting can bring starvation back. Shorter holds improve opportunities but do not prove bounded overtaking. I would accept a change only after checking throughput and individual waiting tails, including the oldest outstanding request, against the service's requirements.","follow":"Can an unfair lock still develop a convoy?","seconds":75,"lesson":"priority-inversion-and-convoys","title":"Priority inversion and lock convoys","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"thundering-herd.s1","q":"\"You replaced every broadcast with wake-one to fix a thundering herd. Walk me through when that change is safe and when it can hang the service.\"","points":["Wake-one is suitable for one item when any selected consumer can use it","The waiter must reacquire the mutex and recheck its predicate","Mixed predicates can select an ineligible waiter and strand useful work","Shutdown and gates require notifying all affected waiters; batches need enough notifications or handoff","Wakeup counts depend on the schedule and are not exact context-switch counts","Correctness comes before measuring and reducing contention"],"model":"I would not make that replacement everywhere. Wake-one is appropriate when a state change supplies one unit of work and any selected waiter can use it. For a queue with separate not-empty and not-full conditions, adding one item can signal one consumer. That consumer still reacquires the mutex and checks the predicate, because notification does not reserve the item and wakeups can be spurious. If producers and consumers share a wait set, a notification can select a producer even though only a consumer can proceed. Useful work can then remain stranded. I would separate the conditions or broadcast. Closing a queue is different again: every waiting consumer must learn that it can exit once the queue is drained, and blocked producers must learn that new work is rejected. A batch also needs enough notifications, a broadcast, or an explicit handoff protocol. The performance issue is unnecessary competition. With one item and a hundred waiting consumers, ninety-nine may find no work, but that is not an exact count of operating-system context switches. I would first prove that every state transition wakes enough eligible waiters, then measure predicate failures, contention and throughput before optimising. A shutdown hang is a much worse result than an occasional unnecessary wakeup.","follow":"What changes if producers wait for batches of different sizes?","seconds":90,"lesson":"thundering-herd","title":"Thundering herd and cache stampede","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"thundering-herd.s2","q":"\"A hot cache key expires across two hundred servers and the database falls over. Design the fix, including what happens if the refresher dies.\"","points":["Identify duplicate misses and the positive feedback from a slower source","Use per-key single-flight locally and coordinate refresh ownership across the fleet","Recheck freshness after winning ownership and keep source I/O outside shared locks","Serve stale only within a freshness contract; bound cold-miss waiting and background work","Lease expiry permits takeover but can overlap loads; enforce publication generations and owner-checked release","Combine failure backoff, TTL jitter and global load bounds, then measure results"],"model":"I would suppress duplicate loads, bound refresh work, and decide how stale the response is allowed to be. This is a cache stampede: many requests miss together and query the same row. Extra queries slow the database, which extends the miss window and attracts still more work. Within each server, I would keep one in-flight future per key so concurrent callers share the load and its failure. Across two hundred servers, that still permits two hundred queries, so I would coordinate refresh ownership in the shared cache with a lease. After winning ownership, I would reread the entry because another refresher may already have completed. The source call runs outside shared locks. If the product permits bounded staleness, requests can reuse the old value while a bounded executor refreshes it. A cold miss has no old value, so callers wait with a deadline or receive a controlled failure. If the refresher dies, lease expiry allows takeover. But expiry does not stop a paused holder's query: two loads can overlap. The cache must atomically reject publication from an obsolete generation, and release must check the owner's token so an old holder cannot delete a successor's lease. Failures complete local futures, cleanup removes only the matching flight, and retries back off with jitter. TTL jitter spreads different keys; it does not replace single-flight for one hot key. I would also cap total source concurrency because thousands of distinct misses can still overwhelm it. Finally, I would measure source loads per key, stale age, refresh failures and waiter latency, then test expiry, cancellation, failure and a holder resuming after its lease has expired.","follow":"How would your design change if every response must reflect the latest committed write?","seconds":120,"lesson":"thundering-herd","title":"Thundering herd and cache stampede","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"bug-symptom-map.s1","q":"\"Requests have stopped completing, but CPU is low. Talk me through your first few minutes of debugging.\"","points":["Confirm the affected workload, completion rate and per-thread CPU rather than relying on one aggregate","Capture repeated stacks and correlate them with queue age and dependency latency","Follow a wait-for graph through locks, futures, permits and worker slots","Distinguish a lock cycle, pool self-deadlock, broken notification and slow dependency using evidence","Choose contract-preserving mitigation and validate the mechanism with a controlled regression"],"model":"I would capture evidence and find what the requests are waiting for. Low CPU points me towards waits, but it does not identify the cause or rule out a saturated dependency. First I check which endpoints stopped completing, the age of queued work, and whether one thread or core is still busy. Then I take repeated thread dumps and correlate them with those metrics. I group matching stacks and follow the resource each group needs. A monitor cycle establishes a lock deadlock. If all pool workers are waiting on futures whose children are queued to that same pool, that is pool self-deadlock even without a Java deadlock report. A consumer sleeping while work remains available makes me inspect the predicate and notification protocol. Threads waiting for connections make me examine dependency latency and acquisition deadlines. Repeated stack frames alone are not proof: healthy idle workers can stay in a queue wait, and livelocked threads can change frames without completing anything. Once I have evidence, I restore service with a suitable limit, timeout, rollback or restart. I would preserve required work, especially durable records. Then I reproduce the actual mechanism with controlled scheduling, assert the safety or progress invariant, and add bounded diagnostics so the next stall captures useful evidence.","follow":"What would you do differently if CPU were high but completions were still zero?","seconds":90,"lesson":"bug-symptom-map","title":"From symptom to cause: the bug map","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"bug-symptom-map.s2","q":"\"Two callers occasionally reserve the same seat, but ThreadSanitizer is clean. Does that clear the code? Explain what you would test.\"","points":["A clean ThreadSanitizer run does not exclude logical races or unexecuted schedules","Separately synchronized availability checks and assignments can both succeed logically","Walk through both callers observing free before either reserves","Protect check and assignment together or use a conditional atomic ownership transition","Force the vulnerable interleaving and assert one winner, then exercise failure and cancellation","Separate C++ data-race undefined behaviour from a race condition using otherwise safe operations"],"model":"No. A clean ThreadSanitizer run does not prove that the reservation operation is atomic. It checks executed memory accesses for data races; a logical race can consist entirely of individually synchronized operations. Suppose checking availability takes the seat's mutex and releases it, and assigning the owner takes that same mutex separately. Caller A checks and sees free. Before A assigns, caller B also checks and sees free. Both callers then assign and report success. There may be no unsynchronized memory access for the sanitizer to report, but we have violated the invariant that one available seat has only one winner. I would put the check and assignment inside one critical section, or use a compare-and-set that changes the owner only if it is still unassigned. To reproduce the old bug, I would use a test gate between its check and assignment so both callers reach that boundary. The regression for the repaired API races two reservations and asserts exactly one success, a consistent owner, and completion within a deadline. I would also exercise cancellation and any failure after reservation. Finally, I would keep sanitizer coverage for actual memory races: in C++ those are undefined behaviour, whereas this example is a race condition even though its component accesses are protected.","follow":"How would you make reserving two seats all or nothing without deadlocking?","seconds":90,"lesson":"bug-symptom-map","title":"From symptom to cause: the bug map","track":"core","module":11},{"id":"ordering-threads.s1","q":"\"Your FooBar solution puts a lock around each print. Why does it still print two foos together, and what would you change?\"","points":["Mutual exclusion prevents overlap but does not enforce alternation","A worker can release the lock and acquire it again before its peer runs","Choose two semaphores initialized to one for foo and zero for bar","Acquire the role's permit, finish its callback, then release the other role","The single baton is either available or held; each worker performs exactly n turns","State the callback and worker-progress assumptions"],"model":"The lock prevents overlapping prints, but it does not enforce turns. Foo can print, unlock, then acquire the lock again before bar runs. I would use two semaphores for this fixed alternation: foo starts with one permit and bar starts with zero. Each worker acquires its own permit, completes its print, then releases the other worker's semaphore. That also handles bar starting first, because it waits until foo has completed. The invariant is one logical baton. It is either an available permit or held by the worker between acquire and release; the stored counts do not always sum to one. Each worker loops exactly n times, so both terminate. A monitor with a turn flag and predicate waits is equally valid. Both designs assume callbacks finish successfully and workers keep running; cancellation needs a separate protocol to wake peers and stop the sequence.","follow":"What happens if foo's callback throws after acquiring its permit?","seconds":60,"lesson":"ordering-threads","title":"Making threads take turns","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"ordering-threads.s2","q":"\"My four-thread FizzBuzz prints 1 and 2, then hangs. Every wait uses a loop. What would you inspect?\"","points":["A predicate loop ensures safety but cannot guarantee the right waiter is notified","After number prints 2, a single notify may wake buzz while fizz remains asleep","Use broadcast on the shared condition or a separate condition for each role","Keep state checks, output and counter advancement under the same lock","Include completion in every wait predicate and broadcast after the final value","Test exact output and bounded completion, including empty and small runs"],"model":"I would first inspect the notification policy. A loop prevents a worker from printing on the wrong turn, but it cannot wake the worker whose turn it actually is. Suppose fizz, buzz and fizzbuzz are waiting while number prints 2. The counter becomes 3. A single notification might wake buzz, which finds that 3 is not its turn and waits again. Number must also wait, while fizz remains asleep. The state is valid, but nobody can advance it. I would broadcast after changing the counter, or use separate conditions and signal the role that owns the next value. I would also check that all predicate checks and counter updates use the same lock, and that output completes before ownership passes. For termination, every wait must accept either its turn or the end of the run, with a final broadcast. I would test the exact sequence and require every worker to finish before a deadline, including zero items, one item and a final fizzbuzz. Those checks separate ordering bugs from shutdown bugs.","follow":"Can targeted signalling lose a wakeup if the next worker has not started waiting yet?","seconds":75,"lesson":"ordering-threads","title":"Making threads take turns","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"ordering-threads.s3","q":"\"Make this ordered printer safe to shut down when a worker is interrupted or its callback fails. How would you design that?\"","points":["Choose shared terminal state and a first-failure value protected by the monitor","Wait predicates include stopped or completed as well as the role's turn","Publish failure and wake every waiter before propagating the worker's failure","Do not release a success baton after failed work or assume a stop request wakes a wait","Define callback completion and cancellation boundaries without advancing output early","Join every worker before destroying state, and test failures at handoff boundaries"],"model":"I would use a monitor with an explicit terminal state and a stored first failure. Every worker waits for its turn, normal completion, or shutdown, and checks the terminal state before invoking its callback. If a callback throws, I record the failure under the lock, mark the run stopped and wake every waiting role. Interruption follows the same state transition. In Java I then propagate the interruption or restore the flag at the outer worker boundary. In C++ I catch inside the worker and let the owner report the saved exception after joining. Merely setting a stop flag does not wake a condition variable or semaphore. I would not release the next success baton in a finally block, because that would let dependent work proceed after its prerequisite failed. I would also define what cancellation means for a callback already running: normally it may finish, but no later turn starts once shutdown is published. A callback must not re-enter the coordinator or wait for another role. If callbacks need to run outside the lock, I reserve the turn until completion and publish the next turn only afterwards. Finally, I join every worker before destroying the coordinator and test failure before a handoff, during a callback and while peers are waiting.","follow":"How would you preserve these guarantees if callbacks perform slow I/O outside the monitor?","seconds":90,"lesson":"ordering-threads","title":"Making threads take turns","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"dining-philosophers.s1","q":"\"All five philosophers pick up their left fork and wait. Walk me through the deadlock and prove that your fix prevents it.\"","points":["Name mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait in the example","Choose a strict global fork order with lower ids acquired first","A cycle would require strictly increasing fork ids to return to the starting id","All acquisition paths must obey the order, including locks already held","Nonadjacent philosophers can still overlap; at most two of five can eat","Separate deadlock freedom from starvation freedom"],"model":"I would break the circular wait by numbering the forks and always acquiring the lower numbered fork first. In the broken state, each fork is exclusive, each philosopher holds one while waiting for another, nobody can confiscate a fork, and the waits form a cycle. Those are the four deadlock conditions. With ordering, a philosopher holding fork a can wait only for a higher numbered fork b. Following a supposed cycle would give strictly increasing fork numbers that eventually return to a. That is impossible, so the cycle cannot exist. Every code path must obey the order, including any locks the caller already holds. For this ring, the philosopher between forks zero and four changes acquisition direction. I still allow nonadjacent philosophers to eat together; with five forks, at most two can eat at once. This proves deadlock freedom, not starvation freedom. An unfair lock can keep bypassing one hungry philosopher. If eventual service is required, I would add fair admission and explicitly assume that runnable holders get CPU time and finish their meals.","follow":"Does reversing just one philosopher's acquisition order work for an arbitrary resource graph?","seconds":75,"lesson":"dining-philosophers","title":"The dining philosophers","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"dining-philosophers.s2","q":"\"Your solution finishes a million meals without a hang. Can you claim that nobody starves? What would make that claim defensible?\"","points":["A finite successful run does not prove starvation freedom","Show how an unfair admission policy can repeatedly bypass one hungry philosopher","Ordered blocking acquisition with fair locks needs eventual scheduling and finite meals","Java untimed tryLock bypasses fairness; C++ mutex has no fairness option","A FIFO waiter can reserve both forks atomically and prevent later requests bypassing a blocked head","Explain the concurrency cost and use tests as evidence alongside a proof"],"model":"No. A successful finite run shows progress on the schedules we tested, but it cannot prove that every hungry philosopher eventually eats. With unfair locks, neighbors can repeatedly win while one philosopher keeps waiting, even though the table remains active. For ordered blocking acquisition, I would use fair fork admission and state the remaining assumptions: runnable threads eventually execute, holders finish their meals, and nobody abandons a held resource. The highest ranked fork cannot depend on a higher one, so its holders finish; the argument then works down the order. In Java, fair ReentrantLock instances support that policy, but untimed tryLock can still barge. C++ mutexes provide no fairness switch. Another design is a FIFO queue of whole meal requests. Under a monitor, reserve both free forks for the queue head, then consider the next head. Eating happens outside that monitor. Later requests cannot bypass a blocked head, so existing finite meals drain and it eventually gets its forks. The tradeoff is that a blocked head can leave unrelated forks idle, although consecutive compatible requests can still eat together. I would test ownership, overlapping nonadjacent eaters and bounded completion, but I would use the admission argument, not the meal count, to justify starvation freedom.","follow":"How do you remove a cancelled request without leaving the FIFO waiter stuck?","seconds":90,"lesson":"dining-philosophers","title":"The dining philosophers","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"dining-philosophers.s3","q":"\"We replaced fork locking with try-and-release, but the threads stay busy and nobody eats. What is happening?\"","points":["Identify livelock when failed attempts release resources but repeatedly collide","Describe the lockstep acquire, failed try, release and retry schedule","Verify release on every failure path and use randomized backoff only to reduce collisions","Do not claim fairness or a deterministic waiting bound from random retries","Use a deadline for the whole attempt or choose ordered blocking acquisition or queued admission"],"model":"This can be livelock. The threads are taking steps, but none completes a meal. All philosophers can acquire their left forks, fail to try the right forks, release the left forks, and repeat the same pattern. I would first verify that every failed attempt actually releases its first fork, including exception paths; otherwise we still retain resources while waiting. Randomized backoff can reduce repeated collisions, but it is not a proof of eventual service or a fixed waiting bound. In particular, a fair Java lock does not make untimed tryLock respect its queue. If the operation must give up, I would apply one deadline to the whole attempt, including first-lock acquisition and retries, and return holding nothing. If starvation freedom matters, I would prefer ordered blocking acquisition with fair admission, or a waiter that queues whole fork pairs.","follow":"Why does a timeout on only the second lock fail to bound the total operation time?","seconds":60,"lesson":"dining-philosophers","title":"The dining philosophers","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"readers-writers.s1","q":"\"Design a read-write lock for me. Who can starve, and what would you change if both readers and writers need a turn?\"","points":["State the invariant: one writer alone or multiple readers without a writer","Use one monitor mutex, active readers, a writing flag and waiting writers","Readers wait for active or waiting writers; writers wait for all active users to leave","Writer preference can starve readers and does not order competing writers","Ticket assignment orders admission and consecutive readers share; progress assumes holders finish and eligible threads run","State non-reentrancy, unsupported upgrade, and cleanup on interruption or exceptions"],"model":"I would start with a writer-preferring monitor and state the policy explicitly. The invariant is one writer alone, or any number of readers with no writer. One mutex protects an active-reader count, a writing flag and a waiting-writer count. A reader waits while a writer is active or queued. A writer records that it is waiting, then waits until no reader or writer is active. Every wait rechecks its predicate. The last reader wakes a writer; a departing writer wakes another writer, or all readers if no writer remains. Notifications do not transfer ownership. This prevents new readers continually overtaking waiting writers, but readers can starve under continuous writes, and competing writers are not ordered. If both sides need a turn, I would assign tickets under the mutex. Consecutive reader tickets can enter together, but a writer ticket blocks later readers until earlier readers finish. That limits overtaking after ticket assignment; it does not promise a time bound without finite critical sections and eventual scheduling. My simple lock is non-reentrant and has no upgrade operation. Java interruption must undo waiting counts and wake newly eligible threads. Callers release in finally or through C++ guards, so an ordinary exception does not leak ownership.","follow":"How would a timed-out ticket holder leave the queue without blocking every later ticket?","seconds":90,"lesson":"readers-writers","title":"Readers and writers, three ways","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"readers-writers.s2","q":"\"Searchers may overlap an inserter, but a deleter must run alone. Is a read-write lock plus an insert mutex enough to make the linked list safe?\"","points":["Give searchers shared permission, inserters shared permission plus the insert mutex, and deleters exclusive permission","Explain why concurrent search and insertion still need safe publication of links","Build nodes fully, then publish Java volatile links or C++ release stores with acquire loads","Reclaim only under deletion permission and forbid pointers escaping protected operations","Separate deleter priority from individual fairness and mention the performance trade-off"],"model":"Those locks enforce who may overlap, but the list also needs safe publication. Searchers take shared permission. Inserters take shared permission and the insert mutex, so only one inserts while searches continue. Deleters take exclusive permission, which excludes both kinds. The remaining hazard is a searcher reading the link an inserter changes. I would fully construct a node before linking it into the list. In Java the link is volatile. In C++ it is an atomic pointer, published with a release store and read with acquire loads. Otherwise a search could see an incompletely published node, and ordinary conflicting C++ pointer accesses would be a data race. Deletion can reclaim nodes while exclusive permission is held, provided no pointer or iterator escapes the protected operation. Removing the tail also has to repair the tail pointer. Finally, deleter priority stops new searches extending a wait forever, but it does not order competing deleters. A fair queue addresses that under normal progress assumptions, with extra coordination that I would benchmark against the simpler policy.","follow":"What changes if search returns an iterator that the caller keeps after releasing the lock?","seconds":75,"lesson":"readers-writers","title":"Readers and writers, three ways","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"h2o-and-group-formation.s1","q":"\"Walk me through H2O with semaphores and a barrier. Why can a fast hydrogen from the next molecule not mix with this one?\"","points":["State two hydrogen slots, one oxygen slot and a reusable barrier of three","Acquire a type-specific slot before arriving at the barrier","Run each callback after the barrier and return its slot only after callback completion","Explain why the next barrier phase needs all three previous callbacks to finish","Distinguish valid H,H,O output from a full rendezvous and state callback assumptions"],"model":"I use two hydrogen permits, one oxygen permit, and a reusable barrier of three. Each atom first takes a permit of its own kind, then waits at the barrier. That fixes both the size and composition of the group before any callback runs. After the barrier, each atom runs its callback and returns its permit only when that callback finishes. A fast hydrogen can finish, return its slot, and let a new hydrogen reach the next barrier phase. That is safe: the new hydrogen cannot pass that phase until two hydrogen slots and the oxygen slot have been returned. Those three returns prove that every callback from the previous molecule has finished. A barrier alone could release three hydrogens; permits alone could recycle hydrogen slots before oxygen prints. There is also a shorter scheme that always emits H,H,O, but its hydrogens can print before oxygen even arrives. I would clarify which waiting contract is required. This proof assumes balanced input, callbacks that complete without throwing, and no admitted participant disappearing. Failure handling needs an additional protocol.","follow":"What happens if the only oxygen is queued behind two blocked hydrogen tasks in a two-worker executor?","seconds":75,"lesson":"h2o-and-group-formation","title":"H2O and group formation problems","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"h2o-and-group-formation.s2","q":"\"Your group gate works in tests, then one player cancels and the service hangs. How would you debug and redesign that?\"","points":["Distinguish cancellation before reservation from cancellation after a seat is committed","Explain that unfinished membership includes reserved seats, so abandoning one can strand the group","Remove unreserved waiters under the mutex and recheck feasible groups","Use generation-specific membership and failure state after reservation; wake all affected waiters","Explain why returning a permit or resetting a barrier cannot undo completed callbacks","Add role queues for fairness and test cancellation, missing roles and executor starvation"],"model":"I would first find out whether the player cancelled before reservation or after the group committed a seat to them. Those need different transitions. The unfinished-member count includes reserved seats, not just people already doing work, so silently abandoning a reservation can keep that count above zero forever. Before reservation, I can remove the waiter under the monitor mutex and reconsider which groups are feasible. After reservation, I need explicit membership and a generation identifier. A cancellation marks that generation failed, stops successful completion, wakes its affected waiters, and makes every member observe the same outcome. Any replacement policy must be defined before letting the group proceed. Returning a permit is not enough: another member may already have called an external service, which a counter update cannot undo. Similarly, resetting a Java barrier without coordinating the old participants can put them into a new generation by mistake. For fairness I would keep a queue per role and reserve named waiters. I would test cancellation at each transition, a missing role, a callback failure, and an executor whose workers are all blocked on incomplete groups. Finally, a timeout should fail the team unless the product explicitly permits changing its required composition.","follow":"When can you safely replace a cancelled member, and when must the entire group fail?","seconds":90,"lesson":"h2o-and-group-formation","title":"H2O and group formation problems","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"barber-and-bathroom.s1","q":"\"The barber is asleep even though a customer is waiting. Walk me through how that happens, and how you would fix it and close the shop.\"","points":["A notification is not stored: checking empty, unlocking and then waiting loses the customer's signal","Check the queue and wait in a loop under one lock; waiting atomically releases the lock","Remove a customer under the lock, perform the haircut outside it, then publish that customer's completion","Closing rejects new arrivals, wakes the barber and drains accepted customers before exit","Completion by highest ticket assumes one barber; multiple barbers need per-customer completion"],"model":"The barber has lost a wakeup. He checks an empty queue, releases the lock, and decides to sleep. In that gap a customer queues and signals. Nobody is waiting yet, so the signal disappears. The barber then sleeps without checking again. I would put the queue predicate and the wait in one loop under one lock. Waiting atomically releases that lock, so a customer cannot slip between the check and registration of the wait. A wakeup only means check again; it does not reserve a customer. The barber removes one customer under the lock, cuts hair outside it, then records completion under the lock. Each customer waits for their own completion, because being accepted is not the same as being served. To close, I set a closed flag under the same lock, reject new arrivals and wake the barber. He drains accepted customers and exits only when closed and empty. The owner joins him before destroying the shop. With one barber, tickets finish in order. With several barbers, I need a separate completion result for each customer because later tickets may finish first. That keeps admission, service and shutdown promises separate and testable.","follow":"What if the haircut throws, or an accepted customer wants to cancel?","seconds":90,"lesson":"barber-and-bathroom","title":"The sleeping barber and the unisex bathroom","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"barber-and-bathroom.s2","q":"\"Design a bathroom with three stalls, two groups that cannot mix, and no starvation. Why does your fairness policy work?\"","points":["One monitor protects occupancy, the current group and ticket state","Admit only the next ticket when there is capacity and the room is empty or contains that group","Advance the ticket on entry and wake waiters; consecutive compatible tickets may share","Progress assumes finite visits, eventual lock acquisition and no abandoned tickets","Explain head of line blocking and distinguish group batching from individual fairness","Cancellation removes or skips abandoned tickets; successful admission always requires leave"],"model":"I would use one monitor and admission tickets. The monitor protects the number inside, the current group and the next ticket to serve. A caller takes a ticket under the lock and waits until it is their turn, a stall is available, and the room is empty or contains their group. On entry I advance the ticket and wake waiters, so consecutive people from the same group can share the room. On departure I reduce occupancy, clear the group when empty and wake waiters again. Nobody can overtake an issued ticket. If visits finish and eligible threads eventually acquire the lock, each ticket has only finitely many people ahead and eventually enters. That is not a wall-clock bound or a promise about operating system scheduling. The cost is head of line blocking. A blue person waiting behind two reds prevents later reds from using the third stall. Batching can improve utilisation, but counting batch slots alone does not guarantee fairness to each individual. I would also define cancellation explicitly: an abandoned head ticket must be removed or skipped under the lock. Every successful entry must have a matching departure, even when the caller's work throws.","follow":"How would you support cancellation without leaving a hole in the ticket sequence?","seconds":90,"lesson":"barber-and-bathroom","title":"The sleeping barber and the unisex bathroom","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"web-crawler.s1","q":"\"Your crawler's queue is empty, but one fetch is still running. How do you know when the whole crawl is actually finished?\"","points":["Completion requires no queued or active work, expressed as pending equals queued plus active","Claim and enqueue each same-host URL once under the state lock","Taking work does not decrement pending; fetch outside the lock","Register children before retiring their parent and publish terminal zero to all waiters","Failures and rejected submissions must retire exactly one registration","A transient zero hidden inside one locked update is not visible to other monitor users"],"model":"An empty queue is not enough. The active fetch may discover more work, so I track pending as queued pages plus active pages. I seed it with one for the start URL. Under one lock, a worker takes a URL from the frontier without changing pending. It releases the lock for the slow fetch, then reacquires it to claim new same-host links, queue them and count them before retiring the parent. Claiming and checking must be one operation, otherwise two workers can fetch the same URL. An idle worker waits while the queue is empty and pending is positive. The last completion makes pending zero and wakes everyone, so workers can exit and the caller can join them. Every terminal failure also retires its page; retries must retain or replace that registration. With an executor, I register before submission and undo the registration if submission is rejected. Otherwise the counter can leak forever. One nuance matters: a transient zero inside a single locked update is harmless if nobody tests completion until the update ends. But an atomic decrement that opens a completion latch publishes zero immediately, so children must already be registered. The invariant and its publication point are the proof.","follow":"How would you bound the frontier without deadlocking workers that also produce new URLs?","seconds":90,"lesson":"web-crawler","title":"The multithreaded web crawler","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"web-crawler.s2","q":"\"You added a depth limit, but your parallel crawler misses pages that sequential BFS finds. What went wrong, and which fix would you choose?\"","points":["Concurrent completion order does not preserve shortest-path discovery order","Give a path of length two and another of length three to X with limit three","First claiming X at depth three suppresses expansion and loses a child reachable at depth three","Level barriers restore exact BFS depths with coordinator-owned deduplication","Alternatively requeue on improved depth, accepting repeated expansion or caching fetched links","Define whether boundary pages are returned only or also fetched"],"model":"The visited set records first discovery, but first discovery is not necessarily the shortest path when fetches finish out of order. Suppose the start reaches X through A in two hops, and through B and C in three hops. The limit is three. If A is slow, the longer route claims X first at depth three, so X is not expanded. When A finally returns, the visited set suppresses that shorter route. A child Y of X is therefore missed even though its shortest distance is three. I would choose level-by-level crawling when exact depths and one fetch per expanded page matter. Fetch one level in parallel, wait for it, then let one coordinator deduplicate and build the next level. That also removes concurrent access to the visited set. The cost is a barrier at every level, so one slow page holds up the next level. Alternatively, keep the best known depth and requeue a page whenever that depth improves. This preserves overlap but may repeat fetches or require caching links. I would define the boundary too: our example returns pages at the limit without fetching their outgoing links. If downloading their contents is required, fetch that final level but do not expand it.","follow":"What changes if the graph can change between fetches, or a callback delivers links asynchronously?","seconds":90,"lesson":"web-crawler","title":"The multithreaded web crawler","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"rate-limiters.s1","q":"\"Design a thread-safe limiter that allows bursts of ten and then two requests per second. How would you prove it works?\"","points":["Choose a token bucket with capacity ten and rate two, starting full","Put refill, timestamp update, check and consumption in one critical section","Use injected monotonic elapsed time and preserve fractional credit","Explain the last-token interleaving and the admission bound","Test frozen-time contention and exact refill boundaries without sleeps","Distinguish rate control from concurrency control"],"model":"I would use a token bucket with capacity ten, starting full, and refill it at two tokens per second. That allows the initial burst and then the sustained rate. On each call I take the bucket lock, read an injected monotonic clock, add elapsed time times the rate up to capacity, and update the timestamp. I admit only if a token is available, and consume it before releasing the lock. The whole transition must be protected: otherwise two callers can both see the last token and both return success. Fractional credit must survive between calls. The mathematical bound is the initial capacity plus the rate times elapsed time, not two requests in every rolling second. For tests I freeze time, launch many callers, and require exactly ten successes. Then I advance just short of half a second and to exactly half a second, checking when the next request passes. I also test a long idle period to verify the cap. Finally, this bounds admissions, so I would add a separate concurrency limit if slow downstream work could accumulate.","follow":"What would you change for requests that each consume a different number of tokens?","seconds":75,"lesson":"rate-limiters","title":"Thread-safe rate limiters","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"rate-limiters.s2","q":"\"The requirement is at most one hundred requests in any rolling minute. Which algorithm would you choose, and why not the cheaper counter?\"","points":["Choose an exact sliding log for the strict rolling-window requirement","Expire timestamps at the specified boundary and atomically check and append","Explain a fixed-window boundary burst with concrete numbers","Explain the weighted counter's approximation and possible over-admission","State O(K) storage and amortized O(1) trimming with an O(K) worst call","Use deterministic boundary and concurrent-last-slot tests"],"model":"I would use a sliding log because the requirement says any rolling minute. For each client I keep the timestamps of admitted requests. Under one client lock, I remove entries at least one minute old, reject if one hundred remain, and otherwise append the current timestamp. That defines the window as excluding its left endpoint and including now. A fixed counter would allow one hundred requests just before a minute boundary and another hundred immediately after it, which violates this requirement. The weighted sliding counter saves memory, but assumes the previous window's requests were evenly distributed. If ninety requests arrived at its very end, halfway through the next window they still all count in reality, while the estimate counts only forty-five. So it can over-admit even when its arithmetic is implemented correctly. The exact log stores at most one hundred timestamps per client. Each timestamp is removed once, giving amortized constant work, although one call can trim the entire log. I would test the exact expiry boundary and race several callers for the final available slot.","follow":"How would you bound memory if an attacker continually sends requests with new client identifiers?","seconds":75,"lesson":"rate-limiters","title":"Thread-safe rate limiters","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"rate-limiters.s3","q":"\"You delete idle full buckets, but production sometimes admits twice the burst for one customer. Walk me through the likely race.\"","points":["Identify simultaneous use of an old bucket and its replacement","Verify fullness and mark retirement while holding the bucket lock","Require old-reference callers to retry and remove only the matching mapping","Keep the C++ bucket alive until its lock guard has released the mutex","Account for unchanged configuration and active-key memory limits","Describe a targeted eviction-versus-admission test"],"model":"I would look for two live buckets making decisions for the same key. A request may obtain the old bucket, pause, and then resume after a sweeper removes it and another request creates a full replacement. If both buckets can admit, the customer receives two independent budgets. Fullness alone is therefore insufficient. Under the old bucket's lock, the sweeper must verify that it is full and idle, then mark it retired. Any caller holding that reference checks the flag under the same lock and retries the map lookup. Removal must also match the bucket identity, so a stale sweeper cannot remove a replacement. In C++ I retain a local shared pointer through the entire critical section; erasing the map's last reference must not destroy a still-locked mutex. The full-equals-new argument assumes unchanged configuration. It also only reclaims idle keys, so I still need a policy for too many active keys. My test would pause a caller after lookup, race retirement and replacement, then verify that the old reference cannot grant another burst.","follow":"Can a generic cache with time-based eviction provide these guarantees by itself?","seconds":75,"lesson":"rate-limiters","title":"Thread-safe rate limiters","track":"core","module":12},{"id":"safety-ladder.s1","q":"\"You have a request buffer, a live configuration, some metrics, and an LRU cache. How would you make the service thread-safe?\"","points":["Classify the invariants before choosing primitives","Keep the request buffer confined, including all mutable aliases","Publish immutable configuration snapshots safely and capture one per request","Use metrics adders only when an exact concurrent snapshot is unnecessary","Guard the cache map and recency order with one lock and identify the race","Measure remaining contention before changing the design"],"model":"I would choose a policy for each invariant, rather than one policy for the whole service. The request buffer stays with its request thread, and I make sure no callback or shared field exposes it. The configuration becomes an immutable snapshot. I publish replacements safely and capture one version at the start of a request, so related settings agree. If two administrators update it, I serialize their updates or use a retrying compare and set, otherwise one change could overwrite another. For approximate metrics, an adder is suitable. I would not use its live sum for an exact quota. The LRU cache is different: its map and recency list must describe the same entries, so one lock protects both, including lookups that move an entry. Otherwise a lookup can move an entry while eviction removes it. Finally I measure the actual contention. The cache lock, counters, or allocation could dominate. Sharding is an option, but I would state that it changes global LRU into per-shard LRU.","follow":"What changes if every request must use the latest permissions immediately after an admin update completes?","seconds":75,"lesson":"safety-ladder","title":"The safety ladder","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"safety-ladder.s2","q":"\"Both fields are atomic, yet readers sometimes see an impossible pair. Why, and what would you change?\"","points":["Atomic fields do not make a multi-field snapshot atomic","Give a concrete interleaving producing an inconsistent pair","Choose a lock around the whole invariant or one immutable snapshot","Explain safe publication and lifetime in Java and C++","Capture the holder once and coordinate read-modify-publish updates","Distinguish a short mutex from a nonblocking progress guarantee"],"model":"Atomicity of each field does not give atomicity of the pair. Suppose the pair starts at zero and one. The writer changes the low value to one, the reader reads both fields as one, and only then does the writer change the high value to two. There is no data race on the atomic fields, but the relationship is broken. I can use one lock around both writes and both reads. For read-heavy configuration, I can instead construct an immutable pair and publish one reference to it. The reader captures that reference once. Reading the holder twice could mix versions again. In Java I would use a volatile reference or an atomic reference. In C++ the holder needs synchronization, and shared ownership keeps old snapshots alive. A short mutex around that pointer is correct, but readers can still block. If writers derive a new value from the old one, I also serialize that transformation or retry it with compare and set. Immutability alone does not prevent lost updates.","follow":"If you replace the pointer lock with an atomic shared pointer, have you proved the whole operation lock-free?","seconds":75,"lesson":"safety-ladder","title":"The safety ladder","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"confinement-and-thread-local.s1","q":"\"This buffer is a local variable. Is that enough to prove it is thread-safe? Walk me through your reasoning.\"","points":["A local reference does not prove that its mutable referent is private","Check every alias and asynchronous capture for access by another thread","Use one private partial result per worker and combine after joining","Start and join publish ownership transfers; retaining an unused alias is not concurrent access","A thread-local binding can still point to shared state, and live aggregation needs synchronization"],"model":"No. A local variable is only the starting point of the proof. I need to know which object it refers to and who else can access that object. A local reference to a shared list still shares the list. In C++, even a stack buffer can be shared through a pointer captured by another thread, and that pointer can also outlive the buffer.\nI would trace references stored in fields, returned to callers, or captured by asynchronous work. If this call creates the buffer, uses it privately and returns an independent result, confinement is a good argument.\nFor parallel word counting, I would give each worker its own map and merge the maps after joining. Main can retain references while workers run, provided it does not access those maps. Starting and joining supply the visibility edges for those ownership transfers.\nA thread-local is not a substitute for that proof: its binding could still point to one shared map. And if main wants totals while workers are updating, I need atomics or a snapshot protocol instead of reading their plain counters.","follow":"What changes if a callback retains the buffer after the method returns?","seconds":75,"lesson":"confinement-and-thread-local","title":"Confinement and thread-local state","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"confinement-and-thread-local.s2","q":"\"An anonymous request sometimes runs as the previous user. We keep the user in a thread-local. What would you inspect and change?\"","points":["Pool threads retain bindings between tasks, so a skipped set or cleanup can reuse an old identity","Establish context on every request path and clear it on every exit, failing closed when identity is required","Use Java finally and remove or a C++ RAII guard; nested scopes restore their previous context","Pass context explicitly across asynchronous hops instead of assuming thread-local propagation","Distinguish request context from reusable caches and explain why virtual-thread caches often do not amortize creation","Test reused-worker, exceptional-exit, nested-scope and asynchronous-hop cases"],"model":"I would first check request boundaries on reused workers. A thread-local belongs to the worker, not to the request. If Alice sets her identity and an exception skips cleanup, the next anonymous request can read Alice without any simultaneous access or data race.\nI would establish the context on every entry path, including an explicit anonymous state, and clear it on every exit. The data layer should reject a missing required tenant rather than guess. In Java I would put removal in a finally block; in C++ I would use an RAII guard. For nested scopes, the guard must restore the outer context instead of deleting it.\nThen I would trace asynchronous hops. A continuation running on another pool thread sees that thread's context, so I would capture the identity and pass it explicitly. Inheritable thread-locals are tied to thread creation, not task submission.\nI would keep request identity separate from reusable per-worker caches. A cache can intentionally survive tasks, but it needs bounded contents and no residual user data. With one virtual thread per request, that cache usually creates a new object per request anyway.\nI would test two requests on one worker, an exception, a nested context, and a continuation on a different worker.","follow":"Why can a discarded Java ThreadLocal key still leave its value retained?","seconds":90,"lesson":"confinement-and-thread-local","title":"Confinement and thread-local state","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"single-writer-and-actors.s1","q":"\"Why would you put this map behind an actor instead of locking it, and what ordering would callers actually get?\"","points":["Only serialized handlers access the mutable map; mailbox and replies still synchronize","FIFO gives enqueue order, with no predetermined ordering between concurrent submissions","Commands carry immutable, copied or exclusively transferred data and return independent results","Readers ask the owner or acquire safely published immutable snapshots","Choose actors for batching or asynchronous operations, accounting for queue hops and one owner's capacity","Partition stable keys for throughput while explaining collisions and cross-key transaction limits"],"model":"I would choose an actor when serial ownership makes the map's invariants easier to maintain, especially if I can batch expensive work. Every update and consistent read becomes a command. Only one handler accesses the mutable map at a time, while the mailbox and reply futures provide the synchronization between threads.\nWith a FIFO mailbox, handlers run in successful enqueue order. Two concurrent submissions do not have a predetermined order based on which call started first. If a caller needs to know an update took effect, it waits for the reply after the update, not merely for enqueueing to finish.\nCommands must carry immutable data, copies, or exclusive ownership. Sending a reference does not stop its sender from mutating the object. Replies should return values or immutable snapshots, never the live map. Frequent readers can acquire safely published snapshots if their freshness requirement allows it.\nThe costs are a queue hop, possible waiting, and one owner's serial capacity. For tiny synchronous operations, a lock may be simpler and faster. If measured load exceeds one owner's capacity, I can partition by stable key. Different lanes run in parallel, but hash collisions still serialize some keys, and a transaction across two owners needs additional coordination.","follow":"What happens if a handler starts asynchronous work and the state changes before its completion arrives?","seconds":90,"lesson":"single-writer-and-actors","title":"Single writer, actors and message passing","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"single-writer-and-actors.s2","q":"\"Your actor has a bounded mailbox. How would you close it without losing accepted work or leaving callers waiting forever?\"","points":["Stop admission atomically with respect to accepting commands and reject later submissions","Wake blocked producers, drain accepted commands, then let a controller join the owner","A poison pill requires admission to have stopped so no command can land behind it","Distinguish recoverable operation failure from fatal owner failure and settle affected replies","Do not block owners on replies or full mailboxes; a timeout does not imply non-execution","For a durable writer, complete replies only after the required flush succeeds and report flush failures"],"model":"I would make close a lifecycle transition, not just another message. First I would stop admission atomically with respect to accepting commands. Later submissions fail explicitly, and producers blocked on capacity must wake and either finish an already accepted operation or learn that admission is closed.\nThe owner drains accepted work, completes its replies, and exits. A controller then joins it. A poison pill can implement the exit signal, but only after no sender can put another command behind it. Otherwise that command and its reply are stranded.\nFor an ordinary rejected operation, I preserve the state invariant and complete that reply exceptionally. Catching an exception does not roll back partial changes. If the owner fails fatally, I need to reject new work and settle every affected pending reply before deciding whether recovery is possible.\nI would also check wait cycles. Owners must not block waiting for each other's replies or space in full mailboxes. A timeout only means the caller stopped waiting; the command may still execute, so retries need deduplication when duplicate effects matter.\nFor a durable log writer, draining means writing and successfully performing the required storage flush before acknowledging the batch. A flush failure must fail the affected replies, not report success.","follow":"How would you distinguish a command that never executed from one whose effect happened but whose reply was lost?","seconds":90,"lesson":"single-writer-and-actors","title":"Single writer, actors and message passing","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"pipelines-and-fan-out.s1","q":"\"You have a stream of records to parse in parallel and write in order. Walk me through the pipeline, including what happens when the writer fails.\"","points":["Use bounded queues, a limited parser pool and one writer","Sequence records and bound admission until ordered emission","Count payloads, active work, batches and reorder storage in the memory budget","Batch by size or time and flush the partial batch on graceful close","On failure stop admission and wake blocked operations in both directions","Join all stages and report the first error; cancellation requires cooperative work"],"model":"I would use bounded queues, a limited pool of parsers, and one writer that owns the output. I would number records at admission so the writer can restore input order. The admission limit would cover everything accepted but not yet emitted, including completed records waiting behind a slow earlier record. Otherwise a fixed worker count can hide an unbounded reorder buffer.\n\nThe memory budget includes queue contents, active work, batches, reorder storage, and payload bytes. When the writer slows, its queue fills, parsers block, and eventually the source stops accepting more work. At the service boundary I would choose a deadline or rejection policy instead of letting callers wait indefinitely.\n\nThe writer batches by size or elapsed time. On graceful close, I stop admission, let accepted work drain, count every parser's completion, and flush the final partial batch. If durability is required, acknowledgements follow the successful durability operation.\n\nIf the writer fails, I record the first error and abort every channel so blocked producers and consumers wake. I do not rely on inserting a final marker into a full queue. I then join all stages and report the error. That requires transforms and I/O to terminate or cooperate with cancellation; a stop flag cannot force arbitrary code to return.","follow":"How would you choose queue capacities from a latency budget and variable record sizes?","seconds":90,"lesson":"pipelines-and-fan-out","title":"Pipelines, fan-out, fan-in and batching","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"pipelines-and-fan-out.s2","q":"\"Only four calls can run at once, but memory keeps growing behind a slow first result. What is wrong, and how would you fix it?\"","points":["A running-call limit does not bound completed but un-emitted results","Releasing permits on completion keeps admitting later work","Hold admission permits until ordered emission or explicit cancellation","Drain all admitted completions so the missing head can reach the collector","State the head of line and retained-output trade-offs"],"model":"The limit is on running calls, but the growing memory is in completed results waiting for their turn. Suppose the first call is stuck. The other three finish, release their permits, and let three more calls start. That can repeat across the whole input, even though four calls are never exceeded.\n\nI would acquire a permit before admitting each item and keep it until its result is emitted in order. That bounds admitted but un-emitted work, including the reorder buffer. Cancellation must release those permits or tear down admission explicitly.\n\nThe collector must still drain completions for every admitted item. If it stops reading a full completion queue while waiting for the missing first result, that first worker may be unable to enqueue its result. I would reserve reorder storage for the entire admission window.\n\nA simpler alternative is a window of futures retrieved in input order. Both approaches preserve the memory bound but still suffer head of line blocking. Also, if I collect every emitted result in a returned list, that list still takes space proportional to the input. Streaming emission is needed for bounded total result storage.","follow":"How would you discover a later task's failure promptly while the oldest future is still running?","seconds":80,"lesson":"pipelines-and-fan-out","title":"Pipelines, fan-out, fan-in and batching","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"monitor-object-pattern.s1","q":"\"Explain the monitor object pattern, and show me why a signalled thread must check the condition again.\"","points":["Bundle private guarded state, one lock, invariants and conditions","Acquire the lock before checking the predicate or changing state","Wait atomically releases the associated lock and reacquires it before returning","Walk through another caller stealing the resource before the waiter reacquires","Use a loop or predicate wait and restore invariants before every release","Choose one notification for one eligible waiter and broadcast for close"],"model":"A monitor object puts shared state and the operations that protect it behind one private lock. I start with the invariant, then define the predicates that let each operation proceed. For a bounded queue, the invariant is that size stays between zero and capacity, and the predicates are space available and item available.\n\nA method acquires the lock before checking its predicate. If it cannot proceed, waiting atomically releases that lock and sleeps. Before returning, the wait reacquires the lock, but that does not mean the predicate is still true.\n\nFor example, a producer adds one item and signals a waiting consumer. Before that consumer reacquires the lock, a new consumer gets the lock and takes the item. The original consumer must see the empty queue and wait again. Spurious wakeups are another reason to recheck.\n\nSo I use a loop in Java or a predicate wait in C++. I restore the invariant before waiting or unlocking. After one insertion I can wake one consumer on a dedicated condition. Close changes the answer for every blocked caller, so it broadcasts and every wait checks the closed flag.","follow":"What goes wrong if producers and consumers share one condition and you wake only one thread?","seconds":80,"lesson":"monitor-object-pattern","title":"The monitor object pattern","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"monitor-object-pattern.s2","q":"\"Your permit pool supports close and a timeout. What exactly do those operations guarantee, and how would you test the races?\"","points":["Close rejects acquisitions and wakes all waiters without revoking existing permits","The lock orders an acquire racing with close; release remains valid afterwards","Use one monotonic budget and distinguish condition-wait time from whole-call time","Check accounting, invalid capacity and over-release","Test exhausted capacity, multiple close waiters, timeout and concurrent acquire versus close","Separate notification from joining and destruction; state fairness and cancellation limits"],"model":"I would define close to reject new acquisitions and wake every blocked caller, while allowing existing holders to release their permits. Close is idempotent. An acquisition racing with close succeeds only if its decrement wins under the lock; closing does not revoke a permit already acquired.\n\nThe timeout contract needs equal precision. In this template it budgets the condition wait after locking. It does not bound initial lock acquisition, reacquisition or scheduling delay. If the caller needs a whole-call budget, I compute one monotonic deadline before locking, use timed lock acquisition, and pass only the remaining budget to waits. I never restart the full duration after a wakeup.\n\nI would test an exhausted pool with several waiters, then close it and verify every caller finishes with the documented outcome. I would also test timeout without a release, release racing with timeout, repeated close, and acquisitions racing with close. Coordinated starts and bounded joins make those tests useful; sleeps alone do not establish an interleaving.\n\nAccounting must stay between zero and capacity. I reject negative capacity and obvious over-release, while documenting that full double-release detection needs lease identities. Finally, close only changes state and wakes threads. The owner must join users before destroying a C++ monitor, and fairness requires a separate admission policy.","follow":"How would a FIFO waiter queue handle a timed-out caller at its head?","seconds":90,"lesson":"monitor-object-pattern","title":"The monitor object pattern","track":"core","module":13},{"id":"thread-dumps.s1","q":"\"Our service has stopped answering, CPU is low, and the dump reports no deadlock. Walk me through your next steps.\"","points":["Preserve evidence with bounded captures before a restart when feasible","Use Thread.print with -l for Java platform threads or an all-thread native backtrace for C++","Follow monitor or mutex waiters to the owner and inspect its stack","Trace futures, latches and pool capacity because no deadlock report does not exclude those cycles","Compare repeated stacks with completed work, CPU and dependency metrics","Choose a mitigation and a fix that preserve the application invariant"],"model":"I would capture the wait chains before assuming that no deadlock report means no deadlock. If the incident budget allows it, I take two or three dumps a few seconds apart before a restart. For Java platform threads I use jcmd with Thread.print and the long lock listing. For C++ I collect all thread backtraces and detach the debugger promptly. I group request threads by what they are waiting for. If many wait on one monitor or mutex, I find its owner and inspect its application frames. A holder in a socket read suggests a slow dependency while holding a shared lock. If workers wait on futures, I check where the tasks that complete those futures must run. A full pool waiting for tasks queued to itself can deadlock without a JVM report. Latches and conditions also require tracing the code that should release them. Repeated frames are clues, so I compare completed requests, queue depth and CPU deltas too. Then I choose a mitigation, such as failing over or timing out the dependency. The code fix might move I/O outside the lock, with a recheck before publishing the result. I preserve the evidence and reproduce the dependency chain in a regression test.","follow":"How would your capture strategy change if requests run on Java 21 virtual threads?","seconds":90,"lesson":"thread-dumps","title":"Reading thread dumps and stacks","track":"core","module":14},{"id":"thread-dumps.s2","q":"\"One thread holds account A and wants B. Another holds B and wants A. What have you proved, and how would you fix it?\"","points":["Identify a two-thread wait-for cycle with both locks held during indefinite acquisition","Explain the Java monitor report and how C++ source and stacks establish ownership","Choose a global order on stable unique account ids and handle the same-account case","Explain that timeout retries must release acquired locks and do not guarantee fairness","Test concurrent opposite-direction transfers and conservation of money"],"model":"Assuming both acquisitions wait indefinitely and neither thread releases its first lock, this is a deadlock. The first thread cannot release A until it gets B, and the second cannot release B until it gets A. In a Java platform-thread dump, I match the waiting and held monitor identities. In C++, I use the blocked acquisition frames and the enclosing guard scopes to establish the same cycle. I would acquire account locks in one global order, using stable unique account ids, regardless of transfer direction. I handle a transfer to the same account separately. In C++ a scoped lock over two distinct mutexes is another option for this operation. I would not just add a sleep. If I used timed lock attempts, a failed attempt must release everything already acquired before retrying, with a deadline; that still does not promise fairness. Finally I test opposite-direction transfers concurrently, verify that they finish, and check that total money is conserved. Removing the hang is only half the fix if the balance update becomes unsafe.","follow":"If the owner is waiting on a latch rather than another lock, what evidence would establish the cycle?","seconds":75,"lesson":"thread-dumps","title":"Reading thread dumps and stacks","track":"core","module":14},{"id":"thread-sanitizer.s1","q":"\"Our C++ stress tests passed under ThreadSanitizer a hundred times. Why would you still hesitate to call the class thread-safe?\"","points":["State that no report is evidence rather than proof of thread safety","Explain executed-path coverage and incidental synchronization such as a logging mutex","Name instrumentation gaps, finite history and suppressions as additional limits","Give an atomic check-then-increment interleaving that violates a bound without a data race","Combine TSan with invariant checks, progress deadlines and a targeted regression test","Distinguish Java tools such as jcstress and checked guard annotations"],"model":"I would treat those clean runs as useful evidence, not a proof that the class is thread-safe. ThreadSanitizer reports conflicting memory accesses it can observe and recognize as unordered. It does not verify the class contract. First, our tests might never reach the failing shutdown or error path. Second, a logging mutex can accidentally order accesses in the test schedule, hiding an ordering bug that appears in production. Third, missing instrumentation, finite access history or suppressions can hide an executed race. There is also a different category that TSan cannot diagnose as a data race. Suppose an atomic counter starts at nine and two threads each check that it is below ten before incrementing. Both can read nine, then each increments, giving eleven. Every memory operation is atomic, but the capacity invariant is broken. I would keep TSan in the test suite, inspect both access stacks for every report, and add assertions for the class invariants. I would exercise cancellation, shutdown and resource exhaustion, with deadlines to catch hangs. A discovered production failure becomes a targeted regression test. For Java, I use jcstress for small memory-model outcomes and invariant stress tests plus checked guard annotations for the larger class.","follow":"Can adding logging actually make a race disappear from TSan's report, and why?","seconds":90,"lesson":"thread-sanitizer","title":"ThreadSanitizer and other race detectors","track":"core","module":14},{"id":"thread-sanitizer.s2","q":"\"I made the ready flag atomic, but TSan still reports a race on the payload. Explain the fix and its limits.\"","points":["Distinguish atomicity of the flag from publication of the payload","Write the payload before a release store and require an acquire load that observes it","Explain that relaxed accesses do not provide the required publication edge","State the one-shot and lifetime assumptions, including no later payload mutation","Map publication to Java volatile and use blocking primitives for long waits"],"model":"The flag being atomic prevents a data race on the flag; it does not automatically publish the payload with every memory order. I would inspect the orders on the store and load. In C++, the producer writes the payload and then stores true with release ordering. The consumer loads with acquire ordering and only reads the payload after that load observes the release store. That establishes the happens-before path from the payload write to its read. If both flag operations are relaxed, there is no such publication edge, even if the output looks correct. In Java, a volatile flag gives the publication ordering for the analogous handoff. This assumes a one-shot message: the producer does not modify the payload again after publication, and the object stays alive until the consumer finishes. Resetting the flag and overwriting the message needs another protocol, such as an acknowledgement or a queue. I would rerun the reproducer under TSan and assert the message contents. For a potentially long wait I would use a blocking primitive rather than spend a core polling.","follow":"Why does joining the producer in main not repair an earlier unsynchronized payload read in the consumer?","seconds":75,"lesson":"thread-sanitizer","title":"ThreadSanitizer and other race detectors","track":"core","module":14},{"id":"testing-concurrent-code.s1","q":"\"You have written a bounded blocking queue. Walk me through how you would test it under concurrency.\"","points":["Separate safety properties from progress and define the queue's contract","Use unique items, varied producer and consumer counts, and a start gate","Check exactly-once delivery and FIFO without mistaking completion order for removal order","Distinguish sampled capacity checks from checking every mutation under the lock","Bound startup and completion, propagate worker failures, and clean up or isolate stuck workers","Combine targeted schedules, stress and race detection with a correctness argument"],"model":"I would test both what the queue returns and whether waiting operations can make progress. First I would check sequential FIFO behaviour and capacities one and two. Then I would start several producers and consumers behind a gate, giving every item a producer identifier and sequence number. The gate encourages overlap; it does not choose the schedule. After the workers finish, I would check that every submitted item appears exactly once. Each consumer must observe each producer's sequence in order. I would also use a single-consumer run and small operation histories, because overlapping consumers can finish in a different order from removal. For capacity, synchronized size samples provide useful evidence, but an assertion at each size change under the queue lock catches temporary overflow that sampling misses. I would separately test an empty consumer, a full producer, multiple waiters and Java interruption. Startup and completion need deadlines. Worker exceptions must reach the test thread, and a timeout needs diagnostics plus cancellation or process isolation. Finally I would force suspicious schedules with test hooks, vary the stress configuration and run C++ under ThreadSanitizer. I would explain why the locking invariant is correct as well. Passing tests support that argument; they do not replace it.","follow":"How could your test instrumentation accidentally hide an unsafe-publication bug?","seconds":90,"lesson":"testing-concurrent-code","title":"Testing concurrent code","track":"core","module":14},{"id":"testing-concurrent-code.s2","q":"\"This atomic ticket counter passed thirty thousand stress runs. Would you sign off its check-then-increment logic?\"","points":["Passing stress runs do not make a compound check and increment atomic","Give the last-seat schedule where two callers read four and both increment","Force the schedule with a bounded test-only rendezvous and assert results","Fix with a CAS retry loop or one lock across check and update","Explain why yield, a random seed and a silent sanitizer do not establish correctness"],"model":"No. I would inspect the atomicity of the operation before signing it off. Suppose five tickets are available and four have been sold. Two callers can each read four, both decide there is room, and both increment. Every access is atomic, but the final count is six. ThreadSanitizer has no data race to report. For a regression test I would place a test-only rendezvous after both initial reads, then release both callers and check the successful bookings as well as the count. I would bound the test so a bad hook cannot hang the suite. The fix is one lock across check and update, or a compare-and-set loop that changes four to five only if four is still current. A failed compare-and-set must retry and check the limit again. I would then run the targeted test and broader stress. Yield is only a scheduling hint. A seed repeats generated inputs, not an uncontrolled operating-system schedule. Thirty thousand passes therefore add evidence, but the invariant and the atomic decision are what justify the fix.","follow":"Where would you put the test hook so that CAS retries do not deadlock the test?","seconds":75,"lesson":"testing-concurrent-code","title":"Testing concurrent code","track":"core","module":14},{"id":"performance-and-contention.s1","q":"\"Throughput stops growing after six cores, but the machine has thirty-two cores. What would you check?\"","points":["Compare fixed-work or offered-load measurements and inspect CPU utilization and latency","Use CPU profiles for running work and retries, and lock or off-CPU data for parked waits","Measure lock hold time separately from acquisition wait time","Calculate the per-request lock ceiling and compare it with CPU capacity","Change the measured bottleneck while preserving semantics, then repeat the load test"],"model":"I would first establish what limits useful work before adding threads. I would repeat the same workload at several concurrency levels and record throughput, CPU utilization, queue depth and tail latency. Low CPU with flat throughput suggests waiting, although that wait might be a lock, a dependency or a pool limit. A CPU profile shows running work, including spinning and failed retries. For parked threads I would add lock events or a wall-clock or off-CPU profile, then identify the shared resource and its owner. I would measure hold time separately: time waiting to acquire a lock is not time holding it. Suppose each request takes one metrics lock twenty times and each hold averages eight microseconds. That is one hundred and sixty microseconds of serialized work per request, giving an ideal ceiling of six thousand two hundred and fifty requests per second. I would compare that with cores divided by CPU time per request. If the metrics lock is the measured bottleneck and live totals may be approximate, I would distribute counter updates or hand off local batches safely. Then I would rerun the same load test and check both throughput and p99. The next bottleneck may be bandwidth, CPU or a downstream service, so the measurement must continue.","follow":"Would a lock profile showing eight microseconds of waiting justify that throughput calculation?","seconds":90,"lesson":"performance-and-contention","title":"Performance: contention, Amdahl and profiling","track":"core","module":14},{"id":"performance-and-contention.s2","q":"\"Why not replace every contended counter with a striped counter? How would you decide whether it is an improvement?\"","points":["Choose according to the read and update contract before comparing speed","Explain how striping reduces writes to one shared cache line","State that LongAdder sums and multiple slot reads are not atomic live snapshots","Distinguish metrics from sequence generation, admission limits and exact snapshots","Benchmark the required contract under representative contention and verify correctness separately"],"model":"I would use striping when the read contract permits aggregation. It spreads updates across cells or per-thread slots, reducing the need for every writer to take ownership of one shared cache line. That is attractive for request metrics, but it changes what a read means. A LongAdder sum during updates is not an atomic snapshot, and reading several C++ atomic slots separately does not make the combined result atomic. After writers stop and join, the final total can be exact. A unique sequence number or a strict admission limit needs a single atomic decision, so an adder is not a replacement. An exact snapshot across shards needs extra coordination. To compare performance I would benchmark the contract the application actually requires, including reads, merges and realistic hot-key skew. I would use JMH or Google Benchmark with warmup, repeated measurements and optimized builds, then measure service latency under representative load. I would check correctness separately under stress and sanitizers. A faster update loop is useful only if the complete design still delivers the required result.","follow":"How would you obtain an exact snapshot from per-thread maps without reading them while their owners mutate them?","seconds":75,"lesson":"performance-and-contention","title":"Performance: contention, Amdahl and profiling","track":"core","module":14},{"id":"server-threading-models.s1","q":"\"You have 100,000 mostly idle client connections and blocking database calls. Which threading model would you choose, and where would you put the limits?\"","points":["Choose event loops for many idle sockets and a bounded executor for blocking database work","Distinguish open connections from active requests and budget buffers and descriptors","Use non-blocking admission before offloading; never wait or run rejected work on the loop","Keep connection state on its owner and post worker results back safely","Bound downstream concurrency and total admitted work, with deadlines and overload behaviour","Explain that virtual threads simplify waiting but do not increase downstream capacity"],"model":"I would use event loops for the sockets and a bounded executor for the blocking database calls. Most of those connections are idle, so dedicating an operating-system thread to each would waste memory. I would budget descriptors, socket buffers and application state separately from the number of active requests. A hundred thousand connections does not mean a hundred thousand database queries should be admitted.\nBefore offloading, I would try to acquire admission without blocking the loop. If the database budget or worker queue is full, I would reject promptly or return a permitted degraded response. I would not use a rejection policy that runs the database call on the submitting event loop.\nEach loop owns its connection state. Workers receive immutable input and post results back through a thread-safe queue, so offloading does not quietly introduce shared mutable state. Requests carry deadlines, and late results are discarded if the connection has already finished.\nJava virtual threads are another reasonable implementation when the libraries support them. They simplify the waiting code, but I would retain the same admission and database limits. Finally, I would load-test queue age, loop lag, memory and rejection rates before claiming that this design meets the target.","follow":"How would you stop new work and shut down without losing worker completions?","seconds":90,"lesson":"server-threading-models","title":"Server threading models","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"server-threading-models.s2","q":"\"We added a semaphore around database calls, but when the database slows down even our health endpoint hangs. What would you check?\"","points":["A semaphore limits active calls but can leave every shared worker waiting","Inspect worker stacks, queue age and loop lag to distinguish pool exhaustion from loop blocking","Move admission before shared worker occupation or isolate the dependency in a bounded executor","Propagate deadlines and cancellation without assuming backend work has stopped","Validate unrelated endpoint latency and the bounded wait population under injected dependency delay"],"model":"The semaphore may be limiting database concurrency while still exhausting the shared worker pool. If every worker blocks while acquiring a permit, the health endpoint has no worker left, even though it never uses the database. I would inspect worker stacks and queue age, then check event-loop lag to rule out a blocking call on the loop itself.\nI would move non-blocking admission ahead of occupying shared workers, or isolate database work in its own bounded executor. Excess calls should fail promptly or use an allowed degraded response. The wait population needs a bound as well as the number of active queries.\nI would propagate the request deadline into the database operation and attempt cancellation when that deadline expires. Cancellation is not proof that remote work stopped, so I would also check the client's actual timeout and cancellation behaviour.\nTo validate the fix, I would inject database delay while measuring health-endpoint latency, queue age, rejection counts and the number of waiting requests. More worker threads alone would postpone the failure rather than contain the slow dependency.","follow":"What changes if the request handlers use Java 21 virtual threads?","seconds":75,"lesson":"server-threading-models","title":"Server threading models","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"io-models.s1","q":"\"How can one thread serve thousands of sockets? Compare readiness with completion without just naming APIs.\"","points":["Use non-blocking sockets and one readiness wait for many registered descriptors","Attempt I/O after readiness and handle partial results, would-block, EOF and errors","Contrast repeated full interest-list submission with retained registrations and event batches","Completion reports the result of a submitted operation, not readiness or a whole message","Keep handlers short and bound connection buffers; treat regular-file I/O separately"],"model":"One thread can serve many sockets because it does work only for sockets that can make progress. I would configure them as non-blocking, register their interests and wait for a batch of readiness events. After a notification, I attempt the operation and handle partial data, would-block, EOF or an error. Readiness is not a reservation of bytes or a complete request.\nWith poll, each wait submits the watched array again. Epoll and kqueue retain registrations and return ready events, which avoids repeatedly submitting the whole idle population. There is still kernel bookkeeping and application work, so this is not a promise of constant cost.\nCompletion changes the contract. I submit an operation with a buffer and later receive its result. A successful read has already placed bytes in that buffer, but it may still be partial. The buffer must remain valid until completion.\nIn either design, handlers must be short and buffers bounded. Regular files need special care: readiness does not prevent a cache-missing disk read from blocking, so I would offload that call or use supported asynchronous file operations.","follow":"What would make a mostly idle readiness loop consume an entire core?","seconds":75,"lesson":"io-models","title":"Blocking, non-blocking and multiplexed I/O","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"io-models.s2","q":"\"Our edge-triggered server hangs on large requests. It reads once per event and CPU usage is low. Walk me through the failure and the fix.\"","points":["Unread bytes do not guarantee another edge-triggered notification","Give an interleaving with more buffered data than one read consumes","Use non-blocking reads until would-block, handling interruption, EOF and errors","Apply a fairness budget with an application ready queue if draining stops early","Test buffered excess data with no later arrivals rather than relying on TCP write boundaries"],"model":"The likely bug is waiting for another edge while unread data is already available. Edge-triggered readiness does not promise repeated reminders just because bytes remain in the socket buffer.\nFor example, suppose two thousand bytes are buffered when the event arrives. The handler reads five hundred and twelve, then goes back to waiting. The remaining fourteen hundred and eighty-eight bytes can sit there indefinitely if the client waits for a response and sends nothing more. Low CPU fits that symptom: the loop is waiting, not spinning.\nI would use non-blocking descriptors and keep reading until would-block, while handling interrupted calls, EOF and real errors. A blocking descriptor would make the final read stall the entire loop.\nI would also protect fairness. If a hot connection exhausts its work budget before it is drained, I would put it on an application ready queue and resume it without requiring another kernel event. Level-triggered mode is a simpler alternative.\nThe regression test should leave more data buffered than one read consumes, with no later arrival. One large TCP write alone does not guarantee that setup.","follow":"How does the rule change for writes when the application's output queue becomes empty?","seconds":75,"lesson":"io-models","title":"Blocking, non-blocking and multiplexed I/O","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"io-models.s3","q":"\"An asynchronous read times out. Can I immediately recycle its buffer for another request?\"","points":["A request timeout does not prove the underlying operation stopped using its buffer","Keep storage valid until the operation's completion or cancellation contract permits reuse","Resolve the timeout-completion race once on the state owner and ignore late delivery","Bound outstanding buffers and distinguish local cancellation from remote side effects"],"model":"No. A request timeout does not prove that the underlying read has stopped using the buffer. I have to follow the I/O API's completion and cancellation contract before recycling that storage.\nI would mark the request timed out on its state owner and request cancellation if the API supports it. The completion path still has to run or otherwise reach a documented final state. If completion wins the race, it may deliver bytes; if timeout wins, I discard that late result for the caller, while still performing cleanup exactly once.\nUntil the operation is finished, I keep the buffer valid and avoid resizing or reusing it for another request. I also bound outstanding operations so timed-out requests cannot leave unlimited buffers retained. Finally, cancellation of local waiting says nothing by itself about whether a remote server already performed a write or another side effect.","follow":"How would you prevent a late completion from targeting a newly opened connection that reused the same descriptor number?","seconds":60,"lesson":"io-models","title":"Blocking, non-blocking and multiplexed I/O","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"queueing-math.s1","q":"\"We expect two thousand requests a second, averaging fifty milliseconds. How many threads do we need?\"","points":["Little's law gives 100 requests in flight on average using seconds","The rate, latency and occupancy must describe the same stable boundary","100 threads follows only if one thread is held for the entire request","Connections and CPU need their own occupancy or service-demand calculations","Choose headroom through downstream limits and load testing, not a percentile substitution"],"model":"We need about one hundred requests in flight on average, but that does not yet tell us the thread count. Little's law multiplies two thousand requests per second by fifty milliseconds, or point zero five seconds. I would check that both measurements describe the same stable system boundary and include the same requests. If each request holds a worker for its entire lifetime, those hundred requests occupy about a hundred workers. If requests wait asynchronously, they can share far fewer threads. Database connections have a different hold time, so I would size that pool separately. CPU capacity depends on CPU time per request, not time waiting on the network. I would then choose headroom for bursts, check downstream limits and load test against the latency objective. One hundred is an average, not a safe maximum or a p99 promise. Substituting p99 latency into Little's law does not fix that. If the backlog is growing throughout the measurement, I would first separate arrival rate from throughput and examine the transient.","follow":"What changes if the same requests now take two hundred milliseconds?","seconds":75,"lesson":"queueing-math","title":"Queueing math for design rounds","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"queueing-math.s2","q":"\"Our bottleneck is ninety percent busy. Explain why requests wait so long, and tell me what your formula assumes.\"","points":["Bursts need spare service capacity to drain; average capacity alone is insufficient","State FIFO M/M/1 assumptions and utilization below one","With mean service 10 ms, mean wait is 90 ms and mean total is 100 ms","A shared multi-server pool needs a different model","Measure the real workload; no universal safe utilization threshold"],"model":"Requests wait because bursts take time to drain, and at ninety percent utilization there is little spare capacity. For a concrete calculation, assume one FIFO server, Poisson arrivals, independent exponential service times, and no rejection or abandonment. The mean service time is ten milliseconds. Mean waiting is service time multiplied by rho over one minus rho, so ninety milliseconds. Adding service gives a hundred milliseconds in the system. Those are averages, not deadlines. The steady-state formula requires utilization below one. I would not apply it directly to thirty parallel database connections just because they share one queue. That needs a multi-server model and realistic hold times. There is also no universal safe threshold at seventy percent. I would measure the bottleneck, variability and latency distribution, then test whether added capacity or reduced service demand meets our objective.","follow":"Could perfectly regular arrivals and service behave differently at full utilization?","seconds":60,"lesson":"queueing-math","title":"Queueing math for design rounds","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"backpressure-and-load-shedding.s1","q":"\"Traffic is twelve hundred requests a second, but we can finish only a thousand. What happens, and how would you protect the service?\"","points":["Without rejection or abandonment, backlog grows by 200 requests per second","After a minute the FIFO backlog is about 12,000, implying roughly 12 seconds of waiting at unchanged throughput","Bound admission by items, bytes and useful queue-wait budget; a cap is not a deadline guarantee","Reject cheaply or push back only when producers can safely wait","Propagate deadlines and cancellation; use retry budgets and jitter","Measure useful completions, rejection rate and queue age"],"model":"I would bound admission and shed excess work before it consumes expensive resources. With no rejection or abandonment, the backlog grows by two hundred requests a second. After a minute that is twelve thousand requests. At an unchanged thousand completions a second, a new FIFO arrival waits roughly twelve seconds. A larger queue cannot fix that sustained rate gap. I would budget queued items and bytes from the useful waiting time, after reserving time for service, and limit active work as well. At an external boundary I would reject cheaply with an overload response. Inside the system I could make producers wait briefly, provided they are not event-loop threads or holding locks the consumers need. Every request should carry a deadline, and workers should skip dispensable expired work. Accepted durable effects need their own completion contract. Clients need retry budgets, backoff and jitter, because synchronized retries can keep the service overloaded. I would measure useful completions before deadlines, rejection rate and queue age while testing recovery.","follow":"Why can a bounded queue still exhaust memory if producers are allowed to block without limit?","seconds":75,"lesson":"backpressure-and-load-shedding","title":"Backpressure, bounded queues and load shedding","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"backpressure-and-load-shedding.s2","q":"\"A Kafka consumer is falling behind. Is that backpressure, and how do you work out recovery time?\"","points":["Consumer lag records backlog; it does not automatically slow producers","Distinguish offset lag from record count and delayed commits","For 120,000 pending records, consumption 2,000/s and production 1,500/s, catch-up takes 240 seconds","For one FIFO partition, an item joining its backlog waits roughly 60 seconds at that consumption rate","Bound storage and age, manage consumer progress and use safe replay semantics"],"model":"Consumer lag is backlog, not automatic backpressure on producers. Producers can keep appending while a slow consumer falls further behind. I would first check what the lag metric measures: committed offsets can trail completed processing, and offset distance is not always a record count. Suppose one FIFO partition has a hundred and twenty thousand pending records. If consumers handle two thousand a second while producers add fifteen hundred, we recover at only five hundred a second. Catch-up takes two hundred and forty seconds. That differs from the roughly sixty seconds an item joining the current FIFO backlog waits. I would bound retained bytes and message age, then improve processing or add consumers where partition ownership allows it. Pausing consumption can protect a local worker queue, but the consumer still needs to poll for group progress. Finally, replay and timeout handling must not duplicate unsafe effects.","follow":"What happens if production reaches the consumer group's full processing rate?","seconds":60,"lesson":"backpressure-and-load-shedding","title":"Backpressure, bounded queues and load shedding","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"bulkheads-timeouts-retries.s1","q":"\"Payments gets slow and checkout starts timing out. Walk me through how you stop that from taking down login and search too.\"","points":["Bound payment concurrency and admission waits so unrelated work retains capacity","Propagate one end-to-end deadline and include queues, attempts and response overhead","Request cancellation but keep permits until the protected resource is really released","Retry only safe transient failures at one layer with jitter, attempt limits and a shared budget","Use a breaker with bounded probes and an honest, durable fallback","Measure cancellation lag, rejections and dependency latency to validate isolation"],"model":"I would bound the damage at payments first. Give it a separate concurrency cap and reject quickly when that cap is full. If blocked calls could exhaust the request workers, use a separate bounded pool or nonblocking I/O, and reserve capacity for unrelated work. A semaphore with thousands of waiting callers is not enough. Next, carry one checkout deadline through admission, connection setup, every attempt and the response. For example, an eight hundred millisecond request cannot spend eight hundred milliseconds again at each dependency. On timeout I request cancellation, but I keep the operation counted until it actually releases its resource. Cancellation is cooperative, and an uncertain charge may already have committed. I retry only a transient, safely repeatable operation, using the same idempotency key for a payment. One layer owns those retries, with jitter, a small attempt count and a shared extra-attempt budget. Persistent failures open a circuit breaker; a bounded probe checks recovery. A payment-pending response is valid only after I durably record work that will be reconciled later. Otherwise I return an honest error. Finally I check rejection rates, active calls, cancellation lag and login latency. Those measurements tell me whether the isolation works under failure.","follow":"What happens if the payment client ignores interruption and its socket timeout?","seconds":90,"lesson":"bulkheads-timeouts-retries","title":"Bulkheads, timeouts, retries and circuit breakers","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"bulkheads-timeouts-retries.s2","q":"\"Your circuit breaker uses one lock, but an old successful call closes it while the recovery probe is still running. How would you fix that?\"","points":["Identify a logical state-machine race that locking alone does not prevent","Walk through admission while closed, opening, probing and a late old success","Tie every admitted call to a state generation and ignore stale or duplicate completion","Allow only bounded probes and keep remote work outside the state lock","Bound probe lifetime and classify outcomes without counting local rejection as a dependency failure"],"model":"I would attach an admission ticket to every call, including the breaker's current generation. One lock makes field updates atomic, but it cannot tell whether a result belongs to the current recovery attempt. Suppose call A starts while the breaker is closed. Other calls fail and open it. After the cool-down, probe P enters half-open. If A now succeeds, an untagged completion can incorrectly close the breaker and release a burst before P finishes. Each state transition therefore creates a new generation. Completion checks that its ticket still matches, and that the ticket has not already been reported. A's stale success is ignored; only P can decide whether this half-open generation closes or reopens. I use the lock for admission and completion, never across the network call. I also bound probe concurrency and lifetime so a lost completion cannot block recovery forever. Finally, I distinguish dependency failures from local bulkhead rejection or caller cancellation. Those local events must not be counted as evidence that the dependency is unhealthy.","follow":"How would you test stale completions and simultaneous probe admission without relying on sleeps?","seconds":75,"lesson":"bulkheads-timeouts-retries","title":"Bulkheads, timeouts, retries and circuit breakers","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"bulkheads-timeouts-retries.s3","q":"\"We already cap each request at three attempts. Why do you also want a retry budget and jitter?\"","points":["An attempt limit still permits large aggregate load when all originals fail","Nested layers multiply attempts, for example three layers of three give 27","Define a shared budget using retries divided by original requests over a window","Jitter spreads retry timing but does not guarantee a per-second bound","Preserve idempotency, one deadline and server-requested delays"],"model":"Three attempts caps one request, but can still triple traffic when every original fails. With three retrying layers, that becomes up to twenty-seven calls to the bottom service for one user request. I choose one retry owner and also cap aggregate extra attempts. For example, ten retries per hundred original requests over a defined window limits extra load to ten percent in that window. I specify the denominator because ten percent of all attempts is a different policy. Jitter solves another problem: clients that fail together should not all retry together. It randomises a capped backoff delay, but does not guarantee evenly spaced requests or a hard per-second limit. Every retry must still be safe to repeat, fit the remaining deadline and respect any server-requested delay. If those checks fail, I stop retrying and return a fallback or error.","follow":"Where would you enforce that budget if the application, SDK and proxy can all retry?","seconds":60,"lesson":"bulkheads-timeouts-retries","title":"Bulkheads, timeouts, retries and circuit breakers","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"rate-vs-concurrency-limits.s1","q":"\"We already allow only a hundred requests a second. Why would you add a concurrency limit as well?\"","points":["Rate bounds starts per time while concurrency bounds simultaneous resource use","Compute mean occupancy of 5 at 50 ms and 500 at 5 seconds for 100 per second","State that Little's law uses matching stable means and overload can instead create queues","Show the reverse blind spot: a concurrency cap cannot enforce a strict time-window quota","Combine bounded admission, quota semantics and fleet-aware sizing"],"model":"I would add it because the rate limit does not bound how long requests occupy resources. At a hundred requests per second and a mean fifty milliseconds, average occupancy is five. If duration becomes five seconds at the same completed throughput, average occupancy becomes five hundred. Little's law uses stable means at the same boundary; if the connection pool cannot support that throughput, requests queue or get rejected instead. A concurrency limit caps active work even when latency rises. It has the opposite blind spot, though: twenty slots with one millisecond calls can theoretically serve twenty thousand calls per second. That can exceed a partner quota or budget. I therefore use a rate policy matching the client's contract, including whether bursts are allowed, and a concurrency cap matching resource capacity. Admission either rejects promptly or waits in a small bounded queue within the deadline. I also sum limits across the fleet. Forty slots on each of twenty instances means up to eight hundred calls to the dependency, regardless of each instance looking healthy.","follow":"Does a token bucket refilling at a hundred per second enforce exactly six thousand calls in every rolling minute?","seconds":75,"lesson":"rate-vs-concurrency-limits","title":"Rate limits versus concurrency limits","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"rate-vs-concurrency-limits.s2","q":"\"Design admission control for a multi-tenant service with async calls to a shared database. What are the correctness traps?\"","points":["Define separate tenant rate, tenant concurrency and shared dependency budgets","Atomically refill and consume each tenant bucket and explain rejected-attempt charging","Acquire permits before resource use and unwind partial acquisition on rejection","Keep async permits until resource use ends, including cancellation and fan-out","Coordinate bucket eviction and fleet budgets so they cannot duplicate capacity","Measure outcomes and treat adaptive feedback as a heuristic within hard bounds"],"model":"I would enforce separate budgets for tenant traffic and database capacity. Each tenant gets a rate and burst allowance, plus a concurrency cap so slow queries cannot occupy all shared slots. The database also gets a shared concurrency budget allocated across every caller and instance. For each tenant, refilling and consuming a token must be one atomic operation. I would document whether requests rejected later still spend that token; charging attempts is a reasonable policy, but it differs from billing completed work. Then I acquire tenant and database permits in a consistent order, releasing the first if the second fails. Because the work is async, returning from the submission method cannot release those permits. An exactly-once completion path releases them when the protected resource is actually free. Cancellation of the caller's future is not enough, and each parallel query or hedge needs its own slot. I would also bound client-state memory. Naive bucket eviction can leave an old bucket in use while a new full bucket admits more requests for the same tenant. Finally, I monitor admissions, rejections, queue time, active queries and cancellation lag. Adaptive tuning can help, but I keep hard bounds and do not assume local controllers guarantee a fair global share.","follow":"How would you lower the live concurrency limit below the number of requests already running?","seconds":90,"lesson":"rate-vs-concurrency-limits","title":"Rate limits versus concurrency limits","track":"core","module":15},{"id":"db-isolation-and-anomalies.s1","q":"\"Both transactions used repeatable read, so how did both doctors still go off call? Walk me through it and fix it.\"","points":["Identify write skew and the at-least-one-doctor invariant","Both snapshots show two doctors; the transactions write different rows","Postgres REPEATABLE READ detects same-row write conflicts, not this dependency","Lock all relevant existing rows in a common order at READ COMMITTED, then check the returned rows","Alternatively use SERIALIZABLE for both transactions and retry the whole aborted attempt"],"model":"This is write skew: a consistent snapshot does not make a decision across rows safe. The rule is that at least one doctor stays on call. Alice and Bob each see a snapshot with two doctors. Alice changes only Alice's row, and Bob changes only Bob's row. Both changes can commit under Postgres repeatable read because they do not write the same row. The result is zero doctors, which no valid serial execution of those decisions would produce. One fix is to use read committed, lock all the shift's on-call rows in a consistent order, and count the rows actually returned before deciding. The second request then sees only one doctor and refuses to leave. The check and write must remain in the same transaction, and every relevant writer must follow that protocol. Alternatively, I would run both transactions at serializable and retry an aborted attempt from the beginning, including its reads. I would keep external side effects out of that retry loop. The choice depends on contention and how easily we can identify the complete set of dependencies.","follow":"What changes if the rule depends on rows that do not exist yet?","seconds":75,"lesson":"db-isolation-and-anomalies","title":"Database isolation levels and anomalies","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"db-isolation-and-anomalies.s2","q":"\"We added FOR UPDATE to our availability check, but rooms still get double booked. What would you inspect and change?\"","points":["Check the isolation level, transaction boundaries and whether the query returned any rows","Explain that an empty PostgreSQL locking read cannot exclude competing booking inserts","Distinguish write skew over a predicate from observing a phantom on a repeated query","Use an exclusion constraint with non-null room ids and valid half-open ranges","Offer a parent-row lock followed by a separate fresh check at READ COMMITTED, or SERIALIZABLE with retries"],"model":"I would first check what was actually locked. At Postgres read committed, if both availability queries find no overlapping booking, neither locks a booking row that can exclude the other insert. Both requests can then commit. That is write skew over an absence predicate; neither request needs to repeat its query and observe a phantom. I would inspect the transaction boundaries too, because a lock released by autocommit cannot protect a later insert. For arbitrary time intervals, I would enforce the rule with an exclusion constraint on room equality and interval overlap. Room ids and ranges must be non-null, and valid half-open intervals let one booking end exactly when another begins. A conflicting insert becomes a clear booking conflict. An alternative is to lock an existing room row, then run a separate availability query at read committed before inserting. Every booking writer must cooperate. I would not assume that waiting for that parent lock refreshes a repeatable-read snapshot. Serializable with whole-transaction retries is another option when the invariant cannot conveniently be expressed as a constraint.","follow":"Why does a parent-row lock followed by a check need special care at repeatable read?","seconds":75,"lesson":"db-isolation-and-anomalies","title":"Database isolation levels and anomalies","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"optimistic-vs-pessimistic.s1","q":"\"Would you use optimistic or pessimistic locking for this API? Tell me what you need to know before choosing.\"","points":["Start with the invariant and prefer a conditional statement for a simple single-row rule","Choose version checking for rare conflicts or long user think time","On zero affected rows, handle absence or re-read and recompute with bounded jittered retries","Choose short pessimistic transactions when contention and recomputation cost justify waiting","State that optimistic database writes still lock and neither choice validates an arbitrary multi-row invariant"],"model":"I would start with the invariant, then ask about conflict frequency, transaction duration and the cost of redoing work. If the rule is simply to decrement positive stock, one conditional update is my first choice. I check its affected-row count and commit before reporting success. For a document that a user may edit for minutes, I would use a version check or a strong ETag. That avoids holding a lock during think time. If no row matches, I handle deletion or fetch the latest value and resolve the conflict. For a replayable operation, I recompute from that value, with bounded retries and jitter; I do not resend the old computed result. If conflicts are frequent and the transaction is short, locking before reading may be cheaper than repeated failed work. I would take multiple locks in a consistent order and measure waits. Optimistic writes still take database locks, so this is not a lock-free guarantee. Finally, if the rule spans several rows, I need to protect all its dependencies, not just add a version to the row being changed.","follow":"How could a pessimistic fallback safely coexist with existing optimistic writers?","seconds":75,"lesson":"optimistic-vs-pessimistic","title":"Optimistic versus pessimistic concurrency control","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"optimistic-vs-pessimistic.s2","q":"\"Twenty workers poll one jobs table. How would you stop duplicate claims and recover when a worker crashes?\"","points":["Claim an unlocked queued row using FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED in a short transaction","Record running state, an expiry and a fresh token per claim, then commit before processing","Renew and finish conditionally on the current token and state; re-queue expired claims","Explain why a reusable worker id cannot distinguish successive claims","Identify at-least-once external effects and require destination idempotency or fencing","State that SKIP LOCKED provides neither strict FIFO nor proof of an empty queue"],"model":"I would claim work in a short database transaction using FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED. The transaction selects an unlocked queued job, marks it running, and records an expiry and a fresh claim token. I commit that claim before doing the work, so the worker does not hold database locks during a long task. A sweeper re-queues expired claims, and long-running workers renew only their own still-valid claim. Completion checks the token and running state, so an old attempt cannot complete a newer attempt's job. The token must change on every claim; a worker id alone is unsafe if that worker later claims the same job again. This still gives at-least-once processing. A worker can send an email or charge a payment and crash before recording completion. The external destination therefore needs idempotency, or fencing where the protected resource supports it. Finally, skipping locked rows means we do not get strict FIFO. An empty poll means no unlocked candidate was found at that moment, not that all work is finished. Other locks and I/O can still delay the query.","follow":"What happens if the payment succeeds but the worker loses its lease before marking the job done?","seconds":75,"lesson":"optimistic-vs-pessimistic","title":"Optimistic versus pessimistic concurrency control","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"idempotency-and-exactly-once.s1","q":"\"The payment timed out, so the client retries. Walk me through how you prevent a second charge, including a server crash.\"","points":["A timeout leaves the outcome unknown, so a retry must retain the logical operation identity","Persist one key per payment, scope it by client and operation, and validate the request fingerprint","Atomically claim the key and commit database effects with the stored response","Concurrent duplicates replay the winner; an aborted database transaction permits another claimant","External charges need the same stable provider key and reconciliation of uncertain outcomes","Define retention and recovery boundaries; an in-memory map or a new retry key is insufficient"],"model":"I would make the payment idempotent around a durable operation identity. A timeout means I stopped waiting, not that the charge failed. The client creates one key before its first attempt, persists it with the pending payment, and reuses it after retries or restarts. The server scopes that key by authenticated client and operation, and rejects a different request under the same key. For database-only effects, I insert the key under a unique constraint, perform the debit, and store the response in one transaction. A concurrent duplicate waits for that claim to resolve. If it commits, the duplicate replays the response; if it rolls back, another request can claim the key. An external card charge needs another boundary. I first persist the pending operation, send a stable key to a provider that deduplicates, and then record its result. If I crash after the charge, recovery queries or retries that same provider operation. I do not delete the key because of a timeout. Finally, I define the retention horizon across clients, queues and provider recovery. After deduplication expires, a blind retry can charge again, so permanent payment identity or reconciliation may still be necessary. An in-memory future map only demonstrates concurrency within one process.","follow":"What if the payment provider has forgotten the key before your recovery job runs?","seconds":90,"lesson":"idempotency-and-exactly-once","title":"Idempotency, retries and exactly-once effects","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"idempotency-and-exactly-once.s2","q":"\"We already use Kafka transactions. Why do you still want an outbox and idempotency keys?\"","points":["Kafka transactions atomically commit Kafka output records and input progress within their boundary","Processing may run again even when only one committed output becomes visible","Database commits and external charges are outside that Kafka transaction","An outbox commits the business change and publication intent locally, then relays with possible duplicates","Consumers deduplicate event ids with their database effects; external providers enforce their own keys","Ordering and retention are separate requirements, not automatic consequences of deduplication"],"model":"Kafka transactions protect Kafka input progress and Kafka output records; they do not automatically include our database or payment provider. A processor may run a batch again after a crash, while only one committed output is visible to consumers reading committed records. Suppose that processor charges a card and then aborts its Kafka transaction. The charge is still real, and replaying the batch can charge again. That call needs a stable payment key enforced by the provider. For a database update followed by event publication, I use an outbox because two independent commits leave a crash window. The business change and an event row commit together in the database. A relay publishes the event and records acknowledgement. If it crashes after publishing, it can publish again, so the consumer records the event id in the same transaction as its own database effect. If the consumer sends email, that is another external boundary with its own delivery contract. I would also explain ordering separately: partitioning by order id preserves publication order, but does not repair two relays publishing business events backwards. Finally, the guarantee lasts only through the retention and recovery assumptions we actually enforce. The useful claim is one observable effect within a named boundary, not processing code that can never execute twice.","follow":"What happens if the relay publishes event 42 before an earlier transaction containing event 41 commits?","seconds":90,"lesson":"idempotency-and-exactly-once","title":"Idempotency, retries and exactly-once effects","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"distributed-locks-and-fencing.s1","q":"\"A worker checks its lease before every write, but an old report still overwrites a new one. Walk me through the failure and the fix.\"","points":["A worker can pause after checking its lease, or its write can be delayed in transit","Expiry permits a replacement without stopping the old worker","Grants carry increasing tokens and storage atomically checks the token with the write","After storage accepts 34, it rejects 33; granting 34 elsewhere alone does not update storage","Token and accepted-generation state must survive failover and every write path must enforce them","Equal-token retries and multiple writes need separate idempotency or operation ordering"],"model":"I would fence writes at storage, because checking a lease in the worker leaves a gap before the effect. Worker A checks its lease and pauses. The lease expires, worker B takes over and writes a new report, and then A resumes and overwrites it. A delayed network request can cause the same failure even without a process pause. I would give each ownership grant an increasing token and include it in every write. Storage checks that token, updates its accepted generation and changes the report atomically. Once it has accepted token 34, it rejects A's token 33. There is an important boundary: merely granting 34 at the lock service does not update storage. If storage has only seen 32, it can still accept 33. B should install its generation before reading the state used to compute its report, or use an ownership check in the resource's transaction. The token allocator and storage's accepted generation must survive failover without going backwards, and every write path must enforce the check. Finally, fencing does not deduplicate two writes with the same token or order a holder's own operations. I would use operation ids or version checks for those requirements, and test both stale writes and delayed duplicate writes.","follow":"Would rejecting equal tokens as well as smaller ones solve duplicate writes?","seconds":90,"lesson":"distributed-locks-and-fencing","title":"Distributed locks, leases and fencing tokens","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"distributed-locks-and-fencing.s2","q":"\"Three schedulers send nightly invoices. Would you use Redis, ZooKeeper, or the database to make this safe?\"","points":["Start with the invariant and use database uniqueness for one invoice per customer and period","Commit invoice creation and email publication intent in one transaction","A database claim alone cannot make an external email exactly once; downstream deduplication or an explicit tradeoff is required","Recover job ownership with an expiry and increasing attempt, checked transactionally with every effect","Redis can reduce harmless duplicate work; consensus coordination still needs resource enforcement","Describe deterministic crash, takeover and lost-acknowledgement tests"],"model":"I would start with database invariants, since the invoices already live there. A unique customer and billing-period key prevents duplicate invoice records. The transaction that creates the invoice also inserts an email outbox row with a stable operation id. Sending the email is a separate boundary. Marking it sent before calling the provider can lose an email after a crash; marking it afterwards can duplicate an email after a lost reply. I need provider deduplication with that operation id, or an explicit decision to tolerate loss or duplication. For scheduler recovery, I would keep a job row with an expiry and an increasing attempt number. A replacement claims an expired run atomically. Each transaction that records progress or queues emails locks that job row and verifies the current attempt, so a resumed old worker cannot commit new effects after takeover. A Redis lease can reduce duplicate computation once those effects are safe. ZooKeeper or etcd can provide stronger coordination state, but neither automatically fences the database or email provider. I would test a paused worker across takeover, a crash after invoice commit, and a provider success whose acknowledgement is lost. That tells us which guarantees we actually have, rather than assuming that one scheduler means one effect.","follow":"How would you recover a pending email if the provider has no idempotency support?","seconds":90,"lesson":"distributed-locks-and-fencing","title":"Distributed locks, leases and fencing tokens","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"ordering-by-ownership.s1","q":"\"Why would you route account updates through one owner, and what problems does that leave you with?\"","points":["One serial owner per key confines both reads and writes; the queue still synchronizes handoff","Queue order is not a business order across concurrent producers","Bound queues and explain one hot key's serial capacity limit","Choose batching or sharding only where the invariant permits it","Recover durable state and fence the old owner during handoff","Cross-owner transfers need coordination rather than a different routing hash"],"model":"I would use one serial owner per account to make its state transitions easy to reason about. Every read and write goes through that owner's queue, so the account map needs no lock. The queue still needs synchronization, and a result or snapshot needs safe publication. The order I get is queue arrival order. If several producers create business events, I also need a sequence and a rule for gaps and duplicates. The tradeoff is capacity. One hot account can overwhelm its owner even while other owners are idle. I would bound the queue, measure lag and batch work where possible. Independent credits can sometimes be sharded and summed, but I cannot authorize a withdrawal against an inconsistent sum. Adding partitions alone does not make one account execute in parallel. When ownership moves, the new owner restores durable state and the storage rejects writes carrying the old epoch. Retries also need deduplication committed with the state change. Finally, a transfer between different owners still needs a transaction or an explicit saga. Choosing one account as coordinator does not let it bypass the other account's ownership. This trades shared-state locking for an explicit queue, capacity limit and recovery protocol.","follow":"What happens if a task synchronously waits for another task queued on its own owner?","seconds":90,"lesson":"ordering-by-ownership","title":"Ordering by ownership: partitions and single writers","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"ordering-by-ownership.s2","q":"\"Offsets 40 and 42 have finished, but 41 is still running. The consumer commits 43 and crashes. Walk me through the bug and the fix.\"","points":["A committed offset is the next record to read, so restart at 43 skips unfinished 41","Track a completed prefix and commit at most 41 in this example","Preserve same-key order with serial lanes and bound their queues","Persist state and deduplication together before advancing the commit","Rebalances need revocation handling and storage fencing for stale workers"],"model":"The commit skips unfinished work. Offset 43 means restart from 43, so after the crash record 41 will not be delivered again through the normal group resume. I can commit 41 here, because 40 has finished, but I must stop at that gap even though 42 completed. After 41 finishes, the completed prefix can advance through 42 and the next position is 43. I would track completion separately for each partition and route same-key records through serial lanes. If 41 and 42 share a key, allowing 42 to apply first is already an ordering bug. The consumer thread owns polling and commits, and pauses overloaded partitions while bounded worker queues drain. State changes and deduplication metadata must be durable together before advancing the commit, since a crash before committing can replay completed work. On reassignment I stop dispatching old work and either drain it within a deadline or abandon it safely. Kafka rejects stale group commits, but a database must separately fence the old worker's writes. External effects still need an idempotency key.","follow":"How would you recover if a permanently failing record blocks the completed prefix?","seconds":75,"lesson":"ordering-by-ownership","title":"Ordering by ownership: partitions and single writers","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"design-cases.s1","q":"\"A customer paid while their seat hold expired. How would your booking flow prevent a double sale and recover the payment?\"","points":["Database owns the seat invariant and a unique constraint prevents duplicate current tickets","Confirm checks the exact hold generation, user and live expiry after acquiring the row lock","Authorize first; commit booking, retry result and capture outbox atomically","Resolve unknown confirmation outcomes before voiding or repeating work","Retry capture with its stable key and reconcile terminal or ambiguous failures"],"model":"I would make the database decide seat ownership and treat payment as a recoverable workflow. Every hold has a unique id, a user and an expiry. To confirm, I lock the seat row and check that exact hold is still live using current database time. Checking only the user is insufficient because an old checkout could consume a later hold belonging to the same user. A unique seat constraint is the final duplicate-ticket backstop. I authorize payment first, then commit the booking, its retry result and a capture request in an outbox together. If the hold has genuinely been lost, I void the authorization. If the database call timed out, I first look up the recorded order result, because the booking may already have committed. After a successful booking, the outbox worker captures with a stable operation-specific key. A crash before capture is then recoverable. A terminal capture failure needs a defined cancellation policy, and an ambiguous provider response needs reconciliation before I release the seat. I never keep the database transaction open across the payment call. This prevents two buyers owning the seat while making every failure between booking and payment visible and repairable.","follow":"How would the transaction change if the customer needs three adjacent seats or none?","seconds":90,"lesson":"design-cases","title":"Design cases: seats, flash sales, schedulers and counters","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"design-cases.s2","q":"\"Our flash sale row holds its lock for two milliseconds. Would a Redis gate fix throughput, and how do you return abandoned stock safely?\"","points":["Two milliseconds of serialized lock time gives an ideal ceiling near 500 purchases per second","A gate limits demand but does not increase the row's write capacity","Split stock into independent allocations and retain a database no-oversell check","Use reservation identities and one atomic transition for expiry versus confirmation","Make cross-store release propagation idempotent and avoid claiming final exhaustion from a refillable scan"],"model":"A gate would control overload, but it would not raise the stock row's write capacity. Two milliseconds per serialized purchase gives an ideal ceiling of about five hundred purchases per second, before other bottlenecks. To add capacity I would split the stock into independent allocations, spread requests across them and measure shared database limits. The gate admits at most its allocated inventory using reservation ids, so a retried request does not consume a second permit. The database still enforces no oversell. Each reservation has held, confirmed and released states. Expiry and confirmation compete in one atomic transition, and only the successful release returns the unit. If the gate is separate, I publish a durable release event and apply it idempotently there; reconciliation repairs disagreements. I also distinguish sold from temporarily reserved. With returns, a scan that saw each bucket empty at different times does not prove all were empty together. I can report temporary unavailability, retry, or coordinate a stronger exhaustion check. Capacity estimates and admission controls are useful only with that reservation lifecycle.","follow":"What if a gate instance crashes and restarts with its original quota?","seconds":75,"lesson":"design-cases","title":"Design cases: seats, flash sales, schedulers and counters","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"design-cases.s3","q":"\"A scheduler worker wakes after its replacement has finished the job. What stops it from doing the work twice?\"","points":["A lease cannot stop paused code from resuming; duplicate execution is possible","Fence job-table writes with occurrence identity, attempt, status and lease validity","Use a stable logical-effect idempotency key across attempts","Downstream deduplication and effect must be atomic; job-table fencing does not protect external effects","Recover retries and schedule recurrence only after a successful fenced completion"],"model":"Nothing about the lease can stop the old code from resuming. I design for duplicate execution and prevent duplicate logical effects at the places that commit them. Each claim increments an attempt number. Heartbeats and completion check the job occurrence, current attempt, running status and live lease, so an old attempt cannot overwrite its replacement's result. That fences the jobs table only. For a payment or message, I send a stable effect key derived from the logical job occurrence, not the attempt number. The receiving system must atomically record that key with the effect and return the original result on retries. If it cannot provide that contract and cannot share a transaction, I cannot promise exactly-once effects. A crash after the effect but before completion then causes a safe retry when deduplication exists. Only a successful fenced finish schedules the next recurring occurrence, under a unique schedule key. I would also add retry backoff, attempt limits and an inspectable failure state, so permanent failures do not loop forever. The guarantee is about committed effects, not how many times the function runs.","follow":"Would a fencing token derived separately for each job protect two jobs writing the same external resource?","seconds":75,"lesson":"design-cases","title":"Design cases: seats, flash sales, schedulers and counters","track":"core","module":16},{"id":"system-coding-rounds.s1","q":"\"You have an hour to build a thread pool using only threading primitives. Talk me through your first few minutes.\"","points":["Clarify worker limits, queue capacity, rejection and result delivery","Distinguish global idleness from a snapshot completion contract","State the locked invariant: pending equals queued plus claimed/running","Describe predicate waits, task execution outside the lock and exception-safe bookkeeping","Separate orderly shutdown, cancellation requests and termination waiting","Reserve time for adversarial tests and follow ups"],"model":"I would agree the contract before writing the worker loop. I would ask how many workers we need, whether the queue is bounded, what happens when it fills, and whether submission returns a future. I would also clarify whether waiting means the pool becomes idle or only the tasks accepted before this call finish. Those are different contracts when submissions continue. My starting design is one mutex protecting the queue, lifecycle state and pending count. Pending equals queued tasks plus tasks already claimed by workers. Workers wait on a condition until work or shutdown is available, claim under the lock, and execute outside it. Completion bookkeeping runs even when a task throws, and the failure reaches its caller. Orderly shutdown rejects new work and drains the queue. Immediate shutdown returns queued work and requests cancellation of claimed tasks; a separate operation waits for termination. I would reserve time to test a full queue, a throwing task and shutdown racing with submission. That gives us a correct base before discussing performance or extra features.","follow":"What changes if waiting must cover only tasks accepted before the call?","seconds":75,"lesson":"system-coding-rounds","title":"System coding rounds: what they are and how they are graded","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"system-coding-rounds.s2","q":"\"The queue passes its throughput test, but the program sometimes hangs during close. What do you check?\"","points":["Check the closed predicate and wake every producer and consumer","Show two empty-queue consumers stranded by a single notification","Keep predicate checks and mutations under the same mutex and wait in a loop","Drain accepted items and reject puts that observe closure","Use controlled schedules and bounded joins; throughput alone proves little"],"model":"I would first check whether close wakes every thread whose waiting condition changed. Suppose two consumers are asleep on an empty queue. Close sets the flag but signals only one. That consumer returns, while the other may stay asleep indefinitely and prevent its join from returning. A spurious wakeup might hide the bug, but it is not a termination guarantee. Close must change the flag under the same mutex used by put and take, then notify all waiting producers and consumers. Each wait loops on both its data predicate and closure. Consumers drain items already accepted and return empty only when the queue is closed and empty. Producers that observe closure reject the item, including producers previously blocked by a full queue. I would reproduce those cases with controlled coordination and bounded joins, including several waiters on each condition. I would also verify that main releases the queue lock before joining. A throughput run can miss all these schedules; exact item counts and clean termination test different parts of the contract.","follow":"Can a put racing with close succeed, and where do you decide that?","seconds":75,"lesson":"system-coding-rounds","title":"System coding rounds: what they are and how they are graded","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"executor-without-libraries.s1","q":"\"Walk me through shutdown and shutdownNow. Include a submission racing with shutdown and the futures of tasks that never run.\"","points":["Linearize acceptance and shutdown under the same mutex","Orderly shutdown rejects later work and drains accepted tasks","Immediate shutdown returns still-queued tasks and requests cancellation of claimed work","Distinguish Java interrupts from C++ cooperative stop requests","Resolve unrun futures explicitly and distinguish future completion from physical task exit","Wait for termination separately without promising a strict wall-clock bound"],"model":"Both shutdown modes close admission; the difference is what happens to accepted work. I serialize submission and the state change under one mutex. If submission wins, its task is accepted. If shutdown wins, submission is rejected. Orderly shutdown keeps the queue and lets workers drain it. Immediate shutdown removes and returns tasks still queued, while requesting cancellation of work already claimed. A claimed task may not have entered its body yet, so the returned list is not every task whose body has not started. Java interrupts the worker. C++ needs a cooperative stop token or flag, and I request stop outside the pool mutex because callbacks can run synchronously. Neither mechanism safely kills an uncooperative task. I must also settle futures for removed work. In this Java wrapper the caller retains the futures, or I add task descriptors with a cancel action. In C++, destroying the last owner of an unrun packaged task produces a broken-promise error. A future reporting cancellation does not prove the task stopped. Finally, a separate termination wait observes worker retirement and performs the required joins. Its waiting budget does not eliminate lock contention or scheduling delay, and destruction must wait for all remaining access to the pool to end.","follow":"What if a stop callback calls back into the executor?","seconds":90,"lesson":"executor-without-libraries","title":"An executor without libraries: submit, wait for all, shutdown and shutdownNow","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"executor-without-libraries.s2","q":"\"All my tasks finished, but waitUntilComplete is still blocked. What are your leading explanations?\"","points":["Define global idleness and explain how other submissions extend the wait","Check exception-safe completion bookkeeping against queued plus claimed/running","Check for a lost wakeup between predicate inspection and waiting","Explain a sequence-boundary snapshot and out-of-order completion","Distinguish a snapshot from per-client task groups and forbid self-waiting"],"model":"I would first check what completion means. This implementation waits for global idleness, so another client's submissions can keep pending above zero after my own tasks finish. Next I would check the invariant: pending equals queued plus claimed or running work. An exception path that skips the decrement leaves a permanent phantom task. Then I would inspect the wait protocol. Checking pending, unlocking, and later waiting without checking again can miss the final notification even if every individual access is protected. The predicate check and condition wait must form one locked protocol. If the requirement is a snapshot, I would assign sequence numbers at acceptance, capture the next sequence under the lock, and wait until no unfinished number is below that boundary. A total completion count is insufficient because later tasks can finish before earlier ones. Remove numbers on completion or queued cancellation and notify all relevant waiters. For only one client's tasks, I need explicit task groups or futures. Finally, no worker may wait for pool-wide completion that includes itself; its own task cannot finish while it waits.","follow":"Why does waiting for a total of N completions fail when tasks finish out of order?","seconds":75,"lesson":"executor-without-libraries","title":"An executor without libraries: submit, wait for all, shutdown and shutdownNow","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"executor-without-libraries.s3","q":"\"Add a per-task timeout. Can you just interrupt its worker and start a replacement?\"","points":["A timeout bounds waiting or requests cancellation; it cannot safely kill a thread","Clarify whether the deadline includes queue time","Prevent a stale watchdog action from interrupting the next task","Distinguish FutureTask cancellation, CompletableFuture completion and per-task C++ stop sources","Keep uncooperative tasks counted and bound resources instead of replacing without limit"],"model":"I would separate the deadline from actual task termination. A timeout can end the caller's wait and request cancellation, but it cannot safely kill a thread. First I would decide whether queue time consumes the budget or the clock starts when execution begins. The watchdog must target a particular task assignment. If it checks a worker, pauses, and interrupts after that worker starts another task, it cancels the wrong work. Clearing the interrupt flag between tasks does not close that race. In Java I would serialize assignment validation and interrupt delivery with completion and reassignment. A correctly used FutureTask already coordinates its own cancellation; cancelling the CompletableFuture wrapper here does not interrupt its computation. In C++, I would capture a stop source belonging to that task and request it outside the pool lock. If the task ignores cancellation, I keep counting it as running until it actually returns. Unlimited replacements would defeat the concurrency bound and exhaust resources. I would limit admission, report the stuck work, and use process isolation when hard termination is a requirement. Cancellation also cannot undo side effects already performed.","follow":"How do timeout and successful completion race to publish exactly one result?","seconds":75,"lesson":"executor-without-libraries","title":"An executor without libraries: submit, wait for all, shutdown and shutdownNow","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"dependency-job-runner.s1","q":"\"You have a graph of jobs and four workers. Walk me through your design, including how you know you are finished.\"","points":["Count unfinished dependencies and seed a ready queue with roots","Protect counts, ready jobs, running count and failure state with one lock","Claim jobs under the lock, execute outside it, and release dependents only on success","Wait only while ready is empty, running is positive and no failure is recorded","Broadcast completion changes and join workers before returning","Reject cycles before execution and state the job-termination assumption"],"model":"I would use a ready queue and a count of unfinished dependencies for each job. I validate the whole graph with a topological pass before starting workers, so a cycle cannot cause partial execution. Initially, only roots enter the ready queue. One lock protects the queue, dependency counts, running count and failure state. A worker claims a job and increments the running count under that lock, then releases it before invoking user code. When the job succeeds, the worker takes the lock again, decrements its children's counts and enqueues each child that reaches zero. For example, if two parents feed one child, the first completion leaves the count at one and only the second releases it. The worker then broadcasts the state change. An empty queue alone does not mean completion: a running parent could release more jobs. A worker waits while the queue is empty, something is running and no failure is recorded. Without ready or running jobs, it can leave. On failure, idle workers can leave immediately, and the caller joins everyone before reporting. This progress argument assumes the jobs eventually finish and workers get scheduled. It does not make an arbitrary blocking job terminate.","follow":"How does a child's read of its parents' results become safe even though jobs run outside your lock?","seconds":90,"lesson":"dependency-job-runner","title":"A parallel job runner that respects dependencies","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"dependency-job-runner.s2","q":"\"A job fails. Can you promise to stop immediately and return with nothing still running?\"","points":["Separate immediate rejection of new claims from cooperative cancellation and eventual return","Record the first observed failure and clear ready work under the claim lock","Treat already claimed jobs as running, even before their bodies execute","Interrupt busy Java workers or request the shared C++ stop token outside callback-sensitive locks","Join every worker and state that uncooperative jobs prevent bounded completion"],"model":"I can stop admitting new jobs immediately after recording failure, but I cannot promise that arbitrary running code stops immediately. Under the same lock used to claim work, I record the first failure and clear the ready queue. Another worker then either claimed its job before that point or sees the failure and exits. An already claimed job might enter its body afterwards, so it should check cancellation before side effects. I interrupt busy workers in Java, or request the shared stop source in C++. In C++, I make that request outside the scheduler lock because registered stop callbacks can execute synchronously. A job that returns early after cancellation must not release its dependents. Finally, I join every worker and report the recorded failure. That guarantees nothing remains running when the call returns, assuming jobs also join any child work they created. If a job ignores cancellation or blocks forever, the call may never return. A timeout can report that situation, but cannot safely kill an arbitrary thread or undo its effects.","follow":"What must happen if the caller itself is interrupted while joining the Java workers?","seconds":75,"lesson":"dependency-job-runner","title":"A parallel job runner that respects dependencies","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"dependency-job-runner.s3","q":"\"Now the graph is hidden. An API returns jobs that are ready given the finished set. What changes?\"","points":["Discover roots using an empty successful-completion set","Deduplicate on enqueue and add only successful jobs to the finished set","Serialize oracle access with a consistent snapshot, preferably through a coordinator for slow calls","Count in-flight discovery when deciding termination and treat oracle errors as failures","Require an expected job universe or explicit completion signal to distinguish hidden blockage from success"],"model":"I replace the dependency counts with discovery through the API. I first call it with an empty finished set to find roots. Whenever a job succeeds, I add it to that set and ask again. I deduplicate when enqueuing, because the oracle may repeatedly return a job that is already queued or running. Calls need a consistent snapshot and serialized access if the oracle is not thread-safe. For a slow oracle, I would use one coordinator so workers never hold the scheduler lock across I/O. Discovery itself counts as outstanding work; otherwise an empty queue could look finished while an oracle call is still running. Oracle errors follow the same failure policy as job errors. I cannot validate hidden cycles up front. Without an expected job set or an explicit completion signal, no ready jobs could mean either completion or permanently blocked work.","follow":"How would delayed retries change your definition of outstanding work?","seconds":60,"lesson":"dependency-job-runner","title":"A parallel job runner that respects dependencies","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"debugging-rounds.s1","q":"\"This service sometimes hangs. CPU is low and the JVM reports no deadlock. What do you check next?\"","points":["A null detector result excludes only the detector's supported ownership cycles","Map threads, lock ownership, wait predicates, work dependencies and shutdown","Inspect repeated stacks and progress rather than inferring cause from WAITING alone","Check signals, retained locks, pool starvation and external I/O","Force the suspected schedule and verify a focused repair with a bounded regression test"],"model":"A null deadlock result does not prove the service can make progress. The detector finds certain lock ownership cycles, not every dependency between tasks. I would map the threads, shared state, locks, wait predicates and shutdown path, then compare repeated thread stacks with progress counters. WAITING could mean a condition, an explicit lock, a join or a future. If workers are in a queue wait, I check the predicate under its lock, who changes it, and whether that path notifies the correct waiters. If the queue is closed and empty, every sleeping worker needs a route out. I also check whether a waiter retains an unrelated lock needed by the producer. If all pool workers are waiting for child futures, those children may be queued in the same exhausted pool. External I/O could simply be stalled. Once I have an interleaving that explains the evidence, I force it with gates in a small test, make one focused repair, and check both the invariant and termination with a deadline. Then I continue looking for bugs the hang hid.","follow":"What does Java 21's deadlock detector miss when virtual threads are involved?","seconds":75,"lesson":"debugging-rounds","title":"Debugging rounds: the bug in someone else's threaded code","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"debugging-rounds.s2","q":"\"The queue uses a loop around wait. Why might it still hang when we close it?\"","points":["A predicate loop handles wakeups but does not create a shutdown protocol","Wait while empty and not closed, using one lock for the check and state changes","Close sets the flag and wakes every waiter","Drain accepted work and reject new submissions atomically with close","Join consumers before destroying the queue and test several sleeping consumers"],"model":"A loop makes wakeups safe, but it does not tell a worker that no more jobs will arrive. The consumer should wait while the queue is empty and it is not closed. Close sets the closed flag under that same lock and wakes every waiter. With two sleeping consumers, waking only one can leave the other asleep forever, even though its exit condition is now true. After waking, consumers drain accepted jobs and exit only when the queue is both closed and empty. Submission must check closed under the enqueue lock, so a late producer cannot add a job after the last consumer leaves. The owner then joins the consumers before destroying the queue or its mutex. I would test closing an empty queue with several blocked consumers, plus close racing with submission, and verify that every accepted job is processed exactly once.","follow":"Why can notifying one consumer on an ordinary single-item enqueue still be correct?","seconds":60,"lesson":"debugging-rounds","title":"Debugging rounds: the bug in someone else's threaded code","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"debugging-rounds.s3","q":"\"The counter is wrong, but only occasionally. It passed under the race detector today. Is the fix done?\"","points":["A clean execution or detector run is evidence, not a proof of correctness","Explain one lost-update interleaving and distinguish Java from C++ undefined behaviour","Use an atomic read-modify-write or one lock over the complete invariant","Explain why join and volatile do not repair concurrent increments","Test exact totals and explain the happens-before argument"],"model":"No. I need a reason the counter update is correct for every allowed interleaving, not just a clean run. Two workers can both read five, both add one and both write six, losing an update. Java permits that wrong result; in C++, unsynchronized conflicting accesses to a plain counter are a data race and undefined behaviour. I would use an atomic read-modify-write for an independent counter, or protect the whole invariant with one lock if other fields must agree with it. Joining workers makes completed writes available to the caller, but does not make those earlier increments atomic. Java volatile alone also leaves the read and write separate, and C++ volatile is not thread synchronization. I would assert exact totals after joins, exercise several worker counts, and run the detector again. The final justification is the atomic operation or lock protocol, with tests as supporting evidence.","follow":"How would your choice change if the count and a transaction log must always agree?","seconds":60,"lesson":"debugging-rounds","title":"Debugging rounds: the bug in someone else's threaded code","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"file-io-and-durability.s1","q":"\"Our service flushes the file and says saved, but records disappear after a power cut. Walk me through the fix.\"","points":["A buffered-stream flush is not a durability acknowledgement","Trace process buffer, kernel page cache, device cache and stable storage","Use a successful platform-appropriate sync before acknowledging; flush alone is insufficient","Persist the parent directory when creating or replacing the file","Treat storage errors as uncertain outcomes and stop accepting writes","Share each sync across a batch without weakening acknowledgement order"],"model":"I would move the saved response after a successful durability sync. Flushing a buffered file stream usually moves bytes from the process into the operating system's page cache. That can survive the process dying, but not the machine losing power. The drive may have another volatile cache, so the operation must cover that boundary too. On Linux I use fsync; on macOS the C++ path requests a full device flush. In Java I use <code>FileChannel.force<\/code> and check the local provider's guarantees. For a newly created file I also persist the parent directory, because durable contents are useless if the filename disappears. If the sync reports a storage error, I fail the pending requests and stop using that log. I cannot assume the bytes are absent, or that another sync will repair them. Once correctness is established, I batch. If a measured sync takes one millisecond, syncing every event caps one writer near a thousand events per second before other costs. Sharing that sync across a hundred events raises capacity, but every receipt still waits for the successful sync covering its own bytes. I would test acknowledgement timing with a fake disk that pauses inside sync; successful readback alone does not test power-loss durability.","follow":"How would you replace a configuration file while preserving a complete version across a crash?","seconds":90,"lesson":"file-io-and-durability","title":"Files, offsets, buffers, flush and fsync","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"file-io-and-durability.s2","q":"\"Two threads seek to different offsets, then write, and records land in the wrong places. What would you change?\"","points":["Seek and write are separate operations on a shared open-file offset","Give a concrete interleaving that redirects one write","Use positional I/O and advance by actual transfer counts","Keep buffers private and coordinate overlapping ranges or truncation","Block and stripe locks can serialize even disjoint byte ranges"],"model":"I would use positional I/O so each write carries its destination offset. With a shared descriptor, A can seek to offset four thousand, B can seek to eight thousand, and then A writes at B's position. Each call can be correct while the pair is wrong. A shared lock around both calls fixes that, but serializes unrelated work. Positional writes avoid the shared offset: <code>pwrite<\/code> in C++, or <code>FileChannel.write<\/code> with a position in Java. I loop on short writes and advance by the actual count, with a separate buffer per thread. Disjoint ranges then need no application lock, assuming nobody truncates the file. Overlapping updates still need coordination, especially a block device's read, modify, write sequence. Block locks are simple but can conflict for disjoint bytes in one block; stripe locks add more false conflicts. Positional I/O fixes placement, not durability.","follow":"What changes if the file was opened in append mode?","seconds":60,"lesson":"file-io-and-durability","title":"Files, offsets, buffers, flush and fsync","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"durable-log-writer.s1","q":"\"You have many producers and one log file. Each caller needs a durable acknowledgement, but one fsync per event is too slow. Design it.\"","points":["Use a bounded admission queue and one file-writing thread","Assign sequence and enqueue atomically to define append order","Drain a batch and perform full writes plus one sync outside the queue lock","Complete receipts only after the covering sync succeeds","State the durable-prefix invariant and snapshot it for flush","Explain admission pressure, batch latency and byte limits"],"model":"I would use one writer thread and group commit, with a bounded queue between producers and the file. Under one lock, append assigns a sequence number and enqueues the event, then returns a future. That defines the order; overlapping callers may be ordered either way. For a synchronous API the caller waits on that future before returning success. The writer takes the queued events as one batch, releases the lock, writes every byte with a short-write loop, and performs one sync. Only after that succeeds does it advance the durable prefix and complete those receipts outside the lock. Producers can fill the next batch while storage is busy, so batching happens naturally under load. The invariant is that every sequence below the durable counter is safely persisted, and that counter never exceeds the accepted frontier. Flush snapshots the accepted frontier once and waits for that prefix, so later producers cannot keep moving its target. If the queue fills, callers block or receive an explicit rejection. I would bound bytes as well as event count and account for the in-flight batch and copied buffer. This improves throughput by sharing sync cost; it does not remove storage latency, overload, or the need to report failures.","follow":"Can a future callback deadlock this design even when you complete futures outside the lock?","seconds":90,"lesson":"durable-log-writer","title":"A concurrent, ordered, durable log writer","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"durable-log-writer.s2","q":"\"The queue is empty, but flush returns too early. Close also sometimes loses the last events. Walk me through the lifecycle.\"","points":["An empty queue can coexist with an unsynced batch in flight","Flush snapshots the accepted frontier under the admission lock","Close closes admission and wakes both producer and writer waiters","Drain, sync, resolve receipts, join and release the file in that order","Report storage failure and define interruption and repeated-close behaviour"],"model":"I would base flush on the durable prefix, not queue emptiness. The writer may have removed the last batch and still be waiting for the disk. Flush captures the next sequence number under the queue lock and waits until the durable counter reaches that fixed target, or a storage failure makes success impossible. Close first shuts admission under the same lock, so every racing append is either accepted into the drain or rejected. It wakes the writer and producers waiting for capacity. The writer then drains accepted work, syncs it, resolves every receipt and exits. Only after joining it do I close the file. That order prevents closing a descriptor underneath an active write. A storage failure still resolves the affected receipts exceptionally and is reported by explicit close. Repeated close must be safe, including concurrent calls. In Java I finish cleanup if the closing caller is interrupted, then restore its interrupt flag; I do not interrupt the file writer to request shutdown. I would test these transitions with a gated fake sync and a full queue.","follow":"Does flush returning imply that every callback attached to those receipts has finished?","seconds":75,"lesson":"durable-log-writer","title":"A concurrent, ordered, durable log writer","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"durable-log-writer.s3","q":"\"The sync succeeded, then the process crashed before the client saw its receipt. Can the client safely retry?\"","points":["A missing or timed-out receipt is uncertain, not proof that the event was absent","Separate durable storage from delivery of the acknowledgement","Use stable request IDs and durable deduplication for retries","Recover framed records with bounded lengths, checksums and sequence validation","Distinguish an incomplete unacknowledged tail from corruption in an acknowledged prefix"],"model":"The client can retry only with a protocol that tolerates duplicates, because a missing receipt does not mean the write failed. The event may already be durable while its acknowledgement was lost. Timing out on the receipt also leaves an accepted append running. I would assign a stable request ID before the first attempt and store enough deduplication information durably with the event, so another attempt can find the existing result instead of applying the effect again. A storage error can also leave an uncertain outcome, so a failed future is not permission to assume the file contains nothing. On restart I would validate the log format before accepting writes. Each record needs a bounded length, a checksum and sequence information, so recovery can find the complete prefix and detect a torn tail. An incomplete unacknowledged tail can be truncated and the repair synced. Corruption inside an acknowledged prefix is different: silently truncating it would violate promises already made, so the system must repair from another copy or stop. Group commit establishes persistence before success; stable identities and recovery establish what retries mean after that success becomes uncertain.","follow":"Where must the deduplication record live so a crash cannot separate it from the event's effect?","seconds":75,"lesson":"durable-log-writer","title":"A concurrent, ordered, durable log writer","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"parallel-file-processing.s1","q":"\"Walk me through a parallel file copy. What has to be true before you remove the lock?\"","points":["Stable source and distinct destination with a defined partial-failure contract","Workers claim disjoint chunks and own separate buffers","Positional reads and writes avoid a shared seek position","Short transfers, zero progress and the final partial chunk are handled","Failures stop new work; all started workers join before handles close","Measure the bottleneck and budget worker buffers before increasing parallelism"],"model":"I can remove the application lock around data transfer when each worker owns its destination range and buffer, and both reads and writes are positional. First I require a stable source and reject a destination that aliases it, because truncation could destroy the input. I hand out chunk numbers from an atomic counter so faster workers can take more work. Each worker loops over short transfers, handles the smaller final chunk, and treats unexpected end of file or persistent lack of progress as failure. The first failure stops new claims. I join every started worker before closing handles or reporting the error, then sync only on success. Direct overwriting can leave a partial destination; atomic replacement needs a temporary file and a durable rename procedure. Finally I measure storage throughput and budget worker buffers. More threads cannot increase a saturated device's bandwidth.","follow":"What changes when the source is a single-head device and the destination is a fast SSD?","seconds":60,"lesson":"parallel-file-processing","title":"Parallel copies, chunked reads and caches over slow storage","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"parallel-file-processing.s2","q":"\"Eight readers miss on the same remote chunk. How do you avoid eight fetches, including when the first fetch fails?\"","points":["Insert one incomplete future per versioned chunk under a lock","Other readers reuse the same future; the owner fetches outside the lock","Publish complete immutable bytes on success","Remove the failed generation and complete existing waiters exceptionally","Resolve every owned future before waiting on unrelated work","Bound fetch admission and retained bytes; one waiter timeout does not cancel a shared fetch"],"model":"I store an incomplete future in the cache before starting the fetch. Under a short lock, the first reader claims the chunk by inserting that future. Later readers find it and wait on the same result. The owner fetches outside the lock, validates the full chunk length, and completes the future with bytes that will not be modified again. On failure, it removes that failed generation and completes its future exceptionally. Existing waiters see the error, while a later request can retry. Every chunk a request claims must eventually complete, even if that request is cancelled. I resolve owned fetches before waiting on someone else's work. For production I also bound fetch concurrency and retained bytes, including chunks pinned by readers. A timeout for one waiter does not cancel everybody's shared fetch, and a mutable file needs its version in the cache key.","follow":"How would you fetch ten cold chunks concurrently without deadlocking a saturated fetch pool?","seconds":60,"lesson":"parallel-file-processing","title":"Parallel copies, chunked reads and caches over slow storage","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"parallel-file-processing.s3","q":"\"Chunk zero is slow while later chunks finish quickly. How do you preserve stream order without running out of memory?\"","points":["One emitter consumes results by sequence number","Admission window bounds how far work may start ahead of the next emission","Bound bytes as well as result count and apply sink back-pressure","The next needed chunk is inside every positive window","Failure wakes emitter and workers; shutdown joins them","A retained output list and a stalled callback remain separate limits"],"model":"I use one emitter and a bounded window of admitted chunk numbers. Workers can finish in any order, but they put results in a map keyed by sequence number. The emitter waits for exactly its next number and advances the window as output progresses. If chunk zero is slow, later work eventually stops at the window boundary instead of filling memory forever. I also bound bytes, because eight very large results can still exceed the budget, and the output sink must apply back-pressure. The window itself does not block the next required chunk, so it creates no circular wait when callbacks finish and threads get scheduled. That is not a promise against a hung storage call. Failures set shared stop state and wake both sides, and cleanup joins workers. A demo that collects all emitted output in a list still uses memory proportional to that output.","follow":"Why can a bounded queue of completed results deadlock if the earliest chunk cannot publish into it?","seconds":60,"lesson":"parallel-file-processing","title":"Parallel copies, chunked reads and caches over slow storage","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"fixed-buffer-queues.s1","q":"\"Design a bounded queue in a fixed array. How do you make it safe for several producers and consumers?\"","points":["Define head, tail and count with a positive fixed capacity","One lock protects slots and all queue metadata","Producers wait while full and consumers while empty, using separate conditions","Update the invariant before signalling and recheck after every wakeup","Close wakes all waiters and allows consumers to drain","Distinguish fixed payload storage from runtime waiter allocations and test unique ids"],"model":"I use a fixed array with a head index, a tail index, and a count, all protected by one lock. Head identifies the next value to remove, tail the next slot to fill, and count distinguishes empty from full when the indices are equal. Producers wait while count equals capacity; consumers wait while count is zero. I use separate not-full and not-empty conditions, always in loops, because a wakeup does not reserve a slot or an item. Each operation updates the slot and metadata before signalling the opposite side. For shutdown I set a closed flag under the lock, reject new puts, let takes drain, and wake everyone. The data array never grows, but Java's lock machinery may allocate waiter nodes. I would test capacity one, repeated wrapping, unique item identities, per-producer order, and closing while both kinds of caller are waiting.","follow":"Would a locked <code>isEmpty()<\/code> followed by <code>take()<\/code> make a nonblocking removal safe?","seconds":60,"lesson":"fixed-buffer-queues","title":"Queues inside a fixed buffer","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"fixed-buffer-queues.s2","q":"\"There is exactly one producer and one consumer. Explain both memory-ordering directions in your ring.\"","points":["Producer alone writes tail; consumer alone writes head","Slot write precedes release publication of tail","Consumer acquires tail before reading the slot","Consumer releases head after reading; producer acquires head before reuse","Own-index accesses need no cross-thread ordering","Power-of-two modular counters survive wrap; extra producers invalidate ownership"],"model":"The lock can go because each index has one writer, but ordering must work in both directions. The producer writes the value into a slot, then release-stores the new tail. The consumer acquire-loads tail before reading that slot, so observing publication also makes the value visible. After reading, the consumer release-stores head. The producer acquire-loads head before reusing the slot, ensuring it cannot overwrite data while the consumer still reads it. Each thread can read its own index with a plain Java or relaxed C++ atomic access. I validate a positive power-of-two capacity and use defined modular counter arithmetic so wraparound preserves both the distance and masked index. Two producers break the ownership proof: both can claim one slot, and C++ then has undefined behaviour. Failed offers and polls return immediately; a surrounding retry loop can still wait forever if its peer stops.","follow":"Why is atomically incrementing tail before writing the slot insufficient for multiple producers?","seconds":60,"lesson":"fixed-buffer-queues","title":"Queues inside a fixed buffer","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"fixed-buffer-queues.s3","q":"\"Two FIFO queues must share one large buffer with little wasted space. What would you build?\"","points":["Use queue head and tail indices plus one shared free list","Enqueue takes a free slot; dequeue returns it while preserving FIFO links","One lock protects the disjoint-list ownership invariant","Shared space condition and per-queue data conditions govern waits","Link chunks to reduce metadata, accepting bounded partial-chunk waste","Reserve capacity for isolation and wake all producers when admission predicates differ"],"model":"I would let both queues draw storage from one free list instead of reserving half the buffer for each. Each queue has its own head and tail. Enqueue removes a free slot and links it after that queue's tail; dequeue removes the head and returns its slot to the free list. One lock protects the invariant that every slot belongs to exactly one queue or the free list. Consumers wait on their own queue's data condition, while producers share a space condition. For a large buffer, I link chunks of values rather than individual values. One four-byte link per four-kibibyte payload chunk costs about a tenth of a percent, at the cost of partially used end chunks. If one queue needs guaranteed capacity, I add a reserve. Producers then have different admission predicates, so a shared single signal is insufficient; I wake all and recheck.","follow":"How does your capacity calculation change when the supplied buffer must contain both payload and link metadata?","seconds":60,"lesson":"fixed-buffer-queues","title":"Queues inside a fixed buffer","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"memory-allocators.s1","q":"\"You have a 16 GB region. Walk me through a thread-safe allocator, including what your complexity claim depends on.\"","points":["Clarify binary capacity and minimum unit: 16 GiB, 4 KiB, 23 orders","Allocate by rounding and splitting; free by XOR buddy lookup and repeated coalescing","State disjoint ownership and cover all metadata transitions with one mutex","Distinguish constant-time indexed metadata from logarithmic tree operations","Explain minimum-block waste, external fragmentation and preallocated metadata"],"model":"I would start with a buddy allocator protected by one mutex. I would clarify that the region is sixteen gibibytes and choose a four kibibyte minimum unit. That gives two to the power twenty-two units and twenty-three orders, including order zero. I keep free blocks by order and record the order and state of every block head. Allocation rounds the request up, finds a non-empty order and splits a larger block. Free checks that the offset is live, finds its buddy by XOR with the block size, and merges while that exact buddy is free at the same order. Every live allocation must be disjoint, so the mutex covers the whole transition across all affected orders. There are at most twenty-two split or merge levels. Constant time per level needs indexed state and removable free-list links; the tree-based demo pays logarithmic set costs. Power-of-two rounding wastes less than half a block for requests above the minimum, but a tiny request can waste nearly a whole minimum block. External fragmentation still exists when neighbours are not buddies. I would preallocate metadata so freeing cannot fail while allocating bookkeeping, then test exhaustion, invalid frees, concurrent ownership and complete coalescing before optimising contention.","follow":"What if a caller frees an old offset after another thread has already reallocated that offset?","seconds":90,"lesson":"memory-allocators","title":"Memory allocators: free lists, buddy allocation and thread safety","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"memory-allocators.s2","q":"\"Adding thread caches improved throughput, but memory use grew and allocation sometimes fails while blocks are idle. What do you check?\"","points":["Distinguish live allocations from free blocks stranded in thread caches","Explain batch refill and draining as the contention versus retention trade-off","Handle producer-consumer imbalance, cache bounds and thread or task exit","Clarify local exhaustion versus a globally exhausted pool","Test cross-thread ownership transfer and shutdown lifetime"],"model":"I would first separate live allocations from free blocks retained in caches. The improvement is plausible because most calls now touch only one thread's stash, and one shared lock acquisition transfers a whole batch. But those idle blocks are unavailable to other threads until they return to the shared pool. A producer may allocate continuously while a consumer frees its objects, so the consumer's cache grows unless it drains above a high-water mark. I would bound each cache, batch its returns and provide a pressure-driven drain or retry path. An empty local cache and empty shared list do not prove that the entire pool is exhausted. I would also check thread exit. The C++ stash destructor returns cached blocks, provided the shared pool is still alive. Java needs an explicit flush in the worker's finally block, and long-lived thread pools may need task-boundary cleanup too. My tests would transfer ownership through a synchronised queue, return blocks from a different thread, end workers with populated caches and verify that all capacity becomes reusable. I would measure both contention and retained memory before choosing batch sizes.","follow":"How would your design reclaim idle caches without adding a lock to every fast-path allocation?","seconds":75,"lesson":"memory-allocators","title":"Memory allocators: free lists, buddy allocation and thread safety","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"machine-coding-with-concurrency.s1","q":"\"You have ninety minutes to build a concurrent reservation service. How do you organise the design and demonstrate that it works?\"","points":["Define booking, cancellation, interval and failure semantics before choosing locks","Assign immutable values and per-resource mutable state clear ownership","Make overlap checking and insertion one atomic transition; order multi-resource locks","Address retries and the semantics of an unsuccessful scan during cancellations","Combine invariant reasoning with gated tests, deadlines and worker-error checks"],"model":"I would get a small end-to-end service running, with one clear concurrency rule for every class. First I would define the API: valid half-open intervals, booking identifiers, cancellation behaviour and what a failed search promises. Messages and reservations would be immutable values, safely published. Each room would own its calendar behind one lock. Booking checks the neighbouring intervals and inserts the reservation in that same critical section, so two callers cannot both reserve an overlapping interval in one room. Cancellation verifies the booking id under the same lock. If an operation spans rooms, I acquire their locks in room-id order and commit all changes together. I would keep callbacks and external work outside those locks. A scan taking one room lock at a time prevents double booking, but with concurrent cancellations a failed scan may miss moving availability. If the contract requires an exact global failure result, I need a coordinated recheck. Retries need a request id stored with the result; a mutex alone does not deduplicate them. Finally I would explain the invariant and linearization points, then test with gated concurrent callers, distinct ids, bounded completion and captured worker exceptions. I would check returned bookings for overlaps and test cancellation and boundary times separately.","follow":"How would you reserve two rooms atomically while unrelated single-room bookings continue?","seconds":90,"lesson":"machine-coding-with-concurrency","title":"Machine coding rounds: thread-safe designs end to end","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"machine-coding-with-concurrency.s2","q":"\"Design a pub-sub broker where one slow subscriber does not stall everyone. What guarantees can you actually make?\"","points":["Use a per-topic ordered log with atomic append and offset assignment","Separate subscriber processing from publication using pull or ordered dispatch","State the bounded-storage, lossless-delivery and nonblocking-publication trade-off","Define retention offsets and contiguous commits for consumer groups","Describe predicate waits, close wakeups and lifetime requirements"],"model":"I would separate publication from subscriber processing with a per-topic log. A topic lock protects appending a message and assigning its offset as one operation, so offsets are unique, dense and ordered. Each subscriber tracks its own position and pulls a bounded batch. Copying that batch still briefly holds the topic lock, but the subscriber's application code runs outside it. For push delivery, I would use ordered dispatch rather than invoke callbacks directly from competing publishers. I would then clarify the overload contract. With a subscriber that can stop forever, finite storage cannot preserve every message while allowing publishers to run indefinitely without waiting. We must choose blocking, dropping, disconnecting the subscriber or spilling to storage. Retention needs a base offset and a defined response when a consumer asks for data already removed. Consumer groups also need ownership or claims; one shared offset does not stop duplicate processing. Commits advance only over the contiguous completed prefix, so finishing message eleven cannot skip unfinished message ten. Pollers wait in a predicate loop and recheck after notification. Closing marks the topic closed, rejects publication and wakes every waiter to drain or exit. Finally, all users must finish before the broker and its condition variables are destroyed.","follow":"What happens if a consumer finishes a side effect and crashes before committing its offset?","seconds":90,"lesson":"machine-coding-with-concurrency","title":"Machine coding rounds: thread-safe designs end to end","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"machine-coding-with-concurrency.s3","q":"\"Your expiring counter returns the right counts, but memory stays high after traffic stops. What do you investigate?\"","points":["Separate logical expiration, lazy removal and retained collection capacity","Use monotonic time sampled under the lock and purge empty keys","Add bounded admission or scheduled cleanup with shutdown when idle reclamation is required","Explain purge latency and the accuracy trade-off of time buckets"],"model":"I would distinguish logical expiry from actual removal and from retained collection capacity. This counter purges on calls, so an idle instance can still contain expired entries until the next operation. Even after removal, a collection may retain its backing storage. I would verify that reads purge expired elements and remove empty keys, using a monotonic clock sampled under the same lock as insertion. If the requirement is reclamation while idle, I would add scheduled cleanup under that lock, with explicit worker shutdown, and bound admission for a strict capacity limit. I would also measure the first call after a long pause: it may remove many entries while holding the lock, despite constant amortized work per element. Time buckets reduce entry count, but are exact only when the contract quantises timestamps; otherwise bucket boundaries introduce approximation. Tests should advance an injected clock through exact expiry boundaries.","follow":"How would you get an exact total after sharding this counter by key?","seconds":60,"lesson":"machine-coding-with-concurrency","title":"Machine coding rounds: thread-safe designs end to end","track":"core","module":17},{"id":"coding-round-playbook.s1","q":"\"You have an hour to build a bounded worker pool. Walk me through your first five minutes.\"","points":["Clarify submission, bounds, shutdown, task failure and cancellation before coding","Confirm permitted libraries and whether runnable code is expected","Identify the queue and lifecycle state under one synchronization protocol","State capacity and accepted-task completion invariants with wait predicates","Choose a simple monitor and reserve time for tests and interleavings"],"model":"I would agree on the contract before writing the worker loop. I would ask whether submission blocks or rejects when the queue is full, whether shutdown drains accepted work, and what immediate shutdown does to queued and running tasks. I would also ask how callers receive task failures and whether cancellation must interrupt a blocked operation. I would propose defaults so that this discussion stays short. Next I would confirm the allowed libraries and whether the code must compile and run. Then I would write down the shared state: the queue, its capacity and the lifecycle state, guarded by one lock. The queue must stay within capacity, and every accepted task must eventually receive a completion, failure or cancellation outcome under our progress assumptions. Workers wait for work or shutdown; blocked submitters wait for space or shutdown. I would start with a monitor because those predicates and transitions fit one lock. Finally, I would reserve time to test capacity one, task failure and submission racing shutdown, and to explain the dangerous interleavings aloud.","follow":"Where exactly does a submission racing shutdown become accepted or rejected?","seconds":75,"lesson":"coding-round-playbook","title":"How to run a concurrency coding round","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"coding-round-playbook.s2","q":"\"Your stress test passes, but production sometimes hangs during shutdown. What do you check?\"","points":["A passing stress run does not prove progress for all schedules","Capture thread stacks and map waiters to predicates and enabling operations","Check the closed flag and notifications under the same locking protocol","Check joins or callbacks under locks, pool self-dependency and cancellation cooperation","Force the shutdown interleaving and bound the entire test process"],"model":"I would capture the hung threads before changing the code. A passing stress run only covers the schedules that happened; it does not prove shutdown can always finish. For each blocked thread, I would identify its predicate and the operation that could make that predicate true. A consumer waiting on an empty queue must also check the closed flag, and close must wake every waiter whose outcome changes. I would verify that checking and changing this state use the same lock. Next I would look for shutdown joining workers while holding a lock those workers need, callbacks running inside critical sections, or tasks waiting for subtasks in the same exhausted pool. I would also check whether cancellation reaches running work and whether that work cooperates. To reproduce the bug, I would use a test hook to place a worker just before its condition wait and then invoke shutdown. The test would assert worker termination and task outcomes, with a watchdog for the entire process. Sleeping to make the schedule likely is useful evidence, but it is not a deterministic reproduction.","follow":"How would you prove that the test actually placed the worker in the wait before close ran?","seconds":75,"lesson":"coding-round-playbook","title":"How to run a concurrency coding round","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"java-cheat-sheet.s1","q":"\"I put a two-second timeout on a future. Why is the service still hanging, and what would you change?\"","points":["Distinguish a timed wait from CompletableFuture timeout completion","Neither timeout automatically stops the underlying computation","CompletableFuture cancellation does not interrupt its computation; Future cancellation is cooperative","Executor close waits for termination and can outlast the future timeout","Use I/O deadlines, bounded admission and an explicit resource cleanup policy"],"model":"The timeout limits an observation of the result, not necessarily the work producing it. A timed <code>Future.get<\/code> stops my wait by throwing a timeout exception. <code>CompletableFuture.orTimeout<\/code> instead completes that future exceptionally, but the underlying computation may continue. That distinction matters if the task is stuck in network I/O. Cancelling a CompletableFuture does not interrupt its computation, even with true as the argument. A future returned by a thread pool may request interruption, but the task still has to cooperate. I would also inspect resource cleanup. If the code created an executor in try-with-resources, its close waits for termination, so the method can remain stuck after the future has timed out. I would put connect and read deadlines in the actual client call, propagate a shared request deadline, and define how cancellation releases resources. I would bound admission as well, so slow calls cannot create an unlimited backlog. Finally, I would test a task that ignores interruption and verify the service reports the timeout without claiming that the task has stopped.","follow":"What happens to the futures of queued tasks returned by <code>shutdownNow()<\/code>?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-cheat-sheet","title":"Java concurrency cheat sheet","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"java-cheat-sheet.s2","q":"\"A teammate says final fields and volatile fields both safely publish the whole object. How would you explain the difference?\"","points":["Final-field initialization safety and volatile publication are distinct guarantees","Correct construction protects final values and construction-time reachable state, not every ordinary field","A volatile reference hand-off publishes preceding initialization through a happens-before edge","Neither final references nor volatile references make later object mutations atomic","Choose immutable published snapshots or locking for a multi-field mutable invariant"],"model":"I would separate initialization safety from publication. Final fields have special guarantees when an object is properly constructed and its reference does not escape during construction. Another thread that obtains that object sees the initialized final values, including the relevant construction-time state reached through a final reference. That is not a general happens-before edge for every ordinary field, and it does not promise that a racing reader will obtain the reference at all. Publishing the completed object through a volatile reference gives a different argument: initialization precedes the volatile write, which happens-before the reader's subsequent volatile read. That chain makes the preceding writes visible. Neither technique turns later mutations into a transaction. A final list reference still points to a mutable list, and a volatile reference does not make a balance update plus a history update atomic. For a configuration snapshot I would build an immutable object and publish it through a volatile reference. For a mutable invariant across fields, I would protect the complete operation and its readers with the same lock.","follow":"Would a successful <code>Future.get()<\/code> let you read a task's ordinary, non-volatile result field?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-cheat-sheet","title":"Java concurrency cheat sheet","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"cpp-cheat-sheet.s1","q":"\"This worker hangs when its owner is destroyed. Walk me through what you would check in the C++ shutdown path.\"","points":["A joinable jthread requests stop and joins, but stop is cooperative and destruction can block","Check whether the owner holds a mutex the worker needs before joining","Check ordinary condition waits and blocking I/O for a cancellation path","A stop-aware condition_variable_any wait returns the predicate value, so explicitly choose stop versus pending work","Stop admission, wake waiters, join users, then destroy shared state; define drain or discard","Test idle shutdown and pending work under a process timeout, and run ThreadSanitizer"],"model":"I would first check what the worker is waiting for and whether shutdown can release that wait. A joining thread owner gives me lifetime management, not guaranteed termination. A joinable <code>std::jthread<\/code> requests stop and then joins, but the worker must cooperate. If the destructor holds the queue mutex while joining, and the worker needs that mutex to exit, I have created a deadlock. I would release the mutex before joining. Next I would inspect the wait. An ordinary condition variable does not wake merely because a stop token changed. I need a closed flag in the protected predicate and notifications, or a stop-aware condition variable wait. That stop-aware wait returns the predicate value, so pending work can make it return true even after stop. I must explicitly choose whether cancellation wins or accepted work drains. A token also cannot interrupt arbitrary blocking I/O; that needs its own cancellation or timeout mechanism. Finally, the queue, mutex and captured references must stay alive until all users have finished. My sequence is stop admission, publish shutdown, wake waiters, join, then destroy state. I would test both an idle worker and pending work, bound the test process runtime, and use ThreadSanitizer for observed data races.","follow":"What changes if shutdown must drain every accepted task, including one that throws?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-cheat-sheet","title":"C++ concurrency cheat sheet, with pthreads","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"cpp-cheat-sheet.s2","q":"\"Can I replace this condition-variable wait with C++20 atomic wait? What changes?\"","points":["Atomic wait fits a condition expressed by one atomic value, while a mutex predicate can cover several fields","wait(old) returns only after observing a different value and hides internal spurious wakeups","A transient change away and back can be missed, so use a generation counter when appropriate","Store the new state before notifying, and use release/acquire if publishing other data","Atomic wait has no standard timed or stop-token overload in C++20; lifetime still needs a protocol"],"model":"I can use atomic wait if one atomic value expresses the condition I need to observe. For a queue whose size and closed flag form one invariant, a mutex and condition variable are usually clearer. Atomic wait takes an old value and returns only after observing a different one. It hides internal spurious wakeups, unlike a raw condition wait. But if a value changes away and back before I observe it, I can miss that transition. A generation counter can represent progress more reliably, provided I address wraparound. The producer stores the new state before notifying. If that state publishes other data, I also need the appropriate release and acquire ordering; notification alone is not the publication mechanism. C++20 atomic wait has no timed or stop-token overload. I would keep the condition-variable design if those requirements matter, and preserve the atomic object's lifetime until every user finishes.","follow":"Does seeing the published pointer also guarantee that the pointed-to object remains alive?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-cheat-sheet","title":"C++ concurrency cheat sheet, with pthreads","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"cpp-cheat-sheet.s3","q":"\"You only have C and pthreads on this Mac. Build me a semaphore that can time out, and explain the traps.\"","points":["Use a nonnegative permit count, mutex and condition variable; macOS sem_init fails with ENOSYS","Acquire loops while the count is zero, then decrements under the mutex; release increments and signals","Compute one absolute deadline with the condition variable's clock, and re-check the count even on timeout","CLOCK_REALTIME can jump, and mutex reacquisition can delay return past the deadline","Handle pthread return codes directly, unlike semaphore errno conventions; define cancellation and overflow policy","Join all users before destroying the semaphore and test permit bounds, wakeups and timeout races"],"model":"I would implement the semaphore with a permit count, a mutex and a condition variable. On this Mac, unnamed POSIX semaphores are unavailable: <code>sem_init<\/code> reports failure with ENOSYS. Named semaphores are another option, but the monitor implementation also lets me explain timed acquisition. Acquire takes the mutex, waits in a loop while the count is zero, then decrements before unlocking. Release increments under the same mutex and signals a waiter. The count remembers permits; the signal itself does not. For timeout, I compute one absolute deadline before entering the wait loop, using the condition variable's configured clock. The default is realtime, so a clock adjustment can change elapsed waiting time. I do not restart the deadline after a wakeup. Even when timed wait reports timeout, I inspect the count under the mutex once more, because a release may have raced with that timeout. Reacquiring the mutex can also delay return, so I would not promise an exact wall-clock bound. I handle pthread errors from return values directly and define valid counts, overflow and cancellation policy. Finally, all users must finish before destruction. I would test the maximum number inside, blocked acquisition followed by release, empty timeout, and a release racing with expiry.","follow":"How would you provide a monotonic timeout on Linux, and what is different on macOS?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-cheat-sheet","title":"C++ concurrency cheat sheet, with pthreads","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"phrasing-bank.s1","q":"\"Two requests both pass a limit of one hundred when the count is ninety-nine. Explain the bug and defend your fix.\"","points":["The admission check and increment are one compound operation; individually atomic accesses are insufficient","Give the interleaving where both read 99 and increment to 100 and 101","Use one lock around check and update, or a CAS loop that re-checks the limit on failure","Name the successful increment or CAS as the admission decision point and preserve the exact limit","LongAdder's live sum cannot make an atomic admission decision; window state must share the protocol","Test boundary values and concurrent callers with a start gate, bounded test runtime and appropriate race detection"],"model":"The bug is that the check and the increment are separate operations. Even if both use an atomic counter, the admission decision as a whole is not atomic. At ninety-nine, caller A reads the count and passes the check. Caller B reads the same count and also passes. Their atomic increments then take the count to one hundred and one hundred and one, admitting two requests for one remaining slot. I would start with one lock around the check and the increment. The successful increment while holding that lock is the decision point. A later caller sees one hundred under the same lock and rejects. If the state really is only a counter, a compare-and-set loop is another valid design: compute a candidate, try the update, and on failure re-read through the failed comparison and re-check the limit. I would not use LongAdder for admission, because summing and incrementing are not one atomic decision. A sliding window also involves expiration state, which belongs in the same synchronization protocol. I would test zero, one remaining slot, and full capacity, then release many callers behind a start gate and assert exactly one additional admission. I would bound test runtime and use ThreadSanitizer for C++ data races, while separately reasoning about this logic race.","follow":"How does the design change when requests expire from a sliding window while new requests arrive?","seconds":90,"lesson":"phrasing-bank","title":"Saying it like a senior","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"phrasing-bank.s2","q":"\"We use Kafka transactions. Can the consumer safely retry an email send after a timeout?\"","points":["Kafka's transaction boundary does not automatically include an external email provider","A timeout can mean the email was accepted but its acknowledgement was lost","Recording sent before the send risks loss, and recording it after the send risks duplication","Require durable provider-side idempotency or a transaction including the effect; otherwise state the weaker guarantee","An outbox atomically records intent with database state but its relay can still repeat delivery","Test a successful send followed by a lost response and a crash before recording completion"],"model":"Only if the email operation itself supports safe retries; Kafka transactions alone do not provide that guarantee. They can commit Kafka output records and consumed offsets together, but the email provider is outside that transaction. Suppose the provider accepts the email and its response is lost. The consumer times out without knowing whether the send happened. Retrying can send a second email. A local sent flag does not close this gap. If I record sent before making the call, a crash between those steps can lose the email. If I record it afterwards, a crash after sending but before recording can duplicate it. I would use a stable operation key that the provider durably deduplicates, with the same payload and a retention period covering our retries. A transaction that actually includes the effect is another solution where available. If neither exists, I must state the weaker guarantee and choose between possible loss and possible duplicates. An outbox is useful for committing database changes and email intent together, but its relay can still repeat delivery, so the provider boundary remains. I would test successful delivery followed by a lost response, then a process crash before completion is recorded. Those failure windows are the reason for the design, not just unusual edge cases.","follow":"If the provider forgets idempotency keys after a day, what retry and reconciliation policy would you choose?","seconds":90,"lesson":"phrasing-bank","title":"Saying it like a senior","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"company-playbooks.s1","q":"\"You cannot use library executors or blocking queues here. How would you structure a worker pool, and what would you clarify before coding?\"","points":["Start with a bounded queue, workers, one state lock and condition variables","Clarify submission, overload, completion and allowed primitives before coding","State admission and shutdown invariants and their shared synchronization","Explain predicate waits, task execution outside the lock and exception delivery","Distinguish draining shutdown from cooperative cancellation and termination","Give targeted tests for blocked callers, failure and shutdown races"],"model":"I would build a bounded task queue and a fixed set of workers, with one lock protecting the queue and lifecycle state. Before coding, I would confirm which primitives are allowed, whether submit blocks or rejects when full, and whether callers need a future for results. I would also separate draining shutdown from immediate cancellation. My admission rule is that a submission either becomes accepted under the state lock or fails without entering the queue. Shutdown changes that same state under the same lock, so no task slips through an unprotected gap. Workers wait in a predicate loop for work or shutdown. They remove work under the lock and execute it outside the lock, so slow tasks do not block queue operations. Task failures reach the future or an explicit error handler, and ordinary task exceptions do not silently kill a worker. Draining shutdown rejects new work but finishes accepted work. Immediate shutdown removes pending work and requests cooperative cancellation of running tasks; it cannot safely force arbitrary code to stop. I would then wait for termination separately. My tests would cover capacity one, a throwing task, a submit racing shutdown, and shutdown with callers already blocked. Every test needs a time bound and checks for lost or duplicated work.","follow":"How would you define wait-for-all if other threads keep submitting tasks while it waits?","seconds":90,"lesson":"company-playbooks","title":"Company playbooks","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"company-playbooks.s2","q":"\"Two requests both see 99 admissions out of 100. Walk me through the bug, your fix, and what changes for several servers.\"","points":["State the invariant and the check-then-act interleaving that admits two requests","Protect check and increment together, identifying the success and failure linearization points","Explain why separate atomic operations or a concurrent map alone do not fix it","Choose per-key synchronization only when the budget is per key","Use shared authoritative admission or partitioned budgets across servers and test the boundary"],"model":"The bug is that the capacity check and admission are separate, so the system can accept two requests when only one place remains. Both callers read 99, both decide there is space, and both report success. Even if the stored count ends at 100, we have admitted 101 requests in total. I would protect the check and increment with one lock. Success takes effect at the increment, and failure takes effect at the protected full-budget check. The second caller then sees 100 and fails. An atomic load followed by an atomic increment still has the decision gap; a compare-and-swap loop can close it. A concurrent map alone also does not make a sequence on one entry atomic. For independent user budgets, I can move to per-key state, provided its creation and eviction are coordinated. Across servers, a local lock cannot enforce one shared limit. I need an authoritative atomic admission operation or an explicit partition of the budget. I would test the last-slot race and explain the behavior when that authority is unavailable.","follow":"How would you add expiry without resetting the same user's budget twice?","seconds":75,"lesson":"company-playbooks","title":"Company playbooks","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"final-checklist.s1","q":"\"Your queue passes the producer and consumer test. Walk me through what you check before calling it done.\"","points":["Name the queue and closed flag, their common lock, and the capacity invariant","Identify enqueue, dequeue and close linearization points, including a put racing close","Explain predicate rechecks and notification of both wait sets on close","Distinguish draining from cancellation, and verify exception paths and object lifetime","Describe targeted lifecycle tests, bounded duration and the limits of a clean sanitizer run"],"model":"I would check the contract and shutdown paths before calling it done. The deque and closed flag share one lock, and the size must stay between zero and capacity whenever that lock is released. Enqueue and dequeue take effect when they mutate the deque. Close takes effect when it sets the flag. If a put wins that race, draining close preserves its item; if close wins, the put fails. Producers wait for space or closure, and consumers wait for data or closure. Every wakeup rechecks the predicate, and close wakes both groups. A closed queue drains its remaining items, then reports end of stream. I would check that exceptions preserve the invariant and release the lock, and that timed waits use one monotonic budget. Cancellation is a separate contract, not a promise that running code stops immediately. I would test close before waiting, close while producers or consumers are blocked, capacity one, interruption where supported, and repeated close. I would bound the tests so a hang becomes a failure, and run C++ under ThreadSanitizer. Passing tests supports the implementation, but I still owe the invariant and ordering argument. Finally, I would join the workers before destroying their shared state.","follow":"What changes if close must discard queued items instead of draining them?","seconds":90,"lesson":"final-checklist","title":"The final checklist","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"final-checklist.s2","q":"\"Shutdown sometimes hangs even though the stop flag is atomic. What do you inspect first?\"","points":["Distinguish flag visibility from waking a blocked thread","Inspect stacks and the exact predicate and wait set","Explain the check-to-wait gap and using the same mutex for predicate changes and waiting","Wake every affected wait set and explain Java interruption versus C++ cooperative stop","Join outside locks the worker needs and bound tests for both close orderings"],"model":"I would first check whether the worker is blocked rather than reading the flag. Making a flag atomic does not wake a condition wait, an I/O call or a future wait. I would inspect the stacks and identify the exact predicate and wait set. For a condition variable, the closer must change the predicate under the same mutex used by the waiter. Otherwise the closer can set the flag and notify after the worker checks it but before the worker starts waiting. That notification is not saved. I would update the predicate under the mutex and wake every affected wait set. The worker then rechecks the predicate after reacquiring the lock. In Java, interruption can end interruptible waits, but it must be handled correctly. In C++, requesting stop does not automatically wake an ordinary condition variable. Finally, I would join outside any lock the worker needs and ensure the object outlives its threads. Tests should force both close-before-wait and close-while-waiting, with a timeout to catch regressions.","follow":"What if the worker is blocked in I/O that does not respond to your cancellation mechanism?","seconds":75,"lesson":"final-checklist","title":"The final checklist","track":"core","module":18},{"id":"cpp-how-it-runs.s1","q":"\"My threaded program compiles each source file, but the final build says a counter method is undefined. Walk me through the build and where you would look.\"","points":["Successful per-file compilation followed by an undefined symbol points to linking, not a runtime threading failure","Preprocessing expands headers into translation units; compilation produces object files with definitions and unresolved references","A declaration permits compilation, but the linker still needs the matching definition","Check the definition, signature, namespace and whether its object file or library is linked","Include guards act within one translation unit; they neither supply missing definitions nor prevent cross-file duplication","Compile and link sanitizer builds with the sanitizer flag before exercising threaded paths"],"model":"I would investigate the link step. Compiling a call proves that the compiler saw a suitable declaration; it does not prove that the program contains the method's definition. First, preprocessing expands each source file's includes and macros into a translation unit. The compiler turns each translation unit into an object file, recording both the symbols it defines and the symbols it still needs. The linker then combines those objects and libraries and resolves the references. For the missing counter method, I would check that its body exists, that its parameter types, const qualification and namespace match the declaration, and that the object file containing it is actually in the link command. An include guard cannot fix that. It prevents repeated inclusion within one translation unit, not missing definitions or duplicate definitions across different files. Once the program links, I would exercise the threaded paths in a sanitizer build. The ThreadSanitizer flag belongs on both compilation and linking so the instrumentation and runtime are present. A successful build gets me to runtime testing; it does not establish thread safety.","follow":"What changes if the missing method belongs to a template whose definition is hidden in a source file?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-how-it-runs","title":"How C++ becomes a running program","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-how-it-runs.s2","q":"\"A worker polls a plain boolean stop flag. It seems fine in debug builds and hangs when optimized. Would you blame the compiler or add volatile?\"","points":["The unordered read and write of a plain flag form a data race and cause undefined behavior in C++","Optimization may reuse a loaded value or transform the loop; the symptom alone does not prove a specific transformation","C++ volatile supplies neither inter-thread synchronization nor atomicity","Use an atomic flag or one shared mutex; publishing associated data also needs an appropriate happens-before edge","Use ThreadSanitizer and optimized tests as evidence, not a proof that all races are absent"],"model":"I would fix the data race first. The worker reads an ordinary boolean while another thread writes it, and there is no synchronization ordering those accesses. In C++, that makes the program's behavior undefined, including in the debug build that seemed to work. The optimizer may load the flag once and reuse the value, or transform the loop another way. The hang is consistent with that, but I would inspect the generated code before claiming a particular transformation happened. Adding volatile is not a thread-safety fix in C++; it supplies neither atomicity nor the ordering needed between these threads. I would use an atomic boolean, starting with its default sequentially consistent operations, or protect the state with one mutex. If the flag also publishes a result, I must ensure the reader observes the publishing store before reading that result, and that no later unsynchronized writes race with it. Finally, I would run ThreadSanitizer and optimized tests. They can expose failures on exercised paths, but a clean run is supporting evidence, not a proof covering every execution.","follow":"If the flag only requests stopping and publishes no other data, could relaxed atomic operations be enough?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-how-it-runs","title":"How C++ becomes a running program","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-how-it-runs.s3","q":"\"ThreadSanitizer says two counter updates race, but both lines hold a mutex named <code>stats_mutex<\/code>. The report names different mutex addresses. How would you explain that?\"","points":["Matching source names do not imply the same mutex object; different addresses explain missing exclusion","A namespace-scope <code>static<\/code> mutex in a header has internal linkage and creates a separate object in each translation unit","One <code>extern<\/code> counter can therefore be updated concurrently while each thread holds its own mutex","Encapsulate the counter and mutex in one source file, or use an extern declaration and one definition","C++17 inline variables provide one shared object with external linkage; inline is not a request for guaranteed call substitution","Rerun the concurrent count check and ThreadSanitizer, and inspect all access paths"],"model":"The two updates are holding different mutex objects. The same spelling in source code does not guarantee the same object in the running program. I would inspect the header for a namespace-scope <code>static<\/code> mutex or an unnamed namespace. That gives the mutex internal linkage, so each translation unit gets its own instance. If the counter is declared extern and defined once, both threads still reach one counter while holding different locks. Each lock works locally, but neither excludes the other update. My preferred repair is to put the counter and its mutex together in one source file and expose operations that always use that mutex. Alternatively, the header can declare the mutex extern and one source file can define it. In C++17, an inline variable with external linkage is another way to provide one shared object from a header. Here inline concerns definitions and identity; it does not promise that calls are substituted by the optimizer. I would then check every counter access, rerun the exact-total test concurrently, and run ThreadSanitizer again to look for remaining conflicting accesses.","follow":"Why would adding an include guard to that header leave the original race unchanged?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-how-it-runs","title":"How C++ becomes a running program","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-values-references-pointers.s1","q":"\"How would you explain values, references and pointers to a junior who is passing data to a worker thread? Does const make the sharing safe?\"","points":["A value parameter is a separate object, but copied pointers, views and shared owners can still alias underlying data","A reference is bound to one object and cannot be reseated; a pointer can be null or reseated","Both references and pointers can dangle and do not by themselves establish ownership","Const restricts writes through that access path, not writes through other aliases","Check lifetime and ordering of conflicting accesses; prefer independent values or read-only sharing when appropriate"],"model":"I would separate three questions: what object the worker reaches, who keeps it alive, and how concurrent accesses are coordinated. A value parameter is a separate object. Copying a vector of integers gives independent elements, but copying a pointer or a shared owner still leads to the same underlying data. A reference is another name for one object: it is bound when initialized and cannot be reseated. A pointer holds an address and can be null or changed to point elsewhere. Both references and pointers can dangle if the object dies, and neither a raw pointer nor a reference establishes ownership by itself. Const does not solve the threading problem. A const reference prevents writes through that name, while another alias can still modify the object. If those ordinary reads and writes are unordered across threads, they race. I would prefer an independent value when copying is reasonable, or read-only sharing when the object's lifetime is guaranteed. If mutation must be shared, I need a synchronization policy covering every conflicting access. Lifetime safety and data-race freedom are separate obligations.","follow":"If two workers each hold a copy of the same shared pointer, which of those obligations does it help with?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-values-references-pointers","title":"Values, references and pointers","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-values-references-pointers.s2","q":"\"A worker takes <code>const int&amp;<\/code>. I launch it with <code>x<\/code> equal to 7, then set <code>x<\/code> to 9. What does it see, and what changes if I pass <code>std::cref(x)<\/code>?\"","points":["With an ordinary x argument, <code>std::thread<\/code> stores a copy made during construction before the caller's later assignment","The const reference parameter binds to that stored copy, so the worker reads 7","<code>std::cref<\/code> stores a reference wrapper, so the worker instead reads the original object","An unordered assignment to the original then races with the worker read; const and a later join do not repair that race","Sharing requires the original to outlive access and conflicting accesses to be synchronized, or mutation delayed until after join"],"model":"With the ordinary argument, the worker reads seven. The thread constructor stores a copy of the integer in the launching thread before the later assignment to nine. The worker's const reference binds to that stored copy, not to the caller's original integer. With <code>std::cref(x)<\/code>, the stored object is instead a reference wrapper, and the worker reaches the original integer. Now the assignment to nine conflicts with the worker's read. Unless something orders those accesses, that is a data race and undefined behavior; I cannot answer that it simply prints seven or nine. Const only prevents mutation through the worker's reference. Joining afterward waits for completion, but it does not order the earlier assignment against a read that happened inside the worker. I would either retain the independent copy, delay changing the original until after joining, or synchronize the read and write using an appropriate shared protocol. I would also ensure the original remains alive until the worker finishes using it. Choosing a wrapper is therefore a sharing decision with both lifetime and synchronization consequences, not just a way to avoid a copy.","follow":"What happens if the worker takes <code>int&amp;<\/code> and you pass <code>x<\/code> without a reference wrapper?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-values-references-pointers","title":"Values, references and pointers","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-values-references-pointers.s3","q":"\"This getter locks a mutex and returns a const reference to its vector. Writers use the same mutex. Why can the caller still crash, and what would you return instead?\"","points":["The guard releases the mutex when the getter returns, before the caller uses the reference","A writer can modify or reallocate vector storage while the caller iterates, creating a race and potentially dangling element references","Const restricts the caller's writes but does not prevent the writer from changing the vector","Return a copy made while locked, or perform a narrow lookup under the lock and return its result by value","Large read-mostly data can use immutable snapshots with synchronized publication and retained ownership"],"model":"The lock protects obtaining the reference, not using it afterward. As soon as the getter returns, its guard releases the mutex. The caller may then begin iterating while a writer acquires the mutex and pushes another element. If the vector reallocates, the reader's element references and iterators can point into freed storage. Even without reallocation, unsynchronized access to changing vector state can race. Returning a const reference only prevents the caller from modifying through that reference; it does not prevent the writer from changing the vector. My simplest fix is to return a vector copy constructed while the mutex is held. The caller owns that snapshot and can iterate independently. If it only needs one answer, I would instead perform the lookup under the lock and return a copied result. Both approaches keep the actual shared-data access inside the protected interval. For a very large, mostly read collection, immutable snapshots can reduce copying, but publication must be synchronized and each reader must retain ownership for its whole use. That adds complexity, so I would justify it with the workload.","follow":"If the vector contains shared pointers to mutable objects, does copying the vector also make the pointed-to objects safe to read?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-values-references-pointers","title":"Values, references and pointers","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-lifetime-stack-heap.s1","q":"\"A detached worker captures a local vector by reference. It passes every test on your laptop but crashes under load. Walk me through the bug and your fix.\"","points":["The reference capture does not extend the vector's lifetime","The caller can return and destroy the vector before the worker finishes, causing undefined behavior","Move the vector into the worker when the caller no longer needs it","Alternatively join before the vector dies, including on exception paths","Shared ownership preserves lifetime but does not synchronize mutations"],"model":"I would fix the ownership first. The lambda holds a reference to the vector, and that reference does not keep the vector alive. Under load, the worker can be delayed until after the caller returns. The vector is destroyed, its buffer is released, and the worker reads a dead object. That is undefined behavior, even if earlier runs happened to finish in time. If the caller no longer needs the data, I would move the vector into the lambda so the worker owns it. If the worker must borrow it, I would join before the vector leaves scope, using a jthread declared after the vector to cover exceptions too. Shared ownership is another option when several tasks need the same data. It keeps the object alive, but concurrent mutations still need synchronization. I would document both who owns the data and when the worker finishes.","follow":"What changes if the captured value is a <code>std::string_view<\/code> rather than an owning vector?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-lifetime-stack-heap","title":"Object lifetime, the stack and the heap","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-lifetime-stack-heap.s2","q":"\"Your class owns a queue, a mutex and a jthread. Its destructor sometimes hangs, and another version crashes at shutdown. How would you review the lifetime design?\"","points":["Join the worker before destroying any member it uses","Construction follows member declaration order; destruction reverses it","Declare the worker last and finish initializing everything it accesses before starting it","A jthread requests cooperative stop and joins; blocked waits must be made responsive to stopping","Never join while holding a mutex the worker needs to finish","The destructor body must also preserve worker-visible state until the join completes"],"model":"I would make the worker finish before any state it uses is destroyed. Members are constructed in declaration order and destroyed in reverse order, regardless of the initializer list. I would declare the queue and synchronization objects before the jthread, with the worker last. That ensures its members exist before the worker starts, and its destructor joins before those members disappear. I would also check that the worker does not depend on setup still happening in the constructor body. For the hang, I would inspect the stop path. A jthread requests a stop and joins; it does not kill the worker. An ordinary condition variable wait does not automatically wake for that request. The worker needs a stop-aware wait or a shutdown predicate and notification. I would release any mutex the worker needs before joining. Finally, the enclosing destructor body runs before member destruction. It must not clear the queue or invalidate other worker state before the worker finishes. Declaration order helps, but the constructor body, destructor body and shutdown protocol must all respect the same lifetime rule.","follow":"How would you implement the same ownership and shutdown guarantees with C++17 <code>std::thread<\/code>?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-lifetime-stack-heap","title":"Object lifetime, the stack and the heap","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-lifetime-stack-heap.s3","q":"\"Why would you use a function-local static for a lazy registry, and exactly what does its thread-safety guarantee cover?\"","points":["C++11 guarantees synchronized initialization of a function-local static","One caller initializes successfully and concurrent arrivals wait for completion","A throwing initializer can be retried; recursive re-entry during initialization is undefined behavior","Later mutations still require their own synchronization","Join users before static destruction; use call_once for per-object lazy setup"],"model":"I would use a function-local static because C++11 already provides the synchronization for lazy construction. When several callers arrive, one performs initialization and the others wait for it to finish. They then receive the same fully initialized registry. The precise promise is one successful initialization. If construction throws, that call fails and a later attempt can retry. Recursively entering the same declaration while initialization is in progress is undefined behavior, so I would avoid that dependency cycle. This protects construction only. If the registry later updates a map, those updates and its reads still need a mutex or another suitable protocol. I would also join every user before shutdown destroys the registry. For lazy setup that belongs to each connection object, rather than one registry for the whole program, I would use a separate once flag and call_once in each connection.","follow":"Would making the registry <code>thread_local<\/code> solve the same problem, or change the design?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-lifetime-stack-heap","title":"Object lifetime, the stack and the heap","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-classes-raii.s1","q":"\"A function holds a lock_guard, updates one field, and throws before updating a second field that must agree with it. Has RAII made this safe?\"","points":["RAII releases the mutex during unwinding but does not restore the two-field invariant","The next thread taking the mutex can observe the partial update","The basic exception guarantee preserves invariants; the strong guarantee leaves the logical state unchanged on failure","Perform throwing work before a nonthrowing commit, or roll back while still holding the lock","Preparation outside the lock requires revalidation if it depends on mutable shared state"],"model":"RAII has made lock cleanup safe, but it has not made the update safe. When the exception unwinds this scope, the guard releases the mutex. The next thread can acquire it and observe the first field changed while the second still has its old value. If those fields must agree, the object has lost its invariant. The basic exception guarantee requires invariants to survive failure. The strong guarantee goes further and leaves the logical state unchanged. I would arrange the operation so everything that can throw happens before a commit that cannot throw. For example, prepare a new profile name and tags, then swap both into place under the same lock. If I must change shared state before a later step can fail, I need a reliable rollback before releasing the lock. Preparing outside the lock can reduce contention, but if that preparation depends on current shared state, I must revalidate it under the lock. My review therefore checks both resource cleanup and the state another thread can observe after failure.","follow":"How would you test that the invariant survives an allocation failure in the second update?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-classes-raii","title":"Classes, constructors, destructors and RAII","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-classes-raii.s2","q":"\"You are implementing a queue and a transfer between two accounts. How do you choose among lock_guard, unique_lock and scoped_lock?\"","points":["Use a named lock_guard for one mutex held for the whole scope","Use unique_lock for condition variable waiting or controlled unlock and relock","Use C++17 scoped_lock to acquire multiple distinct mutexes with deadlock avoidance","Handle a transfer to the same account before attempting to lock both mutexes","An unnamed temporary guard expires at the semicolon and protects no following statement"],"model":"I choose the guard according to the operation. For a single mutex held until scope exit, I use a named lock_guard. It is the simplest ownership model and releases the mutex on return or during unwinding. In a blocking queue, I use unique_lock because the condition variable must release the mutex while waiting and reacquire it before checking the predicate again. It also supports an explicit unlock and relock when the algorithm needs that. For a transfer between two different accounts, I use C++17 scoped_lock with both mutexes, so acquisition uses deadlock avoidance. I handle a transfer to the same account first, because passing the same nonrecursive mutex twice is invalid. In each case the guard needs a name. A temporary guard dies at its semicolon, leaving the next statement unprotected. The guard's scope should cover the entire invariant update and no unrelated slow work.","follow":"What can still deadlock if code under those guards calls a helper that locks one of the same mutexes?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-classes-raii","title":"Classes, constructors, destructors and RAII","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-classes-raii.s3","q":"\"A class with a mutex no longer copies. We need a snapshot while another thread updates it. How would you design copying and assignment?\"","points":["A mutex is neither copyable nor movable, so default memberwise copying is deleted","If copying is required, give the destination a fresh mutex and copy data while holding the source mutex","Assignment checks for self-assignment and locks both objects with scoped_lock","Locking both objects together avoids deadlock between assignments in opposite directions","Preserve invariants if copying throws, and state that explicit copy operations suppress implicit moves"],"model":"I would give each object its own mutex and copy only the protected data. A mutex cannot be copied or moved, so the compiler correctly rejects default memberwise copying. If the API only needs snapshots, returning a separate value object is often simpler. If the class itself must be copyable, its copy constructor can default construct a fresh mutex, lock the source, and copy the data while that lock is held. The destination is not yet published, so the source lock is the one that matters. Assignment is different because both objects already exist. I would first handle self-assignment, then lock both mutexes with scoped_lock. That avoids locking one mutex twice and avoids a deadlock when two threads assign in opposite directions. I would prepare any throwing copy before committing, or otherwise preserve the destination's invariants on failure. Finally, declaring copy operations suppresses implicit moves. I would state that policy explicitly rather than assume the compiler supplies a cheap move. The snapshot represents one protected moment; it is independent of later updates.","follow":"If you add a destructor only for logging, how can that change move behavior for a class containing a vector?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-classes-raii","title":"Classes, constructors, destructors and RAII","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-const-and-mutable.s1","q":"\"Your balance getter is const, but its mutex is mutable. Explain why that is legitimate and whether const makes the getter thread-safe.\"","points":["The getter preserves the account's logical value even though locking changes internal mutex state","A const member function cannot lock an ordinary non-mutable mutex member","Mutable permits modification through const access but supplies no synchronization","Readers and writers protect the balance with the same mutex","Const access is shallow and does not stop writes through other aliases"],"model":"The getter is logically const because reading the balance does not change the account's value. Locking still changes the mutex's internal state. Through a const member function, an ordinary mutex member would be const, and its lock operation requires a non-const mutex. Declaring that member mutable allows this bookkeeping without making the balance mutable. The keyword supplies permission to modify the member, not synchronization. The actual safety comes from the getter and every writer taking the same mutex around the balance. A const qualifier alone does not make a user-defined function safe to call concurrently. It can still write a mutable cache, or follow a pointer to shared data. A const reference also does not stop another alias from modifying the account. I would therefore explain constness as the interface promise and the mutex protocol as the mechanism that keeps concurrent access safe.","follow":"Why would returning a const reference to a protected string be risky after the getter releases its lock?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-const-and-mutable","title":"const correctness and mutable","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-const-and-mutable.s2","q":"\"Two callers use a const getter under shared locks. You add a lazy cache and now ThreadSanitizer reports a race. What happened, and which fix would you choose?\"","points":["Shared locks admit both callers; filling the cache is a physical write despite logical constness","Unsynchronized conflicting accesses are a data race even when both compute the same result","An exclusive lock must protect checking, computing or publishing, and reading the cache","For immutable input, eager computation or call_once can avoid repeated cache locking","Changing input requires synchronized invalidation; call_once is not a resettable cache mechanism","Choose lock mode by actual writes, including LRU recency updates"],"model":"The getter stopped being physically read-only when it began filling the cache. Shared locks let both callers enter, so both can see an empty cache and write its value and readiness state. That is a data race and undefined behavior, even when they compute the same answer. My simplest fix is an exclusive lock protecting the cache check, initialization and read. If the input never changes, I would also consider computing eagerly in the constructor. That removes lazy writes entirely, provided the finished object is safely published. If computation is expensive and often unused, call_once is a good alternative for one successful initialization. If the input can change, I need writers to invalidate the cache under the same protocol; a once flag is not resettable and does not solve that case. I would choose using the cost of computation and the update pattern, then measure contention. The same reasoning applies to an LRU getter: it may return a value without changing that value, but moving its list node is still a write. Lock mode follows the implementation's accesses.","follow":"Would making only the cache's ready flag atomic be enough, and what publication and initialization rules would still be needed?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-const-and-mutable","title":"const correctness and mutable","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-const-and-mutable.s3","q":"\"A teammate says STL containers always need a mutex. Another says const access is always safe. Give me the precise rules you would use in a review.\"","points":["Concurrent standard container read operations are supported with safe publication and lifetime","Certain non-const overloads count as const for data races, such as find and at; associative operator[] does not","Existing distinct vector<int> elements may be updated concurrently without structural changes","Vector<bool> is excluded from the distinct-element guarantee","Structural changes and same-element conflicting access need appropriate synchronization","Safe individual calls do not make a multi-call check and update atomic"],"model":"I would inspect which operations run together and which memory they touch. Standard containers support concurrent read operations on a safely published, live container. The rules also treat some non-const overloads as const for data races, including find and at. Map subscript is excluded because it can insert a missing key, so I would use find or at for lookup. A pre-sized vector of integers allows two threads to modify different existing elements while its structure stays fixed. The packed vector of bool is excluded from that guarantee, because separate bits can share storage. If threads access the same element and one writes, I need synchronization for that element. If an operation changes the container's structure, such as push_back, I need to coordinate accesses that can conflict with metadata changes or invalidation. A mutex around those operations is the straightforward design. Finally, safe individual calls do not make a check followed by an insertion atomic. I would protect that whole decision with one protocol. Const is useful information, but I still check aliases, element behavior and the complete operation.","follow":"Would reserving enough vector capacity make concurrent <code>push_back<\/code> and <code>size<\/code> safe?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-const-and-mutable","title":"const correctness and mutable","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-types-casts-and-initialization.s1","q":"\"The queue has locks everywhere, but passing minus one as its capacity gets past validation. Walk me through what you check.\"","points":["Check the signed input before any unsigned conversion or allocation","Explain that conversion to size_t turns minus one into a huge positive value","Check range and application maximum, then convert once","Distinguish vector count construction with parentheses from initializer-list construction with braces","Separate arithmetic and initialization correctness from locking"],"model":"I check the type boundary first. Locks cannot repair a capacity that was converted incorrectly. If the constructor receives a signed integer and stores it in size_t, minus one becomes a huge unsigned value. A later test that the capacity is positive accepts it. I validate the original input for positivity, representability and our application limit before converting or allocating. Then I convert once and use that validated value consistently. I also inspect the vector initializer. Parentheses with three create three elements; braces with three create one element containing three. That can leave the capacity field disagreeing with the storage even for valid input. Finally, I test negative, zero, maximum and just-too-large capacities. Once those sequential invariants hold, I examine the locking around queue operations. The conversion rule and the synchronization rule need separate proofs.","follow":"What changes if the API must return the queue size as an int?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-types-casts-and-initialization","title":"Types, casts, conversions and initialization","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-types-casts-and-initialization.s2","q":"\"You see a static cast from a task base pointer to a concrete task. Would you keep it, replace it, or redesign the interface?\"","points":["Keep a static downcast only with a proven dynamic type and valid inheritance relationship","Use dynamic_cast for an uncertain live polymorphic object and handle pointer or reference failure","Prefer virtual dispatch when the operation belongs on the interface","Explain why reinterpret_cast and const_cast do not solve a dynamic-type mismatch","Check lifetime and synchronization independently of cast success"],"model":"I would keep it only if the surrounding design proves the concrete type and the inheritance relationship permits that downcast. A static cast does no runtime type check, so the wrong object makes the downcast undefined. If the type is uncertain, I use dynamic cast through a polymorphic base and handle failure. A pointer cast returns null; a reference cast throws bad cast. If every task supports the operation, I would instead put it on the virtual interface and avoid type tests at each caller. Reinterpret cast does not repair a wrong dynamic type, and const cast only changes qualification. I also check who owns the task and whether another thread can destroy or mutate it. Even a successful checked cast does not keep the object alive or synchronize access. Those are separate responsibilities of the task handoff.","follow":"Why might a static downcast be rejected even when you know the concrete type?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-types-casts-and-initialization","title":"Types, casts, conversions and initialization","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-types-casts-and-initialization.s3","q":"\"Two source files both lock a mutex from the same header, yet updates still race. What declaration would you look for?\"","points":["Look for namespace static or an anonymous namespace creating one mutex per translation unit","Explain why identical spelling does not imply the same lock","Use an extern declaration with one definition, or an external-linkage inline variable since C++17","Distinguish class static shared state from instance state and match the lock to it","Explain that safe local-static initialization does not protect subsequent mutation"],"model":"I look for a namespace static mutex or a mutex in an anonymous namespace in the header. Each translation unit then gets its own object. Both threads can hold their locks simultaneously, so identical spelling does not establish mutual exclusion around shared data. I would declare the mutex extern in the header and define it in one source file, or use an external-linkage inline variable in C++17 or later. I then check that every conflicting access uses that same mutex. The related class bug is a static counter protected by separate instance mutexes. The counter is shared across instances, so its protection must be shared too. Finally, if the object comes from a function-local static, only initialization is automatically coordinated. Later map changes or counter updates still need synchronization. I verify the lock's identity as well as its presence.","follow":"Does inline force the compiler to substitute the function body at each call?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-types-casts-and-initialization","title":"Types, casts, conversions and initialization","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-memory-and-object-layout.s1","q":"\"This struct has only six bytes of fields. Walk me through why <code>sizeof<\/code> says twelve, and whether you would reorder it in our concurrent service.\"","points":["State the size and alignment assumptions before calculating an ABI-dependent result","Place char, int, char at offsets 0, 4 and 8, then round the complete size to 12","Reordering int, char, char gives offsets 0, 4 and 5 and a size of 8","Check ABI compatibility, initializer positions and construction dependencies before reordering","Different writers can false-share a line; padding does not make shared writes safe","Measure layout and workload performance instead of assuming smaller is always faster"],"model":"The extra bytes are alignment padding. Assuming a one-byte char and a four-byte int aligned to four bytes, the first char sits at offset zero. The int cannot start at offset one, so the compiler leaves three bytes and places it at four. The last char lands at eight. Three tail bytes bring the size to twelve, which keeps the next object in an array correctly aligned. If I put the int first, the chars can occupy offsets four and five, and the complete size becomes eight. Before shipping that change, I would check binary compatibility, aggregate initializers and any constructor dependency on declaration order. In a concurrent service I would also ask which threads write these fields. Packing independent hot counters together can increase false sharing, while padding cannot make writes to the same plain counter safe. I would measure the actual offsets and size on our compiler, then benchmark the real workload. Smaller objects help capacity, but their placement can still hurt throughput.","follow":"What changes if I align the array itself to 64 bytes instead of aligning its element type?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-memory-and-object-layout","title":"Memory and object layout: sizeof, alignment, new and delete","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-memory-and-object-layout.s2","q":"\"We already have a buffer. Why can't I just cast its address to <code>Job*<\/code> and later delete that pointer? Compare the correct options.\"","points":["A cast does not run a nontrivial Job constructor; storage and object lifetime are separate","Require enough storage, correct alignment and exclusive access before placement construction","Use placement new and its returned pointer, then explicitly destroy the constructed object exactly once","Release backing storage using its original allocation family; an automatic buffer is not deleted","Handle constructor failure without destroying an incomplete object; retain RAII ownership of storage","Ordinary new/delete combine both stages and smart owners are the default outside allocator internals"],"model":"A cast gives me a different pointer type; it does not construct a nontrivial Job. I first need enough storage, the right alignment and exclusive access to that slot. Then I can use placement new to construct Job there and keep the pointer it returns. When I remove the job, I explicitly run its destructor exactly once. That releases the job's own resources but leaves the slot's storage available for reuse. I must return that storage according to its original owner. For a local byte array there is no manual deallocation. For malloc storage I eventually call free on the original allocation. Ordinary delete is wrong for either placement pointer because it couples destruction with a different deallocation contract. If construction throws, I leave the slot empty and preserve or release its storage through its owner; there is no complete Job to destroy. Ordinary new and delete combine those stages for me. Outside allocator internals I would use an RAII owner, usually a container or unique pointer, to keep failure paths correct.","follow":"What protects a reference another thread obtained just before you destroyed and reused the slot?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-memory-and-object-layout","title":"Memory and object layout: sizeof, alignment, new and delete","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-memory-and-object-layout.s3","q":"\"Our process grows after every failed request, but ThreadSanitizer reports nothing. How would you investigate on this Mac?\"","points":["A quiet ThreadSanitizer run does not check leaks; distinguish live allocations from resident memory","Repeat and drain a bounded workload, then compare live allocation counts or bytes","The tested Apple Clang runtime rejects detect_leaks; use Instruments or a verified supported runtime","Follow allocation stacks to skipped cleanup, retained work or ownership cycles","Repair ownership with RAII and retest failure paths as well as success"],"model":"I would investigate ownership and retention, because a quiet ThreadSanitizer run says nothing about leaks. First I would repeat one bounded request workload, wait for outstanding work to drain, and compare live allocation counts or bytes. Resident memory alone can grow because of allocator caching or fragmentation. I would then inspect allocation stacks and ask which owner should have released each surviving allocation. On this Mac, the tested Apple Clang runtime rejects the AddressSanitizer leak option, so I would use Instruments or a supported LeakSanitizer runtime validated with a known leak. I would check exceptional exits, abandoned queued work, unbounded caches and ownership cycles. For skipped cleanup, the repair is to establish an RAII owner as soon as allocation succeeds. Finally I would rerun both successful and failing requests and verify that the live allocations return to the expected baseline.","follow":"Could a reachability-based leak detector miss an unbounded cache that still holds every pointer?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-memory-and-object-layout","title":"Memory and object layout: sizeof, alignment, new and delete","track":"cpp","module":1},{"id":"cpp-move-semantics.s1","q":"\"I wrote <code>std::move<\/code>, but a large string was still copied. What does <code>std::move<\/code> actually do, and what would you check?\"","points":["<code>std::move<\/code> is a cast to an rvalue reference; the consuming operation determines whether resources move","Moving from const string selects copying because its ordinary move constructor needs a non-const source","A user-declared destructor can suppress implicit moves and leave a copy fallback","A named rvalue-reference parameter is an lvalue expression when passed onward","A moved-from standard object remains valid; its documented state and operation preconditions govern reuse"],"model":"I would check overload resolution, because std::move does not move anything by itself. It casts the expression to an rvalue reference; the constructor or assignment that consumes it decides what happens. If the string is const, the cast preserves constness. The ordinary string move constructor cannot bind that source, so the copy constructor is selected. For a wrapper class, I would also check whether a user-declared destructor suppressed its implicit moves and left copying as the fallback. Another trap is forwarding from a parameter declared as an rvalue reference. Its name is still an lvalue expression, so passing it onward without another cast can copy. After a real move, I would follow the type's contract. A string remains valid but its value is unspecified; checking empty is allowed, assuming it is empty is not. The cast enables a choice, not a guarantee of cheap transfer.","follow":"Why might a vector copy existing elements during reallocation even when their type has a move constructor?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-move-semantics","title":"Copy, move and move-only types","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-move-semantics.s2","q":"\"Design a queue that hands large tasks from producers to workers without copying the payload. Where does ownership move, and where do you still need locking?\"","points":["Represent each task with unique_ptr so ownership is explicit and cannot be copied","Build the payload before locking and move the owning pointer into the queue under its mutex","Wait with a predicate, then move the front pointer out and remove its slot under the same mutex","Process the owned task after unlocking; a noexcept pointer move avoids losing it through a throwing return move","Moving shared state is a write and does not create a synchronization edge by itself","Ownership transfer does not eliminate escaped aliases or guarantee allocation-free queue operations"],"model":"I would queue unique pointers to tasks, so each task has one explicit owner and the payload stays in place. The producer builds the large task before taking the queue mutex, then moves its pointer into the queue while holding that mutex. A successful move leaves the producer's pointer null. The worker waits with a predicate for an available item. Still under the same mutex, it moves the front pointer into a local and removes the empty slot. It then releases the lock and processes the task through its local owner. Moving the pointer is nonthrowing, which also makes returning the removed task safe from a throwing move. The queue itself still needs synchronization: moving out of its front element modifies shared state, and std::move supplies no ordering. I would also check that the producer has not retained a raw pointer and continued using the task. Unique ownership does not erase aliases. Finally, queue insertion may allocate even though the payload move is cheap. For strict allocation limits, I would choose storage that is prepared in advance.","follow":"What failure guarantees would change if the queue stored a task type whose move constructor could throw?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-move-semantics","title":"Copy, move and move-only types","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-move-semantics.s3","q":"\"Replacing a worker with <code>workers[i] = std::thread(run)<\/code> terminates the process, even when the old worker has finished. Explain the failure and your replacement protocol.\"","points":["Move assignment to a joinable std::thread calls terminate","Finishing the thread function does not make its handle non-joinable","Signal and wake the old worker as needed, join it, then create and assign the replacement","Do not join while holding a mutex needed by the worker, and serialize handle management","Jthread move assignment requests stop and joins the old worker, but stopping remains cooperative","The replacement starts before assignment, so inline replacement can overlap old and new workers"],"model":"The destination handle is still joinable, so thread move assignment calls terminate. Finishing the thread function is not enough: the owner must still join or detach that handle. For an owned worker, I would signal it to stop, wake any blocking wait as required, and join it. Only after the join would I create and assign the replacement. I would release any mutex the old worker needs before joining, and serialize management operations so two callers cannot replace the same slot concurrently. A jthread changes the assignment behavior: it requests a stop and joins the old thread before accepting the new handle. That helps with cleanup, but cancellation is cooperative, so an unresponsive worker can still hang the assignment. There is another timing detail in the original expression. The new thread starts when the right-hand temporary is constructed, before assignment begins. Replacing it inline with a jthread can therefore overlap the old and new workers. If the design requires exactly one active worker for that slot, I would keep stopping, joining and starting as separate ordered steps.","follow":"What state is the source thread handle in after a successful move, and what happens if you call <code>join<\/code> on it?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-move-semantics","title":"Copy, move and move-only types","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-smart-pointers.s1","q":"\"A teammate says our config is thread-safe because it is in a <code>shared_ptr<\/code>. How would you review that claim?\"","points":["Separate control-block safety, access to one pointer variable, and access to the pointee","Different owning handles can be copied or destroyed concurrently without corrupting ownership","Copying a shared variable while another thread assigns it needs a mutex or atomic shared pointer","An immutable config snapshot permits reading outside the pointer lock; mutable fields need their own protection","Build outside the lock, copy or swap under it, and release the old owner outside it","The final owner triggers destruction on its thread, which can affect latency and locking"],"model":"The reference count is safe, but that does not make the configuration thread-safe. I would review three separate things: the ownership bookkeeping, the pointer variable, and the config itself. Different shared pointers can safely own the same object while different threads copy or destroy their own handles. That protects lifetime. But if a reload thread assigns one global pointer while a reader copies that same variable, those operations race. An atomic reference count does not make copying the handle atomic. I would guard the copy and replacement with one mutex, or use an atomic shared pointer where the C++20 library supports it. Next I would make each published config immutable. A reader takes the mutex, copies an owning handle, unlocks, and uses that snapshot. The writer builds a complete replacement outside the lock and swaps it in under the lock. Existing readers can finish on the old version. I would also release the writer's old handle outside the lock, because releasing it might run a destructor. Whichever thread drops the final owner triggers destruction, so cleanup must fit that thread's latency and locking constraints. This design separates safe publication, stable lifetime, and immutable contents; mutable contents would still need their own synchronization.","follow":"Would checking that <code>use_count()<\/code> is one let the writer safely modify the config in place?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-smart-pointers","title":"unique_ptr, shared_ptr and weak_ptr","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-smart-pointers.s2","q":"\"You are handing work to a background executor. When would you capture a <code>unique_ptr<\/code>, a <code>shared_ptr<\/code>, or a <code>weak_ptr<\/code>?\"","points":["Move a unique pointer when the task takes sole ownership and existing aliases will not access the object","Capture a shared pointer by value when the task must keep the object alive alongside other owners","Capture a weak pointer when work should be skipped if the object has died","Call weak-pointer lock once, test the result, and retain that owner throughout use","An expired check followed by lock can fail between the two calls","Ownership does not synchronize mutable state, and stored strong callbacks can create cycles"],"model":"I choose according to the lifetime the work needs. If a job belongs entirely to one task, I move a unique pointer into the closure and stop accessing the job through any old aliases. That gives the task sole ownership without reference counting. If several operations must keep a session alive, I capture a shared pointer by value. The queued task then owns a share even after its caller returns. If the operation is optional, like a delayed refresh that should disappear with its session, I capture a weak pointer. When the task runs, I call its lock function once and check the returned shared pointer. A successful result keeps the session alive for the whole operation. Checking expired first is insufficient: the last owner can disappear before the later lock call. Finally, none of these choices serializes access to the session's fields. Shared mutable state still needs protection. I also check for cycles: a session that stores a callback owning that same session can keep itself alive forever. A weak capture can break that ownership cycle.","follow":"What happens if the session calls <code>shared_from_this()<\/code> from its constructor?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-smart-pointers","title":"unique_ptr, shared_ptr and weak_ptr","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-smart-pointers.s3","q":"\"You are implementing a reference counted pointer. Walk me through weak-pointer promotion while another thread releases the last strong owner.\"","points":["The weak reference retains the control block but does not retain the managed object","Promotion uses compare-exchange to increment the strong count only while it is nonzero","If promotion wins from one to two, the releasing owner leaves one owner and cannot destroy the object","If release wins from one to zero, promotion fails and must never resurrect the object","Ordinary copies may increment relaxed; releases need release ordering and final destruction needs acquire ordering","Free the control block only after weak ownership ends, and synchronize access to each mutable handle separately"],"model":"Promotion must compete atomically with the final release, and it must never bring a zero strong count back to life. The weak reference keeps the control block allocated, so it can safely inspect the count even after the managed object is destroyed. I would load the strong count and use a compare-exchange loop to increment it only while it is nonzero. Suppose the count is one. If promotion changes one to two first, the original owner's release changes two to one. The promoted handle now keeps the object alive. If release changes one to zero first, destruction can begin. Promotion must fail, observe zero, and return empty. A separate positive check followed by an unconditional increment would leave a gap that could resurrect a destroyed object. For ordinary copies, an existing owner already keeps the object alive, so a relaxed atomic increment is enough. Releases need release ordering, and the final release needs acquire ordering before destruction; an acquire-release decrement is a simple implementation. The control block survives until its weak ownership is also gone. None of this protects concurrent reassignment of the same handle, or concurrent mutation of the pointee. Those remain separate synchronization responsibilities even when every count update is atomic.","follow":"Why can this implementation still leak two objects that own each other?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-smart-pointers","title":"unique_ptr, shared_ptr and weak_ptr","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-lambdas-callables.s1","q":"\"My pool protects its queue with a mutex, but a task sometimes crashes after <code>submit<\/code> returns. The queued lambda captures the task parameter by reference. What would you check?\"","points":["The reference capture borrows the parameter and does not extend its lifetime","Submit can destroy the parameter before the worker invokes the queued closure","The queue mutex protects queue operations, not the lifetime of borrowed task state","Move the callable into the queue, or capture it by move in a wrapper","Audit pointers, views and references inside the callable for further borrowed lifetimes","Run tasks outside the queue lock and join workers before destroying executor state"],"model":"I would first check lifetime. Capturing the task parameter by reference does not keep it alive. The queue can hold a perfectly valid closure whose reference points to a destroyed parameter. For example, submit pushes the wrapper, returns, and destroys its local task. A worker later pops the wrapper and calls through that dangling reference. The result is undefined behavior, even though every queue operation used the mutex correctly. I would move the task directly into the queue. If a wrapper is necessary, I would use an init capture that moves the task into the closure. That gives the queued object ownership of the callable until execution or cancellation destroys it. I would then inspect the callable's own captures. Owning a closure does not make a borrowed pointer or string view inside it safe. Those need an independently valid lifetime or an owning replacement. Finally, workers should remove tasks under the queue lock and invoke them after unlocking, and shutdown must join workers before destroying the state they use. The queue lock solves access ordering; the captures must also solve lifetime.","follow":"What if the moved task owns a <code>unique_ptr<\/code>, but the queue stores <code>std::function&lt;void()&gt;<\/code>?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-lambdas-callables","title":"Lambdas, captures and callables","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-lambdas-callables.s2","q":"\"A member function queues a callback with <code>[=]<\/code>. Does that give the callback a safe snapshot of the object? What would you choose instead?\"","points":["When the body accesses members, implicit this capture copies the pointer, not the object","Destruction can leave the callback dangling; concurrent member mutation can race even while the object lives","Capture selected owning values for an independent snapshot, taking synchronization while copying if required","Capturing star-this copies the object but leaves pointer-like members sharing their targets","Capture a shared owner for required work or a weak observer for work that may be skipped","Keeping the object alive still leaves its mutable state needing synchronization"],"model":"No. When the callback uses a member, the default value capture captures the this pointer, not a snapshot of the whole object. If the object dies before execution, accessing the member is undefined behavior. If it survives but another thread changes that member without synchronization, there can still be a data race. For a task that only needs a name and a timeout, I would capture those values explicitly. If they are concurrently mutable, I would take their snapshot under the appropriate lock. Capturing star-this is another option for a copyable object, but it uses the object's copy semantics. Raw pointers, views and shared pointers inside the copy may still refer to shared state. If the callback needs the original object, I would capture a shared owner when completion must keep it alive, or a weak observer when it should skip work after destruction. The weak callback must lock and check the resulting owner before use. Either way, preserving lifetime does not protect mutable fields. I would state both the ownership rule and the synchronization rule before calling the callback safe.","follow":"What ownership cycle could a callback capturing a shared owner create if it is stored inside that same object?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-lambdas-callables","title":"Lambdas, captures and callables","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-lambdas-callables.s3","q":"\"In C++20, design a pool submission that accepts a lambda owning a <code>unique_ptr<\/code>, returns a future, and queues work in <code>std::function&lt;void()&gt;<\/code>. Why is a direct conversion rejected?\"","points":["A unique-pointer capture makes the closure move-only; <code>std::function<\/code> requires a copyable target","Move the callable into a packaged task whose future carries its result or exception","Own the packaged task through a shared pointer and queue a lambda capturing that pointer by value","Copyable wrappers share one task, so the executor must invoke each accepted task exactly once","Execute outside the queue lock and define what happens to accepted work during shutdown","C++23 <code>std::move_only_function<\/code> can remove this copyability workaround if supported by the queue"],"model":"The direct conversion fails because the closure is move-only, while a C++20 <code>std::function<\/code> requires a copyable target. Moving the lambda into the wrapper does not remove that requirement: the wrapper must support being copied later. I would move the user's callable into a packaged task and obtain its future. Then I would put that packaged task in a shared pointer and queue a small lambda that captures the pointer by value and invokes the task. That wrapper is copyable because copying it copies an owning handle, not the unique pointer inside the original callable. The packaged task carries the return value or a thrown exception to the future. There is still only one logical task, so the pool must invoke each accepted submission exactly once, even if it copied the wrapper internally. A worker pops under the queue mutex and calls the wrapper after unlocking. I would also define shutdown: either drain accepted tasks or abandon them with a documented result, rather than leave futures waiting indefinitely. In C++23, a queue of <code>std::move_only_function<\/code> can hold move-only wrappers directly and avoid the shared ownership workaround. For C++20, sharing the packaged task is a practical bridge between unique resource ownership and a copyable queue element.","follow":"What does the future observe if its packaged task is destroyed before it is invoked?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-lambdas-callables","title":"Lambdas, captures and callables","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-templates-auto.s1","q":"\"You made the integer blocking queue generic. Walk me through what changes, and what could still go wrong with a move-only element.\"","points":["Template the element type while keeping capacity and size as counts","Take by value and move into storage; lvalues copy and move-only arguments need ownership transfer","Move the front into an owning result before removing it, under the mutex","A throwing extraction or return move can damage or lose the item; require nonthrowing moves","Keep predicate waits and notifications, handle insertion failure, and define shutdown separately"],"model":"I would template the element type and keep capacity and size as counts. Enqueue takes the element by value and moves it into the deque. A copyable lvalue is copied into that parameter; a move-only object must be passed as a temporary or explicitly offered with a move. Dequeue waits for an item under the mutex, constructs an owning result from the front, and only then removes the front. The moved-from object still exists until removal, so moving is not the same as destroying. The difficult part is exception safety. A throwing move can modify the front before extraction succeeds, and a throwing return move after removal can lose the item. I would require nonthrowing move construction and destruction for this API, as our implementation does. Allocation can still fail during insertion; in a bounded queue I pass the wakeup to another producer because the slot remains free. The locking and predicate waits stay unchanged. Finally, I would define close separately, wake all waiters on close, and join users before destroying the queue.","follow":"How would you support a type whose move constructor can throw without losing queued work?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-templates-auto","title":"Templates, auto and type deduction","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-templates-auto.s2","q":"\"This locked wrapper returns <code>auto<\/code>. Does that prove callers cannot escape the lock with shared data?\"","points":["No: plain auto removes references but does not guarantee an owning result","A string returned by value is copied while the wrapper still holds its lock","Pointers, views and proxies copied by value still alias shared storage","decltype(auto) preserves a returned reference; parenthesized names can produce references","Require owning snapshots and trusted callbacks, with no retained aliases or reentry"],"model":"No. Plain auto prevents one particular reference leak, but it does not prove ownership. Suppose the callback returns a reference to a string. The wrapper's auto return type becomes string, so the caller receives a copy constructed before the lock guard is destroyed. That is useful. But if the callback returns a pointer, a string view, or a proxy object, copying that result still leaves an alias to protected storage. The callback could also save an address somewhere else. Changing the wrapper to decltype auto makes matters sharper: it preserves the reference returned by the callback. Parentheses matter too. For an integer variable, decltype of its name gives int, while decltype of the parenthesized expression gives int reference. I would make the API promise an owning snapshot, or expose specific operations that complete under the lock. If we retain callbacks, they must be trusted not to save aliases, reenter the same mutex or block indefinitely. The synchronization boundary is the entire access to shared state, not just the lookup that found it.","follow":"Would writing plain <code>auto<\/code> at the caller fix a getter that returns a reference after unlocking?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-templates-auto","title":"Templates, auto and type deduction","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-templates-auto.s3","q":"\"Why won't <code>std::thread t(fill, data)<\/code> compile when <code>fill<\/code> takes a vector reference, and which fix would you choose?\"","points":["Thread construction stores decayed argument values in the calling thread","An lvalue vector is copied; the worker invokes fill with the stored argument as an rvalue","A non-const lvalue reference cannot bind to that rvalue","Use std::ref or reference capture for sharing, or move ownership into the worker","Sharing requires sufficient lifetime and synchronization; join orders later caller reads"],"model":"The thread constructor does not automatically preserve the vector reference. It stores argument values with decayed types, copying this lvalue vector in the calling thread. The new thread then invokes the function using the stored argument as an rvalue. Fill expects a non-const lvalue reference, which cannot bind to that rvalue, so the program fails to compile. If the worker is supposed to fill the caller's vector, I would use std ref or a lambda that captures the vector by reference. The wrapper or lambda is stored by value, but it deliberately refers back to the original vector. That makes lifetime my responsibility: the vector must remain alive until the worker finishes. I also need a mutex if another thread accesses the same vector concurrently. In this example I can simply avoid touching it until join returns, which also makes the worker's writes visible. If sharing is unnecessary, I would move ownership into the worker instead and accept a vector by value. Adding move to the original reference-taking call alone would not fix its signature.","follow":"What changes if the argument is a local character array and the worker expects a string?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-templates-auto","title":"Templates, auto and type deduction","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-stl-containers.s1","q":"\"Two workers update different keys in an unordered map. Is that enough to make it safe?\"","points":["Different keys alone do not authorize concurrent insertion or other structural changes","Insertions share container metadata and can rehash; lookups need the same synchronization policy","Distinct existing mapped values can be updated under a stable-structure ownership contract","Use one mutex first, copy results under it, and distinguish lifetime from value synchronization","Scale with independent map shards; read locks require genuinely nonmutating lookups"],"model":"No. Different keys are not enough if either operation can insert or otherwise change the map's structure. Inserts share metadata, can touch the same bucket, and can trigger a rehash. I would first protect all container operations with one mutex, including lookups and size. There is a narrower guarantee for different existing elements. If we populate the map before starting workers and freeze its structure, each worker can update its own mapped value through a suitable reference or at, provided the values do not share other mutable state. That is a different contract from concurrent insertion. For the general registry API, I would copy an owning result while holding the mutex. Rehash preserves element references, but erase can destroy the object and an update can race with a reader, so address stability alone is insufficient. If measurement shows contention, I would shard into independent maps, each with its own mutex. For a read-heavy workload, shared locks are another option, but lookup must use find or at. Map subscript can insert a missing key and is not a read-only lookup API.","follow":"Why does reserving all the buckets in advance still not make concurrent insertion safe?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-stl-containers","title":"STL containers, complexity and iterator invalidation","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-stl-containers.s2","q":"\"We take a reference while holding a mutex, unlock, then sometimes crash when using it. How would you investigate that?\"","points":["The lock protected obtaining the reference, not its later use","Vector reallocation and erasing a referenced node can end the referenced object's lifetime","Deque end insertion and unordered-map rehash distinguish invalid iterators from valid references","Stable lifetime still does not prevent a race on the referenced value","Copy under the lock or extend protection; use sanitizers as evidence rather than proof"],"model":"I would first check whether the reference is used after the lock is released. The lookup may be protected while the actual access is not. With a vector, another thread can append beyond capacity, moving elements to new storage and destroying the old objects. The reference then dangles. With a node-based map, rehash is different: it invalidates iterators but preserves references to elements. Erasing the referenced element still destroys it. A deque gives another useful distinction: insertion at either end preserves references but invalidates all iterators. I would identify the exact container and operation before calling this an invalidation bug. Even if the object stays alive, a concurrent update to the same value can still be a data race. My default fix is to copy the required value while holding the lock, or keep protection through the entire operation. Shared ownership can extend lifetime, but the object's state still needs synchronization. I would exercise the failure under AddressSanitizer for lifetime errors and ThreadSanitizer for races in separate builds. Clean runs would not replace that lifetime and synchronization argument.","follow":"Why is <code>auto snapshot = getterReturningReference()<\/code> still unsafe if the getter unlocks before returning?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-stl-containers","title":"STL containers, complexity and iterator invalidation","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-stl-containers.s3","q":"\"Choose the containers for a thread-safe LRU cache, and explain the two failure paths you would review most carefully.\"","points":["List for recency and unordered map from key to list iterator give average constant-time operations","Single-node splice preserves the list iterators stored in the map","Get mutates recency; one exclusive mutex protects both containers and returned copies","Map insertion failure after list insertion needs rollback to preserve their invariant","Eviction invalidates the victim's handles; shared reads or escaped references are unsafe"],"model":"I would use a list for recency and an unordered map from key to list iterator. That gives average constant-time lookup and single-node promotion, excluding value copies and lock waiting. Splice moves a node to the front without invalidating its iterator, so the index remains valid. Rehashing the index invalidates the map's own iterators, but not the list iterators stored as values. One exclusive mutex protects both containers, and get takes it too because a cache hit changes recency. I return an owning value copied under that lock. The first failure path is allocation: we may successfully create the list node and then fail to insert its map entry. I roll back the new list node before propagating the exception, so the two containers still agree. The second path is eviction: remove the victim from the index and list under the same lock, and never let callers retain references to that node. A reader lock on get would be wrong because two hits could splice concurrently. I would test zero capacity, repeated keys, eviction order and allocation failure.","follow":"If this mutex becomes a bottleneck, what does sharding change about global LRU eviction?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-stl-containers","title":"STL containers, complexity and iterator invalidation","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-vocabulary-types.s1","q":"\"Design the return types for a queue that supports polling, timeouts and shutdown. Why would you use optional, and where is it not enough?\"","points":["Use one locked check-and-take operation; optional alone provides no synchronization","Explain the two-consumer race between empty and front","Define empty results separately for polling, timeout and closed-and-drained blocking pop","Use an explicit status or variant when timeout and closure must be distinguished","Require nonthrowing moves and destruction for the illustrated value-returning pop","Close under the mutex, wake all consumers, and join users before destruction"],"model":"I would return an optional from a single check-and-take operation, with the whole decision protected by the queue's mutex. The optional describes the result; it does not supply synchronization. If two consumers separately check that a queue is nonempty, one can remove the last item before the other calls front. Returning a reference also lets guarded storage escape the lock.\nFor polling, an empty optional means no item was available at that operation. For blocking pop, I would keep draining accepted items after close and return empty only when closed and drained. A timed operation needs a documented meaning for absence. If the caller must distinguish timeout from closure, optional has too few states, so I would return a named status result or a variant with distinct alternatives.\nI would also constrain this implementation's element type to nonthrowing moves and destruction. Otherwise, extraction or return can fail after changing or removing the queued item. Finally, close changes the predicate under the same mutex and wakes every waiting consumer. The owner joins all users before destroying the queue. That gives callers an explicit result and a lifecycle they can reason about, while unexpected allocation or system failures can still propagate separately.","follow":"What would you change if the payload has a move constructor that can throw?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-vocabulary-types","title":"optional, pair, tuple, string_view and friends","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-vocabulary-types.s2","q":"\"Our asynchronous logger sometimes prints corrupted request names. It queues string_view values. What would you check, and how would you fix it?\"","points":["A string_view borrows characters and does not extend their lifetime","A temporary concatenated string dies at the end of the caller's full expression","Queue synchronization does not preserve the backing storage","Copy into string before returning, or retain an immutable owner with a lifetime contract","Check storage invalidation and conflicting writes independently of lifetime","Views are safe for joined readers over stable immutable data; data is not necessarily null-terminated"],"model":"I would first check whether the logger owns the characters it queues. A string view only borrows a pointer and length. If a caller passes a concatenated temporary string, that string dies at the end of the statement. A background writer reading later has a dangling view, even when the queue itself is perfectly synchronized.\nMy default fix is to keep the view parameter for convenient synchronous access, then copy it into an owning string before log returns. For larger immutable buffers, retaining a shared owner can avoid copying, but the message must carry that ownership contract through consumption.\nI would check mutation separately. Keeping an object alive does not prevent reallocation from invalidating the view, or another thread writing characters while the worker reads them. Views can safely cross threads when storage stays stable and accesses are ordered, as with read-only spans whose owner joins all readers before destruction. Finally, a string view need not end with a null character, so the output path must respect its length rather than treat data as an arbitrary C string.","follow":"Would copying a tuple containing string_view fields under a mutex give the worker an independent snapshot?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-vocabulary-types","title":"optional, pair, tuple, string_view and friends","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-vocabulary-types.s3","q":"\"A worker accepts append, flush and stop commands. Would you use any or variant, and what could go wrong during dispatch?\"","points":["Choose variant for the closed set of command types, with owning payloads","Visit requires a valid handler for every alternative and consistent deduced return types","Type-based access requires a unique type; get throws on the wrong active alternative","Distinguish an intentional monostate from valueless_by_exception after a throwing type change","Variant supplies neither queue synchronization nor a shutdown policy"],"model":"I would use a variant because the supported command types form a known, closed set. Append would own its string, while flush and stop could be small marker types. Any would defer type checking to casts and would not express which commands the worker accepts.\nI would dispatch with visit and make every alternative compile. With the deduced-return overload, every branch must return the same type and value category. If I inspect manually, get throws for the wrong active alternative, whereas get-if returns null. Type-based access also requires that type to appear exactly once.\nA variant normally holds one alternative, but a throwing type change can leave it valueless, making visit throw. An intentional monostate is a separate, ordinary alternative. Finally, variant only represents the message: I still need a synchronized queue and a contract for whether stop drains or discards pending work.","follow":"How would you ensure adding a fourth command fails compilation until its behavior is implemented?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-vocabulary-types","title":"optional, pair, tuple, string_view and friends","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-exceptions.s1","q":"\"You used a lock guard around a balance transfer. Are you now exception safe if appending to the journal throws?\"","points":["RAII releases the mutex during unwinding but does not restore balances","Changing balances before a failed journal append breaks the balance-journal invariant","Define basic safety versus the strong guarantee for the operation's state","Validate IDs, amount and overflow, append safely, then commit with nonthrowing updates","All observers use the same mutex; external side effects need their own protocol"],"model":"The lock guard gives me cleanup, but it does not give the transfer an exception guarantee by itself. If I change both balances and then journal allocation fails, the guard unlocks during unwinding. The next reader taking that mutex can see money moved with no journal record. That breaks the invariant even though there is no leaked lock.\nI would state the guarantee explicitly. Basic safety means no leaks and valid invariants after failure. Strong safety means the operation leaves its documented state unchanged on failure. For this in-memory transfer, I would validate the account IDs, positive amount, available funds and destination overflow first. Then I would append the trivial journal entry, relying on the vector insertion's strong guarantee. Once that succeeds, the checked integer updates cannot throw, so they form the commit.\nEvery reader must take the same mutex to avoid observing the intermediate state. This reasoning covers the in-memory state. If journaling means writing to an external service, I need a transaction or recovery protocol; a lock guard cannot undo an external side effect.","follow":"What happens to an object's resources if its constructor throws before the object is fully constructed?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-exceptions","title":"Exceptions, noexcept and exception safety","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-exceptions.s2","q":"\"A task in your pool throws. How does the submitter learn about it, and what changes if the first failure should stop the whole batch?\"","points":["An exception escaping a thread function terminates; join does not transport it","Capture each task failure with packaged_task or a promise and exception_ptr","Future get rethrows the failure while the worker can continue; wait only observes readiness","For batch failure, serialize work admission and first-error recording under one mutex","Already admitted work may run; cancellation is cooperative and first means first recorded","Join all workers before reporting failure and use RAII ownership for partial startup"],"model":"I would capture the exception inside the worker and deliver it through that task's future. An exception escaping the thread function terminates the process; joining does not transport it back. A packaged task already captures failures from its callable. With a manual promise, I catch everything and set the current exception on the shared state. The submitter's get then rethrows it, while the worker can take another task. Waiting alone only observes readiness.\nStopping a batch is a different policy. I would protect job admission and the first stored exception with one mutex. Under that mutex, a worker either claims the next job or observes failure and exits. The first handler to record an error closes admission. A separate atomic flag checked before claiming work leaves a gap where another job can be admitted after failure.\nI would explicitly allow already admitted jobs to finish, even if their function bodies have not started yet. Stopping those jobs requires cooperative cancellation, not killing their threads. I then join every worker before rethrowing the recorded failure to the caller. RAII thread ownership also handles an exception while creating later workers. Finally, the reporting path itself must not silently swallow errors or leave accepted futures pending indefinitely.","follow":"If two tasks throw almost simultaneously, which exception is the first one your API promises to report?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-exceptions","title":"Exceptions, noexcept and exception safety","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-exceptions.s3","q":"\"Why is vector copying my tasks during growth, and should I fix it by putting noexcept on every move constructor?\"","points":["Copying preserves old elements when moving could throw and copying is available","Noexcept promises no exception escapes; violation terminates instead of propagating","Mark or infer noexcept only when member operations support that promise","A move-only type with a throwing move still works but can lose the strong guarantee","Capacity growth and relocation counts are implementation dependent","Destructors must not let a new exception escape during unwinding"],"model":"I would inspect the move constructor's exception specification before changing anything. During growth, vector relocates existing elements. If moving might throw and copying is available, copying preserves the original elements until relocation succeeds. A move that changes several source objects before failing would make rollback much harder.\nNoexcept promises that no exception escapes the function. It is not a request to suppress errors: violating it calls terminate instead of propagating to the caller. I would mark a move noexcept only after checking every operation it performs, or default the move so its members determine the specification. Generic wrappers can use a conditional noexcept based on the contained type.\nIf the type is move-only, vector can still use a potentially throwing move. The tradeoff is weaker exception guarantees if that move fails. Exact relocation counts depend on the implementation's capacity growth policy. I would also check cleanup: a destructor invoked during unwinding must not let another exception escape. A fallible close operation should report errors explicitly, while destruction provides reliable cleanup rather than a second failure.","follow":"Can a noexcept function legally call code that throws, if it catches the exception internally?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-exceptions","title":"Exceptions, noexcept and exception safety","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-chrono.s1","q":"\"Our queue has a 100 millisecond timeout, but it sometimes waits much longer. Walk me through what you would check.\"","points":["Compute one steady-clock deadline before locking so contention consumes the same budget","A loop that restarts wait_for can extend the budget after every wakeup","Use a predicate wait and interpret its result as the final protected state","Mutex acquisition, reacquisition and scheduling can delay return beyond the deadline","Define whether success after the deadline is acceptable; check time explicitly for a strict cutoff"],"model":"I would check whether we restart the budget after each wakeup. I would compute one steady-clock deadline before acquiring the mutex and pass that deadline to the predicate form of <code>wait_until<\/code>. That prevents notifications and spurious wakeups from buying another hundred milliseconds. For example, if unrelated notifications arrive every ninety milliseconds, a loop that repeatedly calls <code>wait_for<\/code> with the full duration may never time out. The predicate overload also checks the queue again after reacquiring the mutex, including when the timeout expires. Its result describes that final state, so true does not prove the operation finished before the deadline. Then I would measure lock contention and scheduling delay. An ordinary mutex acquisition is not timed, and a condition-variable wait must regain the mutex before returning. A fixed deadline cannot guarantee that the function returns within a hundred milliseconds. If the contract forbids accepting an item after expiry, I would check the clock under the lock before removing it. I would state that acceptance rule separately from the return-time limitation.","follow":"How would you share the same timeout budget across a lock acquisition, queue wait and retry?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-chrono","title":"std::chrono: durations, clocks and deadlines","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-chrono.s2","q":"\"Why would you choose steady_clock instead of high_resolution_clock, and when would system_clock be the right choice?\"","points":["Choose steady_clock for elapsed-time budgets because it is guaranteed steady","High_resolution_clock promises a short tick period, not steadiness or precise wakeups","System_clock expresses wall time and can change with clock corrections","Use wall time for calendar events and shared timestamps; do not serialize steady epochs","Suspension behaviour and clock resolution depend on the platform"],"model":"I choose <code>steady_clock<\/code> for an elapsed-time budget because its readings do not go backward and its tick rate is steady. A name like <code>high_resolution_clock<\/code> does not give that guarantee. It may alias the wall clock, and a fine tick period says nothing about how promptly the operating system schedules my thread. I use <code>system_clock<\/code> for a calendar appointment or a timestamp another machine must interpret. That clock can be corrected, so a deadline on it can change its elapsed meaning. For a local request, I store a steady deadline and propagate the remaining budget. I would never send its raw epoch value to another machine as universal time. If suspension matters, I also check whether the platform's steady clock counts suspended time. Those are separate choices: clock meaning, numeric resolution, and wakeup latency.","follow":"A client sends an expiry timestamp from another machine. How would clock skew affect your local deadline?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-chrono","title":"std::chrono: durations, clocks and deadlines","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-chrono.s3","q":"\"Your scheduler is waiting for a task ten seconds away. I submit one due in a second. What happens, and how would you test it?\"","points":["Store tasks in a mutex-protected min-heap of steady deadlines","Insertion notifies the worker; the wait predicate detects an earlier head or shutdown","Recompute the earliest deadline and execute due tasks outside the mutex","Fake time tests also need a controlled wait and wake mechanism","A slow running task delays other tasks; cancellation and failure policies are separate"],"model":"The new task must wake the worker and replace its current waiting deadline. I keep a min-heap of steady-clock due times under a mutex. Submission inserts the task, releases the lock and notifies the condition variable. The worker's timed-wait predicate checks for shutdown or a head earlier than the deadline it captured. If that predicate becomes true, it loops and recomputes the deadline. Later tasks and spurious wakeups do not restart the existing budget. Once a task is due, I remove it under the mutex and invoke it outside the lock. For a deterministic test, I control both the clock and the waiting mechanism, then advance fake time and wake the worker. Injecting the clock alone is insufficient. This single worker can still run late behind a slow callback, so I would separately define task failure, cancellation and shutdown policies.","follow":"If a task's future times out, has the task stopped, and could destroying that future still block?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-chrono","title":"std::chrono: durations, clocks and deadlines","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-stl-for-coding-rounds.s1","q":"\"You have a batch of scores and need the largest ten. Now writers keep updating the scores. Walk me through your choice and what the answer means.\"","points":["Choose <code>nth_element<\/code> plus prefix sorting for a stored batch, or <code>partial_sort<\/code>","State average O(n + k log k), with no worst-case linear selection claim","Handle empty input, zero k, k greater than n, and ties","Copy scores consistently under the protecting lock and compute outside it","Describe the result as a snapshot, not as continuously current","Choose a bounded min heap when the input becomes a stream"],"model":"I would take an owning snapshot of the scores, select the ten largest values, and sort just those winners. For a stored batch, <code>nth_element<\/code> followed by sorting the prefix takes average linear selection time plus k log k for the output order. I would not claim worst-case linear selection. <code>partial_sort<\/code> is another good choice when I directly want a sorted prefix. I clamp k to the number of scores and return empty for zero or empty input. If equal scores need deterministic winners, I compare an identifier after the score. With concurrent writers, I copy the scores while holding the lock that protects them, then release it before selection. Otherwise sorting or selecting would mutate shared data while writers use it. The result describes the copied snapshot; it does not include updates made later. If the input becomes a stream, I switch to a bounded min heap. Its top is the weakest retained score, so after inserting a new score I remove the minimum whenever the heap exceeds k.","follow":"What changes if callers require an exact current top ten while updates continue?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-stl-for-coding-rounds","title":"The STL for coding rounds: algorithms, heaps, hashing and traps","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-stl-for-coding-rounds.s2","q":"\"My deadline queue runs the latest task first, and equal deadlines come out unpredictably. How would you debug and fix it before adding worker threads?\"","points":["Identify the default max heap or a less-than comparator as the reversed-priority cause","Return true for a later deadline so the earliest is on top","Break ties with a monotonic sequence number and preserve strict weak ordering","Keep priority fields stable while entries remain in the heap","With threads, assign sequence and insert under one lock; check, copy top and pop together","Run outside the lock, recheck deadlines after wakeups, and distinguish removal from execution order"],"model":"I would check the comparator: a default priority queue is a max heap. To expose the earliest deadline, I return true when the first deadline is later than the second. For equal deadlines I compare sequence numbers in the same direction, so the smaller sequence is removed first. I use strict comparisons; a heap does not otherwise promise FIFO ties. I test equal deadlines and a later deadline. Stored ordering fields must stay unchanged; updates need reinsertion or stale entries with validation. With concurrent producers I assign the sequence and insert under the same mutex. Workers check emptiness, copy the top and remove it within one critical section, then execute outside the lock. That guarantees removal order, not execution order across workers. A waiting worker rechecks the head after waking, because a producer may have inserted an earlier deadline.","follow":"How would you support cancelling an arbitrary task without scanning the heap?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-stl-for-coding-rounds","title":"The STL for coding rounds: algorithms, heaps, hashing and traps","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-stl-for-coding-rounds.s3","q":"\"A producer queues string views, and the consumer sometimes logs the next request's text. The queue is locked. What do you check?\"","points":["Identify borrowing from a reused input buffer as the likely cause","Explain that copying a view copies address and length, not ownership","Distinguish overwritten bytes, dangling storage after reallocation, and a possible data race","Explain why the queue lock alone does not protect later backing-buffer reads","Queue owning strings or retain immutable backing storage through consumption"],"model":"I would check the lifetime and reuse of the backing input buffer first. A string view borrows characters; copying it into the queue copies an address and length, not the text. If the producer reads the next request into the same string, the consumer may see that new text. If the string reallocates, the old view can dangle. If the producer writes while the consumer reads those bytes, there is also a data race. The queue mutex protects insertion and removal, but it does not automatically protect later accesses to the buffer. My simplest fix is to queue an owning string so each request retains its text. If copying is too expensive, I would retain immutable backing storage until all consumers finish. I would test reuse, growth and delayed consumption, because a small happy-path input can hide this lifetime bug.","follow":"Would capturing the string_view by value in a lambda fix its lifetime?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-stl-for-coding-rounds","title":"The STL for coding rounds: algorithms, heaps, hashing and traps","track":"cpp","module":2},{"id":"cpp-build-debug-sanitizers.s1","q":"\"This C++ service hangs once every fifty runs. You have a debugger and twenty minutes. What do you do?\"","points":["Keep the hung process and use a debug-symbol build to collect every thread stack","Identify waiting resources and confirm a lock ownership cycle before diagnosing deadlock","For a condition wait inspect the protected predicate, notifier and shutdown paths","Create a controlled reproducer without relying on sleeps for ordering","Fix the invariant or progress protocol, then rerun targeted and stress tests"],"model":"I would preserve the hung process and collect every thread's stack before changing the code. With debug symbols available, I attach LLDB and run <code>bt all<\/code>. I start from my application frames and identify what each thread needs in order to progress. If two threads are blocked acquiring mutexes, I map the addresses to objects and check who owns each lock. Two blocked threads alone do not prove deadlock; a cycle of ownership dependencies does. If a consumer is parked on a condition variable, I inspect its protected predicate, the producer's state transitions, notification paths and shutdown state. A finished producer and a true predicate can expose a missed-notification bug. Next I make a small reproducer using explicit synchronization to control the relevant ordering, instead of assuming a sleep forces it. I fix the lock order or waiting protocol, then rerun that reproducer and a stress test under ThreadSanitizer. I also check exact results and completion, because a clean race report says nothing about whether the service makes progress.","follow":"What would you check if every sampled thread is running rather than blocked?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-build-debug-sanitizers","title":"Building, debugging and sanitizers","track":"cpp","module":3},{"id":"cpp-build-debug-sanitizers.s2","q":"\"The counter prints the right answer, but ThreadSanitizer reports a race. Which evidence do you trust, and how do you fix it?\"","points":["Correct output does not make a C++ data race defined","Read both conflicting accesses, the address and thread creation stacks","Determine which synchronization edge is missing; joining both does not order the workers","Use atomic read-modify-write for a counter or a shared mutex for a compound invariant","A clean rerun adds evidence but cannot prove correctness or race freedom"],"model":"I take the race report seriously. Correct output does not make a C++ data race safe; the program has undefined behaviour even when this execution appears successful. I read both access stacks, check that they name the same storage, and identify the missing happens-before edge. Joining both workers lets main safely observe their completion, but does not order their earlier writes against each other. For a single hit counter I would use an atomic read-modify-write, such as <code>fetch_add<\/code>. If the update must preserve an invariant across fields, I would put that whole operation under one shared mutex. Then I rerun the reproducer and check the expected count as well as the reports. A clean run adds evidence, but missing instrumentation, suppressions and untested paths can hide bugs. Separately locked check-then-act operations can also be logically wrong without a data race.","follow":"Would an atomic load followed by an atomic store implement the same counter increment correctly?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-build-debug-sanitizers","title":"Building, debugging and sanitizers","track":"cpp","module":3},{"id":"cpp-build-debug-sanitizers.s3","q":"\"How would you set up sanitizer builds for a C++ service, and investigate memory freed by main but read by a worker?\"","points":["Build separate TSan plus UBSan and ASan plus UBSan binaries","Instrument each controlled translation unit and pass sanitizer flags at link time","Keep debug symbols and useful stack unwinding, with separate object directories","Use the ASan read, free and allocation stacks to reconstruct lifetime","Fix ownership or join before destruction, then test without relying on a timing delay"],"model":"I would keep two test builds: ThreadSanitizer with UBSan, and AddressSanitizer with UBSan. ThreadSanitizer and AddressSanitizer cannot share one binary. I compile each translation unit I control with the chosen instrumentation, debug symbols and frame pointers, then link through the C++ compiler driver with the same sanitizer flags. Separate object directories prevent stale objects from silently losing coverage. For a use-after-free report, I read the access stack, the freeing stack and the original allocation stack. If a worker captured a string view while main owned the string, capturing that view by value copied only the pointer and length. It did not extend the string's lifetime. I would move or copy the owning string into the task, or join before destroying it. Finally I rerun a controlled lifetime test under ASan. Adding a sleep changes timing and cannot repair ownership.","follow":"What can ASan miss inside a live allocation, and which tool would you use for uninitialized values on a supported platform?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-build-debug-sanitizers","title":"Building, debugging and sanitizers","track":"cpp","module":3},{"id":"cpp-under-the-hood-pthreads.s1","q":"\"Walk me through what happens underneath <code>std::thread<\/code> on Linux. Which parts would you rely on in portable C++?\"","points":["Usual Linux C++ libraries use pthread_create; this is an implementation choice","The runtime obtains a stack, prepares thread-local storage and creates a kernel task","Linux uses clone or clone3 with sharing flags; each thread is independently scheduled","Creation, stacks and scheduling have costs; defaults and timings are platform-dependent","Portable reasoning uses lifecycle and synchronization guarantees, including successful join","Native handles are platform-specific and must not bypass the C++ object's lifecycle ownership"],"model":"On a typical Linux system, a C++ thread is backed by a pthread and an independently scheduled kernel task. That is an implementation choice, not a requirement that portable C++ exposes. The library stores the callable and its arguments, then uses pthread creation. Glibc obtains a stack, prepares thread-local storage and creates the task through clone or clone3, with flags that share the process's address space and other resources. Each thread still has its own execution state and stack. Those resources, creation work and scheduling costs explain why a pool can be useful for many small tasks. Stack defaults and timings depend on the system, so I would measure rather than promise a fixed cost. In portable code, I rely on the documented lifetime and synchronization contracts: keep captured objects alive and join before consuming a worker's result. A native handle is only for platform-specific operations. I would not join it through pthreads, because the C++ object would still think it owns a joinable thread. On Windows the implementation changes, while those C++ guarantees remain.","follow":"How would you change a worker's stack size, and can <code>std::thread<\/code> do that portably?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-under-the-hood-pthreads","title":"Under the hood: how std::thread maps to the OS","track":"cpp","module":3},{"id":"cpp-under-the-hood-pthreads.s2","q":"\"My pthread consumer occasionally hangs during shutdown. Walk me through the predicate and wakeups you would check.\"","points":["Check queue contents and producer-completion state under the same mutex","Wait while empty and producers remain, using a while loop","Condition wait atomically releases the mutex and starts waiting, then reacquires it","The last producer broadcasts so all consumers can observe completion","Drain existing items, exit only when empty and finished, then join before destruction","Signals are not stored permits and wakeups do not imply the predicate is true"],"model":"I would first check whether shutdown is part of the state that the consumer waits on. For this buffer, queue contents and the number of remaining producers belong under the same mutex. A consumer waits only while the buffer is empty and at least one producer remains. The wait goes in a while loop because a wakeup does not reserve an item, and it can be spurious. The condition wait atomically releases the mutex and starts waiting, then reacquires the mutex before the successful return. That closes the gap in which a producer could otherwise change the state and send a missed signal. When the final producer finishes, it decrements the producer count under the mutex and broadcasts to all consumers. Signalling only one can leave the others asleep forever. Each consumer drains any remaining item and exits only when the buffer is both empty and finished. Then the owner joins every thread before destroying the synchronization objects. I would test both an empty shutdown and a shutdown with queued items. The shared state remembers completion; notifications alone do not.","follow":"What would change if you also wanted to cancel producers blocked on a full buffer?","seconds":75,"lesson":"cpp-under-the-hood-pthreads","title":"Under the hood: how std::thread maps to the OS","track":"cpp","module":3},{"id":"cpp-inheritance-and-virtual-functions.s1","q":"\"Walk me through a virtual call in a base constructor and why a worker started there is dangerous.\"","points":["The synchronous call dispatches in the current constructor class","Derived state is not ready","A worker can overlap construction","Start after full initialization and join before task destruction begins"],"model":"The synchronous call uses the override in the base constructor's class. The derived part is not ready, so it cannot be treated as a ready implementation. Starting a worker there introduces a second problem: <code>this<\/code> escapes while construction is still happening. The worker can race with initialization or touch a member whose lifetime has not begun. I would build the complete object first, then start it through a controlled lifecycle operation. For virtual calls from a worker, I would use an external owner that stops and joins the worker before the task's destructor begins. That keeps dispatch and member access clear of teardown. A sleep is not an ordering guarantee.","follow":"Why is joining only in a base destructor too late?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-inheritance-and-virtual-functions","title":"Inheritance and virtual functions","track":"cpp","module":4},{"id":"cpp-inheritance-and-virtual-functions.s2","q":"\"Why does my vector of base objects keep calling the base implementation?\"","points":["Insertion copies only the base subobject","The resulting dynamic type is <code>Base<\/code>","References preserve behavior without ownership","Default-deleting unique owning base pointers need virtual destruction"],"model":"The vector stores actual base values. Inserting a derived object copies its base portion and discards the derived portion, which is slicing. Calling a virtual function then correctly dispatches on a base object. I would use base references for temporary borrowing, or a vector of unique owning base pointers for a heterogeneous collection. The base needs a virtual destructor because those pointers delete through the base type. If the design also needs copies, I would make polymorphic cloning explicit instead of trying to get it from the base copy constructor.","follow":"Would using a shared pointer automatically repair a poorly designed base destruction contract?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-inheritance-and-virtual-functions","title":"Inheritance and virtual functions","track":"cpp","module":4},{"id":"cpp-polymorphism-and-interfaces.s1","q":"\"Would you inject a dependency with a template, <code>std::function<\/code>, or a virtual interface?\"","points":["Identify when the implementation is selected","Templates preserve concrete types and support compile time constraints","Callbacks fit one operation","Interfaces fit related operations and require ownership contracts"],"model":"I start with when the choice is made and how many operations belong together. A template is useful when the concrete type is known at compilation and I want generic code without a runtime base class. A concept makes its expression requirements explicit. A callback is a compact choice for one operation, such as a clock reading. A virtual interface fits a service with several related operations and implementations chosen at runtime. I would then state who owns the dependency and whether calls can overlap. None of these mechanisms automatically makes the implementation thread safe.","follow":"Can a concept prove that a callback never races?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-polymorphism-and-interfaces","title":"Polymorphism, abstract classes and operator overloading","track":"cpp","module":4},{"id":"cpp-polymorphism-and-interfaces.s2","q":"\"Explain why overloading brackets can make a thread safe container unsafe.\"","points":["A reference can escape the locked method","Unlock ends protection before the caller uses it","Const does not repair shared mutation elsewhere","Return a copy or expose a controlled operation"],"model":"A bracket operator often returns a reference. If my implementation locks, finds an element, unlocks, and returns that reference, the caller now touches the element without the container lock. Another thread could modify or erase it. Even a const reference can race with a writer and can become invalid after erasure. I would return a value snapshot when practical, or expose a narrow operation that keeps the required protection for the whole action. I would also avoid running arbitrary user code under an internal lock unless that is an explicit, carefully constrained contract.","follow":"What lifetime problem remains if the returned reference is const?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-polymorphism-and-interfaces","title":"Polymorphism, abstract classes and operator overloading","track":"cpp","module":4},{"id":"cpp-polymorphism-and-interfaces.s3","q":"\"Two workers call the same const <code>std::function<\/code>, and a race detector flags its captured counter. What do you check?\"","points":["A const wrapper can invoke a mutable target","Overlapping writes to the same plain counter need synchronization","Copying a value capture can separate state but changes the counter's meaning","Reference and shared pointer captures can keep state shared across copies","Ownership and shutdown must keep the target and dependencies alive"],"model":"I would inspect what the lambda captures and whether the workers invoke the same stored target. A const function wrapper can still invoke a mutable lambda. If both calls increment the same plain integer without synchronization, that is a data race. Copying a lambda that owns an integer gives separate counters, which may change the intended result. Copying one that holds a reference or shared pointer still reaches the same underlying counter. For a shared total, I would use an atomic counter or a mutex, depending on whether other state belongs to the invariant. I would also ensure shutdown waits for calls to finish before destroying the wrapper or its borrowed dependencies.","follow":"Would replacing a reference capture with a shared pointer fix the data race?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-polymorphism-and-interfaces","title":"Polymorphism, abstract classes and operator overloading","track":"cpp","module":4},{"id":"cpp-oop-design-for-lld.s1","q":"\"Walk me through how you would structure a thread safe reservation in C++.\"","points":["Clarify deadline and state semantics","Write the invariant and transitions","Put the lock with the complete state","Inject time with an explicit lifetime and test boundaries"],"model":"I start with the state transitions. A held reservation can become confirmed or expired, and both outcomes are terminal. One mutex protects the check and transition, so only one confirmation succeeds. I inject a clock by reference and require it to outlive the reservation and all callers. A clock read before the deadline permits confirmation, even if the call returns later; equality expires the hold. Expiry happens when a transition checks time, while the const getter only reports stored state. I test before, at, and after the deadline, then repeated and contending confirmations. A full inventory service also needs synchronization shared by every reservation competing for that stock.","follow":"How would you prove that two confirmations cannot both succeed?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-oop-design-for-lld","title":"Object oriented design for LLD and machine coding rounds","track":"cpp","module":4},{"id":"cpp-oop-design-for-lld.s2","q":"\"Why do you snapshot listener handles before calling them, and what does that choice cost?\"","points":["Snapshot shared listener handles while protecting membership","Run callbacks after releasing the lock","Allow reentry and avoid lock-held latency","Define snapshot delivery and concurrent callback behavior","Explain O(n) snapshot cost and captured-object lifetimes"],"model":"I snapshot shared handles under the registry mutex, then unlock before invoking listeners. Copying handles preserves listener identity and avoids copying user callables under that lock. A callback can register another listener, and slow delivery does not block registration. The snapshot costs linear time and space. With simple removal, already captured deliveries can still run. Concurrent publishers may invoke the same callable together, so its state needs its own synchronization; a const function wrapper does not make that state immutable. The handles keep callables alive, but objects captured by reference still need valid lifetimes. I count callback failures and continue to remaining listeners.","follow":"How would unsubscribe wait until every earlier delivery has finished?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-oop-design-for-lld","title":"Object oriented design for LLD and machine coding rounds","track":"cpp","module":4},{"id":"cpp-oop-design-for-lld.s3","q":"\"A class owns a worker and its destructor sometimes hangs. What would you check?\"","points":["Check that shutdown rejects new work and wakes blocked workers","Check that joining holds no lock the worker needs","Check cooperative stop handling and dependency destruction order","Require callers to finish before destruction begins"],"model":"I first check the shutdown protocol. It should reject new work, decide whether accepted work drains, and wake any worker waiting for input. Then I check whether the destructor joins while holding a lock the worker needs to finish. That creates a deadlock. A jthread requests stop and joins, but the task must cooperate and blocking waits need a wakeup path. The worker must finish before its mutex, queue, or borrowed dependencies are destroyed. I also require the owner to finish external calls before destroying the object. Concurrent close calls need a separate contract; a destructor is not a safe race against ongoing calls.","follow":"What happens if a callback tries to close and join its own worker?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-oop-design-for-lld","title":"Object oriented design for LLD and machine coding rounds","track":"cpp","module":4},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-threads.s1","q":"\"Explain thread arguments, <code>std::ref<\/code>, and <code>std::move<\/code> as if you were reviewing a new worker.\"","points":["Default argument storage is by value","Reference wrappers borrow with lifetime and synchronization obligations","Move transfers ownership when the type supports it","Join bounds borrowed lifetimes and result reads"],"model":"By default the thread constructor stores values for the callable and arguments. Passing an integer copies it; passing a pointer copies the address, so the pointee can still be shared. I use <code>std::ref<\/code> when the worker intentionally needs the original object, and then prove that object remains alive and its accesses are safe. I use <code>std::move<\/code> to let a resource such as a unique pointer transfer ownership into the worker. The move operation performs that transfer; the cast alone does not. Finally I join before destroying borrowed objects or reading ordinary result slots written by the worker.","follow":"Does capturing <code>this<\/code> by value copy the whole object?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-threads","title":"The toolkit: std::thread, std::jthread and thread_local","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-threads.s2","q":"\"Why can a finished thread still crash the process at scope exit?\"","points":["Joinable means associated, even after execution finishes","Thread destruction terminates if joinable","Join or detach discharges association","Prefer <code>std::jthread<\/code> with a real cooperative shutdown path"],"model":"The handle still owns a joining obligation after the function returns. A <code>std::thread<\/code> remains joinable until it is joined, detached, or moved from. Its destructor calls <code>std::terminate<\/code> if that association remains, even when the worker has already finished. I normally join, because that gives a clear completion and lifetime boundary. Detaching gives up that boundary and needs a separate lifetime design. In C++20 <code>std::jthread<\/code> helps with automatic ownership cleanup, but its worker still needs a way to finish. A destructor can block forever joining a worker that waits for an event nobody will produce.","follow":"What happens if you join while holding the worker's mutex?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-threads","title":"The toolkit: std::thread, std::jthread and thread_local","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-threads.s3","q":"\"We replaced every thread in a service with <code>std::jthread<\/code>, but shutdown still hangs. Walk me through your review.\"","points":["Stop is cooperative; explicit join does not request it","Identify the wait or operation preventing worker progress","Wake blocked workers and request stop on the whole group before joining","Hold no lock required by workers or stop callbacks during shutdown calls","Keep borrowed members alive until joining completes"],"model":"I first find what each worker is waiting for. A joining thread owns cleanup, but it cannot force arbitrary work to stop. If the worker waits on an ordinary condition variable, I need a shutdown predicate and notification, or a stop-aware wait. A blocking socket operation needs its own cancellation or deadline design. I request stop for every interdependent worker before joining any of them, because the first worker may need a peer to finish. I also check that the caller holds no lock needed by a worker or a stop callback. Explicit join does not send a stop request, so simply joining a loop that waits for stop will hang. Finally, I check member order and the destructor body: every borrowed object must remain usable until its worker has joined. Automatic cleanup is useful only after that exit path exists.","follow":"Can a timed wait on a future make it safe to destroy the worker's borrowed buffer?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-threads","title":"The toolkit: std::thread, std::jthread and thread_local","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-mutexes-and-locks.s1","q":"\"Explain <code>std::lock_guard<\/code>, <code>std::unique_lock<\/code>, and <code>std::scoped_lock<\/code> without listing every method.\"","points":["All provide scoped ownership","Lock guard is the simple one-lock choice","Unique lock supports changing and moving ownership","Scoped lock acquires several distinct mutexes safely"],"model":"All three make releasing locks follow object lifetime. Lock guard is my default for one mutex held for a whole scope. Unique lock is useful when ownership must change within that scope, especially for condition variable waits, timed attempts, or explicitly unlocking before a callback. Scoped lock is the convenient choice for taking several distinct mutexes together using deadlock avoidance. I would still handle a self-operation before giving it duplicate mutexes, and I would not assume it fixes dependencies involving other locks or joining threads.","follow":"Why can a condition variable not use a <code>std::lock_guard<\/code>?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-mutexes-and-locks","title":"The toolkit: mutexes and lock guards","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-mutexes-and-locks.s2","q":"\"A developer says a shared lock lets this getter update its cache. How would you review that?\"","points":["Shared owners can overlap","A hidden write is still a write","Use exclusive ownership or another proven protocol","Upgrade requires releasing and rechecking"],"model":"A shared lock allows several threads into the method simultaneously. Calling it a getter does not make a cache update read-only. If those callers write the same cache state, they still need exclusive access or a separate proven synchronization protocol. I would either use an exclusive lock for that operation or read under a shared lock, release it, acquire exclusive ownership, and recheck before filling the cache. Standard <code>std::shared_mutex<\/code> does not provide an atomic upgrade. I would also measure whether shared locking helps for such a short operation.","follow":"Could an atomic hit counter coexist with a shared lock on the data?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-mutexes-and-locks","title":"The toolkit: mutexes and lock guards","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-condition-variables.s1","q":"\"Walk me through the predicate wait in a bounded queue.\"","points":["Lock the predicate state","Predicate checks closed or availability","Wait atomically releases and later reacquires","Recheck and commit while locked"],"model":"I acquire the queue mutex and check whether the queue is closed or an item is available. If neither is true, the wait atomically releases that mutex and blocks. When it wakes, it reacquires the mutex and checks again. That loop handles a spurious wake and another consumer taking the item first. Once the predicate is true, I still distinguish shutdown from useful work. An empty closed queue ends the stream; a nonempty one returns an item while preserving the same lock around the removal. The notification itself never reserves an item for me.","follow":"Why does close need to wake blocked producers too?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-condition-variables","title":"The toolkit: condition variables","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-condition-variables.s2","q":"\"Compare the return values of timed waits and stop-aware waits.\"","points":["Raw timed waits return <code>std::cv_status<\/code>","Predicate timed waits return predicate truth","Timeout budget uses one deadline","Timed stop-aware false needs context and an outcome priority rule"],"model":"A raw timed wait returns a timeout status, which is not proof about my application state. A predicate timed wait returns whether the predicate passed at its final protected check, even if that happens after the deadline. That is usually the useful API for a queue. I use one monotonic deadline so repeated wakeups do not reset the budget. The stop-aware <code>condition_variable_any<\/code> overload also reports predicate truth, so readiness can be true even when cancellation was requested. With its timed overload, false can mean timeout or stop. Both may be observable together, so the service defines which outcome wins.","follow":"Can a timed wait return after the deadline because of scheduling?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-condition-variables","title":"The toolkit: condition variables","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-condition-variables.s3","q":"\"The flag is atomic and already true, but a worker is stuck waiting. ThreadSanitizer is silent. What do you check?\"","points":["Atomic state alone does not make a condition-variable protocol correct","Walk through false check, producer store and notify, then wait","A notification is not stored for a future waiter","Update predicate state under the same mutex and retain the predicate wait","ThreadSanitizer does not prove absence of lost wakeups"],"model":"I would check whether the producer takes the waiter's mutex when it changes the flag. An atomic flag prevents a data race, but it does not close the gap before sleeping. The worker can check false, then the producer stores true and notifies before the worker actually starts waiting. That notification is not saved for a future wait, so the worker may sleep indefinitely. I would update the predicate state under the same mutex and keep the predicate overload. Notification can follow unlocking as long as the object stays alive. ThreadSanitizer can stay silent because this is a progress bug in a program without a data race.","follow":"Would changing <code>notify_one()<\/code> to <code>notify_all()<\/code> close that gap?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-condition-variables","title":"The toolkit: condition variables","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-atomics.s1","q":"\"How would you explain <code>compare_exchange_weak<\/code> and its <code>expected<\/code> argument?\"","points":["Compare and replace is one atomic operation","Failure updates <code>expected<\/code>","Weak can fail spuriously","Retry recomputes <code>desired<\/code> and checks the operation remains valid"],"model":"Compare exchange checks whether the atomic still matches my expected value and, on success, replaces it with the desired value in one step. Expected is passed by reference. On failure it receives the observed value, which gives my next attempt a new starting point. The weak form may fail spuriously, so it is normally used in a loop that tolerates that. I recompute desired after failure and check whether the business condition still permits a change. I also keep irreversible side effects outside the speculative retry, because the loop body can run several times.","follow":"When would you choose the strong form?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-atomics","title":"The toolkit: std::atomic and memory orders","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-atomics.s2","q":"\"Choose memory orders for a statistics counter and a flag publishing ordinary data. Why might your choices differ?\"","points":["Independent counting needs atomic arithmetic","Publication also orders unrelated memory","Release must be observed by acquire","Lifetime and multi-field invariants remain separate"],"model":"An independent statistics counter needs increments not to overwrite each other, but it may not need to order any other data. Relaxed can provide that atomic arithmetic. A publication flag has a second job: it tells the reader that ordinary payload writes are ready to use. I need a release operation and an acquire that observes that publication to establish the ordering. Merely making the flag atomic is not enough. I would still check payload lifetime and whether the publisher modifies it again. Atomics do not automatically protect a larger multi-field invariant either.","follow":"Does sequential consistency make two balance updates a single transfer?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-atomics","title":"The toolkit: std::atomic and memory orders","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-atomics.s3","q":"\"We call <code>notify_all()<\/code> but an atomic waiter stays blocked. What do you check?\"","points":["Check the atomic value changes before notification","Wait returns only after observing a value different from its argument","Transient changes can be missed through ABA","Notification does not supply publication ordering","Use a condition variable when a timeout is required"],"model":"I first check the value, because notification is not a stored event. If the atomic still equals the value passed to wait, the call keeps waiting. I change the value before notifying and check whether another thread can change it back, hiding the transition. A generation counter can help detect a change, while a queue is needed to preserve individual events. I also check the release and acquire operations: notification does not publish ordinary payload data by itself. If this operation needs a timeout, I choose a condition variable because C++20 atomic wait has no timed overload.","follow":"How would you wake several readers after one-time publication?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-atomics","title":"The toolkit: std::atomic and memory orders","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-futures-and-async.s1","q":"\"Compare a thread, a promise, a future, and a packaged task. Which parts would you use for a worker pool?\"","points":["Thread supplies execution","Promise fulfills a shared state","Future observes its value or exception","Packaged task connects callable invocation to fulfillment"],"model":"A thread is an execution resource. A promise is the provider side of a shared result state, and a future is the consumer side that waits and retrieves a value or exception. A packaged task combines a callable with that result channel: invoking it stores either the return value or the thrown exception. It does not start a thread by itself. That makes it useful in a pool, where the queue and workers decide when to execute it. I can return the future to the submitter while keeping thread ownership inside the pool.","follow":"What happens if a queued provider is destroyed without ever running?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-futures-and-async","title":"The toolkit: async, future, promise and packaged_task","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-futures-and-async.s2","q":"\"Why can destroying a future unexpectedly block, and why is that dangerous under a lock?\"","points":["<code>std::launch::async<\/code> states have a last-reference completion rule","Temporary future destruction can serialize launches","Promise futures do not universally join producers","Waiting under a needed mutex creates a cycle"],"model":"The special case is a shared state created with <code>std::launch::async<\/code>. Releasing its last reference can wait for the associated execution to finish. That can happen at the end of a statement when a temporary future is discarded, so apparently parallel launches may become serial. It is also dangerous while holding a mutex: if the worker needs that mutex before returning, destruction waits for a worker that is waiting for the caller. I keep the result handles deliberately and place completion waits outside such locks. I would not generalize the behavior to every promise-produced future.","follow":"Does destroying that future rethrow a stored task exception?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-futures-and-async","title":"The toolkit: async, future, promise and packaged_task","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-cpp20-sync.s1","q":"\"Compare a semaphore, latch, and barrier with concrete uses.\"","points":["Semaphore counts permits","Latch records one finite arrival batch","Barrier separates repeated phases","Each needs lifetime and failure-path handling"],"model":"A semaphore counts available permits, so I use it to limit simultaneous downloads or hand a signal from one thread to another. A latch counts arrivals down to zero once, which fits waiting for a startup batch. A barrier coordinates repeated phases, such as several rounds of parallel computation, and can run a completion step between phases. They are not interchangeable shutdown mechanisms. I must still account for a task that throws before its arrival, pair only successful permit acquisitions with releases, and keep each object alive until its users have finished.","follow":"What changes if one worker permanently leaves a barrier?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-cpp20-sync","title":"The toolkit: semaphores, latches, barriers and stop tokens","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-cpp20-sync.s2","q":"\"What surprises can stop callbacks create in otherwise simple cancellation code?\"","points":["Stop requests run callbacks synchronously","Late registration can invoke immediately","Destruction can wait for its own callback invocation on another thread","Avoid locks needed by callbacks and keep them nonthrowing"],"model":"Requesting stop can execute callbacks synchronously, so it is more than setting a flag. A callback registered after the request can also run immediately during registration. Its destructor may wait for its own callback already running on another thread. Those facts matter for both lock ordering and lifetime. I avoid requesting stop while holding a mutex a callback needs, and I keep callbacks short and nonthrowing. For a condition wait I prefer the standard stop-aware overload, because a notification callback alone does not establish a durable shutdown predicate or close the wait-registration gap.","follow":"Does the stop request interrupt a blocking socket operation?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-cpp20-sync","title":"The toolkit: semaphores, latches, barriers and stop tokens","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-cpp20-sync.s3","q":"\"Your service hangs while destroying its worker threads. They are all <code>std::jthread<\/code> objects. What do you check?\"","points":["A joinable jthread destructor requests its own stop state and then joins","The worker must observe the same stop state and have a wakeup path","Semaphore, latch, and barrier waits are not stop-aware","Request cancellation for every dependent worker before joining any","Keep shared state alive until joins finish"],"model":"I first check how the workers notice cancellation and leave their waits. A joinable jthread destructor requests its own stop state, then joins. It cannot force the worker to exit. A worker using a separate group's token will not observe that request, and an ordinary semaphore or barrier wait will not wake just because stop was requested. I request the group's source, or every worker's own source, before joining any worker that depends on its peers. Then I check the wakeup and failure protocols for each blocking operation. Shared state must stay alive until all those joins finish.","follow":"How would you make semaphore admission cancellable without creating extra permits?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-cpp20-sync","title":"The toolkit: semaphores, latches, barriers and stop tokens","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-putting-it-together.s1","q":"\"Walk me through the acceptance and shutdown race in your pool.\"","points":["Acceptance and closed state use one mutex","Earlier accepted work drains","Later admissions reject and both waiter roles wake","Owner joins outside the lock and waits for external callers before destruction"],"model":"A submission becomes accepted only when its task is inserted under the queue mutex. Close sets the closed flag under that same mutex, so those operations have a definite order. If insertion wins, shutdown must drain that task. If close wins, admission rejects even if space becomes available. A submitter that already decided timeout is also unaccepted. Close wakes producers and consumers. Workers drain remaining tasks and exit when closed and empty. The owner joins outside the queue lock, and separately waits for external submitters before destruction. Running jobs still have to return for shutdown to finish.","follow":"How would wait-until-idle differ from observing an empty queue?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-putting-it-together","title":"Putting it together: a thread safe class from scratch","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-toolkit-putting-it-together.s2","q":"\"What would you test before claiming this class is thread safe?\"","points":["State invariants and exactly-once identities","Forced blocking and overlapping task counters","Timeout, exceptions, close and partial startup","Sanitizer plus bounded deadlock tests"],"model":"I would check the queue capacity invariant and track task identities to detect duplicates or losses. I would use gates to force a full queue and blocked workers, then prove actual overlap with an active-call counter. I would test timeout, task exceptions, rejection after close, draining, and both kinds of waiter waking during shutdown. I would also review partial thread creation failure and borrowed capture lifetimes. Then I would run ThreadSanitizer with an external timeout and varied schedules. A clean sanitizer run is useful evidence, but it does not prove logical ordering or rule out every deadlock.","follow":"What failure can an empty-queue-only test miss?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-toolkit-putting-it-together","title":"Putting it together: a thread safe class from scratch","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-interview-style.s1","q":"\"You have a bounded queue in C++. Talk me through your locking rule, shutdown contract, and the failure cases you would test.\"","points":["One mutex protects deque and closed flag; capacity is positive and never exceeded","Producer and consumer predicates include closed, with separate condition variables","Close rejects new insertions, allows draining, and notifies every waiter","Insertion, removal and setting closed identify the operations' linearization points","Element moves cannot throw; failed insertion wakes another producer and rethrows","Owner closes and joins before destruction; tests cover capacity 1, racing close and exact item delivery"],"model":"I would use one mutex for the deque and the closed flag, with a positive fixed capacity. That makes the space check and insertion one critical section, so two producers cannot claim the same last slot. Producers wait for space or closure. Consumers wait for an item or closure. I use separate condition variables because a normal insertion should wake a consumer and a removal should wake a producer. Close sets the flag under that mutex and notifies everyone in both groups. Push then rejects new insertions; pop drains accepted items and returns an empty optional only when closed and empty. The operation takes effect at insertion, removal, or setting the closed flag. That also defines a push racing with close: whichever wins the mutex determines acceptance. For generic values, I require non-throwing moves and destruction. If insertion allocation fails, I wake another producer before propagating the exception, because the free slot is still available. The owner must stop new callers, close, and join every user before destroying the queue. I would test capacity one, shutdown with an empty or full queue, a push racing with close, and exact item identities under multiple producers and consumers. ThreadSanitizer adds race detection, while bounded completion checks catch hangs.","follow":"How would you support a type whose move constructor can throw after modifying its source?","seconds":90,"lesson":"cpp-interview-style","title":"Writing C++ in an interview","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"cpp-interview-style.s2","q":"\"All my workers sometimes print the same loop index. I captured by reference and joined them after the loop. Why is that still broken?\"","points":["Workers refer to one loop variable while the creating thread modifies it","Unsynchronized conflicting accesses are a C++ data race and undefined behaviour","The loop variable dies before the joins, creating a possible dangling reference","Capture the index by value; separately preserve lifetimes of genuinely shared state","Join synchronizes completion with later reads, not earlier concurrent writes"],"model":"Joining after the loop does not make the earlier accesses safe. Every worker refers to the same loop variable while the creating thread keeps changing it. Those accesses are not synchronized, so this is a C++ data race and undefined behaviour. I cannot even promise that the output is limited to the loop's range. There is a second bug: a variable declared in the for statement dies when the loop ends. A delayed worker can read through a dangling reference before we reach its join. I would capture the index by value, giving each worker its own copy, and capture shared state by reference only when its lifetime and synchronization are established separately. Join lets the caller safely consume completed worker results after joining. It does not retroactively order the parent's loop increments against worker reads. I would fix the capture first, then run the corrected code under ThreadSanitizer.","follow":"Does capturing <code>this<\/code> by value keep the object alive?","seconds":60,"lesson":"cpp-interview-style","title":"Writing C++ in an interview","track":"cpp","module":5},{"id":"java-how-it-runs.s1","q":"\"Walk me from a Java source file to running machine code. Where do class initialization and concurrency guarantees enter that story?\"","points":["Javac produces class files containing bytecode, which a JVM loads and verifies","Loading and linking can happen early; active use triggers initialization subject to the language rules","Class initialization coordinates competing threads and publishes the completed static state","HotSpot interprets code and uses profiling to compile hot methods or loops into native code","Compiled execution must obey the Java Memory Model as well as each thread's semantics","Initialization does not make later mutations of a shared object thread-safe"],"model":"Java source becomes bytecode first, and the JVM then executes it, using native code for hot paths. The compiler writes class files. The JVM loads, verifies and links classes, potentially before they are needed. Initialization is a separate step, triggered by active use such as calling a static method or reading a nonconstant static field. If two threads first use the same class, one performs initialization and the other waits, so successful initialization safely publishes the completed static state. HotSpot initially interprets code and collects execution profiles. Its tiered compilers turn hot methods and loops into machine code; a running loop can switch into compiled code without returning first. Those optimizations must still obey the Java Memory Model. The compiler cannot ignore synchronization, although a plain racy field may have reads reused or moved. For example, a map created during class initialization is safely visible to later users. That does not make future updates to a mutable map safe. I would use an immutable map, a concurrent collection, or a common lock for those updates. Initialization publishes state; it does not permanently protect it.","follow":"Why can reading a <code>static final<\/code> compile-time constant leave its declaring class uninitialized?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-how-it-runs","title":"How Java runs: JVM, bytecode, JIT and GC","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-how-it-runs.s2","q":"\"A plain boolean stop flag works in short tests but sometimes spins forever in production. Someone proposes adding a sleep or a log line. How would you respond?\"","points":["The unsynchronized write and read form a data race with no guaranteed visibility","HotSpot may reuse or hoist the read; short tests and debugger runs do not establish correctness","Sleep supplies no synchronization, and logging can change timing or introduce incidental ordering","Use a volatile boolean or <code>AtomicBoolean<\/code> for a simple flag, or the same lock for every access","Volatile visibility does not make compound operations such as increment atomic","Interruption supports cooperative cancellation of interruptible waits; handle <code>InterruptedException<\/code> and join the worker"],"model":"I would fix the communication between threads. A plain flag read and written without synchronization has no guarantee that the worker will observe the write. HotSpot may compile the hot loop so that it reads the flag once and reuses that value. That explains one possible production hang, but it is not a prediction that every run must hang. Short tests and debugger runs can produce different timing or compilation decisions. Sleeping does not establish a happens-before edge. Logging can change optimization and timing, and sometimes introduce incidental ordering through a shared output lock, but it is not a reliable cancellation protocol. For a simple stop flag I would use a volatile boolean or an <code>AtomicBoolean<\/code>. A common lock around every access also works. Volatile gives the visibility needed for this flag; it does not make a read, increment and write into one atomic operation. If the worker can block in a queue operation or sleep, I would consider interruption so it can also leave an interruptible wait. The worker must cooperate, including handling <code>InterruptedException<\/code> by exiting or propagating it appropriately. Finally I would join the worker before treating shutdown as complete. Tests can exercise cancellation, but the synchronization rule is the correctness argument.","follow":"Would declaring a shared counter <code>volatile<\/code> make <code>count += 1<\/code> safe for two writers?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-how-it-runs","title":"How Java runs: JVM, bytecode, JIT and GC","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-how-it-runs.s3","q":"\"All requests in one Java process stall at the same time, then recover. How would you check whether garbage collection is involved, and what could that break besides latency?\"","points":["Correlate request stalls with GC and safepoint logs rather than infer GC from latency alone","Separate time reaching a safepoint from time spent doing stop-the-world collection work","Inspect allocation rate, retained live data and collector configuration before choosing a mitigation","Lower allocation or a concurrent collector may reduce pauses but cannot guarantee their absence","A paused lease holder can resume after its lease expires and another holder takes over","Fencing tokens let storage reject older writers after a newer token has been accepted"],"model":"I would correlate the stalled request timestamps with GC and safepoint logs before blaming the collector. A stop-the-world collection can pause application work across the process, so synchronized latency spikes are a useful clue, not proof. I would separate time spent getting threads to a safepoint from time spent collecting, then inspect allocation rate, retained live data and the collector configuration. Reducing temporary allocations or choosing a mostly concurrent collector may help, but neither guarantees that the process will never pause. The correctness risk is work that relies on a lease still being valid. Suppose a node owns a ten-second lease and pauses long enough for it to expire. Another node acquires the lease and starts writing. The first node resumes with its old local belief that it is still the owner. Shorter GC pauses only make this less likely. I would use increasing fencing tokens and require storage to reject older tokens after it has accepted a newer one. That protects against a stale writer even when a process pause is longer than expected.","follow":"If the Java heap is mostly empty but creating a platform thread throws <code>OutOfMemoryError<\/code>, what resources would you investigate?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-how-it-runs","title":"How Java runs: JVM, bytecode, JIT and GC","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-objects-references.s1","q":"\"Java passes objects by value, so each worker gets its own copy, right? Explain what actually happens when I pass a list to two tasks.\"","points":["Java passes the reference value, so both tasks can reach the same list object","Mutating through the parameter affects that object; reassigning the parameter does not reassign the caller's variable","Local and effectively final references do not make their targets confined or immutable","Each invocation has private locals, but concurrent list mutations need synchronization","Choose a concurrent collection, one shared lock, immutable input or separate task results","A shallow list copy separates the list structure but can still share mutable elements"],"model":"Each worker gets a copy of the reference, not a copy of the list. Java always passes values, and for an object argument that value identifies the same object the caller already uses. If a method adds an element through its parameter, the caller sees the changed list. If the method assigns a new list to that parameter, only the method's local variable changes. Passing the reference to two tasks creates the same kind of aliasing. The fact that the original variable is local, final or effectively final does not make the list private or immutable. Each invocation has its own local variables, but both can call add on one shared <code>ArrayList<\/code>, which needs protection. I would choose a concurrent collection or one common lock if the tasks must share updates. If they only need input, I would safely create an immutable snapshot. Another option is separate task results that the caller merges after completion. I would also inspect the elements: copying a list copies its element references, so two separate lists can still share the same mutable objects.","follow":"What changes if the list is final but contains mutable account objects?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-objects-references","title":"Objects, references and the heap","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-objects-references.s2","q":"\"Our counter uses <code>synchronized (pending)<\/code> where <code>pending<\/code> is an <code>Integer<\/code>. Every increment is inside the block. Why can updates still disappear?\"","points":["Synchronized locks the referenced object, while incrementing an <code>Integer<\/code> rebinds the field","One thread can wait on the old wrapper while another starts using the next wrapper as its lock","Different monitors allow both increments to read the same value and lose an update","Cached wrappers can also be shared with unrelated code; avoid them and interned strings as locks","Use a private final lock with a primitive counter, or an atomic increment for a standalone counter","Protect reads too, and retain a common lock when the invariant spans additional fields"],"model":"The monitor changes when the counter changes. Synchronized locks the object referenced at entry. An <code>Integer<\/code> cannot be modified, so incrementing it unboxes the number, adds one, boxes the result and reassigns the field to another wrapper. Imagine thread A locks the wrapper for zero while thread B waits for that same wrapper. A stores a reference to the wrapper for one and unlocks. B can now enter holding the old wrapper, while A starts its next increment holding the new wrapper. Both can read one and write two, losing an update despite both being inside a synchronized block. Cached wrappers add another problem: unrelated code can hold the same monitor. Interned strings have that sharing risk too. I would use a private final lock object with a primitive counter, and take that same lock for reads and writes. For a standalone counter, an <code>AtomicInteger<\/code> with <code>incrementAndGet<\/code> is simpler. If the count must stay consistent with another field, I would keep one lock around the whole invariant. Making the wrapper field volatile would not fix the changing monitor or the compound increment.","follow":"Would using a final <code>Integer<\/code> as the lock solve the problem of sharing that monitor with unrelated code?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-objects-references","title":"Objects, references and the heap","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-objects-references.s3","q":"\"Every method in this price book is synchronized, but its getter returns the internal list. Is the reporting thread safe when it iterates that result?\"","points":["The getter releases its monitor on return, while the returned alias still reaches the internal mutable list","A later writer can modify the list during the caller's unlocked iteration","<code>ConcurrentModificationException<\/code> is only a possible symptom, not a synchronization guarantee","Copy the list while holding the same lock used by writers; an unmodifiable view still shares the original","<code>List.copyOf<\/code> is sufficient for immutable <code>Integer<\/code> elements but not for mutable element state","Copying costs linear time under the lock; immutable replacement shifts copying to writers but still needs safe publication"],"model":"No. The getter protects the act of returning the reference, but it releases the lock before the reporting thread iterates. That reference still reaches the book's internal mutable list. A writer can then hold the book's lock and change the list while the reporter reads it without that lock. The reporter might get <code>ConcurrentModificationException<\/code>, but that exception is not guaranteed and cannot serve as synchronization. I would create the snapshot while holding the same monitor as the writers, then return the copy. Simply returning an unmodifiable view is insufficient because it still reflects the original list's mutations. Here, <code>List.copyOf<\/code> gives an unmodifiable snapshot, and the <code>Integer<\/code> elements are themselves immutable, so sharing those element references is fine. If the elements were mutable price records, I would need immutable value objects, deeper copies, or separate synchronization for their state. Copying costs linear time while holding the lock. If reads dominate, I might instead publish immutable lists and copy on writes. The replacement reference still needs safe publication, and concurrent writers still need a coordinated update. The goal is a stable snapshot with an explicit sharing rule.","follow":"How would you safely obtain the snapshot if the caller, rather than the price book, owns and concurrently mutates the input list?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-objects-references","title":"Objects, references and the heap","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-classes-interfaces.s1","q":"\"A subclass overrides a synchronized counter method to add auditing. Is it still thread-safe? How would you design this so the next subclass cannot break it?\"","points":["An override does not automatically acquire the overridden method's monitor","Calling <code>super.increment()<\/code> locks only the parent call, not the surrounding audit code","Keep mutable fields private and protect every access with the same lock","Use a final class or final invariant-preserving methods, and prefer composition for added behaviour","Keep external auditing outside the critical section where the required ordering permits it"],"model":"Not automatically. An overriding method does not inherit the synchronized modifier. If it directly increments a protected counter, concurrent calls race even though the parent method was safe. Calling <code>super.increment()<\/code> protects the parent's update, but it does not put the surrounding audit code under that lock. I would first decide whether the audit and increment must be one observable operation. If they must, the class needs an explicit design that preserves that relationship. For a counter I own, I would keep the field private and take one private lock for both updates and reads. I would make the class final, or make the methods that preserve its invariant final if extension is required. To add logging, I would usually wrap the counter through composition instead of exposing its state to subclasses. External auditing can block or acquire another lock, so I would keep it outside the critical section when the ordering requirement allows that. The proof is about every path to the state, not just the modifier on the original method.","follow":"What changes if the audit record and the counter must always be observed together?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-classes-interfaces","title":"Classes, interfaces and inheritance","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-classes-interfaces.s2","q":"\"An interface's default method acquires three permits by calling a thread-safe one-permit method three times. Walk me through what can go wrong and how you would fix it.\"","points":["Atomic calls do not make the complete default method atomic or all-or-nothing","With four available permits, two callers can each acquire two and then both fail","Earlier successful acquisitions remain consumed unless the implementation explicitly restores them","Override the batch method to validate, check availability and subtract under one lock","Document the batch contract; default methods have no automatic synchronization guarantee"],"model":"The batch is not atomic, even if every one-permit call is thread-safe. Suppose four permits are available and two threads each request three. The first thread takes one, the second takes one, and they alternate again. Each now holds two permits. Both next attempts fail, so both batch calls return false even though all four permits have been consumed. No individual call was broken. The missing guarantee was across the whole request. I would override the batch method in the implementation that owns the permit count. It validates the request, takes the same lock as every other access, checks that enough permits exist, and subtracts the entire request only on success. On failure it changes nothing. I would make the single-permit method delegate to that implementation so there is one policy to maintain. Trying to roll back a partial batch adds more concurrency reasoning and still needs a clear contract. The interface should promise all-or-nothing behaviour explicitly; a default method gets no such guarantee merely because it calls safe methods.","follow":"Could a default method itself use a synchronized block, and what lock agreement would that require?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-classes-interfaces","title":"Classes, interfaces and inheritance","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-classes-interfaces.s3","q":"\"A refactor replaces a lambda with an anonymous Runnable, and list updates start getting lost. Both versions say synchronized on this. What would you inspect?\"","points":["A lambda uses the enclosing instance as <code>this<\/code>; an anonymous class uses its own instance","Separately created tasks therefore acquire different monitors","Those monitors also differ from the outer object's synchronized-method monitor","Use <code>Outer.this<\/code> consistently or one private final lock shared by all access paths","Avoid constructor escape when registering or starting these callbacks"],"model":"I would check which object each synchronized block actually locks. A lambda uses the enclosing instance as <code>this<\/code>. An anonymous Runnable has its own <code>this<\/code>, so the refactor changes the monitor. If each submission creates a new task, every task locks a different object. Those locks also fail to exclude the outer class's synchronized methods. Two tasks can then update the same list concurrently despite both containing synchronized blocks. One fix is to name <code>Outer.this<\/code> explicitly, matching the outer methods. My preferred design is one private final lock object, used by every task and every outer method that accesses the list, including readers. I would also check how callbacks are registered: registration belongs after construction, so a worker cannot observe a partly initialized owner. Changing the callback syntax is safe only after we check its lock identity and what it captures.","follow":"What would a long-lived scheduler retaining this task imply for garbage collection of its enclosing object?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-classes-interfaces","title":"Classes, interfaces and inheritance","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-final-immutability.s1","q":"\"We pass a record containing a list of strings between worker threads. A teammate says its final fields make it immutable. Would you approve that?\"","points":["A record's final component field fixes the reference, not the list's contents","The caller or an accessor can expose the same mutable list to other threads","Use <code>List.copyOf<\/code> in the compact constructor to retain an unmodifiable snapshot","Copying the list does not copy its elements; strings are immutable but mutable elements need their own policy","Build the snapshot from stable input and publish the completed record safely"],"model":"I would approve it only after checking the list's ownership. A record makes its component fields final, but that freezes the references, not the objects they refer to. If the constructor stores an ArrayList supplied by the caller, the caller can still add an element, and the generated accessor exposes that same list. Passing the record through a queue does not freeze it. For a list of strings, I would use <code>List.copyOf<\/code> in the compact constructor. That gives the record an unmodifiable snapshot of the membership, and strings themselves are immutable. Wrapping the original with an unmodifiable view would not be enough, because changes through the original list still show through. Copying the list also does not copy its elements: if they were mutable jobs, I would need immutable job values or defensive copies of those too. The input must be stable while I copy it. Finally, I would publish the completed record through a queue, a volatile reference or another safe handoff. Immutability protects the snapshot; publication delivers it.","follow":"How would your design change if the record held a byte array instead of strings?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-final-immutability","title":"final, immutability and records","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-final-immutability.s2","q":"\"One background thread reloads configuration while two hundred request threads read several flags per request. Design this so readers never see half a reload.\"","points":["Build and validate a complete immutable configuration before publishing it","Use one volatile reference for the current configuration with a single reloader","Each request captures that reference once and reads every flag from that snapshot","Keep the old snapshot if validation fails; existing requests can finish using it","For multiple writers deriving updates from current state, use a lock or an atomic reference update with a side-effect-free function","State the allocation cost and the fact that volatile does not make a read-modify-write atomic"],"model":"I would publish whole immutable snapshots through one volatile reference. The reloader parses the file into a new configuration, copies any mutable collections and validates the complete result before assigning that reference. A bad reload leaves the old configuration in service. Each request reads the current reference once into a local variable and uses that object for every flag lookup. That last detail matters: reading the shared reference again for each flag could combine values from different versions. A request already holding the old object can finish with it while later requests use the new one. No request needs to lock the configuration, because neither version changes after construction. The volatile handoff makes the new snapshot and its initialized state visible. With one reloader, a simple replacement is enough. If several writers derive changes from the current configuration, plain read, build and write can lose an update. I would then serialize updates with a lock or use an atomic reference update whose function has no side effects, because it may be retried. The cost is allocating new snapshots and temporarily retaining old ones. I would choose this when reads dominate and the configuration is small enough that copying remains reasonable.","follow":"Why can a request still observe mixed versions if the shared reference is volatile but it reads that reference twice?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-final-immutability","title":"final, immutability and records","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-final-immutability.s3","q":"\"If final fields are visible across threads, why do you still publish immutable objects through volatile fields or queues? Explain the difference.\"","points":["Final-field semantics protect initialization of properly constructed objects, not delivery of the shared reference","Construction must finish without exposing <code>this<\/code> to the reader early","A reader that obtains the properly constructed object sees its initialized final fields","A plain shared reference can remain null or stale; volatile or another handoff supplies publication ordering","The guarantee does not synchronize later mutations of objects reached through final references"],"model":"Final fields protect initialization; they do not deliver a new reference to another thread. If I construct an object without letting it escape and assign its final limit field to ten, a reader that obtains that properly constructed object gets ten for that field. This special guarantee can hold even when the reference was published through a plain field. But that reader is not guaranteed to discover the reference at all. It may keep seeing null or an older object. I still use a volatile reference, a queue or another synchronization mechanism to establish the handoff. Construction also matters: if I register a callback from the constructor and it runs before the limit is assigned, final does not prevent that callback from observing the default value. Finally, a final reference to a list does not make later list changes visible or safe. The initialization guarantee covers the state reached through that reference at construction, not arbitrary future mutations. I separate the two questions: is this snapshot fully built, and how does another thread receive it?","follow":"Does replacing a volatile immutable snapshot with a modified copy remain safe when two writers do it concurrently?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-final-immutability","title":"final, immutability and records","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-equals-hashcode.s1","q":"\"Our ConcurrentHashMap cache keeps growing, but looking up a key we inserted returns null. The request handler increments the page number inside the key. Walk me through your diagnosis.\"","points":["Mutating equality or hash fields while a key is stored can strand its entry","The map retains the insertion hash and a reference to the original key object","A changed hash makes lookup miss; a fresh key for the old page also fails equality against the mutated key","ConcurrentHashMap protects its structure, not mutations inside keys","Use an immutable key and a new instance for the next page; rebuild or clear affected cache entries safely","Keep equality state stable and test equivalent independent keys, not only the original instance"],"model":"I would suspect a mutable key before changing the map's locking. The entry stores the hash computed at insertion and a reference to the key object. It does not save a copy of the key's page number. Suppose we insert a query for page one and then change that same object to page two. If its hash changes, a lookup uses the new hash, while the entry still has the old one. The stored hash check rejects it, even if both hashes happen to choose the same bucket. A fresh key for page one also misses because the stored object now compares as page two. The entry still counts toward size and retains its value. ConcurrentHashMap cannot prevent this: its synchronization protects the map's structure, not arbitrary writes inside keys. I would make the key an immutable record of the query text and page, and create a new key for each page. That prevents new damage; an already affected cache may need a controlled rebuild or invalidation. I would test lookups with separately constructed equal keys and verify that callers cannot change equality fields after insertion. Eviction can limit memory, but it does not fix the key contract.","follow":"Would making the page field volatile or synchronizing its setter repair the existing map entry?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-equals-hashcode","title":"equals, hashCode and objects as keys","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-equals-hashcode.s2","q":"\"Explain why overriding equals without hashCode breaks a deduplication set, but returning the same hash for every key can still be correct.\"","points":["Equal objects must have equal hashes; unequal objects may share a hash","Hash collections select candidates using hashes before comparing equality","Inherited identity hashes can differ for two logically equal objects, causing missed matches","A constant hash satisfies the equality implication but forces collisions","Collisions hurt lookup cost and can concentrate concurrent writers on one bin lock","Use stable immutable equality fields and a well-distributed hash derived from them"],"model":"The contract goes in one direction: equal objects must have equal hashes, but equal hashes do not prove equality. A hash collection first uses the hash to find candidates and then uses equality to distinguish them. If I override equals to compare a user identifier but inherit Object's identity hash, two separate objects for the same user can have different hashes. The lookup can reject the stored entry before equals is called, so deduplication fails. An accidental identity-hash collision might hide the mistake in one case, but it cannot make the implementation correct. A constant hash has the opposite problem. It obeys the contract because every equal pair certainly shares a hash. Distinct keys can still be distinguished by equals, but they all collide. That increases lookup work and, in a concurrent hash map, concentrates writes on the same bin lock. I would use immutable fields that represent the logical identity, implement equals consistently, and derive a reasonably distributed hash from those fields. A record with immutable components is usually a straightforward way to do that.","follow":"Does converting a collision bin to a tree guarantee logarithmic lookup for every possible key class?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-equals-hashcode","title":"equals, hashCode and objects as keys","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-equals-hashcode.s3","q":"\"Design a key and lock-stripe selection for a per-tenant, per-endpoint counter. What would you reject in a code review?\"","points":["Use an immutable composite key whose equality includes tenant and endpoint","Avoid ambiguous string concatenation and mutable request objects as keys","Map a stable hash to a positive stripe count with <code>Math.floorMod<\/code> or a valid power-of-two mask","<code>Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE)<\/code> stays negative and can produce an invalid index","All operations on the same logical key must use the same stripe; stripe collisions cost concurrency, not correctness","A stripe does not make writes to one shared ordinary HashMap safe under different locks"],"model":"I would use an immutable record containing the tenant identifier and endpoint. Both fields define equality, so two independent requests for that pair select the same counter and the same stripe. I would reject using the mutable request object, and I would avoid joining strings with a separator unless the encoding is unambiguous. For a fixed positive number of stripes, I would select the index with <code>Math.floorMod<\/code> of the key's hash and the stripe count. Taking the absolute value is a trap: the absolute value of the minimum integer is still negative, so the remainder can be a negative array index. Every read and update that belongs to this locking policy must use the same stripe rule. Two different keys sharing a stripe is safe; it only reduces parallelism. I would also inspect the storage behind the locks. Different stripes do not make concurrent structural writes to one ordinary HashMap safe. I would use a separate map per stripe, or a concurrent map with the extra locks protecting a clearly defined compound operation.","follow":"How would you acquire locks for an operation involving two keys that might map to the same stripe?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-equals-hashcode","title":"equals, hashCode and objects as keys","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-types-pitfalls-and-modern-java.s1","q":"\"Our boxed request IDs compare equal in small tests and fail later. Walk me through what you would check, especially if threads also update a boxed counter.\"","points":["Distinguish reference identity from value equality and use equals or Objects.equals","State the guaranteed Integer cache range and that caching may extend beyond it","Explain that primitive comparison unboxes and null unboxing throws","Explain that boxed increment replaces a reference through a compound update","Separate the equality fix from atomicity and overflow policies"],"model":"I would check for reference equality first. Two Integer references compared with double equals test identity, not their numeric values. Small tests can hide that because Integer caches minus 128 through 127, and an implementation may cache more. I would use equals, or Objects.equals when null is allowed, and test both small and large IDs. Comparing a wrapper with a primitive unboxes it, so that comparison is numeric but throws if the wrapper is null. The counter is a separate issue. Incrementing an Integer reads a reference, unboxes, adds, boxes and writes a reference back. The old object stays immutable, but two threads can still overwrite each other's updates. Volatile does not combine those steps. I would choose an atomic update or a stable lock, then specify whether overflow should wrap, reject the operation, or require a wider representation.","follow":"Would using <code>Math.addExact<\/code> inside the assignment make the update atomic?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-types-pitfalls-and-modern-java","title":"Types, pitfalls and modern Java (17 to 21)","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-types-pitfalls-and-modern-java.s2","q":"\"Choose a Java representation for commands sent to a worker. Why would you use records and a sealed interface, and what changes if our interview compiler is Java 17?\"","points":["Choose records for payloads and a sealed interface for a closed variant family","State that records have final component fields but mutable referents need an ownership policy","Explain Java 21 exhaustive pattern switching and specific cases before broader cases","Give an explicit null policy and distinguish default from case null","Use instanceof patterns on Java 17 and retain a separate safe handoff mechanism"],"model":"I would use one record per command and a sealed interface for the closed family. Each record carries only the payload that its command needs. Final component fields help, but an array or mutable list still needs copying or a clear ownership rule. On Java 21 I can dispatch with an exhaustive pattern switch. If I add a permitted variant and recompile, the missing case becomes a compiler error. I put guarded cases before broader cases and choose a null policy explicitly, because default does not catch null. On Java 17 the records and sealed interface still work; I replace the switch with instanceof patterns and test every variant because the if chain is not checked for exhaustiveness. None of this makes command execution atomic or publishes the command safely. I still use the queue or synchronization mechanism chosen for the worker.","follow":"When would an open interface with polymorphic methods fit better than a sealed family and a switch?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-types-pitfalls-and-modern-java","title":"Types, pitfalls and modern Java (17 to 21)","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-types-pitfalls-and-modern-java.s3","q":"\"A buffer size goes negative even though we store it in a long and compute it under a lock. How would you debug that?\"","points":["Check operand types because a long destination does not widen an int operation","Show a concrete overflowing product and widen an operand before multiplication","Use exact arithmetic and checked narrowing where the contract requires rejection","Separate range correctness from the lock's coordination guarantee","Test boundary values and reject invalid input independently of overflow checking"],"model":"I would inspect the types of the operands before changing the locking. If two ints are multiplied, Java computes an int result first, even when the destination is long. Fifty thousand times fifty thousand exceeds the int range, so the product wraps and the later conversion preserves the wrong negative value. I would convert one operand to long before multiplying, or use the long overload of multiplyExact when overflow must reject the request. Casting the finished product is too late. If an API ultimately needs an int, I would use toIntExact rather than silently discard bits. The lock coordinates threads; it does not validate arithmetic. I would test zero, the largest valid size, the first overflowing size and negative inputs. Exact arithmetic handles representable range, but rejecting a negative requested capacity is still a separate domain check.","follow":"How would you compute a fractional average without first truncating it through integer division?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-types-pitfalls-and-modern-java","title":"Types, pitfalls and modern Java (17 to 21)","track":"java","module":1},{"id":"java-generics.s1","q":"\"You have written an integer blocking queue. Make it generic and explain why the array and the cast are safe.\"","points":["Introduce a type parameter with typed insertion and removal methods","Erasure prevents creating <code>new T[capacity]<\/code>; retain a private <code>Object[]<\/code>","Keep the unchecked element cast in one small helper with a documented invariant","Only typed insertion may populate occupied slots; raw aliases or exposed storage would break that proof","The existing lock, wait predicates and signalling still provide concurrency safety","Clear removed slots and never return an Object array disguised as a concrete element array"],"model":"I would parameterize the queue with T, accept T in put and return T from take. The storage stays a private Object array. Java checks type arguments at compile time and erases them, so the generic class cannot create an array with T as its runtime component type. I would put the unchecked element cast in one small private helper and explain its invariant: every occupied slot was written through the typed insertion method, and no caller can access the array. That makes the cast justified as long as callers do not bypass the API with raw types or unchecked casts. I would suppress the warning only at that helper, not across the whole class. Generics do not change the concurrency proof. Head, tail, count and array accesses still use the same lock, waits still recheck their predicates, and successful operations signal the appropriate condition. After removal I clear the slot so the queue does not retain the element unnecessarily. I would never return the Object array pretending it was a String array, because the caller's runtime cast would fail. The result is one queue implementation with checked element types, plus the same explicit synchronization that made the integer version safe.","follow":"What if callers need a correctly typed array of the queued elements?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-generics","title":"Generics","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-generics.s2","q":"\"Your queue holds Orders. Why should drainTo accept a collection of Object, and how would you write the wildcard?\"","points":["The destination consumes queue elements, so use <code>Collection&lt;? super T&gt;<\/code>","A queue of Order can drain into collections of Order or its supertypes","<code>Collection&lt;T&gt;<\/code> is unnecessarily restrictive because generic types are invariant","<code>? extends T<\/code> describes a producer and cannot safely accept arbitrary T values","Wildcard bounds provide type safety, not locking or atomicity of the transfer","Preserve queue invariants if destination insertion throws, and define destination access rules"],"model":"The destination consumes elements from the queue, so I would accept <code>Collection&lt;? super T&gt;<\/code>. For a queue of Orders, that permits a collection of Order, Record if Order is a record, or Object. Every one of those can hold an Order. A plain collection of T is too restrictive because generic types are invariant: a list of Object is not a list of Order. Using extends would reverse the guarantee. The destination might then be a collection of some narrower subtype, and inserting an arbitrary T would be unsafe. That is producer extends, consumer super, applied from the method's perspective. The wildcard only proves that the types fit. It does not make a transfer atomic across the queue and destination, or protect another thread reading the destination. I would specify how callers coordinate access to that collection. I would also handle an insertion that throws: already removed items must be reflected in the queue's count, and waiting producers must be signalled for the space actually freed. Type correctness and synchronization are separate obligations.","follow":"Could draining into a collection whose add method acquires another lock introduce a deadlock?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-generics","title":"Generics","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-generics.s3","q":"\"A consumer throws ClassCastException while reading a List of String that another thread populated. The code compiled. Where do you look first?\"","points":["Investigate raw types and unchecked casts at the producer or container boundary","Erasure allows a polluted Object-backed collection to store an Integer without checking String","The compiler inserts a String cast at typed retrieval, so the symptom appears in the consumer","Trace all aliases and writes, remove unchecked entry paths, and keep warning suppression narrow","Check publication and collection synchronization separately; generics do not make ArrayList thread-safe"],"model":"I would trace how values entered the list, especially raw aliases and unchecked casts. A declaration saying list of String is a compile-time promise, not a runtime check on every insertion. If a producer treats an ordinary ArrayList as a raw List and adds an Integer, the insertion can compile with a warning and succeed. The list stores Object references after erasure. Later, when the consumer assigns a retrieved element to a String, the compiler's inserted cast fails. The consumer's stack trace identifies where the bad value was discovered, not necessarily where it was introduced. I would follow every alias that can write into that container, remove the raw entry path and fix unchecked warnings instead of suppressing them broadly. If a legacy boundary cannot be typed, I would validate values there before admitting them. I would separately check that the handoff establishes visibility and that concurrent mutations use an appropriate lock or concurrent collection. Even a perfectly typed ArrayList is not safe for unsynchronized sharing. Repairing the type boundary and repairing the threading policy solve different problems.","follow":"Would comparing the runtime classes of a List of String and a List of Integer reveal their element types?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-generics","title":"Generics","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-lambdas-functional.s1","q":"\"This lambda captures a final ArrayList and several workers call add on it. My teammate says final makes it safe. Walk me through your review.\"","points":["<code>final<\/code> fixes the captured reference, not the mutable list it points to","Captured locals must be final or effectively final; Java copies their values, not their objects","Concurrent adds to the same <code>ArrayList<\/code> can lose elements or corrupt its state","<code>Thread.start()<\/code> or executor submission publishes earlier writes but does not protect later mutations","Prefer private task results combined through futures; otherwise guard all list access, including iteration, with one lock"],"model":"It is unsafe if that list is an ordinary <code>ArrayList<\/code>. Final prevents reassignment of the reference; it does not freeze the list or synchronize its methods. A lambda captures the value of a local variable, and that local must be final or effectively final. When the value is a reference, every copy still points to the same object. For example, two workers can both append using the same current size and overwrite an element. Concurrent resizing can also leave the list inconsistent. Starting a thread or submitting a task safely publishes the state established beforehand, but that does not make later concurrent adds safe. My first choice would be for each task to build a private list and return it through a <code>Callable<\/code>. The coordinator retrieves the futures and combines the results on one thread. That removes shared mutation and also gives failures somewhere to go. If callers really need one live shared list, I would protect every access with the same lock, including iteration. I would choose that based on the required sharing, not the final modifier.","follow":"What changes if the lambda reads an instance field instead of a captured local?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-lambdas-functional","title":"Lambdas, functional interfaces and method references","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-lambdas-functional.s2","q":"\"You are submitting a task that waits for work and returns a result. Would you use Runnable, Callable or Supplier, and what happens if it is interrupted?\"","points":["Choose <code>Callable<\/code> for an executor task that returns a result and may throw checked exceptions","<code>Runnable<\/code> returns no result; <code>Supplier<\/code> returns a result but declares no checked exceptions","Let <code>InterruptedException<\/code> propagate from the <code>Callable<\/code>; a <code>Runnable<\/code> should restore the flag and stop","<code>Future.get()<\/code> exposes a task failure through <code>ExecutionException<\/code> and its cause","An interrupt of the thread waiting in <code>get()<\/code> is separate from cancellation of the worker task"],"model":"I would choose a <code>Callable<\/code>, because it returns a value and its method allows checked exceptions. That lets a blocking operation propagate interruption to the task boundary. A <code>Runnable<\/code> returns nothing and cannot declare checked exceptions. A <code>Supplier<\/code> returns a value, but it also cannot declare checked exceptions, so it does not solve that part. If I had to use a runnable, I would catch interruption, restore the current thread's flag, and return promptly. After submission I keep the future and call <code>get<\/code> to observe the result. If the task throws, get throws <code>ExecutionException<\/code>, whose cause identifies the failure. That differs from the waiting thread itself being interrupted: its get call throws interruption directly, and the worker may still be running. If the result is no longer needed, I separately request task cancellation. I never treat merely submitting the work as observing its outcome.","follow":"Why does adding <code>return null<\/code> make a block containing <code>Thread.sleep<\/code> fit the Callable overload?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-lambdas-functional","title":"Lambdas, functional interfaces and method references","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-lambdas-functional.s3","q":"\"An atomic update lambda sends an audit event. Under load we get more events than updates. Why, and would moving it into ConcurrentHashMap.compute solve the problem?\"","points":["Atomic update functions may be reapplied after a failed compare-and-set","Give an interleaving where another update wins and the first caller retries","Keep the update function free of externally visible side effects","Moving the event after a successful update avoids retry duplicates but does not make delivery atomic","<code>ConcurrentHashMap.compute<\/code> calls its function once per invocation but can block competing updates","Keep map callbacks short and do not use them to make remote event delivery transactional"],"model":"The extra events come from retrying the function, even though each atomic update commits only once. Imagine two callers read the same value. Both compute replacements and send events. One wins the compare and set. The other loses, reads the newer value, and may run its function again, sending another event. I would keep that function pure: calculate a replacement without sending messages or mutating another object. For simple logging, I can emit after the update returns, using the returned value. That avoids retry duplicates, but a crash between the update and the event still leaves a gap, and events from different threads can arrive out of order. <code>ConcurrentHashMap.compute<\/code> has a different contract: it invokes its function once per call while coordinating the update, and competing updates can block. A slow event service inside that callback would therefore delay map operations. It still would not make a remote event and an in-memory update one transaction. I would keep that callback short too, and design a separate durable delivery protocol if the event must survive failures.","follow":"How would you share one slow cache load among callers without performing the load inside a map callback?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-lambdas-functional","title":"Lambdas, functional interfaces and method references","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-collections.s1","q":"\"Our LRU cache uses an access-ordered LinkedHashMap. Reads take a read lock and writes take a write lock, but the cache still breaks. What would you change?\"","points":["An access-order <code>get<\/code> can relink an entry, so it needs exclusive protection","Two shared-lock readers can concurrently change the same recency links","Use one exclusive lock for <code>get<\/code>, <code>put<\/code>, eviction and other access to the private map","Trace capacity three: a, b, c; get a; insert d; evict b","<code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code> alone cannot coordinate the map and a separate recency list","Striping can reduce contention but gives up exact global LRU order"],"model":"I would put gets under the same exclusive lock as puts, because an access-order get can change the recency list. Calling it a read does not make its implementation read-only. Two threads holding the shared read lock can both unlink and append entries, changing the same links concurrently. I would keep the map private and guard lookup, insertion, eviction, and any inspection of its state with one exclusive lock. At capacity three, inserting a, b, and c gives that recency order. Getting a changes it to b, c, a. Inserting d then evicts b, leaving c, a, d. That entire bookkeeping needs to remain consistent with the stored entries. Replacing the hash table with <code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code> would protect its own operations, but would not coordinate a separate recency list or the capacity rule. The simple lock is my starting point because the operations are short and easy to reason about. If measurement shows contention, I could partition the cache into independent stripes, while explicitly accepting that eviction would no longer follow one exact global LRU order.","follow":"How would you return the keys for inspection without exposing a mutable view of the cache?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-collections","title":"The collections you will use","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-collections.s2","q":"\"A synchronized list still contains duplicate user IDs. The code checks contains and then calls add. Show me how that happens and choose a fix.\"","points":["The wrapper synchronizes individual calls, not the entire check and add sequence","Both threads can complete <code>contains<\/code> and observe absence before either adds","Lock the wrapper across both calls if retaining the list","Use a concurrent set and its <code>add<\/code> result when uniqueness is the actual requirement","Iteration of the synchronized list also requires its wrapper lock for the entire traversal"],"model":"The list is safe at the method level, but the decision spans two methods. Thread A checks contains and sees the ID is absent, then releases the wrapper's lock. Thread B performs the same check and also sees absence. Both then add the ID, so we have a valid list containing a duplicate. If we keep the list, I would synchronize on the wrapper around both calls. That is the same lock its methods use, and the monitor is reentrant. If the requirement is simply unique membership, I would choose the set returned by <code>ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet<\/code> instead. Its add operation tells exactly one caller that it inserted a previously absent ID. That also avoids a linear search through a list. I would separately review iteration: a synchronized list still requires its wrapper lock for the whole traversal. Safe individual calls do not automatically make a larger operation atomic.","follow":"What if the caller that wins the set insertion then fails to submit the corresponding work?","seconds":65,"lesson":"java-collections","title":"The collections you will use","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-collections.s3","q":"\"Choose the collection for a bounded FIFO work queue. Why not remove from the front of an ArrayList, and what changes if jobs have priorities?\"","points":["Choose a bounded <code>BlockingQueue<\/code> for normal use or <code>ArrayDeque<\/code> under a lock when implementing it","<code>ArrayList.remove(0)<\/code> shifts remaining entries in O(n) while holding the queue lock","<code>ArrayDeque<\/code> supports efficient end operations but its initial capacity is not a hard bound","Check capacity and mutate under one lock, waiting in predicate loops when implementing blocking","Use <code>PriorityQueue<\/code> for priority order; <code>poll<\/code> is ordered but iteration is not","Break priority ties explicitly and retain an admission limit for backpressure"],"model":"For production use I would start with a bounded blocking queue, such as <code>ArrayBlockingQueue<\/code>. If the interview asks me to build one, I would use an <code>ArrayDeque<\/code> under a lock. Removing index zero from an array list shifts the remaining entries, so each dequeue takes linear work while other threads wait. A deque removes from the front in constant time and appends in amortized constant time. Its constructor capacity is only an initial allocation, though; it does not enforce a bound. I must check the limit and insert under the same lock, with producers waiting while full and consumers waiting while empty. Both waits recheck their predicates after waking. If jobs have priorities, I would replace the deque with a <code>PriorityQueue<\/code>. Offering and polling then cost logarithmic time. Polling chooses the next priority; iterating the heap does not give sorted order. I would include a sequence number if equal priorities must preserve admission order. Priority ordering also does not impose a capacity limit, so I would keep explicit backpressure rather than allowing slow consumers to grow memory without bound.","follow":"How would a newly submitted earlier deadline wake a scheduler that is waiting for the current head?","seconds":80,"lesson":"java-collections","title":"The collections you will use","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-iterators-cme.s1","q":"\"This single-threaded for-each loop removes from an ArrayList. Sometimes it throws ConcurrentModificationException and sometimes it quietly skips an item. Explain both outcomes and fix it.\"","points":["For-each over <code>ArrayList<\/code> uses an iterator with an expected modification count","Direct structural removal changes the list's count without updating the iterator","A later <code>next()<\/code> call detects the mismatch even with only one thread","Removing b from a, b, c leaves cursor and size at two, so <code>hasNext()<\/code> ends the loop and skips c","Use <code>removeIf<\/code> or <code>Iterator.remove<\/code>; neither supplies synchronization for concurrent writers","Fail-fast checks are best effort and cannot establish thread safety"],"model":"The loop invalidates its own iterator by removing directly from the list. No second thread is needed. A for-each over an array list uses an iterator, which remembers the modification count it saw at creation. Removing through the list changes that count but does not update the iterator's expected count. A later next call detects the mismatch and throws. The quiet case comes from where the checks happen. In the OpenJDK implementation, consider a list containing a, b, and c. After the iterator returns b, its cursor is two. Removing b makes the list size two. Has-next now says there is nothing left, so next is never called again and c is skipped without an exception. I would use <code>removeIf<\/code> for a straightforward filter, or an explicit iterator and its remove method when I need more control. Those maintain the iteration correctly in one thread. They do not make an ordinary list safe against concurrent writers. For sharing I still need a locking or snapshot policy. Fail-fast detection is useful evidence of a bug, never proof that a successful run was correct.","follow":"Why can <code>list.set<\/code> avoid the modification-count check while still being unsafe against another thread's reads?","seconds":80,"lesson":"java-iterators-cme","title":"Iterators and ConcurrentModificationException","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-iterators-cme.s2","q":"\"You need to traverse a shared collection while writers keep working. Compare locking, copy-on-write and ConcurrentHashMap iteration, and tell me what the caller actually sees.\"","points":["Holding the same lock as writers, from iterator creation through the full traversal, excludes their changes","Copy under that lock, then process the copy outside it; copying costs O(n)","<code>CopyOnWriteArrayList<\/code> iterators retain the array present at creation and ignore later structural changes","That snapshot copies references, so mutable element state needs separate protection","<code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code> iteration visits entries that remain present throughout traversal; concurrent changes may or may not appear","Choose by consistency needs and write frequency; a live aggregate may match no single instant"],"model":"I would choose based on whether the caller needs one coherent view or can tolerate a moving view. Holding the same lock as every writer, from iterator creation through the entire traversal, gives a stable structure but delays writers for the whole scan. Copying under that lock lets me release it before processing, at the cost of a linear copy. For a listener list with rare changes, <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList<\/code> is another option. Its iterator retains the array present when it was created, so later additions and removals do not change that traversal. The snapshot contains references, though. It does not freeze mutable objects stored in those slots. With <code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code>, iteration can proceed during updates without a concurrent modification exception, but it is weakly consistent. Entries that remain present throughout traversal are visited once; later changes may or may not appear. A sum across entries can therefore describe no single moment of the map. I would accept that for approximate monitoring, but use coordinated locking or published immutable snapshots when the result must be coherent. The choice is a consistency contract as well as a performance choice.","follow":"Would copying a ConcurrentHashMap into a HashMap without coordinating writers create a point-in-time snapshot?","seconds":85,"lesson":"java-iterators-cme","title":"Iterators and ConcurrentModificationException","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-iterators-cme.s3","q":"\"A cleanup thread checks that a session is expired, then removes its key from a ConcurrentHashMap. A request sometimes refreshes the session and still loses it. Walk me through the race.\"","points":["A thread-safe map does not make the expiry check and unconditional removal one atomic action","The cleaner can read an expired value, then a request replaces it before removal","Use <code>remove(key, observedValue)<\/code> to compare and remove atomically","Refresh must replace immutable values and equality must distinguish the refreshed version","Mutating the same object or ignoring the expiry version in <code>equals<\/code> leaves the race open","Weakly consistent cleanup may defer concurrent changes; it is not a complete snapshot sweep"],"model":"The race is between the decision and the removal. The cleaner reads an expired session. Before it removes the key, a request installs a refreshed session under that key. An unconditional remove then deletes the fresh value, even though every map call was individually safe. I would retain the value I checked and call the two-argument <code>remove<\/code> with the key and that observed value. The map atomically removes only if the current value still compares equal to what I observed. That condition needs a careful value design. Refreshes should replace an immutable session, and equality must distinguish the refreshed expiry or version, or use object identity. If a request mutates the same session object in place, the map still contains that object and the conditional removal can succeed after the refresh. A new object whose equality ignores the refresh version has the same problem. Finally, the scan remains weakly consistent. It need not inspect every concurrent change during this pass. That is acceptable for periodic cleanup when later passes can revisit entries, but it is not an exact snapshot of all expired sessions.","follow":"If sessions must remain mutable, how would you coordinate refresh and expiry removal under the same lock or map operation?","seconds":80,"lesson":"java-iterators-cme","title":"Iterators and ConcurrentModificationException","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-exceptions.s1","q":"\"Shutdown hangs even though we interrupt the worker. Its catch block logs InterruptedException and loops back to queue.take. What is happening, and how would you fix it?\"","points":["Interruption is a cooperative request, not forced thread termination","<code>BlockingQueue.take<\/code> responds to interruption by throwing and clearing the flag","Logging and continuing loses the request and allows the worker to block again","Propagate <code>InterruptedException<\/code> from blocking APIs; in <code>Runnable<\/code> restore the flag and exit promptly","Place the catch outside the loop or return from an inner catch; release resources in <code>finally<\/code>","CPU work must check cancellation, and a real pool should also track shutdown state"],"model":"The worker consumes the shutdown request and then resumes waiting. Interrupting a thread is cooperative; it does not forcibly end execution. When the worker is blocked in <code>take<\/code>, interruption makes that call throw and clear the interrupt flag. The catch logs the exception but neither restores the flag nor exits, so the next take can block indefinitely. Even a loop condition that checks the flag now sees false. In a blocking library method I would let <code>InterruptedException<\/code> propagate so the caller owns the cancellation decision. In a <code>Runnable<\/code>, I would catch it, restore the flag with the current thread's interrupt method, and finish promptly. I can put the catch outside the whole loop or return from a catch inside it. Locks and resources still need cleanup through finally or try-with-resources. Restoring the flag and immediately retrying the same wait would just create repeated exceptions. If processing includes long CPU work, that work also needs cancellation checks. In a real pool I would maintain shutdown state as well, so a task that clears its interrupt cannot accidentally send the worker back to an endless queue wait.","follow":"Why can <code>shutdownNow<\/code> still fail to stop a task that never waits and never checks interruption?","seconds":80,"lesson":"java-exceptions","title":"Exceptions, InterruptedException and try-with-resources","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-exceptions.s2","q":"\"A submitted task throws, but there is no stack trace and the worker keeps running. Compare that with a raw thread and with ThreadPoolExecutor.execute.\"","points":["Standard executor submission captures task failure in the returned <code>Future<\/code>","<code>Future.get()<\/code> throws <code>ExecutionException<\/code> with the original failure as its cause","A raw thread's uncaught failure terminates that thread and reaches its handler; <code>join()<\/code> does not rethrow it","A directly executed <code>Runnable<\/code> that throws can terminate a <code>ThreadPoolExecutor<\/code> worker","Keep and inspect futures or arrange explicit completion reporting","<code>ExecutorService.close()<\/code> waits for termination but does not report individual task failures"],"model":"That is the expected outcome for a task submitted through a standard executor when nobody observes its future. The task wrapper captures the failure, marks the future complete, and lets the worker continue. I would keep that future and call <code>get<\/code>; it throws <code>ExecutionException<\/code>, and its cause is the original task failure. With a raw thread, an uncaught exception instead terminates that thread and reaches its uncaught exception handler. Joining the thread only waits for termination; it does not rethrow the failure in the joining thread. With a runnable passed directly to <code>ThreadPoolExecutor.execute<\/code>, an escaping exception can terminate the worker and reach that handler, and the pool replaces the worker when needed. That distinction depends on what is actually executed: executing a future wrapper still allows the wrapper to capture the failure. My design would make failure observation explicit, through collected futures or a completion reporting path. I would not assume that closing an executor reports task errors. Close manages termination; the individual outcomes still need inspection. Otherwise a batch can finish with missing results and no useful error report.","follow":"What happens to later executions when one run of a scheduled periodic task throws?","seconds":80,"lesson":"java-exceptions","title":"Exceptions, InterruptedException and try-with-resources","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-exceptions.s3","q":"\"The work throws, and closing its resources throws too. Which failure should I see, and how is that different from a finally block that throws?\"","points":["Try-with-resources preserves the body failure as the primary exception","Successfully initialized resources close in reverse declaration order","Close failures are attached as suppressed exceptions and inspected with <code>getSuppressed()<\/code>","Without a body failure, the first close failure becomes primary","A throwing or returning <code>finally<\/code> can replace or discard the original failure","For explicit locks, acquire before <code>try<\/code> and release in <code>finally<\/code> only after successful acquisition"],"model":"With try-with-resources, I should see the work's failure as the primary exception, with close failures attached as suppressed exceptions. Suppose I open resource a, then resource b, and the body fails. Java attempts to close b before a, and still attempts a if closing b throws. I inspect <code>getSuppressed<\/code> to see cleanup failures without losing the original cause. If the body succeeds, the first close failure becomes primary and later close failures are suppressed onto it. A handwritten finally does not automatically provide that behaviour: throwing from it replaces the exception already in flight, and returning from it can discard that exception entirely. I would therefore use try-with-resources for closeable resources. For an explicit lock, I acquire before entering the try and unlock in finally after successful acquisition. That keeps a failed acquisition from triggering an invalid unlock that hides the real problem.","follow":"If initializing the second resource throws, which resources are closed and which exception remains primary?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-exceptions","title":"Exceptions, InterruptedException and try-with-resources","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-collections-for-coding-rounds.s1","q":"\"Two equal-priority jobs enter a tree, but one disappears. Walk me through the bug and what you would change before sharing this scheduler across threads.\"","points":["The comparator returns zero for distinct jobs, so the tree treats them as one key","TreeSet rejects the second add; TreeMap replaces the existing value","Use a comparison key that preserves identity and agrees with the intended equality","Keep ordering fields immutable or remove before changing and reinserting","A lock protects shared operations but cannot repair an incorrect ordering rule"],"model":"The comparator is treating two different jobs as the same tree key. If it compares only priority, equal priorities return zero. A tree set then rejects the second insertion, and a tree map replaces the value already stored at that key. I would define the identity rule first. If both jobs must survive, I would compare priority and then a distinguishing id, with equality designed consistently. For example, priority four with id a must differ from priority four with id b. I would also keep ordering fields immutable while stored. To change priority, remove the old entry and insert a replacement. Before sharing the scheduler, I would protect its state transitions and reads with the chosen synchronization policy. A lock prevents overlapping operations, but it cannot make a comparator that discards valid jobs correct.","follow":"When would you choose a priority queue instead, and what would happen to equal-priority arrival order?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-collections-for-coding-rounds","title":"Collections for coding rounds: comparators, heaps, trees and traps","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-collections-for-coding-rounds.s2","q":"\"Several workers need to append values to groups in a map. Why is <code>computeIfAbsent<\/code> not the whole thread-safety answer?\"","points":["Atomicity depends on the map implementation; HashMap has no such concurrency guarantee","ConcurrentHashMap can coordinate installation of the value for one key","The following ArrayList.add is a separate operation on shared mutable state","Choose a value-level lock, a concurrent collection, or immutable replacement","Include readers and group removal in the same ownership and lifecycle contract"],"model":"It can make group creation safe on a concurrent hash map, but it does not automatically make the group safe. On a plain hash map, even the compute operation needs external protection. With a concurrent hash map, two workers can safely obtain the same installed list, then both call add on that ordinary array list at once. Those appends are outside the map operation and can lose data or damage the list. I would choose a policy for the value: a lock used by every reader and writer, a suitable concurrent collection, or immutable replacement through an atomic map update. I would also ask whether groups can be removed. An append to a list that was just detached from the map can disappear from future lookups even if the list itself is synchronized. The full operation needs a clear lifecycle contract.","follow":"If the operation must update two groups together, is one atomic map operation per key sufficient?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-collections-for-coding-rounds","title":"Collections for coding rounds: comparators, heaps, trees and traps","track":"java","module":2},{"id":"java-under-the-hood.s1","q":"\"Walk me through two threads entering <code>synchronized<\/code> on the same object in Java 21. Which parts are Java guarantees, and which are HotSpot details?\"","points":["Java guarantees mutual exclusion, reentrancy and an unlock-to-later-lock happens-before edge on the same monitor","A synchronized block uses monitor bytecodes and exception cleanup; a synchronized method uses a flag","HotSpot's default Java 21 stack locking uses the mark word, a stack lock record and CAS for an initial thin-lock acquisition","Contention can inflate the lock into an ObjectMonitor; a contender may spin, then park","Unlocking makes a waiter eligible to compete again, without a fairness guarantee","Biased locking is gone in Java 21; elision and coarsening mean source-level locks do not imply a fixed cost"],"model":"Java guarantees that only one thread owns this object's monitor at a time. The owner can enter it again, and its unlock happens-before a later acquisition of the same monitor. That last rule gives the next owner visibility of earlier writes. For a synchronized block, javac emits monitor entry and exit bytecodes, including cleanup when the body throws. A synchronized method instead carries a flag that tells the JVM to manage the monitor. Now for HotSpot details: in Java 21's default stack locking mode, the first thread saves the object's mark word in a stack lock record and uses compare and swap to point the header at that record. If a second thread contends, HotSpot can inflate the lock into an <code>ObjectMonitor<\/code> with ownership and waiting information. The contender may spin briefly, then park if necessary. Suppose the first thread updates a balance and leaves. A waiting thread becomes eligible to compete for the monitor, but a new arrival might acquire it first. Java promises no fairness here. Biased locking is gone in Java 21. Still, I would not assign every source-level lock a fixed instruction cost: the JIT can eliminate locks on confined objects or combine adjacent acquisitions. I would measure actual contention before choosing another primitive.","follow":"What changes if the owner calls <code>wait<\/code>, and does <code>notify<\/code> hand the monitor straight to a waiting thread?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-under-the-hood","title":"Under the hood: object headers, monitors and the JIT","track":"java","module":3},{"id":"java-under-the-hood.s2","q":"\"Our shutdown flag works in short tests but a worker spins forever under load. It even happens on one core. What would you check and fix?\"","points":["Check for a plain shared flag with no happens-before edge between the writer and reader","Explain that JIT hoisting or register reuse can stop repeated reads even on one core","Treat interpreter-versus-JIT behaviour as diagnostic evidence, not a correctness guarantee","Use a volatile flag for independent stop state; its ordering also publishes writes preceding the signal","Distinguish visibility from compound atomicity, with both threads reading 7 and writing 8","For a blocking worker, use cooperative interruption or an explicit wakeup protocol as well"],"model":"I would first check whether the stop flag is a plain field shared without synchronization. If it is, the worker has no guarantee of seeing the shutdown write, even on one core. Once the loop gets hot, the JIT may reuse a value in a register or hoist the read outside the loop. The operating system can schedule both threads perfectly and the worker can still keep testing the old value. Running with the interpreter can help diagnose that difference, but a successful run does not make the plain field correct. For an independent stop signal, I would make the flag <code>volatile<\/code>. A reader that observes the signal also sees writes that happened before the writer published it. This constrains compiler transformations and supplies the hardware ordering needed by the architecture; it does not require flushing every cache to RAM. Visibility also does not make a compound update atomic. Two threads can both read seven from a volatile counter and both write eight. That needs an atomic increment or a lock. Finally, a visible flag cannot wake a worker stuck in a blocking call. I would use interruption where supported, or a wakeup protocol, and ensure the worker responds by exiting. Then I would test shutdown after warmup and while the worker is blocked.","follow":"Would <code>Thread.sleep<\/code>, <code>Thread.yield<\/code> or <code>Thread.onSpinWait<\/code> make the plain flag safe?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-under-the-hood","title":"Under the hood: object headers, monitors and the JIT","track":"java","module":3},{"id":"java-under-the-hood.s3","q":"\"A benchmark says locking a local object is almost free, but production lock waits are huge. How would you investigate before replacing <code>synchronized<\/code>?\"","points":["A local-object benchmark may measure lock elimination rather than actual acquisition","Escape analysis can remove unobservable locks; coarsening can merge repeated acquisitions","Measure genuinely shared locks with warmup, representative contention and useful work using JMH","Inspect long critical sections and blocking calls before changing the primitive","Correlate long waits with safepoint or GC pauses, which do not release an owner's monitor"],"model":"I would check whether the benchmark measured a lock acquisition at all. If the object never escapes the thread, escape analysis may let the JIT eliminate its synchronization. Repeated acquisitions can also be coarsened into one larger critical section. Near-zero time in that setup tells me little about a shared production lock. I would use JMH with warmup, a genuinely shared lock, representative thread counts and work whose result is consumed. I would measure both throughput and the distribution of waiting times. Next I would inspect what the owner does while holding the lock. For example, one network call inside the critical section can make every contender wait, regardless of which lock class I choose. I would move that work outside the lock where the invariant allows it. I would also correlate the long waits with GC and safepoint logs. A stop-the-world pause freezes the owner without releasing its monitor, so waiters inherit that delay. The next change should address the measured cause: the benchmark, the critical section, contention or pauses.","follow":"On Java 21, how would blocking inside <code>synchronized<\/code> affect virtual threads and their carriers?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-under-the-hood","title":"Under the hood: object headers, monitors and the JIT","track":"java","module":3},{"id":"java-inheritance-and-polymorphism.s1","q":"\"A worker holds a superclass reference, but the object is a subclass. Walk me through how Java decides which method runs when there are overloads too.\"","points":["Choose the signature at compile time using the receiver and argument types","Dispatch the selected overridable instance signature using the runtime receiver","Give an Object-typed argument holding a String as an example","Distinguish static hiding and field selection from instance dispatch","Explain that a cast changes the view while super explicitly chooses a parent body"],"model":"Java makes two decisions. First the compiler chooses a signature from the receiver's declared type and the argument types. Then, for an overridable instance method, the runtime object chooses the implementation of that signature. Suppose the parent exposes accept taking Object, and the child overrides it while also overloading accept for String. Through a parent reference, passing a string still selects accept taking Object, and the child implementation of that signature runs. An Object variable holding a string also remains Object for overload selection. Static methods and fields are different: they use the compile-time view, so I qualify static calls with the class name. Casting the receiver to its parent does not turn off instance dispatch. Inside a subclass, an explicit super call selects the parent body. I trace those decisions separately before discussing what the worker does.","follow":"What if that parent body itself calls an overridden helper?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-inheritance-and-polymorphism","title":"Inheritance, overriding and polymorphism","track":"java","module":4},{"id":"java-inheritance-and-polymorphism.s2","q":"\"Our service sometimes fails during construction. The parent logs a description by calling an overridden method. Why is that dangerous, and would final or synchronized fix it?\"","points":["Identify dispatch into an incompletely initialized subclass as the immediate defect","Trace allocation defaults, superclass constructor, subclass initializers, and subclass constructor body","Explain why final fields and a final override do not repair an early read","Separate the single-thread construction bug from possible this escape to another thread","Move callbacks and publication after construction and choose a valid publication mechanism"],"model":"The immediate problem is calling subclass code before the subclass is initialized. Java still dispatches to the override while the parent constructor is running. Allocation has given every field its default value, so a subclass name may still be null. The subclass field initializers run only after the parent constructor returns, followed by the subclass constructor body. A final field does not make a future assignment happen early, and making the override final only prevents further overriding. Synchronization does not repair that ordering either. This can fail with just one thread, for example when the override dereferences the name. It becomes a publication problem as well if the override registers this or starts a worker. I would remove the callback from construction, finish creating the object, and then log or register it through a factory or explicit lifecycle method. For another thread, I also need safe publication, such as starting it after construction. Private or final construction helpers are acceptable only when their entire call path avoids uninitialized state and escape.","follow":"If a final constructor helper calls another overridable method, have you fixed the problem?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-inheritance-and-polymorphism","title":"Inheritance, overriding and polymorphism","track":"java","module":4},{"id":"java-inheritance-and-polymorphism.s3","q":"\"A subclass adds bookkeeping before and after calling a synchronized parent method. The override has no synchronized modifier. Is the whole operation protected, and how would you fix it?\"","points":["Synchronization is not inherited by the overriding method","The explicit parent call locks the same receiver only for its execution","Without an enclosing lock, the before and after steps can interleave with other calls","Synchronizing the override on the same monitor protects the whole sequence and allows reentrant parent calls","A final synchronized entry method preserves the boundary but hooks still need a locking contract"],"model":"No. A legal override can drop synchronization. If the caller holds no lock, the bookkeeping starts unprotected. The call to the parent acquires this object's monitor, and returning from that call releases it, leaving the final bookkeeping unprotected too. If those three steps form one invariant, I would synchronize the override on the same object and ensure every other access to that state uses that monitor. Java monitors are reentrant, so calling the synchronized parent while already holding the monitor is allowed. Alternatively, a final synchronized entry method can preserve the boundary for subclasses. I would still review any hooks it calls, because subclass code runs while that lock is held and could block or acquire another lock.","follow":"If the override locks a separate private lock, which accesses to the parent state still need review?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-inheritance-and-polymorphism","title":"Inheritance, overriding and polymorphism","track":"java","module":4},{"id":"java-oop-principles.s1","q":"\"All the fields in this class are private. Why are you still worried about encapsulation and thread safety?\"","points":["Private access alone does not preserve a class invariant or coordinate threads","An alias returned by a getter can bypass the class's locking policy","Separate <code>size<\/code> and unchecked <code>add<\/code> calls can both be locked but exceed capacity together","Expose <code>offer<\/code> as one check and update under the same private lock used by readers","Return an independent snapshot with immutable elements and keep the delegate owned"],"model":"Private fields are a useful starting point, but I need to know which rules the public operations preserve and whether any aliases escape. A getter that returns the internal list lets a caller mutate it without taking our lock. Even if no reference escapes, separate size and unchecked add methods can each be synchronized and still allow two threads to fill the last slot. Both can observe space before either adds. I would expose <code>offer<\/code> as the operation: it checks capacity and inserts while holding one private lock, or returns false without changing state. Reads of the deque take that same lock. For inspection I would copy the contents while locked and return an unmodifiable snapshot whose elements are immutable. The caller can then use that result independently. I would also check construction: if we retain a deque supplied by somebody else, that owner may still bypass our wrapper. The proof needs ownership and valid state transitions as well as access modifiers.","follow":"Would an unmodifiable view of the internal list be enough?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-oop-principles","title":"Encapsulation, composition and SOLID","track":"java","module":4},{"id":"java-oop-principles.s2","q":"\"Why would you choose composition here, and how does that relate to SOLID?\"","points":["Composition keeps the inbox API and synchronization boundary under its owner's control","Inheritance would expose mutation paths that may bypass the inbox invariant","Liskov substitution requires preserving behaviour, including promised thread safety","Small reader and writer capabilities illustrate interface segregation","Abstractions support needed variations without requiring an interface for every class"],"model":"I would choose composition because an inbox needs a small set of operations, while inheriting a collection exposes many ways to mutate its contents. Owning a private deque lets me enforce capacity and locking at one boundary. That connects to substitution: any implementation of my inbox interface must keep its behavioural promises, including atomic admission for concurrent callers. Matching signatures is not enough. Interface segregation asks a related question from the client's side. A reporting client may need only snapshots, so it should not depend on deletion or repair methods. Dependency inversion lets the client rely on that capability while application setup chooses the implementation. I would introduce those boundaries where they clarify real requirements. I would not create a large hierarchy just to claim SOLID compliance. The trade-off is writing some forwarding code in exchange for a contract I can explain and enforce.","follow":"When would inheritance be a reasonable choice instead?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-oop-principles","title":"Encapsulation, composition and SOLID","track":"java","module":4},{"id":"java-oop-principles.s3","q":"\"Our expiry tests sleep and occasionally fail. How would you redesign the time dependency?\"","points":["Inject a <code>Clock<\/code> through the constructor and use it for every time read","Define the inclusive expiry boundary explicitly","Test just before, at and after expiry with fixed clocks and no sleeps","Distinguish an absolute wall-clock deadline from a monotonic elapsed budget","Keep any expiry check and dependent mutation correctly coordinated"],"model":"I would pass a clock into the constructor and make every time read use it. Production can supply a system clock; tests can supply fixed instants. First I would define the boundary: an item is expired when now is equal to or after its deadline. Then I can test one nanosecond before, exactly at and one nanosecond after the deadline without sleeping. The expected answers are false, true and true. I would also check what kind of time the requirement means. An absolute calendar deadline can use an instant from a clock. An elapsed timeout or token refill budget needs a monotonic ticker instead. Injecting time makes the tests controllable; it does not provide that monotonic guarantee. Finally, if checking expiry authorizes a state change, I still need to coordinate the check and mutation so another thread cannot invalidate the decision in between.","follow":"What must be true of a mutable fake clock shared by several test threads?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-oop-principles","title":"Encapsulation, composition and SOLID","track":"java","module":4},{"id":"java-oop-design-for-lld.s1","q":"\"Your listener list is a <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList<\/code>. Why would you still move notifications outside the lock, and what guarantees do I lose?\"","points":["Thread-safe membership does not make listener code safe under the domain lock","Commit state and capture an immutable event plus recipients, then unlock before callbacks","Slow or reentrant listeners can delay operations or introduce lock cycles","Outside-lock delivery permits reordered and overlapping callbacks; versions identify commits","Unsubscribe affects later snapshots and listener failures need an explicit policy","Enqueue events in commit order and use bounded serial dispatch for ordered delivery"],"model":"I would move callbacks outside the domain lock because a thread-safe listener list only protects membership. It does not control what a listener does. I commit the state, capture an immutable event and the recipient snapshot, release the lock, and then invoke callbacks. A listener might perform slow I/O, call back into my service, or acquire another lock. Keeping it inside my lock would extend the critical section and could create a lock cycle. Moving it out allows publishers to deliver out of commit order. Publisher one can commit and pause, allowing publisher two to deliver first. The same listener can also be called concurrently, so its own mutable state needs protection. I include the commit version in the event rather than asking listeners to reread live state. Removal only affects later snapshots, so an already-selected callback may still run. I also define whether one listener failure stops delivery; here I collect runtime failures and continue. If ordered delivery is required, I enqueue events in commit order under the state lock and use one dispatcher that invokes listeners after unlocking. That dispatcher needs bounded capacity, an overload policy and shutdown rules. Copy-on-write alone does not provide those guarantees.","follow":"What would unsubscribe need to guarantee that no callback is running when it returns?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-oop-design-for-lld","title":"Design patterns for LLD and machine coding rounds","track":"java","module":4},{"id":"java-oop-design-for-lld.s2","q":"\"You have a concurrent map, a booking command and a pricing strategy. Walk me through what prevents two users getting the same seat.\"","points":["The invariant is at most one owner per seat; patterns alone do not enforce it","The registry resolves one stable entity per seat ID throughout operations","The entity lock covers checking availability and assigning the owner as one operation","Strategy selects behaviour and command packages an action without adding thread safety","Multi-seat operations need the required locks in a fixed order and one atomic update","Test contention and independent entities; process-local locks do not coordinate servers"],"model":"The seat's atomic reservation operation prevents double booking. The invariant is that each seat has at most one owner. My concurrent map resolves one stable seat object for each ID, and that seat owns a private lock. Inside the lock I check whether it is free and, if it is, assign the owner before releasing the lock. A competing caller reaches the same object and sees the changed state. I must not remove and recreate that entity while callers still hold references, because then two different locks could protect the same logical seat. The pricing strategy only chooses the pricing algorithm. The command only packages the request for execution. Neither makes that check and update atomic. For a request covering two seats, I would acquire both locks in a fixed ID order, check both seats, and update both before unlocking. Two separate successful-looking method calls are not a transaction. I would test two callers released together and assert exactly one winner, then verify that independent seats can progress without sharing a domain lock. Finally, this proof covers threads in one process. With several servers, the shared database or another authoritative owner must enforce the reservation rule.","follow":"How would you add a deadline and an idempotent retry, and define which result wins when cancellation races with booking?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-oop-design-for-lld","title":"Design patterns for LLD and machine coding rounds","track":"java","module":4},{"id":"java-toolkit-threads.s1","q":"\"Walk me through <code>Runnable<\/code>, <code>Callable<\/code> and <code>Thread<\/code>. Why would you implement an interface instead of extending <code>Thread<\/code>?\"","points":["Runnable and Callable describe tasks; Thread supplies an execution lifecycle","Runnable returns void without checked exceptions; Callable returns a value and may throw Exception","Direct run or call is synchronous; start schedules a thread once","Composition lets tasks be tested directly and scheduled by different owners","Completion and failure need joins plus reporting, or futures"],"model":"I separate the work from the mechanism that runs it. A Runnable describes a task with no return value and no declared checked exceptions. A Callable can return a value and throw a checked exception. Neither interface starts anything: calling run or call directly executes on the caller. A Thread gives a Runnable an execution lifecycle, and start schedules that thread once. Extending Thread works, but it couples a domain task to one execution mechanism and uses its single class inheritance slot. I prefer a task object that I can test directly, give to a platform thread, or submit to an executor. Then the owner chooses how to collect completion and failure. With a raw thread I join and report failures separately; with a Callable I submit it and inspect the future. That keeps task behaviour separate from lifecycle policy.","follow":"What happens if you call run, then start, then join, then start again on one platform thread?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-toolkit-threads","title":"The toolkit: Thread, Runnable, Callable and virtual threads","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-threads.s2","q":"\"Our request timed out after a join, but its worker kept changing state. What would you check and fix?\"","points":["A timed join limits the caller's wait and does not stop the worker","Inspect isAlive after the void overload or the Duration overload's boolean","Interrupt requests cancellation and needs a cooperative task","Interruption of a joining caller does not cancel the worker","Bound cleanup separately and retain ownership if termination remains unconfirmed","Reuse or read result state only after safe completion or an explicit late-result policy"],"model":"The timeout only ended the caller's wait. It did not stop the worker, so I would first check whether the code wrongly treats a timed join as completion. After the void overload I check isAlive; with the Duration overload I inspect its boolean. If the work is no longer wanted, I request interruption and check that the task actually cooperates, either through interruptible waits or explicit checks between bounded pieces of work. I also distinguish cancellation of the worker from interruption of the caller while it is joining. The latter ends the caller's wait and does not automatically cancel the worker. Next I give cleanup a separate bounded budget. If termination is still unconfirmed, I report that honestly and retain ownership rather than claiming the worker stopped. Finally, I stop reusing state that the old worker may still touch. I collect a private result after safe completion or define a policy that rejects late results. That fixes both the lifetime bug and the unsafe result access.","follow":"What if the task never checks interruption and blocks in an API that does not respond to it?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-threads","title":"The toolkit: Thread, Runnable, Callable and virtual threads","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-threads.s3","q":"\"Would you switch this blocking request service to virtual threads, and what would you review first?\"","points":["Choose virtual threads for many waiting tasks, not added CPU capacity","Use a virtual thread per task and own completion, failure and cancellation","Bound scarce dependencies and admitted work independently of thread cost","Review Java 21 pinning during blocking synchronized or native execution","Review ThreadLocal cleanup and avoid expensive per-thread resource caches"],"model":"I would consider virtual threads if the service has many requests waiting on blocking I/O. They reduce the cost of waiting; they do not add CPU capacity or make shared state safe. I would use a virtual thread per task and keep an explicit owner for results, failure and cancellation. An executor scoped with try with resources helps await termination, but its close can still wait indefinitely for uncooperative work. Next I would preserve a limit on database connections and remote calls, and bound admitted requests. Cheap threads must not become unlimited pressure on a dependency. Since this service runs Java 21, I would inspect long blocking operations inside synchronized regions or native execution for carrier pinning. Finally I would review thread locals. A small request id is reasonable with clear cleanup, but an expensive connection per virtual thread would multiply resource usage. I would borrow that connection from a bounded pool and return it reliably. Then I would measure the actual workload before claiming a throughput improvement.","follow":"If each request is pure CPU work, what execution policy would you choose instead?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-threads","title":"The toolkit: Thread, Runnable, Callable and virtual threads","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-synchronized.s1","q":"\"Walk me through what <code>synchronized<\/code> actually guarantees. Which object am I locking, and can I call another synchronized method from inside it?\"","points":["Instance methods lock the receiver, static methods lock the declaring <code>Class<\/code> object, blocks lock their supplied object","Exclusion applies only to code acquiring the same monitor","An unlock happens-before subsequent acquisition of that same monitor, providing visibility for ordinary guarded fields","Reentrancy permits the owner to enter again; other threads wait until all holds are released","Normal and exceptional exit release holds but do not roll back mutations","Use a stable private lock and protect reads as well as writes"],"model":"Synchronized gives mutual exclusion and visibility through one particular monitor. An instance method locks its receiver. A static method locks the declaring class's Class object. A block locks the object in its parentheses. That identity matters: two instances have different monitors, and a static method does not exclude an instance method merely because both say synchronized. The visibility rule is that an unlock happens before subsequent acquisition of the same monitor. So ordinary fields written before unlocking are visible after that acquisition, provided readers follow the same protocol. I can call another synchronized method on the same receiver because intrinsic locks are reentrant. The thread adds another hold and releases it when the inner method returns. Other threads still cannot enter until the outer hold is released too. Return and exception paths release the relevant holds automatically, but exceptions do not undo mutations. For an encapsulated component, I usually choose a stable private lock and use it for every access to its guarded state, including getters. I also restore invariants before any exit.","follow":"What changes if the inner synchronized method belongs to a different object?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-synchronized","title":"The toolkit: synchronized, wait and notify","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-synchronized.s2","q":"\"Your bounded queue works with one producer and one consumer. Now I add more of both and it hangs. What do you check in the wait and notify code?\"","points":["Check one monitor guards every predicate check, mutation, wait and notification","Every wait is in a <code>while<\/code> loop because of spurious wakeups and competing consumers or producers","Wait releases the monitor and reacquires it before continuing; notification retains ownership","One wait set contains producers and consumers, so notify can choose the wrong kind and strand progress","Use <code>notifyAll()<\/code> after each successful state change and explain its extra contention","Check cancellation or closure so departed producers cannot strand consumers"],"model":"I first check the monitor protocol, especially whether a single notification can wake the wrong kind of waiter. Every queue access and condition check must use the same monitor. A producer waits while full and a consumer waits while empty, using while loops. Wait releases that monitor so someone else can change the queue, then reacquires it before returning. A notification does not transfer ownership or reserve an item. Another consumer can take the item before the notified consumer gets back in, which is why an if statement is unsafe. Spurious wakeups also require rechecking. With intrinsic monitors, both kinds of waiter share one wait set. Imagine two consumers asleep on an empty queue. A producer inserts, wakes one consumer, then waits on its next insertion because the queue is full. The consumer removes the item but notifies the other consumer. That consumer finds nothing and waits again, leaving the producer asleep despite available space. I use <code>notifyAll<\/code> after each successful change so every waiter can recheck. The cost is extra contention; separate conditions can target wakeups more precisely. Finally, I check whether a producer exited or was interrupted. A service needs an explicit closure protocol so consumers do not wait forever after production ends.","follow":"How would you add a timeout without restarting the full timeout after each wakeup?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-toolkit-synchronized","title":"The toolkit: synchronized, wait and notify","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-volatile-and-atomics.s1","q":"\"Why would a volatile stop flag work, but a volatile request counter still lose updates?\"","points":["A stop flag needs visibility of an independent assignment; an increment needs an indivisible read-modify-write","A volatile write happens-before subsequent reads of that same field, ordering preceding writes too","Two increments can both read 5 and both write 6 despite individually atomic accesses","Choose an atomic increment for an exact counter, or <code>LongAdder<\/code> for a statistical aggregate","A flag does not wake blocked work and does not replace interruption or blocking coordination"],"model":"A volatile flag can publish a stop request, but it cannot make a counter increment indivisible. An assignment and an increment need different guarantees. A volatile write happens-before subsequent reads of that field, which also orders the writer's preceding changes. So a worker that checks the flag can observe the request without an explicit lock. An increment still reads, adds one, and writes. Two threads can both read five and both write six, losing one request even though each access is atomic. For an exact counter I would use an atomic increment. For a heavily updated metric, <code>LongAdder<\/code> may be suitable if its aggregate read contract is acceptable. Finally, a stop flag only helps a worker that checks it. It does not wake a blocked queue operation, so cancellation may also need interruption or an explicit shutdown protocol.","follow":"What changes if the flag is set before initialization starts, and readers treat it as ready?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-toolkit-volatile-and-atomics","title":"The toolkit: volatile and the atomic classes","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-volatile-and-atomics.s2","q":"\"Walk me through a Java reservation that uses CAS, and tell me where you would put the payment call.\"","points":["Read available stock, reject insufficient stock, compute the candidate, then <code>compareAndSet<\/code>","A successful CAS is the reservation's linearization point","After failure, reload and revalidate; two buyers for one unit cannot both succeed","Keep payment out of the retry computation because retries may repeat side effects","Handle failure after reservation with an explicit lifecycle; CAS alone does not give payment exactly once","Prefer a lock or one immutable aggregate when the invariant spans additional state"],"model":"I would reserve with a CAS loop and keep payment outside the retried computation. First I read the available stock, reject an invalid quantity or insufficient stock, and compute the reduced value. I compare and set from the exact value I read to that candidate. Success is the instant the reservation takes effect. On failure, I read again and repeat the check. With one unit left, two buyers may both read one, but only one can change one to zero. The loser rereads zero and rejects. I would not call payment inside an update function or retry loop, because a retry could charge again. After a successful reservation, payment needs its own explicit lifecycle: record the reservation, attempt an idempotent payment, and define confirmation or release on failure. A process crash between those steps needs recovery; the atomic integer cannot solve that. If the reservation also changes a mutable order list, I would protect both with one lock or replace one immutable aggregate. The primitive must cover the business invariant, not just the number.","follow":"If the reservation state returns to the same object while payment is pending, how would you reject a stale completion?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-volatile-and-atomics","title":"The toolkit: volatile and the atomic classes","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-volatile-and-atomics.s3","q":"\"My CAS succeeds even though another thread removed and reinserted the same node. What is happening, and does Java's garbage collector fix it?\"","points":["Name ABA: A changes to B and back to the same A, so ordinary identity CAS accepts it","GC prevents reclaiming a referenced object but not reuse of that same live node by the algorithm","Read reference and stamp together with <code>AtomicStampedReference.get(holder)<\/code>","Advance the stamp on each relevant transition and compare both identity and stamp","Stamp reuse or wraparound can defeat the protection; a version is a protocol, not automatic history"],"model":"That is ABA: the reference changed from A to B and back to the same A, so an ordinary CAS cannot detect the intervening changes. Java's garbage collector keeps an object alive while it is still referenced, but your algorithm can remove and reinsert that same live node. If that history invalidates the pending operation, I would attach a version. <code>AtomicStampedReference<\/code> stores the reference and an integer stamp together. I read both using <code>get<\/code> with a local stamp holder, then compare and set both expected components. If another thread changes A at stamp zero to B at one and A at two, my old expectation fails. Every relevant writer must advance the stamp; the class does not do it automatically. I would also address stamp reuse and wraparound, because seeing the same reference and stamp again defeats the check.","follow":"Why is calling <code>getReference()<\/code> and then <code>getStamp()<\/code> insufficient for taking the initial snapshot?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-toolkit-volatile-and-atomics","title":"The toolkit: volatile and the atomic classes","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-locks.s1","q":"\"Why would you choose ReentrantLock here, and how will you make waiting cancellable?\"","points":["Choose explicit locking for timed or interruptible entry or multiple conditions","State the protected invariant and keep the lock private","Acquire before try and release each successful hold in finally","Distinguish timeout false from InterruptedException and preserve cancellation","Explain fair admission and the untimed tryLock exception"],"model":"I would choose ReentrantLock when the component needs timed entry, interruptible entry, or separate waiting queues. First I would name the invariant and keep the lock private so every method uses the same protocol. For example, a mailbox's item and closed flag belong under one lock. I acquire immediately before the protected try block and release in finally, only after successful acquisition. If callers need cancellation while waiting for entry, I use lockInterruptibly. If they also need a waiting budget, I use timed tryLock and handle false as an ordinary timeout. InterruptedException is a separate cancellation outcome. I propagate it from a component method, or restore the flag and leave at a Runnable boundary. Plain lock does not abort acquisition on interruption. I would consider a fair lock only if reducing overtaking matters enough to justify its throughput cost. It does not promise a return deadline, and untimed tryLock can still barge. My choice therefore follows the waiting contract, not a belief that explicit locks are always faster.","follow":"How would a single request deadline cover both lock acquisition and a subsequent condition wait?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-locks","title":"The toolkit: ReentrantLock, Condition, ReadWriteLock and StampedLock","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-locks.s2","q":"\"Your queue sometimes hangs when closing. Walk me through what you would inspect.\"","points":["Check closed state and all waiting predicates under the same lock","Identify both producer and consumer condition queues","Explain await releasing the lock and reacquiring before predicate recheck","Broadcast closure to every affected queue while owning the lock","Distinguish rejecting producers from draining accepted items and ending consumers"],"model":"I would first check whether every blocked caller has a way to observe closure and finish. The closed flag and the queue contents must use the same lock. Producers may be waiting for space, while consumers may be waiting for an item, so I inspect both waiting queues. Each await belongs inside a while loop whose predicate also includes closure. Await releases the associated lock while waiting and reacquires it before continuing. A signal only makes a waiter eligible to compete; it does not transfer the lock or reserve an item. Close must set the flag while holding the lock and signal all waiters on both conditions. Signalling just one can leave the rest asleep forever. Then I check the intended API behaviour. Producers reject after closure. Consumers keep taking already accepted items, then return an end result once the queue is empty. I would test closure with blocked producers and blocked consumers separately, plus a race between close and put. Finally, I would join every worker so a stranded waiter fails the test.","follow":"Why is signal often sufficient during ordinary put and take operations, but insufficient for closing?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-locks","title":"The toolkit: ReentrantLock, Condition, ReadWriteLock and StampedLock","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-locks.s3","q":"\"Compare ReentrantReadWriteLock and StampedLock for a read-heavy service. What could go wrong?\"","points":["Require genuinely read-only work and measure whether shared access helps","Explain shared readers and exclusive writers with ReentrantReadWriteLock","Reject retained-read upgrades and describe release, write acquisition and recheck","Describe stamped copy, validate and locked fallback in that order","Mention non-reentrancy, no conditions and unsafe speculative side effects"],"model":"I would start by checking that the reads do not mutate anything and that their duration justifies more lock machinery. ReentrantReadWriteLock allows concurrent readers and an exclusive writer. Its rules are relatively straightforward, and its modes support reentrancy. But a reader cannot keep its read hold and block for write mode. I must release, acquire write mode, and recheck because someone else may have changed the state in that gap. StampedLock adds optimistic reads for small snapshots. I obtain a stamp, copy the fields into locals, validate, and use those locals only after success. On failure I recopy under a real read lock. Validating before the reads protects nothing that happens afterwards. I would avoid speculative collection traversal and callbacks because validation cannot undo an exception or external side effect. StampedLock also lacks reentrancy and conditions, which makes nested calls more delicate. For a two-integer snapshot it may be suitable; for an LRU lookup that changes recency, neither shared nor optimistic reading permits that write. I would measure before choosing the more complex option.","follow":"What happens to your existing read hold when tryConvertToWriteLock returns zero?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-locks","title":"The toolkit: ReentrantLock, Condition, ReadWriteLock and StampedLock","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-executors-and-futures.s1","q":"\"Our pool has a maximum of 100 threads, but it stays at 10 while latency and memory grow. What would you inspect and change?\"","points":["Inspect core size and queue type; an unbounded queue explains lack of growth beyond core","Explain admission: create below core, queue, grow only after queue failure, then reject","Relate backlog to retained memory and queueing latency","Choose queue capacity and rejection based on workload and downstream limits","Explain CallerRunsPolicy's caller-thread cost and silent-discard future risk","Measure queue age, rejection and dependency latency before tuning workers"],"model":"I would inspect the queue first. If core size is ten and the queue is unbounded, this behavior is expected. ThreadPoolExecutor creates workers below core, then tries to queue. Only when queueing fails does it grow toward maximum. Changing maximum to a hundred does not drain that backlog. Each queued task retains memory and waits longer before starting, explaining both symptoms. I would choose queue capacity and an overload response based on the latency budget and downstream capacity. AbortPolicy makes rejection visible so the caller can shed load or retry under a controlled policy. CallerRunsPolicy slows the producer by executing work on its thread, which can be unacceptable on an event loop. Silent discard is especially dangerous when callers hold futures that never complete. I would measure queue age, rejection counts, worker activity and dependency latency before changing concurrency. More workers can help some blocking workloads, but they can also overwhelm the database. The goal is bounded admitted work with an observable failure policy, not just a larger thread count.","follow":"What happens if every worker submits a child to this pool and waits for it?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-executors-and-futures","title":"The toolkit: executors, Future and CompletableFuture","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-executors-and-futures.s2","q":"\"Compare thenApply, thenCompose and thenCombine for me. Then tell me which thread runs the callbacks.\"","points":["Apply transforms a value, compose flattens a dependent stage, combine merges independent successes","Give dependent and independent lookup examples","Explain why joining a child inside apply can starve a bounded pool","Non-async callbacks can run during completion or registration","Async uses the default executor unless one is supplied; the preceding executor is not inherited","Observe the terminal result before closing executors needed by the graph"],"model":"Apply transforms a value, compose chains an asynchronous dependency, and combine joins independent successful results. If I have a user, apply can extract an identifier. If that identifier starts an asynchronous order lookup, compose gives me a future of orders instead of a future containing another future. If an independent discount lookup is running, combine can merge its result with the order total. I would not flatten the nested result by joining inside apply. That occupies a worker, and the child may need the same saturated pool. Non-async callbacks have no dedicated worker guarantee. They can run during completion, or immediately on the registering thread when the source is already complete. Async methods on ordinary CompletableFuture use the common pool by default, subject to its low-parallelism fallback. An explicitly supplied executor controls that boundary only; it is not inherited by later async calls. I would keep inline callbacks short, specify executors where isolation matters, and observe the terminal stage before closing any executor the graph still needs.","follow":"How does anyOf differ from invokeAny when the fastest operation fails?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-toolkit-executors-and-futures","title":"The toolkit: executors, Future and CompletableFuture","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-executors-and-futures.s3","q":"\"A request timed out, so I cancelled its future and shut down the pool. Can I tell the caller that the work has stopped?\"","points":["Timeout ends a wait, not the task","Pool Future cancellation can request interruption but cannot stop uncooperative work or roll back effects","CompletableFuture cancellation does not interrupt its supplier","Shutdown rejects new work; shutdownNow attempts interruption and returns pending tasks","Await termination and settle abandoned queued futures; close has no deadline","Only the owner shuts down the pool; remote effects need separate confirmation"],"model":"No. Those calls do not prove the work has stopped. Timed get limits my wait. Cancelling a pool task future can request interruption, but the task must cooperate, and cancellation does not roll back effects. CompletableFuture cancellation completes the outcome exceptionally without interrupting its supplier. Shutdown rejects new work while accepted tasks finish. ShutdownNow attempts interruption and returns queued tasks that never started; I must settle their futures so callers are not stranded. I would await termination and check its result before reporting that the executor's tasks finished. Close waits too, but has no deadline and can hang on uncooperative work. Finally, only the owner should shut down the executor. Stopping a borrowed pool affects unrelated requests. Even local termination does not confirm that a remote payment or write was undone, so that requires a separate application-level contract and confirmation.","follow":"How would you cancel a supplier blocked in an external API call?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-toolkit-executors-and-futures","title":"The toolkit: executors, Future and CompletableFuture","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-collections-and-synchronizers.s1","q":"\"We use a <code>ConcurrentHashMap<\/code>, yet our cache sometimes loads twice and our lists lose entries. Walk me through what you would inspect.\"","points":["The map protects mappings, not arbitrary call sequences or mutable value contents","<code>putIfAbsent<\/code> evaluates a supplied load before insertion; <code>computeIfAbsent<\/code> defers computation","Null results, exceptions and removal allow later computation; once forever is not guaranteed","Callbacks must stay short and must not modify the same map; expensive loads need shared in-flight state","Fix mutable lists with immutable replacement inside <code>compute<\/code> or separately synchronized values"],"model":"I would inspect the exact update operation and the value type. A concurrent map protects its mappings; it does not make all surrounding work atomic. If the code passes a load call into <code>putIfAbsent<\/code>, every caller can run that load before the map chooses a winner. <code>computeIfAbsent<\/code> defers computation until needed and coordinates it for the key, but it is not a once-forever promise. A null result, an exception or later removal allows another computation. The callback must not modify that map. I would also avoid slow I/O inside it because it can delay other updates. For an expensive load, I would represent the shared in-flight result explicitly and define retry after failure. For lost list entries, I would look for an <code>ArrayList<\/code> stored as the value and mutated after the map call returns. That list is still unsafe. One fix is <code>compute<\/code> that copies the old list, appends and publishes an immutable replacement. Another is a value with its own synchronization. I would choose based on list size and update frequency, then test concurrent callers and loader failure.","follow":"\"What if a thread removes the key while another thread still holds its old mutable value?\"","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-toolkit-collections-and-synchronizers","title":"The toolkit: concurrent collections and synchronizers","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-collections-and-synchronizers.s2","q":"\"You have a batch of tasks that discovers more tasks as it runs. Only eight remote calls may run at once. Which Java primitives would you choose, and how do failures change the design?\"","points":["Use a semaphore with eight permits for simultaneous remote calls, not for counting completions","Use a phaser for dynamic task membership, with an initial coordinator party","Register before submission; compensate exactly once for rejected or never-started tasks; running tasks deregister in <code>finally<\/code>","Acquire successfully before entering the release scope; record failures separately from completion","Define cancellation because phaser waiting timeout does not deregister or break the phaser"],"model":"I would separate the concurrency limit from completion tracking. An eight-permit semaphore limits remote calls. Each task acquires before the call and releases in a finally block entered only after acquisition succeeds. For a dynamically growing batch, I would use a phaser rather than a fixed-count latch. The coordinator starts registered so the phase cannot finish during initial submission. Every child is registered before submission, and rejected submission must undo that registration. A parent must register its children before it deregisters itself, so there is no gap where the batch appears complete. Each running task deregisters in finally and records its result or failure separately. Cancellation before startup needs coordinator cleanup that discharges that registration exactly once. Completion does not mean success. I would also define a batch cancellation policy. An interruptible phaser wait that times out neither removes a party nor breaks the phaser. For collective abort, the coordinator can terminate the phaser and request task cancellation, then join or otherwise observe task completion before consuming mutable results. The semaphore still needs cleanup on every successful acquisition, including during that cancellation.","follow":"\"What bounds memory if discovery produces tasks much faster than those eight calls finish?\"","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-toolkit-collections-and-synchronizers","title":"The toolkit: concurrent collections and synchronizers","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-collections-and-synchronizers.s3","q":"\"Choose between a bounded blocking queue, a <code>SynchronousQueue<\/code> and an <code>Exchanger<\/code> for handing buffers between threads.\"","points":["A bounded blocking queue stores up to a chosen number of waiting buffers and supports producer backpressure","<code>SynchronousQueue<\/code> stores nothing and requires a matching consumer for each transfer","<code>Exchanger<\/code> pairs participants and returns each other's buffer, supporting full-for-empty swaps","Queue timed <code>offer<\/code> and <code>poll<\/code> return false or null; timed <code>exchange<\/code> throws <code>TimeoutException<\/code>","Successful transfer publishes earlier writes but requires ownership discipline and a shutdown policy"],"model":"I would choose based on whether we need buffering or a paired swap. A bounded blocking queue lets producers get ahead by a fixed number of waiting buffers. Timed offer lets the caller reject or retry when that capacity is exhausted. A synchronous queue has no storage: a transfer needs a matching consumer, so it fits direct handoff. It does not wait for the consumer to finish processing. An exchanger is different because both participants supply a buffer and receive the other's. That is useful when a producer trades a full buffer for an empty one to reuse. I would specify timeouts and failure handling: timed queue operations return false or null, while timed exchange throws a timeout exception. A successful transfer publishes earlier writes, but both threads must respect ownership afterwards. None of these choices provides a complete shutdown protocol, so I would define what happens if a partner exits.","follow":"\"Can a queue with capacity ten still have more than ten unfinished jobs?\"","seconds":65,"lesson":"java-toolkit-collections-and-synchronizers","title":"The toolkit: concurrent collections and synchronizers","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-putting-it-together.s1","q":"\"You have a bounded queue and two workers. Walk me through a clean shutdown while producers are still submitting.\"","points":["Close means reject new admission and drain accepted jobs","One lock guards the closed check and insertion; overlapping calls are ordered by their effects","Close sets the flag and signals all producer and consumer waiters","Consumers return queued elements after closure and terminate only when closed and empty","Workers execute outside the lock; the owner joins after closing and releasing the lock","Draining requires available workers and terminating tasks; interruption is a separate cancellation policy"],"model":"I would close admission first, drain accepted jobs, and then wait for the workers. The queue's closed flag and contents share one private lock. An offer checks the flag and inserts while holding that lock, so there is no gap where shutdown can slip in. If insertion happens first, the job is accepted. If closure happens first, the offer is rejected. An accepted offer can return after close because those calls overlapped.\nClose sets the flag and signals all waiters on both conditions. Producers waiting for space need to wake and reject; consumers waiting for work need to wake and observe the terminal state. Consumers keep removing existing jobs after closure. They return end of stream only when the queue is both closed and empty.\nEach worker runs its task after releasing the queue lock. The owner then joins the workers outside that lock, because a waking worker must reacquire it before exiting. An empty queue alone is insufficient: a worker may still be executing its last job.\nThis is graceful shutdown, assuming workers remain available and tasks eventually finish. Interrupting workers is a different cancellation policy. For bounded shutdown latency, I would add a termination timeout and define what happens to unfinished jobs.","follow":"What changes if shutdown must return immediately and give the caller every job that has not started?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-toolkit-putting-it-together","title":"Putting it together: a thread safe class from scratch","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-toolkit-putting-it-together.s2","q":"\"All the fields are private and every method takes a lock. Is the class thread safe now? What would you review next?\"","points":["Private fields and individual locking are insufficient for a compound invariant","Check that admission and lifecycle state use one lock across check and update","Review waiting predicates, loop rechecks and shutdown notifications","Review timeout budgets, interruption and exception cleanup","Check escaped references, callback placement and a concrete adversarial test"],"model":"No, I still need to check the class's contract across methods. Two individually locked calls can leave a race between checking that the queue is open and inserting a job. I would identify the invariant and ensure the same lock protects the whole decision, including lifecycle state and reads.\nNext I would write each waiting predicate and identify every change that can make a waiter proceed. Waits need loops, and closing this queue must wake both producers and consumers. I would check that timeouts share one budget and that interruption leaves no partial insertion and releases the lock.\nThen I would inspect references and callbacks: no internal collection should escape, and task execution belongs outside the lock. Finally, I would force a producer to wait on a full queue, close it, and verify rejection while the original job remains drainable.","follow":"How would you test that a blocked consumer is woken by close without using sleep to arrange the schedule?","seconds":60,"lesson":"java-toolkit-putting-it-together","title":"Putting it together: a thread safe class from scratch","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-interview-style.s1","q":"\"Design a bounded Java job queue with graceful shutdown. Before you type, talk me through the contract, the synchronization and how you would test it.\"","points":["Clarify allowed libraries, full-queue behaviour, shutdown semantics and job failure reporting","State one lock for the deque and closed flag, with size between zero and capacity","Choose two conditions, acquire interruptibly before try, wait in while loops and unlock in finally","After mutations signal the opposite waiter group; close wakes both groups, rejects producers and lets consumers drain","Run jobs outside the queue lock and handle task failures separately from worker cancellation","Test capacity-one contention, exact item identities, shutdown races and interruption with bounded test termination"],"model":"I would start with one lock guarding the queue and its closed flag. Before coding, I would confirm whether library queues are allowed, whether a full queue blocks or rejects, whether shutdown drains accepted work, and how job failures are reported. Assuming we build it ourselves with blocking submission and graceful drain, the invariant is that the size stays between zero and capacity. I would use a private <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> with conditions named not empty and not full. Blocking methods acquire interruptibly before entering the try block and always unlock in finally. Producers wait while full and open; consumers wait while empty and open. Both waits use while loops because waking does not reserve a slot or an item. An insertion signals a consumer, and a removal signals a producer. Close sets the flag under the same lock and wakes both groups. A producer checks closed again after waiting and rejects if necessary. Consumers keep taking queued jobs, then receive an end marker only when closed and empty. That also resolves a submit racing with close at the shared lock. Workers run jobs outside the queue lock, report task failures, and treat interruption as a separate cancellation decision. I would test capacity one, several producers and consumers, exact job identities, a failing task, interruption, and close racing with submission. Tests need timed joins plus a failure path that terminates the test process; a timeout alone does not stop stuck workers.","follow":"How would immediate shutdown differ, especially for a job already running or one just removed from the queue?","seconds":105,"lesson":"java-interview-style","title":"Writing Java in an interview","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-interview-style.s2","q":"\"Why did you choose <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> for this bounded buffer? Could you write a correct version with <code>synchronized<\/code>, and what would you lose?\"","points":["Both choices provide mutual exclusion and visibility when all guarded accesses follow the same locking policy","ReentrantLock supports separate notFull and notEmpty conditions for targeted signalling","A synchronized version uses one wait set with while-loop waits and notifyAll for mixed waiters","Explicit locking supports timed or interruptible acquisition; Object.wait is interruptible but monitor acquisition is not","Fair mode favours the longest-waiting acquirer, costs throughput and does not guarantee scheduling or cover untimed tryLock"],"model":"Both can implement a correct buffer. I chose <code>ReentrantLock<\/code> because producers and consumers wait for different predicates, so two conditions make targeted signalling straightforward. One lock still protects the whole invariant, and readers follow the same policy as writers. With synchronized methods, I would keep the same guarded state and while-loop checks, but use the object's single wait set. After a change, I would use notify all so waking a producer when only a consumer can proceed cannot strand the queue. The tradeoff is extra wakeups and competition to reacquire the monitor. Explicit locking also gives me interruptible acquisition and timed acquisition. Object wait itself is interruptible, but a thread waiting to enter a synchronized block cannot cancel that acquisition through interruption. If fairness is required, a fair lock favours the longest-waiting acquirer, usually at a throughput cost. It does not control thread scheduling, and untimed try lock can still barge. I would choose based on those requirements and explain the policy, rather than assume one keyword or class is universally faster.","follow":"If several producers submit batches of different sizes, is signalling one producer on <code>notFull<\/code> still sufficient?","seconds":75,"lesson":"java-interview-style","title":"Writing Java in an interview","track":"java","module":5},{"id":"java-interview-style.s3","q":"\"A worker lambda captures a changing loop index and calls <code>await<\/code>. After some quick compile fixes, starting the workers never returns. How would you review this code out loud?\"","points":["Copy the loop index into a final or effectively final local for each worker","A Runnable cannot propagate InterruptedException; restore the flag and exit at the worker boundary","Check for run instead of start, which executes the endless worker loop on the caller","Acquire before try, release in finally, and recheck the predicate after await reacquires the lock","Run jobs outside the lock, report task failures and provide a close protocol that wakes waiters","Verify worker identities, completion and interruption with timed joins and a bounded test failure path"],"model":"I would separate the compile errors from the lifecycle bugs. First, a changing loop index cannot be captured by the lambda. I would copy it into a final local inside each iteration and use that value to identify the worker. Second, <code>await<\/code> throws a checked interruption exception, but a <code>Runnable<\/code> cannot declare it. At the worker boundary I would catch it, restore the interrupt flag and exit, rather than continue an endless loop. For the symptom that starting never returns, I would check whether the code calls run instead of start. Run executes on the caller, so the first worker can trap the caller before any other worker is created. Next I would inspect lock ownership. Acquisition belongs before try, unlocking belongs in finally, and the wait belongs in a while loop. Await releases the lock and reacquires it before returning or throwing an interruption exception. After removing a job, I would release the lock before running it and report any task failure. I would also require a close path that wakes idle workers and defines whether queued work drains. Finally, I would verify actual worker identities, job completion and interruption. Timed joins detect stalls, but the test also needs daemon workers or a bounded process exit on failure.","follow":"Why can catching an interruption, restoring the flag and then continuing the same await loop turn into a busy loop?","seconds":90,"lesson":"java-interview-style","title":"Writing Java in an interview","track":"java","module":5}];
